window.DEEP_STORIES["vaccines"] = {"slug":"vaccines","title":"Vaccines","emoji":"💉","tagline":"How people learned to train the body to beat deadly diseases","intro":"For thousands of years, a disease called smallpox swept through towns and cities, killing about three in every ten people it infected and scarring many survivors. Nobody knew what caused it, and nobody could stop it. Then people around the world noticed something strange: if you survived smallpox once, you almost never caught it again. That clue started one of the greatest detective stories in history. In these 20 parts you will travel from ancient Egypt to Chinese and African healers, from an English country doctor and a milkmaid to Louis Pasteur's laboratory in Paris. You will find out how your immune system remembers its enemies, watch scientists race to stop polio, and join the worldwide team that wiped smallpox off the planet. Finally you will see how mRNA vaccines were built in record time, and what scientists are working on next. This is a story about history and science, not medical advice.","outline":[{"n":1,"title":"The Speckled Monster","when":"Ancient times – 1700s"},{"n":2,"title":"Fighting Fire with Fire: Variolation","when":"1500s – early 1700s"},{"n":3,"title":"Lady Mary and Onesimus","when":"1706 – 1722"},{"n":4,"title":"Empresses, Armies and a Dangerous Gamble","when":"1720s – 1790s"},{"n":5,"title":"The Milkmaid's Secret: Edward Jenner","when":"1774 – 1798"},{"n":6,"title":"Around the World, Arm to Arm","when":"1798 – 1900"},{"n":7,"title":"Invisible Enemies: Germ Theory","when":"1840s – 1890s"},{"n":8,"title":"Pasteur's Weakened Germs","when":"1879 – 1888"},{"n":9,"title":"Antitoxins, Toxoids and a New Wave of Vaccines","when":"1890 – 1940s"},{"n":10,"title":"Your Body's Defence Force, Part 1","when":"1880s – today (science)"},{"n":11,"title":"Your Body's Defence Force, Part 2: Memory","when":"1950s – today (science)"},{"n":12,"title":"Inside a Vaccine: Types, Making and Testing","when":"1900s – today (science)"},{"n":13,"title":"The Summer Plague: Polio","when":"1890s – 1952"},{"n":14,"title":"Salk, Sabin and the Polio Pioneers","when":"1952 – today"},{"n":15,"title":"Wiping Smallpox Off the Earth","when":"1958 – 1980"},{"n":16,"title":"The Golden Age of Childhood Vaccines","when":"1954 – 2000s"},{"n":17,"title":"Side Effects, Myths and Trust","when":"1970s – today"},{"n":18,"title":"The mRNA Breakthrough","when":"1990s – 2021"},{"n":19,"title":"New Frontiers","when":"2006 – the future"},{"n":20,"title":"From Scabs to Code: The Whole Story","when":"Ancient times – today"}],"people":[["Lady Mary Wortley Montagu (1689–1762)","English writer who survived smallpox and promoted inoculation in Britain after seeing it in Constantinople"],["Onesimus (late 1600s – after 1716)","enslaved West African man in Boston who described African inoculation to Cotton Mather"],["Cotton Mather (1663–1728)","Boston minister who pushed for inoculation during the 1721 epidemic"],["Zabdiel Boylston (1679–1766)","Boston doctor who inoculated about 240 people in 1721 and recorded the results"],["Charles Maitland (around 1668–1748)","surgeon who inoculated Lady Mary's children and the Newgate prisoners"],["Emanuel Timoni (died around 1718) and Giacomo Pylarini (1659–1718)","doctors who reported Ottoman inoculation to the Royal Society"],["Caroline of Ansbach (1683–1737)","Princess of Wales who had her daughters inoculated in 1722"],["Benjamin Franklin (1706–1790)","American statesman who lost his son to smallpox and later supported inoculation"],["Daniel Bernoulli (1700–1782)","Swiss mathematician who calculated the benefits of inoculation in 1760"],["Thomas Dimsdale (1712–1800)","English doctor who inoculated Catherine the Great"],["Catherine the Great (1729–1796)","Empress of Russia, inoculated in 1768"],["George Washington (1732–1799)","smallpox survivor who ordered the Continental Army inoculated in 1777"],["Benjamin Jesty (1736–1816)","Dorset farmer who used cowpox on his family in 1774"],["Edward Jenner (1749–1823)","English country doctor who tested and published cowpox vaccination (1796–1798)"],["James Phipps (1788–1853)","the 8-year-old boy Jenner vaccinated in 1796"],["Sarah Nelmes","milkmaid whose cowpox sores supplied Jenner's 1796 vaccine"],["Thomas Jefferson (1743–1826)","US president who promoted vaccination"],["Francisco Javier de Balmis (1753–1819)","Spanish doctor who led the 1803–1806 vaccine expedition"],["Isabel Zendal (around 1773 – after 1811)","nurse who cared for the orphan boys on the Balmis Expedition"],["James Gillray (1756–1815)","British cartoonist who mocked vaccination in 1802"],["Ignaz Semmelweis (1818–1865)","Hungarian doctor who showed handwashing saved mothers' lives"],["Louis Pasteur (1822–1895)","French chemist and microbiologist; anthrax and rabies vaccines"],["Émile Roux (1853–1933) and Charles Chamberland (1851–1908)","Pasteur's key collaborators"],["Jean-Joseph-Henri Toussaint (1847–1890)","French veterinarian who worked on an anthrax vaccine at the same time as Pasteur"],["Joseph Meister (1876–1940)","9-year-old boy treated for rabies in 1885"],["Robert Koch (1843–1910)","German doctor who proved specific bacteria cause anthrax and tuberculosis"],["Dmitri Ivanovsky (1864–1920) and Martinus Beijerinck (1851–1931)","scientists whose work led to the discovery of viruses"],["Emil von Behring (1854–1917)","German scientist; diphtheria antitoxin; first Nobel Prize in Medicine (1901)"],["Kitasato Shibasaburō (1853–1931)","Japanese bacteriologist; co-discovered antitoxins"],["Gaston Ramon (1886–1963)","French vet who developed toxoids"],["Almroth Wright (1861–1947)","British doctor who developed a typhoid vaccine"],["Waldemar Haffkine (1860–1930)","scientist who developed cholera and plague vaccines used in India"],["Albert Calmette (1863–1933) and Camille Guérin (1872–1961)","French scientists who created the BCG tuberculosis vaccine"],["Pearl Kendrick (1890–1980) and Grace Eldering (1900–1988)","American scientists who developed a whooping cough vaccine"],["Ernest Goodpasture (1886–1960)","American scientist who grew viruses in chicken eggs"],["Max Theiler (1899–1972)","South African-born scientist; yellow fever vaccine; Nobel Prize 1951"],["Élie Metchnikoff (1845–1916)","scientist who discovered phagocytes; Nobel Prize 1908"],["Paul Ehrlich (1854–1915)","German scientist who developed early antibody theory; Nobel Prize 1908"],["Frank Macfarlane Burnet (1899–1985)","Australian scientist; clonal selection theory"],["Franklin D. Roosevelt (1882–1945)","US president paralysed in 1921; founded the March of Dimes"],["Philip Drinker (1894–1972) and Louis Agassiz Shaw Jr. (1886–1940)","inventors of the iron lung"],["John Enders (1897–1985), Thomas Weller (1915–2008) and Frederick Robbins (1916–2003)","grew poliovirus in tissue culture; Nobel Prize 1954"],["Henrietta Lacks (1920–1951)","African American woman whose cells (HeLa), taken without her consent, were used in polio research and much more"],["Jonas Salk (1914–1995)","American scientist; inactivated polio vaccine"],["Thomas Francis Jr. (1900–1969)","American scientist who led the 1954 polio vaccine trial"],["Albert Sabin (1906–1993)","American scientist; oral polio vaccine"],["Mikhail Chumakov (1909–1993)","Soviet scientist who led huge trials of Sabin's vaccine"],["Viktor Zhdanov (1914–1987)","Soviet scientist who proposed global smallpox eradication"],["D.A. Henderson (1928–2016)","American doctor who led WHO's smallpox eradication programme"],["William Foege (1936–2026)","American doctor who developed surveillance-and-containment for smallpox"],["Benjamin Rubin (1917–2010)","American microbiologist who invented the bifurcated needle"],["Rahima Banu (born around 1972)","Bangladeshi girl who had the last natural case of variola major (1975)"],["Ali Maow Maalin (1954–2013)","Somali hospital cook who had the last natural smallpox case (1977) and later worked against polio"],["Janet Parker (1938–1978)","medical photographer, the last known person to die of smallpox"],["Thomas Peebles (1921–2010)","American doctor who helped isolate the measles virus"],["Maurice Hilleman (1919–2005)","American scientist who developed more than 40 vaccines including MMR"],["Michiaki Takahashi (1928–2013)","Japanese scientist who developed the chickenpox vaccine"],["Baruch Blumberg (1925–2011)","American scientist who discovered the hepatitis B surface antigen"],["Ian Frazer (born 1953) and Jian Zhou (1957–1999)","scientists whose virus-like particle work led to the HPV vaccine"],["Andrew Wakefield (born 1956)","former British doctor whose retracted 1998 paper falsely linked MMR to autism; struck off in 2010"],["Katalin Karikó (born 1955)","Hungarian-American biochemist; mRNA pioneer; Nobel Prize 2023"],["Drew Weissman (born 1959)","American immunologist; mRNA pioneer; Nobel Prize 2023"],["Pieter Cullis (born 1946)","Canadian biochemist; lipid nanoparticle research"],["Barney Graham (born 1953) and Jason McLellan","scientists who designed stabilised coronavirus spike proteins"],["Uğur Şahin (born 1965) and Özlem Türeci (born 1967)","founders of BioNTech"],["Sarah Gilbert (born 1962)","British vaccinologist who led the Oxford COVID-19 vaccine team"],["Margaret Keenan (born 1930)","first person to receive an authorised COVID-19 vaccine outside a trial (8 December 2020)"]],"glossary":[["smallpox","a deadly disease caused by the variola virus, now eradicated"],["variolation (inoculation)","deliberately infecting someone with material from a smallpox sore to cause a usually milder case and lifelong protection"],["cowpox","a mild disease of cows (and the people who catch it) related to smallpox"],["vaccine","something that safely teaches the immune system to recognise a germ before a real infection; from Latin 'vacca', cow"],["immune system","the body's defence network of cells, tissues and proteins that fights germs"],["germ (pathogen)","a microbe, such as a bacterium or virus, that can cause disease"],["virus","a tiny particle that can only copy itself inside living cells"],["antigen","a molecule (often on a germ's surface) that the immune system recognises, like a name tag"],["antibody","a Y-shaped protein made by B cells that locks onto one specific antigen"],["B cell / T cell","white blood cells of the adaptive immune system; B cells make antibodies, T cells coordinate defence or destroy infected cells"],["memory cell","a long-lived immune cell that remembers a germ so the body can respond faster next time"],["attenuated","weakened so it causes little or no illness but still trains the immune system"],["inactivated","killed, so it cannot cause infection"],["toxoid","a bacterial poison (toxin) made harmless, used as a vaccine"],["antitoxin","antibodies against a toxin, given as a treatment (not a vaccine)"],["adjuvant","an ingredient that helps the immune system respond more strongly to a vaccine"],["herd (community) immunity","when enough people are immune that a disease struggles to spread"],["clinical trial","a careful scientific test of a medicine or vaccine in volunteers, in phases"],["eradication","removing a disease from the whole world permanently"],["mRNA","messenger RNA, a short-lived set of instructions a cell uses to build a protein"]],"parts":[{"n":1,"title":"The Speckled Monster","when":"Ancient times – 1700s","summary":"Long ago, a sickness called smallpox travelled the world with sailors, soldiers and traders. It gave people a high fever and a bumpy rash, and many did not survive. But people noticed something amazing: anyone who got better almost never caught it again.","sections":[{"heading":"Meet the Speckled Monster","paragraphs":["Imagine living in a town in the 1700s. Every few years, news spreads from house to house: the smallpox has come back. Shops close early. Families keep their children indoors. Church bells ring more often than usual. In England, people had a nickname for this disease that tells you how much they feared it. They called it the 'speckled monster'.","Where did the name come from? The word 'pox' is an old English word for a disease that causes pocks, the bumps or pits on the skin. In the 1500s, English speakers began calling this illness the 'small pox' to tell it apart from another disease of the time with larger sores, which people called the 'great pox'. So 'smallpox' did not mean a small illness. It referred to the size of the spots, not the size of the danger.","Smallpox was a disease caused by a virus called variola. A virus is a tiny particle, far too small to see even with an ordinary microscope, that can only make copies of itself inside living cells. Variola only infected humans. It had no animal hiding place and no insects to carry it, so it could survive only by jumping from one person to the next, again and again, for thousands of years.","The jump usually happened when people were close together, face to face. A sick person breathed out, coughed or spoke, and tiny droplets carrying the virus floated into the air. Someone nearby breathed them in. Less often, the virus spread through things a sick person had used, like bedding or clothing. That is why smallpox thrived in crowded towns, busy ports, army camps and ships, anywhere many people lived and worked side by side.","After someone caught the virus, nothing seemed to happen at first. For about one to two weeks the person felt completely normal while the virus quietly multiplied inside their body. Doctors call this hidden stretch the incubation period. Then the illness arrived suddenly: a high fever, a pounding headache, a sore back, aching muscles and a feeling of being terribly ill. This stage lasted two to four days.","Next came the part that gave smallpox its name. Small red spots appeared in the mouth and throat, and soon afterwards on the face. Over a day or so they spread to the arms, legs and body. The spots changed day by day in a regular pattern. First they became raised bumps. Then they filled with clear fluid, like tiny blisters. Then they filled with thicker, cloudy fluid and became firm, round bumps called pustules. After about two weeks the pustules dried into crusts, or scabs, which slowly fell off.","One thing doctors noticed was that the rash was usually thickest on the face, hands and feet, rather than on the chest and back. That pattern helped tell smallpox apart from chickenpox, a much milder illness whose spots cluster on the body instead.","A person with smallpox could pass it on from the moment the rash began until the very last scab dropped off, a period of roughly three weeks. That is why families and towns tried to keep the sick apart from everyone else, and why whole households could fall ill one after another.","Where the scabs had been, many survivors were left with small, pitted scars, especially on their faces. Between about two thirds and four fifths of survivors kept these marks for life. Some people also had damage to their eyes. In eighteenth-century Europe, smallpox is thought to have been the cause of about one third of all blindness."]},{"heading":"Two Faces of One Disease: Major and Minor","paragraphs":["Not all smallpox was equally dangerous. Doctors eventually realised that the variola virus came in two main forms, and the difference between them was enormous.","The first and more common form was called variola major. 'Major' means bigger or more serious, and the name fits. On average, about three in every ten people who caught variola major died. Children and older people were often hit hardest. The danger also depended on how thick the rash was. When the spots stayed separate, most people survived. When they ran together into large patches, the illness was far more likely to be deadly. A small number of cases took rarer, even more severe forms that almost nobody survived.","The second form was called variola minor, sometimes known by its older name, alastrim. It caused the same kind of fever and rash, but it was much gentler. Historically, about one person in a hundred, or fewer, died from it. Doctors only clearly recognised variola minor as a separate form around the end of the 1800s, and in the 1900s it became the main kind of smallpox in some places, including parts of the Americas and Africa.","Here is a fascinating twist: catching either form protected a person against the other. Someone who survived variola minor was safe from variola major, and the other way round. That tells us the two forms were close relatives, recognised by the body as almost the same enemy. Keep that idea in your pocket. Using a milder relative of a dangerous germ to protect against the deadly one will turn out to be one of the biggest ideas in this whole story.","It is worth pausing on what 'three in ten' really means. If smallpox swept through a village of 1,000 people who had never had it, and most of them caught it, several hundred might die within a few months. Almost every family would lose someone. That is why the arrival of smallpox felt like a disaster, not just an illness."]},{"heading":"Clues in a Pharaoh's Face","paragraphs":["How old is smallpox? Scientists who study the family tree of viruses think variola probably appeared somewhere between about 3,000 and 4,000 years ago. Its closest living relatives are two other pox viruses: camelpox, which infects camels, and taterapox, which was found in wild African rodents called gerbils. Many researchers suspect smallpox began when an ancient virus from animals learned to spread between people, but no one knows exactly where or when that happened.","For a long time, the most famous clue came from ancient Egypt. In 1898 the French archaeologist Victor Loret explored a hidden tomb in the Valley of the Kings near Luxor. Ancient priests had moved many royal mummies there for safekeeping, to protect them from tomb robbers. Among them was the mummy of Pharaoh Ramses V, a king who ruled for only about four years and died around 1145 BCE, while he was probably still in his early thirties.","In 1905 the anatomist Grafton Elliot Smith unwrapped and examined the mummy. He noticed something unusual: the pharaoh's face, neck and other parts of his body were covered in small, raised bumps. Later researchers compared them with smallpox pustules. Today you can see Ramses V at the National Museum of Egyptian Civilization in Cairo.","So did Ramses V die of smallpox? The honest answer is that we do not know. The bumps look like they could be smallpox scars or pustules, and many books describe him as one of the earliest possible victims. But no one has proven it, for example by finding the virus's genetic material in his body. Some researchers have even suggested other illnesses, such as plague, could explain the marks on his body. So the careful way to say it is that Ramses V may have had smallpox. Ancient Egyptian records also tell us that his burial was strangely delayed, but they do not tell us what killed him.","The strongest proof of smallpox's age came not from Egypt but from northern Europe, and it came from teeth. Teeth are excellent time capsules. When a person is ill, viruses travelling in the blood can leave traces of their genetic material inside the tooth, where it may survive for centuries.","In July 2020, a large team led by Eske Willerslev, working at the University of Cambridge and the University of Copenhagen, published a study in the journal Science. They had searched the remains of people buried during the Viking Age and earlier, at sites in places such as Denmark, Norway, Sweden, Russia and the United Kingdom. In the teeth of several skeletons they found the DNA of the variola virus. These were the oldest confirmed traces of smallpox ever discovered, about 1,400 years old. The team even rebuilt nearly complete genetic codes for four of the ancient viruses.","The results held a surprise. The Viking-age smallpox was a different strain from the one that was later wiped out in the 1900s. It belonged to a separate branch of the family that has since died out. Scientists still do not know whether that ancient strain was as deadly as the later one. One of the study's senior authors, Terry Jones, said that finding smallpox 'so genetically different in Vikings is truly remarkable.' The discovery reminds us that viruses change over time, and that the smallpox of history was not one unchanging thing."]},{"heading":"Hitching a Ride with Traders and Armies","paragraphs":["A virus that can only live inside people needs a steady supply of new people to infect. In a small, isolated village, smallpox would burn through everyone who could catch it and then vanish, because survivors could not catch it again. But in big, crowded places with lots of births and lots of visitors, there were always new children and newcomers who had never had it. As cities grew and trade routes linked them together, smallpox found a permanent home in human history.","Ancient writers described terrible epidemics, and historians still argue about which ones were smallpox. A great sickness that struck Athens in 430 BCE might have been smallpox, though it could have been something else. The Antonine Plague, which swept through the Roman Empire from about 165 to 180 CE after soldiers returned from wars in the east, is thought by many historians to have been smallpox. Estimates of its death toll run into the millions, though nobody counted carefully at the time.","Written descriptions that clearly match smallpox appear in China by the 300s CE and in India in medical writings from the early centuries CE. In India, people came to link the disease with a goddess named Sitala, who was believed to both bring and cool the fever. In 581 CE, a bishop in France named Gregory of Tours wrote an eyewitness account of an epidemic that sounds very much like smallpox.","Around the year 910, a brilliant Persian doctor named al-Razi, known in Europe as Rhazes, wrote a famous book about smallpox and measles. He described how the two illnesses looked and behaved, and he is often credited as the first doctor to clearly tell them apart. His book was copied and translated for hundreds of years.","Smallpox also reached islands that had been isolated for a long time. In Japan, an epidemic between 735 and 737 CE is believed to have killed a huge share of the population, possibly as much as a third. When a disease arrives in a place where nobody has met it before, everybody is vulnerable at once, adults as well as children.","Big historical movements helped too. The rapid expansion of Arab armies and traders from the 600s onwards linked North Africa, the Middle East and Spain. Later, the Crusades, a series of wars fought between Christian European armies and Muslim states from 1096 onwards, sent tens of thousands of soldiers and pilgrims back and forth between Europe and the eastern Mediterranean. Every one of these great movements of people was also a highway for germs, and historians think smallpox travelled along with them.","Over the following centuries, armies, pilgrims and merchants carried the virus along every road and sea lane. Wars moved crowds of soldiers. Trade caravans crossed deserts. Ships connected ports from the Mediterranean to the Indian Ocean. Each journey was a chance for the virus to travel to a new town. By the 1500s, smallpox was found across most of Asia, Europe and Africa. It had become what doctors call endemic: always present, flaring into epidemics every few years whenever enough unprotected children had been born."]},{"heading":"A New World with No Defences","paragraphs":["For thousands of years, the peoples of North and South America lived separately from Europe, Asia and Africa. Many Old World diseases, including smallpox, measles and influenza, had never crossed the ocean. That meant that when Europeans began arriving after Christopher Columbus's voyage in 1492, the people of the Americas had never been exposed to these germs. Nobody had survived them before, so nobody was protected. Historians sometimes call outbreaks like this 'virgin soil' epidemics, because the disease is landing on ground where it has never been.","Smallpox was first recorded in the Americas in 1518, in the Caribbean. From there it travelled to the mainland. In 1520 it reached Mexico with Spanish expeditions. At that time Tenochtitlan, the island capital of the Aztec (Mexica) Empire, was one of the largest cities in the world, with perhaps 200,000 people. While the Aztecs were fighting the Spanish soldiers led by Hernán Cortés, smallpox swept through the city. Historians estimate that about four in ten of its people died. Among those who died was the Aztec ruler Cuitláhuac, who had led the resistance against the Spanish. He died after only about 80 days in power, probably of smallpox, although the earliest records do not say for certain.","The disease moved faster than the soldiers. Smallpox, or a disease very like it, seems to have spread south and reached the Inca Empire in the Andes mountains before Francisco Pizarro's expedition arrived. The Inca emperor Huayna Capac and his chosen heir died during an epidemic in the 1520s. Their deaths helped trigger a civil war between two of his other sons, leaving the empire divided when the Spanish came.","The transatlantic slave trade also carried smallpox across the ocean. Millions of African people were forced onto crowded ships and taken to the Americas in terrible conditions. Some of them came from regions where smallpox was common, and outbreaks broke out on board and in the ports where the ships arrived. The cruelty of that trade and the spread of disease were bound together, and both caused enormous suffering.","Smallpox was not the only killer. Measles, influenza, typhus and other diseases arrived too, along with war, forced labour and the destruction of farms and communities. Together, these disasters caused one of the largest population collapses in human history. Historians estimate that a very large share of the Indigenous population of the Americas, possibly as much as 80 to 95 percent in some regions, died within about 100 to 150 years after 1492. The exact numbers are debated, because nobody knows for sure how many people lived in the Americas before 1492, but there is no doubt that the loss was enormous.","Further north, smallpox struck again and again. Epidemics swept through Indigenous communities in New England in the 1600s, and another great epidemic spread across much of North America between 1775 and 1782. Each time, whole villages lost elders, parents and children, along with knowledge, languages and traditions. When historians talk about how smallpox changed the world, the Americas are where its effects were most devastating."]},{"heading":"Queens, Kings and a Young George Washington","paragraphs":["Smallpox did not care whether you were a farmer or a king. In eighteenth-century Europe, it is estimated to have killed around 400,000 people every year. That is roughly like losing the whole population of a large city, every single year. It killed several reigning monarchs during that century, along with princes, princesses and heirs to thrones.","Some royal deaths changed history. Tsar Peter II of Russia died of smallpox in January 1730, aged just 14, on the very day that had been set for his wedding. King Louis XV of France died of it in 1774, aged 64, even though he had reigned for almost 60 years. Earlier, in 1694, Queen Mary II of England had died of smallpox in her early thirties. Each royal death could set off a scramble over who would rule next.","Some famous people survived. In October 1562, Queen Elizabeth I of England, then 29 years old, fell dangerously ill with smallpox at Hampton Court Palace near London. For several days her advisers feared she would die, and they began arguing about who should take the throne, since she had no children. She recovered. One of her ladies, Mary Sidney, nursed her through the illness, caught smallpox herself and was left badly scarred. The queen's close call made Parliament even more anxious that she should marry or name an heir.","Two centuries later, a young musician also had a narrow escape. In 1767, an 11-year-old boy named Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart caught smallpox while his family was travelling and staying in the city of Olomouc, in what is now the Czech Republic. He was ill for days, and according to family accounts his eyes were affected for a time, but he recovered and went on to write some of the most famous music ever composed.","Across the Atlantic, another young man caught smallpox on his only trip outside mainland North America. In 1751, 19-year-old George Washington sailed to the island of Barbados in the Caribbean with his older half-brother Lawrence, who was suffering from tuberculosis and hoped the warm climate would help him. In Barbados, George caught smallpox. He spent weeks recovering, and the disease left faint scars on his face for the rest of his life.","That illness turned out to matter. Because he had survived smallpox, Washington could no longer catch it. Years later, when he became commander of the American army during the Revolutionary War, he could walk among sick soldiers without fear for himself, and he understood exactly how dangerous the disease could be to an army. You will see what he did about that later in this story."]},{"heading":"The Clue Everyone Noticed","paragraphs":["Across thousands of years and many cultures, people noticed the same remarkable fact: if you survived smallpox once, you almost never caught it again. The disease that had tried to kill you seemed to leave behind an invisible shield.","This idea was not new even in ancient times. When the Greek historian Thucydides described the great sickness in Athens in 430 BCE, he noted that people who had recovered could care for the sick without danger, because the illness did not attack the same person twice, at least not fatally. Whether that disease was smallpox or something else, the observation was the same one people kept making.","In many places this knowledge shaped everyday life. Families often chose people who had already survived smallpox to nurse the sick, because they could do it safely. In Europe, servants who had already had smallpox were sometimes preferred by employers, and advertisements for jobs occasionally mentioned it. In some communities, a face marked with smallpox scars was almost like a badge that said: this person cannot catch it again.","People also noticed a second pattern. In big cities where smallpox came back every few years, most adults had already had it as children. So in places like London, smallpox was mainly thought of as a children's disease, and parents waited anxiously to see whether each child would survive it. In remote places where the virus arrived only rarely, it struck people of every age at once, which made those outbreaks even more frightening.","Nobody at the time understood why this happened. They did not know about viruses, or white blood cells, or the immune system, the body's defence network of cells, tissues and proteins that fights germs. Today we know that after a serious infection, the immune system keeps a kind of memory of the invader and can recognise it instantly the next time. But for most of history, people only saw the result, not the reason.","Meanwhile, smallpox kept killing. Even in the 20th century, long after people had learned how to fight it, the disease is estimated to have killed about 300 million people around the world. In the early 1950s there were still around 50 million cases each year. Very few diseases in human history have caused so much suffering.","Yet that one simple clue, that surviving smallpox protects you, contained the seed of a solution. Healers in different parts of the world began to think about it in a daring new way. If surviving a case of smallpox gives you lifelong protection, what if you could choose when and how you caught it? What if you could catch a smaller, gentler case, at a time when you were healthy and well cared for, and come out the other side protected forever? Could people give themselves a mild case of smallpox on purpose?"]}],"deepDive":"How do we know what smallpox did in the past when the virus is now gone? Historians and scientists use several kinds of evidence, and each has limits. Written records are the oldest tool, but ancient writers did not use modern disease names, and smallpox, measles and chickenpox could be confused until physicians such as al-Razi described their differences around 910 CE. That is why historians still argue about whether the Plague of Athens (430 BCE) or the Antonine Plague (about 165 to 180 CE) were smallpox. Physical evidence such as the bumps on the mummy of Ramses V is suggestive but not proof, because other diseases can leave similar marks, and the mummy has never yielded variola DNA. The newest evidence comes from ancient DNA. In 2020, researchers led by Eske Willerslev and including Barbara Mühlemann and Terry Jones reported variola DNA in Viking-age and earlier remains from northern Europe, about 1,400 years old, and reconstructed four near-complete genomes. Those ancient viruses belonged to an extinct branch of the variola family, showing that smallpox evolved over time; some researchers think the later, highly deadly form may have spread more recently than once assumed. Molecular-clock studies, which estimate when viruses split apart by counting genetic differences, had earlier suggested a younger age for the modern virus, and the debate over smallpox's origins is far from settled. Numbers are another challenge. Estimates such as about 400,000 European deaths a year in the 1700s, or about 300 million deaths in the 20th century, come from incomplete records and careful modelling, so you will see ranges (for example 250 to 500 million) in different sources. Similarly, estimates of how many Indigenous Americans died after 1492 depend on how many people lived there beforehand, a figure historians still dispute. What no serious historian doubts is the overall pattern: variola major killed about 30 percent of those it infected, it shaped empires and families for centuries, and survivors gained lasting immunity. That last fact, recorded independently in many cultures, became the starting point for the first deliberate attempts to prevent the disease.","funFact":"Scientists found the oldest confirmed smallpox DNA not in a pharaoh's tomb but inside the teeth of Viking-age skeletons, about 1,400 years old.","check":{"q":"What did people all over the world notice about people who survived smallpox?","a":"They almost never caught smallpox again","wrong":["They always caught it again the next year","They could no longer catch any illness at all","They passed smallpox on to everyone for the rest of their lives"]},"sources":[{"title":"History of Smallpox","publisher":"US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/smallpox/about/history.html"},{"title":"Edward Jenner and the history of smallpox and vaccination (Riedel, 2005)","publisher":"Baylor University Medical Center Proceedings, via NIH PubMed Central","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1200696/"},{"title":"Vikings had smallpox and may have helped spread the world's deadliest virus","publisher":"University of Cambridge","url":"https://www.cam.ac.uk/research/news/vikings-had-smallpox-and-may-have-helped-spread-the-worlds-deadliest-virus"},{"title":"Smallpox","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smallpox"},{"title":"History of smallpox","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_smallpox"},{"title":"Ramesses V","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramesses_V"},{"title":"Columbian exchange","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbian_exchange"},{"title":"Early life of George Washington","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_life_of_George_Washington"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/01-1.jpg","alt":"The painted passage of tomb KV9 in Egypt's Valley of the Kings, begun for Pharaoh Ramesses V.","file":"Tumba de Ramsés V y de Ramsés VI, Valle de las Reyes, Luxor, Egipto, 2022-04-03, DD 69.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tumba_de_Rams%C3%A9s_V_y_de_Rams%C3%A9s_VI,_Valle_de_las_Reyes,_Luxor,_Egipto,_2022-04-03,_DD_69.jpg","author":"Diego Delso","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/01-2.jpg","alt":"Opening page of Book 12 of the Florentine Codex (1570s), about the Spanish conquest of Mexico, showing ships arriving.","file":"General History of the Things of New Spain by Fray Bernardino de Sahagún- The Florentine Codex. Book XII- The Conquest of Mexico WDL10623.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_History_of_the_Things_of_New_Spain_by_Fray_Bernardino_de_Sahag%C3%BAn-_The_Florentine_Codex._Book_XII-_The_Conquest_of_Mexico_WDL10623.jpg","author":"Sahagún, Bernardino de, 1499-1590","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/01-3.jpg","alt":"Smallpox virus particles seen through an electron microscope, magnified about 370,000 times.","file":"Smallpox virus virions TEM PHIL 1849.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smallpox_virus_virions_TEM_PHIL_1849.JPG","author":"Content Providers(s): CDC/ Dr. Fred Murphy; Sylvia Whitfield","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3775},{"n":2,"title":"Fighting Fire with Fire: Variolation","when":"1500s – early 1700s","summary":"Long before modern medicine, healers in China, India, Africa and Turkey tried a brave idea. They gave healthy people a tiny bit of smallpox on purpose, so they would catch a milder case and be safe forever after. It usually worked, but it was still risky.","sections":[{"heading":"A Dangerous Idea That Usually Worked","paragraphs":["At the end of the last part, we met a puzzle. If surviving smallpox protects you for life, could you catch it on purpose, in a gentler way, and skip the worst danger? It sounds like a frightening idea, a bit like fighting a fire by lighting a small, controlled fire first. Yet for hundreds of years, in many different parts of the world, people did exactly that.","The title of this part borrows an idea from firefighting. When a huge wildfire is coming, firefighters sometimes burn a strip of land on purpose, in a controlled way, so that when the big fire arrives there is nothing left there for it to burn. It sounds backwards, but a small fire that you choose and control can protect you from a giant one you cannot control. Variolation worked on a similar principle, with a small, chosen illness standing in for a big, deadly one.","The method is called variolation. The word comes from 'variola', the Latin-based name for smallpox, so variolation simply means 'smallpoxing' someone. In English it was also often called inoculation. That word has a lovely history of its own. It comes from the Latin 'inoculare', which gardeners used for grafting, when you take a bud from one plant and set it into a cut on another so it grows there. Early writers saw a similarity: a healer took a tiny bit of smallpox material and set it into a small cut in a person's skin, where it 'took' and grew.","Here is how variolation worked. A healer took material from the sores or scabs of someone with smallpox. They then put that material into a healthy person who had never had the disease, either by scratching it into the skin or by blowing powdered scabs into the nose. After a week or two, the person usually became ill with smallpox, but in most cases it was a much milder illness than catching smallpox the ordinary way. They might have a fever and a few dozen spots instead of hundreds. When they recovered, they were protected against smallpox for the rest of their lives, just as if they had survived a natural case.","What did it feel like? Accounts from the 1700s describe a fairly regular timetable. For the first few days after the scratch, almost nothing happened, apart from a little redness and itching where the material had been placed. Around a week later, the person usually developed a fever, a headache and aches, much like the start of natural smallpox. A few days after that, spots appeared, often only a few dozen, with the biggest cluster near the scratch itself. Within two to four weeks of the procedure, most people had recovered and the last scabs were dropping off.","The numbers explain why people were willing to take the risk. Natural variola major killed roughly 30 in every 100 people it infected. Variolation killed roughly 1 or 2 in every 100. It was not safe, but it was far less dangerous than waiting for the next epidemic to arrive.","Why was variolation usually so much milder? Even today, scientists are not completely sure. One idea is that the route mattered. Natural smallpox usually entered through the airways and spread inside the body in a particular way, while variolation put the virus into the skin, where the body's defences may have had a head start. Another idea is that healers often chose material from people with mild cases, which may have helped. The dose may have mattered too: a tiny amount in a scratch is very different from breathing in a sick person's air for days. Probably several of these reasons worked together.","Before going further, it is important to remember that variolation was used only for smallpox and only in the past. It was replaced by much safer methods, which you will meet later in this story. Nothing in this part is a guide to what anyone should do today. It is a story about how people long ago tried to solve a terrifying problem with the knowledge they had."]},{"heading":"Powder and a Silver Tube: China","paragraphs":["Some of the best-documented early variolation comes from China. There, the most famous method did not involve cutting the skin at all. Instead, healers dried smallpox scabs, ground them into a fine powder and blew it into the nose of the person being protected, through a small blowpipe. This method is called nasal insufflation, which simply means blowing something into the nose.","When did this begin? Chinese stories tell of a much older origin. One tale says that in the late 900s or early 1000s, during the Song dynasty, a powerful official named Wang Dan lost a child to smallpox and searched for a way to protect his other children, until a wise hermit or holy person from a sacred mountain showed his family the secret. It is a good story, but it comes from a book written in the early 1800s (usually dated 1808), roughly 800 years after the events it describes. Historians, including the famous British scholar of Chinese science Joseph Needham, treat it as a legend rather than proven history. It might contain a seed of truth, or it might not; there is no firm evidence either way.","The first reliable written mention appears in the mid-1500s. A doctor named Wan Quan mentioned the practice in a book about smallpox and measles published in 1549, though he did not explain the details. Needham, after studying many old texts, concluded that variolation was spreading widely in China during the reign of the Longqing Emperor, between 1567 and 1572. Through the 1600s, more Chinese doctors wrote about it in their books.","Chinese variolators developed careful rules. They preferred scabs from children who had had mild cases with few spots. They wrapped the scabs in paper, sealed them in a bottle, labelled them with the date and kept them away from sunlight and heat. They noticed that scabs stayed effective longer in cold weather than in hot weather, so they used them within a certain time depending on the season. Some practitioners also aged the scabs for a while before use, which may have weakened the virus a little. The person who had been variolated was kept apart from others until the rash had cleared, because they could spread smallpox to others.","The details of the nose method were precise. Old Chinese descriptions say that three or four dried scabs were ground into powder, sometimes mixed with a grain of musk, a strong-smelling substance used in traditional medicine, and wrapped in a little cotton. The material was packed into the end of a silver blowpipe and puffed up one nostril. There was even a rule about which side: the right nostril was used for boys and the left nostril for girls. Healers believed that fresh scabs were more likely to cause a full-blown, dangerous infection, which is why they preferred scabs that had been left to dry for some time.","Variolation even reached the imperial palace. The Kangxi Emperor, who ruled China from 1661 to 1722, had survived smallpox himself as a child. According to many historians, his survival was one reason he was chosen to become emperor, because he was now protected from the disease that killed so many. Kangxi approved of variolation and had his children inoculated. Accounts say he had soldiers inoculated as well.","News of the Chinese method travelled. In 1700, two separate reports describing it reached the Royal Society in London, Britain's leading scientific club. The members found them interesting, but nobody tried the method in England. The idea had arrived too early, and nobody important was ready to champion it yet."]},{"heading":"The Tika Makers of India","paragraphs":["In India, variolation used a different technique. Instead of blowing powder into the nose, practitioners made small scratches or needle pricks in the skin, usually on the arm, and rubbed in material from smallpox sores or scabs. In some places the material was mixed with a little water before use.","Our most detailed accounts of Indian variolation come from European visitors in the 1700s. A writer named Oliver Coult described it in 1731. In the late 1760s, a British surgeon named John Zephaniah Holwell, who had lived in Bengal, wrote a longer account. He described travelling inoculators, often Brahmins, members of a priestly group, who moved from village to village before the smallpox season. They followed strict rules about diet and cold baths for the people they inoculated, and they treated the procedure as both medicine and religious ceremony, often with prayers to Sitala, the goddess linked with smallpox.","Another letter from 1768 described two methods used in Bengal: rubbing smallpox material into small pricks in the skin, and, more unusually, giving people scab material to swallow in a little sugar. Practitioners of the skin method were called tikadars. The word 'tika' is still used in many Indian languages today to mean vaccination, a living echo of this old practice.","How old was Indian variolation? Holwell said it had been used for hundreds of years, and some writers have claimed it goes back thousands of years to ancient Sanskrit texts. Modern historians are more cautious. No ancient text clearly describes inoculation, and some scholars have argued that there is no strong reason to assume the practice began in India. The honest answer is that it was well established by the 1700s and was probably older, but its true starting date is unknown."]},{"heading":"Buying the Smallpox: Africa","paragraphs":["Inoculation against smallpox was also widely practised across Africa, in many different forms. Because most African communities passed knowledge on by speaking and teaching rather than in written books, the oldest records we have mostly come from travellers, doctors and, importantly, from Africans themselves who described the practice to outsiders.","In West Africa, people of many regions knew the technique. It was known, for example, among the Ga-Adangbe people around Accra, in what is now Ghana. The basic method was similar to the Indian one: make a small cut or scratch in the skin and introduce material from a smallpox sore.","In the Sudan region of north-east Africa, travellers recorded a custom with a memorable name. It was called 'tishteree el jidderi', which means 'buying the smallpox'. A mother would take her child to the home of another child who had a mild case. She would tie a strip of cloth around the sick child's arm and then bargain with the family over the price of each pustule. The cloth, carrying the material, was then tied around the arm of the healthy child. A second custom was called 'dak el jedri', or 'hitting the smallpox', in which fluid from a pustule was rubbed into a small cut in the skin. Versions of these practices were still in use in Sudan in the late 1800s.","Notice what these customs have in common with the methods used in China and India. The donor was chosen carefully, usually a child with a mild case. Only a small amount of material was used. And in many African and Ottoman communities the practice was organised by women, especially mothers and older women healers, who passed the knowledge down through families. Across thousands of kilometres, often with little or no direct contact, people had arrived at strikingly similar rules.","The practice also crossed the Atlantic in a way that would change history. Millions of Africans were enslaved and forcibly taken to the Americas, and some of them carried the knowledge of inoculation with them. In the colonies, several enslaved Africans described to curious Europeans how they had been protected as children back home, and showed the marks on their arms. One of them, a man known as Onesimus who lived in Boston, will play a starring role in the next part of this story.","This is an important reminder about how ideas travel. The knowledge that helped bring inoculation to Europe and America did not come only from famous scientists in big cities. Much of it came from ordinary healers, mothers and grandparents in Asia, Africa and the Middle East, whose names were never written down."]},{"heading":"The Old Women of Constantinople","paragraphs":["In the early 1700s, the Ottoman Empire was one of the great powers of the world. Its capital, Constantinople (today's Istanbul), sat where Europe meets Asia and was full of merchants, diplomats and travellers from many lands. Inoculation was well known there, and it was usually carried out not by doctors but by local women healers, often older women who were experts at the technique.","Where did the Ottomans learn it? The French writer Voltaire, writing in the 1730s, claimed that the Turks had learned inoculation from the Circassians, a people living in the Caucasus mountains between the Black Sea and the Caspian Sea. Some later historians say that Circassian traders brought the practice to the Ottoman Empire around 1670. Like many such origin stories, it is hard to prove, but it shows that people at the time saw inoculation as an idea that had travelled along trade routes.","The Ottoman method was simple. In the cooler months, when smallpox was thought to be less severe, families arranged for an inoculator to visit. She brought material from a mild smallpox case, sometimes carried in a nutshell. She made a few small scratches or pricks with a needle, often on the arms or legs, and placed a tiny drop of the material in each. A few days later the person usually developed a fever and a modest number of spots, rested for a while, and recovered with lifelong protection.","Two doctors helped carry the news from Constantinople to the scientists of Western Europe. The first was Emanuel Timoni, a Greek physician born in 1669 on the island of Chios, whose father had worked as an interpreter at the Sultan's court. Timoni had studied medicine at the universities of Oxford in England and Padua in Italy, then became a physician in Constantinople, including at the Sultan's court. He wrote a letter describing what he had seen of inoculation, and in 1714 it was read to the Royal Society in London and published in the Society's journal, the Philosophical Transactions, one of the oldest science journals in the world.","The second was Giacomo Pylarini, another Greek physician, born in 1659 on the island of Cephalonia. He had studied law and then medicine at Padua, travelled widely, practised medicine in Smyrna (today's İzmir) and Constantinople, and served as the consul, or official representative, of the Republic of Venice in Smyrna. Pylarini wrote his own account of inoculation, published in Latin, and a version of it appeared in the Philosophical Transactions in 1716. Its Latin title promised 'a new and safe method' of giving smallpox by transplanting it from one person to another.","The members of the Royal Society read these reports with interest. They discussed them and printed them. But reading about something and actually doing it are very different things. No English doctor rushed to try it. Inoculation seemed foreign, strange and frightening, and nobody wanted to be the first to deliberately give smallpox to a healthy English child."]},{"heading":"The Price of Protection","paragraphs":["Variolation saved many lives, but it came with real dangers, and it is important to be honest about them.","The first danger was to the person being inoculated. Variolation gave them an actual smallpox infection, not a pretend one. Usually the case was mild, but sometimes it was not. Roughly 1 or 2 people out of every 100 who were variolated died from the smallpox it caused. Some accounts from different times and places give slightly higher figures, around 2 or 3 in 100. Either way, every family that chose inoculation knew that their healthy child might become seriously ill, or even die, because of a decision they had made.","Let us picture the choice with numbers. Imagine 1,000 children in a city where smallpox returns every few years. If all of them eventually caught natural variola major, around 300 might die. If all of them were variolated, perhaps 10 to 20 might die. Those 10 to 20 are real children, and that is a heartbreaking cost. But it is far smaller than 300. Parents had to weigh a smaller, chosen risk now against a larger, uncertain risk later.","The second danger was to everyone else. A variolated person had real smallpox, so they could pass it on. And the people who caught it from them did not get the gentle, scratched-in version. They caught ordinary smallpox, through the air, with all its usual dangers. One careless inoculation could start a full epidemic among neighbours who had never agreed to take any risk at all.","That is why careful variolators insisted that inoculated people be kept apart from others while they were sick, often for several weeks, until the last scab had fallen off. In China, the inoculated were isolated until the rash cleared. In later centuries, special inoculation houses were built where people stayed until they were no longer contagious. When these rules were broken, outbreaks followed.","Experienced inoculators also learned that timing mattered. Wherever possible, they preferred to inoculate healthy people during calm periods, rather than in the middle of a raging epidemic, when the person might already have caught natural smallpox before the inoculation could protect them. In the Ottoman Empire and in India, inoculation was often arranged for particular seasons, when the disease was thought to be gentler. Healers also tended to avoid people who were already weak or ill. These were rules of thumb, built up from long experience rather than from experiments, but they show how seriously the best practitioners took the risks.","There were other problems too. Variolation could only be done with material from a person who currently had smallpox, so healers needed a constant supply. If the donor had another disease in their blood, that could sometimes be passed on as well. And the process took time, cost money in many places and meant days or weeks of illness. In the end, as you will discover, the risk of variolation spreading smallpox to others was one of the main reasons it was eventually replaced by something much safer and then banned in some countries."]},{"heading":"News Travels to a Determined Noblewoman","paragraphs":["Let us step back and look at the map. By around 1700, deliberate smallpox inoculation was being practised in China, India, many parts of Africa, the Middle East and the Ottoman Empire. Different cultures used different techniques: powder in the nose, scratches on the arm, cloth tied around a child's limb. But they all rested on the same discovery: a carefully chosen, milder case of smallpox could protect a person for life.","Even in Europe, the idea may not have been completely unknown. A medieval medical text linked to the famous medical school at Salerno in southern Italy mentions putting material from pustules into infants. In Wales, records from near the town of Haverfordwest suggest that ordinary country people were using a form of folk variolation as early as about 1600, long before doctors took any interest. Later in the 1700s, in the remote Shetland Islands north of Scotland, a self-taught man nicknamed Johnnie Notions became famous locally for his careful home-made inoculations. These scattered folk practices show that people in many places, not just in Asia and Africa, had noticed the same clue and acted on it.","None of these healers, in any country, knew what smallpox actually was. Nobody had seen a virus. There was no theory of germs, no idea of an immune system, no laboratory tests. Variolation was built entirely on careful observation and experience, passed from one generation to the next: this works, this is dangerous, this kind of donor is better, keep the sick apart. It is a powerful example of how people can discover something that works long before anyone can explain why it works.","Western Europe was one of the last places to hear about it, and even after the reports from Timoni and Pylarini, it did nothing. What the idea needed was not more evidence on paper. It needed a champion: someone brave and stubborn enough to try it on their own family and then convince others to follow.","In 1717, a new British ambassador arrived in Constantinople with his family. His wife was an intelligent, witty writer from a noble English family. She had survived smallpox only two years earlier. It had left her face scarred and taken away her eyelashes, and the disease had already killed her younger brother. Now, walking the streets of Constantinople and visiting Ottoman households, she was about to see something that would change her life, and the history of medicine in Britain. Her name was Lady Mary Wortley Montagu."]}],"deepDive":"Historians of medicine debate two big questions about variolation: where it began, and why it worked as well as it did. On origins, the evidence is patchy and often filtered through outsiders. In China, the first credible written mention is in Wan Quan's 1549 book on smallpox and measles, and Joseph Needham argued that the practice was widespread by the Longqing reign (1567 to 1572). Stories placing it in the 900s or 1000s rest on texts written centuries later, especially an 1808 account of the official Wang Dan, so they are treated as legends. For India, the best descriptions come from Europeans such as Oliver Coult (1731) and John Zephaniah Holwell (late 1760s); claims of an ancient Sanskrit origin are not supported by any clear ancient text, and a 2012 study argued there is no reason to assume India was the source. African practices are even harder to date because they were passed on orally, yet testimony from enslaved West Africans in the Americas in the early 1700s shows the practice was established across a wide region. Many scholars now think variolation may have been invented independently in several places, or spread along trade routes in ways we cannot trace. On mechanism, the key puzzle is why a scratch inoculation usually caused milder disease than natural infection with the same virus. Proposed explanations include the route of entry (skin rather than airways), a small dose, the choice of donors with mild disease, and, in China, ageing of scab material that might have reduced the amount of live virus. The death rate was usually quoted at about 1 to 2 percent, with some sources giving 2 to 3 percent, against roughly 30 percent for natural variola major. But that personal benefit came with a public cost: inoculated people could transmit ordinary smallpox. This tension between individual protection and community risk is a theme that would shape public health debates for centuries, and it is one reason the safer, non-contagious method developed after 1796 replaced variolation so quickly. Britain eventually made variolation illegal in 1840.","funFact":"The word 'inoculate' originally came from gardening: it described grafting a bud from one plant into another, and the Latin root 'oculus' means 'eye', the old name for a plant bud.","check":{"q":"Roughly how many variolated people died, compared with about 30 in 100 for natural variola major?","a":"About 1 to 2 in 100","wrong":["About 50 in 100","None at all","About 30 in 100, the same as natural smallpox"]},"sources":[{"title":"Variolation","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Variolation"},{"title":"Inoculation","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inoculation"},{"title":"History of Smallpox","publisher":"US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/smallpox/about/history.html"},{"title":"Edward Jenner and the history of smallpox and vaccination (Riedel, 2005)","publisher":"Baylor University Medical Center Proceedings, via NIH PubMed Central","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1200696/"},{"title":"Emanuel Timoni","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emanuel_Timoni"},{"title":"Giacomo Pylarini","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giacomo_Pylarini"},{"title":"Johnnie Notions","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnnie_Notions"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/02-1.jpg","alt":"The Kangxi Emperor of China in court dress. He survived smallpox and had his children inoculated.","file":"Portrait of the Kangxi Emperor in Court Dress.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_the_Kangxi_Emperor_in_Court_Dress.jpg","author":"AnonymousUnknown author Qing Dynasty Court Painter","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/02-2.jpg","alt":"Lady Mary Wortley Montagu in Turkish dress with her young son Edward in Constantinople, painted by Jean Baptiste Vanmour.","file":"Lady Mary Wortley Montagu with her son, Edward Wortley Montagu, and attendants by Jean Baptiste Vanmour.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lady_Mary_Wortley_Montagu_with_her_son,_Edward_Wortley_Montagu,_and_attendants_by_Jean_Baptiste_Vanmour.jpg","author":"Jean Baptiste Vanmour","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/02-3.jpg","alt":"A hand-coloured French print of ships in the port of Constantinople (Istanbul), seen from the north.","file":"Vue du Port de Constantinople, prise du côté du Nord.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_du_Port_de_Constantinople,_prise_du_c%C3%B4t%C3%A9_du_Nord.jpg","author":"Unknown authorUnknown author Verleger : A Paris chez J. Chereau rue St.…","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3779},{"n":3,"title":"Lady Mary and Onesimus","when":"1706 – 1722","summary":"A brave English writer saw women in Turkey protecting children from smallpox, and she brought the idea home to London. Across the ocean in Boston, an enslaved African man named Onesimus explained the very same trick. Together, their knowledge sparked a huge argument, and a few bold experiments.","sections":[{"heading":"A Face Changed Forever","paragraphs":["In December 1715, in a grand house in London, a 26-year-old woman lay in bed with a burning fever. Her name was Lady Mary Wortley Montagu, and she was one of the cleverest, sharpest and most talked-about people in English high society. She wrote poems, sparkling letters and funny, sometimes cutting, verses about the royal court. She had taught herself Latin as a girl by sneaking into her father's library, at a time when most people thought girls did not need to learn such things. Now she had smallpox.","Lady Mary knew exactly how dangerous that was. Two years earlier, in 1713, her younger brother had died of the same disease. As her illness went on, the fever was followed by the rash of bumps that gave smallpox its name, spreading across her face and body. For days nobody could be sure whether she would live.","She survived. But the disease left its mark. Her smooth skin was left pitted with small scars, and she lost her eyelashes, which never grew back. In the 1700s, a beautiful complexion was considered one of a noblewoman's most valuable features, and many people in her circle would have seen her scars as a terrible loss. Lady Mary was too tough and too clever to hide away, but she never forgot what smallpox had done to her family and to her face.","While she was still recovering, there was more trouble. Some of her private satirical poems, called the 'court eclogues', were passed around and gossiped about. One of them was read by many as poking fun at Caroline, Princess of Wales, the wife of the heir to the throne. This is worth remembering, because just a few years later Lady Mary and Princess Caroline would end up on the same side of a very important argument.","Lady Mary was married to Edward Wortley Montagu, a politician. The couple had run away together to marry in 1712 because her father disapproved of the match. They had a small son, also called Edward, born in 1713. Within a year of Lady Mary's illness, the whole family would be packing their trunks for one of the most extraordinary journeys any English family made in that age."]},{"heading":"Engrafting in Constantinople","paragraphs":["In 1716, Edward Wortley Montagu was appointed British ambassador to the Ottoman Empire, the vast realm ruled by the Sultan from Constantinople (today's Istanbul, in Turkey). His job was to help negotiate peace in a war between the Ottomans and Austria. In August 1716 the family set off overland, travelling through Vienna and across central Europe and the Balkans, through snowy mountains and along muddy roads, to the Ottoman city of Adrianople (now Edirne) and then on to Constantinople. The journey took months.","Lady Mary was fascinated by everything she saw. She visited Turkish bathhouses where women met and talked, learned some Turkish, wore Ottoman clothes, and wrote long, lively letters home describing daily life. Those letters were later published as the 'Turkish Embassy Letters' and are still read today. One of them, dated 1 April 1717 and written to a friend in England, became famous for a completely different reason. It described smallpox.","In her letter, Lady Mary explained that smallpox, so deadly in England, was made almost harmless in Turkey by a practice she called 'engrafting', the same thing we call variolation or inoculation. Every autumn, she wrote, in September when the great heat was over, families would arrange gatherings so that children could be engrafted together. An old woman would arrive carrying a nutshell full of material from the sores of someone with a mild case of smallpox. She would open a small scratch in the skin with a needle, put in as much of the matter as would fit on the needle's tip, and bind up the little wound with a piece of shell.","According to Lady Mary's description, the children then played as normal for about eight days. After that a fever came, they stayed in bed for a couple of days, and they usually had only a few spots on the face, which did not leave scars. In about eight more days they were well again. She wrote that thousands of people underwent the operation every year and that she had never heard of anyone dying of it. That was surely too rosy a picture, because we know variolation could kill about 1 or 2 in every 100 people. But compared with natural smallpox, which killed about 30 in every 100 people who caught the deadly form, it was a very good bargain.","Lady Mary was not the first person to tell the English about this practice. A few years earlier, two doctors working in the region, Emanuel Timoni and Giacomo Pylarini, had sent letters describing Ottoman inoculation to the Royal Society in London. Timoni's account was printed in the Society's journal, the Philosophical Transactions, in 1714, and Pylarini's followed in 1716. Members found the reports interesting, but nobody in England actually tried the method. It sounded like a strange foreign folk custom. What made Lady Mary different was that she decided to test it on someone she loved.","In March 1718, she had her son Edward, then nearly five years old, inoculated at the British Embassy. The embassy surgeon, a Scotsman named Charles Maitland, supervised. By the accounts of the time, an elderly local woman who was an experienced inoculator began the operation on one arm, and Maitland himself did the other arm. Little Edward developed a mild illness, recovered, and was protected for life. He is usually described as the first English person to undergo the operation.","Imagine how Lady Mary felt during those days of waiting. She was taking a calculated risk with her own child, based on what she had seen with her own eyes and on the experience of thousands of Ottoman families. Her husband was away at the time, and she wrote to him about the boy's progress. It went well. She was now convinced that inoculation could save English children, and she promised herself she would bring it home. The family returned to London on 2 October 1718."]},{"heading":"Back in London: A Daughter and an Epidemic","paragraphs":["For a little while, nothing much happened. Lady Mary talked about inoculation, but English doctors were not interested in learning a medical technique from Turkish village women, and certainly not from an aristocratic lady who was not a physician. Many doctors also earned good money treating smallpox patients, and some suspected that the whole idea was dangerous nonsense.","Then, in 1721, a serious smallpox epidemic hit London. People were dying across the city, and families were frightened. Lady Mary had a little daughter, also named Mary, who was about three years old and had been born in Constantinople. She had not been inoculated there because she was then too young. Now, with smallpox all around, Lady Mary decided it was time.","Charles Maitland had returned to England too. Lady Mary asked him to inoculate her daughter. He was nervous about doing it in London, where a bad result could ruin his reputation, so he insisted that other doctors be present as witnesses. In April 1721, Maitland carried out the operation. It was the first inoculation known to have been performed in England by a trained surgeon in front of witnesses.","The little girl came through her mild case of smallpox safely. Lady Mary invited important visitors to see her, including Sir Hans Sloane, physician to the King and one of the most famous doctors and collectors in Britain (his collection later helped found the British Museum). Several doctors came to look at the child, and word spread quickly through the city's drawing rooms, coffee houses and newspapers.","From then on, Lady Mary became inoculation's most energetic champion in England. She told everyone she met about it, encouraged her friends to inoculate their children, and later wrote about it in print. In September 1722 she published an article arguing for inoculation in a London newspaper. She did not sign it with her own name. Instead she pretended to be 'a Turkey Merchant', perhaps because people might take a merchant's opinion more seriously than a woman's. In her article she also argued that some English doctors were making the operation more dangerous by cutting too deeply and adding needless treatments.","Not everyone thanked her. Some doctors and clergymen attacked inoculation as unnatural, risky or even sinful. In their view, giving a healthy person a disease on purpose was interfering with the will of God. Lady Mary later wrote that she was criticised and even blamed whenever an inoculated child became ill. But she had something powerful on her side: two healthy children who had both had smallpox on purpose and both lived."]},{"heading":"The Royal Experiment at Newgate","paragraphs":["Two healthy children were encouraging, but doctors and the royal family wanted more proof. Was inoculation truly safe? And did it really protect people for life, or did it only seem to? To find out, the authorities arranged a test that we would find shocking today.","Newgate was London's most notorious prison, a dark, crowded building near the old city wall where people convicted of serious crimes waited to be hanged. In the summer of 1721, prisoners there who had been sentenced to death were offered a deal. If they agreed to be inoculated with smallpox, and if they survived, they would be pardoned and set free. The King, George I, gave his permission, and the plan was supported by Caroline, Princess of Wales, who was very interested in the results.","On 9 August 1721, Charles Maitland inoculated six prisoners, three men and three women, in front of a crowd of doctors and scientists who came to watch. (Some accounts say seven prisoners were offered the chance; the usual figure for those inoculated is six.) Maitland made small cuts in their arms and legs and placed smallpox material into them. Then everyone waited, and the witnesses visited to record what happened.","Five of the six developed mild smallpox and recovered. One man did not seem to react much at all, and it was later suggested that he had already had smallpox earlier in life, which would have made him immune. All six survived, and all six were released, as promised. Newspapers followed the story eagerly.","The doctors wanted one more piece of evidence. Were the prisoners truly protected? One of the women, Elizabeth Harrison, was sent to the town of Hertford, where smallpox was spreading. There she was asked to nurse a sick child and even to sleep in the same bed as a boy with smallpox. She did not catch the disease. To the doctors, this showed that the protection was real.","It is important to say clearly that an experiment like this would never be allowed today. The prisoners were facing execution, so they could hardly make a free choice; saying no meant the gallows. Nobody explained the risks in the careful, honest way that modern rules require, and Elizabeth Harrison was deliberately exposed to a deadly disease. Today, medical research must follow strict ethical rules: volunteers must give 'informed consent', which means they understand the risks and agree freely, and vulnerable people such as prisoners are given special protection. Those rules were written partly because of experiments like this one, and because of much worse abuses in later centuries.","Still, in the eyes of people in 1721, the 'Royal Experiment' was a success. It was one of the earliest attempts in Britain to test a medical procedure on a group of people and watch the results, rather than just relying on old books or opinions."]},{"heading":"A Princess Makes It Fashionable","paragraphs":["Caroline of Ansbach, Princess of Wales, was a thoughtful, well-read woman who loved science and philosophy. She also had a very personal reason to care about smallpox: she had survived it herself in 1707, and she had young daughters. If inoculation worked, it could protect her children, and they were important members of the royal family who might one day marry into other royal houses across Europe.","But she wanted to be sure. After the Newgate trial, a second test was arranged. In early 1722 several orphan children from the parish of St James's in London were inoculated. Like the prisoners, they all survived. Once again, the children had no real say in the matter, which modern ethics would not accept.","Finally, in April 1722, the princess's two daughters, Princess Amelia, aged 10, and Princess Caroline, aged 8, were inoculated. The operation was performed by Claudius Amyand, a French-born royal surgeon, with Charles Maitland's help. Both girls had mild cases and recovered completely.","In the 1700s, whatever the royal family did quickly became fashionable among the rich. Once the princesses had been inoculated, many aristocratic families wanted the same protection for their own children. Inoculation went from being a strange foreign custom to something talked about in the finest homes in London. Lady Mary, whose poem had once seemed to mock the princess, now found Caroline a powerful ally.","The arguments did not stop, though. In July 1722 a clergyman named Edmund Massey preached a famous sermon in London attacking 'the dangerous and sinful practice of inoculation'. He argued that diseases were sent by God, either to test people or to punish them, and that humans had no right to give them to themselves. Other critics pointed to real problems. A few inoculated people died. And, worst of all, inoculated people had a genuine case of smallpox, so they could pass the full, deadly disease on to anyone around them who was not immune.","To settle the argument with facts, a doctor and mathematician named James Jurin, secretary of the Royal Society, began collecting numbers in the 1720s. He gathered reports of as many inoculations and natural smallpox cases as he could find. His figures suggested that roughly 1 in 50 inoculated people died, while somewhere around 1 in 6 or 7 people who caught smallpox naturally died. Counting and comparing like this was a new way of answering a medical question, and it would become one of the most important tools in all of medicine."]},{"heading":"Onesimus: Knowledge From Africa","paragraphs":["Now we must cross the Atlantic Ocean and go back a few years, to the busy port town of Boston in the British colony of Massachusetts. Boston in the early 1700s had about 11,000 people, wooden houses, wharves crowded with ships, and many churches. It was also a place where slavery was legal. Around a thousand or so of its people were enslaved Africans or their descendants.","In December 1706, the members of Boston's North Church gave an enslaved young man as a 'gift' to their minister, Cotton Mather. Mather was one of the most famous preachers in New England, a man who wrote hundreds of books and pamphlets and was deeply interested in science. He renamed the young man Onesimus, after an enslaved man mentioned in the Bible. We do not know the name Onesimus was given by his own family, and we know only a little about his life, mostly from what Mather wrote. He came from West Africa, probably from a region where people spoke languages of the Akan family, though historians are not completely certain.","One day Mather asked Onesimus whether he had ever had smallpox. Onesimus's answer was surprising: both yes and no. He explained that, as a child in Africa, he had undergone an operation that gave him something of the smallpox and protected him from it forever. He showed Mather the scar on his arm. As Mather later reported it, Onesimus described how people took material from smallpox sores, cut the skin, and put in a drop. This was the same basic idea that Lady Mary would see in Constantinople, practised thousands of kilometres away.","Mather was curious enough to ask other Africans living in Boston, and he wrote that several of them described the same practice and said it was common in their homelands. Then, by 1716, he had read Emanuel Timoni's report of Ottoman inoculation in the Philosophical Transactions. Two completely different sources, one from Africa and one from the Ottoman Empire, described the same method. In 1716 Mather wrote a letter to the Royal Society in London describing what Onesimus had told him and saying that, if smallpox ever came to Boston again, he would push for inoculation.","Onesimus's story shows something important about the history of science. Ideas do not only come from famous people in universities and laboratories. African healers had developed and passed down inoculation over many generations, and an enslaved man carried that knowledge in his memory across an ocean. Yet when Mather later promoted inoculation, many people in Boston mocked him for trusting the word of an enslaved African, a sign of the prejudice of the time.","We know that in 1716 Onesimus tried to buy his freedom. He raised money so that Mather could buy another enslaved young man to replace him, and Mather agreed under certain conditions, including that Onesimus would still come and work for the household when needed. Records of what happened to him afterwards are very thin. But his knowledge was about to save lives."]},{"heading":"Boston 1721: Fear, Fury and Figures","paragraphs":["On 22 April 1721, a merchant ship called the Seahorse sailed into Boston harbour from the West Indies. On board were sailors sick with smallpox. Boston had not had a big smallpox epidemic for almost twenty years, which meant that a whole generation of young people had never had the disease and were not immune. By late May, Cotton Mather was writing in his diary that smallpox had entered the town. Soon the disease was spreading from house to house.","Cotton Mather remembered Onesimus. In June 1721 he wrote to Boston's doctors, urging them to try inoculation. Almost all of them refused. Only one answered his call: Zabdiel Boylston, a 42-year-old physician who had trained by apprenticeship rather than at a university.","On 26 June 1721, Boylston inoculated three people: his own six-year-old son Thomas, and two enslaved people he owned, a man named Jack, aged 36, and Jack's two-year-old son, Jackey. He used a method like the one Onesimus had described, putting matter from a smallpox sore into small cuts in the skin. Like Newgate, the choice of enslaved people as some of the first test subjects is something that modern ethics would condemn, since they had no power to refuse. All three recovered.","Boylston carried on, and over the following months he inoculated more and more people. Many Bostonians were furious. The town's best-educated doctor, William Douglass, who had a medical degree from Europe, attacked inoculation as reckless and said ministers should leave medicine to physicians. A newspaper called the New-England Courant, run by James Franklin (the older brother of the young Benjamin Franklin, who worked in the print shop), printed articles against it. Town officials tried to stop Boylston, and he faced threats so serious that, by one account, he hid for 14 days and visited his patients only at midnight, in disguise.","The anger reached a frightening peak in November 1721. In the middle of the night, someone threw a lit grenade, a small bomb, through the window of Cotton Mather's house, into the room where a relative who had just been inoculated was staying. Luckily it did not explode; according to the story usually told, its fuse was knocked off as it smashed through the glass. Tied to it was a note full of angry threats against Mather. Nobody was hurt, but the attack showed how bitterly divided the town had become.","The epidemic peaked in October 1721, when 411 people died in a single month. By the time it was over, the town recorded 5,889 people who had caught smallpox naturally, and 844 of them had died. That is about 14 in every 100, or roughly one in seven.","Boylston, meanwhile, kept careful records. Accounts give slightly different totals, but he inoculated roughly 240 to 290 people during the epidemic, and only six of them died. That is about 2 in every 100. Even allowing for the fact that his patients may have been healthier on average, the difference was dramatic: natural smallpox was about seven times more deadly than inoculation.","This comparison is one of the earliest examples of using numbers from two groups of people to judge whether a medical treatment works. In 1724 Boylston travelled to London, carrying a letter of introduction from Mather to James Jurin, and published his results as 'An Historical Account of the Small-Pox Inoculated in New England'. In 1726 he was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society, one of the first people from the American colonies to receive that honour.","By 1722, then, inoculation had a foothold on both sides of the Atlantic. A noblewoman, a princess, a Scottish surgeon, an enslaved African, a Puritan minister and a stubborn Boston doctor had all played a part. But the story was far from over. Inoculation was still risky, killing roughly one or two people in every hundred. It was expensive, with weeks of illness and care. It could start new outbreaks. And it was fiercely controversial. Over the next seventy years, it would spread to empresses and armies, but it would remain a dangerous gamble."]}],"deepDive":"Historians see the inoculation debates of 1721 and 1722 as a turning point in how medicine decided what works. Before this, doctors relied mostly on ancient authorities such as Galen and on personal experience. The inoculation arguments pushed people to count. Boylston's Boston figures (about 2 percent of inoculated people dying versus about 14 percent of natural cases, out of 5,889 natural infections and 844 deaths) and James Jurin's yearly reports for the Royal Society, which compared roughly 1 death in 50 inoculations with roughly 1 in 6 or 7 natural cases, are among the earliest uses of comparative numbers to judge a medical practice. They were not modern clinical trials. There was no random assignment and no control for age or health, and the people who chose inoculation may have been richer, better fed and less exposed. Modern scholars also point out that such records missed people who caught smallpox from inoculated patients, a real cost that was hard to count. Even so, these early tallies changed the shape of the argument from a clash of opinions into a question that evidence could answer. Another debate is about credit. Older histories often told this story as if English doctors and Lady Mary simply 'discovered' inoculation. Historians now stress that the knowledge came from women healers in the Ottoman world and from African communities, with Onesimus as the best-documented messenger. The prejudice Mather faced for trusting an enslaved man, and the use of prisoners, orphans and enslaved people as early test subjects, are now studied as part of the history of medical ethics. These episodes help explain why modern research demands informed consent and special protection for vulnerable groups. Finally, historians note that the Newgate prisoners' 'success' proved less than it seemed. Six people is a tiny sample, and one of them was probably already immune. The real persuasive power came from the growing pile of numbers over the following years.","funFact":"Cotton Mather, the Boston minister who championed inoculation in 1721, learned about it partly from Onesimus, and the grenade thrown into his house that November failed to explode, reportedly because its fuse was knocked off as it crashed through the window.","check":{"q":"In Boston in 1721, roughly what share of inoculated people died, compared with people who caught smallpox naturally?","a":"About 2 in 100 inoculated people died, compared with about 14 in 100 natural cases","wrong":["About 30 in 100 inoculated people died, compared with about 2 in 100 natural cases","No inoculated people died, compared with about half of natural cases","About the same share died in both groups"]},"sources":[{"title":"Lady Mary Wortley Montagu","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady_Mary_Wortley_Montagu"},{"title":"Edward Jenner and the history of smallpox and vaccination (Riedel, 2005)","publisher":"Proceedings (Baylor University Medical Center), via NIH PubMed Central","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1200696/"},{"title":"Onesimus (Bostonian)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onesimus_(Bostonian)"},{"title":"Cotton Mather","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cotton_Mather"},{"title":"Zabdiel Boylston","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zabdiel_Boylston"},{"title":"Inoculation","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inoculation"},{"title":"1721 Boston smallpox outbreak","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1721_Boston_smallpox_outbreak"},{"title":"Caroline of Ansbach","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caroline_of_Ansbach"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/03-1.jpg","alt":"Lady Mary Wortley Montagu in a Turkish-style turban and jewels, painted by Joseph Highmore.","file":"Portrait of Lady Mary Wortley Montagu (by Joseph Highmore).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Lady_Mary_Wortley_Montagu_(by_Joseph_Highmore).jpg","author":"Joseph Highmore","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/03-2.jpg","alt":"Boston minister Cotton Mather, painted by Peter Pelham.","file":"Cotton Mather by Peter Pelham.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cotton_Mather_by_Peter_Pelham.jpg","author":"Peter Pelham","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/03-3.jpg","alt":"Title page of Zabdiel Boylston's account of smallpox inoculation in New England (London 1726, reprinted Boston 1730).","file":"Boylston - An Historical Account of the Small-pox Inoculated in New England (title).png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boylston_-_An_Historical_Account_of_the_Small-pox_Inoculated_in_New_England_(title).png","author":"Zabdiel Boylston (1679–1766)","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3850},{"n":4,"title":"Empresses, Armies and a Dangerous Gamble","when":"1720s – 1790s","summary":"In the 1700s, inoculation spread from rich families in London to an empress in Russia and a whole army in America. It saved many lives, but it was still a risky gamble that could make people very sick. Even a famous mathematician tried to work out, with numbers, whether the gamble was worth it.","sections":[{"heading":"Inoculation Goes on the Road","paragraphs":["After the excitement of 1721 and 1722, you might expect inoculation to have swept through Britain like wildfire. It did not. For about twenty years it grew only slowly. A few wealthy families used it, but the deaths that sometimes followed, and the fear of starting new outbreaks, kept many people away. Doctors argued about it in pamphlets, and clergymen argued about it in sermons.","In the American colonies, however, epidemics kept forcing people to make a choice. In 1738, when smallpox struck Charleston in South Carolina, a doctor named John Kirkpatrick reported that about 800 people had been inoculated there and only about eight of them had died, around 1 in 100. In a town where natural smallpox was killing far more, numbers like that were hard to ignore.","By the 1740s and 1750s inoculation was becoming more common in Britain. In 1746 a charity hospital opened in London especially for smallpox and inoculation, so that poorer people could be treated too. And across the country, inoculators set up 'inoculation houses': buildings, often outside towns, where people came to be inoculated and then stayed, cut off from their neighbours, until they were no longer infectious. A stay could last two weeks to a month or more.","The operation itself was quick. The inoculator dipped the point of a small, sharp blade called a lancet into fluid or matter from a smallpox sore on someone with a mild case, then made a small scratch or cut on the patient's arm and placed the material inside. For about a week the patient usually felt fine, and the spot on the arm grew red and swollen. Then came a fever, headache and aching back, followed by a crop of spots, often far fewer than in natural smallpox and clustered near the scratch. After two or three weeks the scabs dropped off. Before going home, patients were usually washed, and their clothes were cleaned or aired, because people knew the scabs could still pass on the disease.","What happened inside these houses could be grim. Doctors of the time believed that a person had to be 'prepared' for inoculation to make the illness milder. Preparation often meant weeks of a special, very plain diet without meat, doses of medicines to make people vomit or empty their bowels (called 'purging'), and bleeding, which meant cutting a vein to let out some blood. We now know that none of this helped. In fact, bleeding and starving people probably weakened them.","We have a vivid example. In 1757 an eight-year-old boy in Gloucestershire went through this kind of preparation. He was bled repeatedly and given very little to eat for weeks before his inoculation, and he remembered the experience for the rest of his life. Recovery took about a month. His name was Edward Jenner, and you will meet him again soon.","Then came a family of inoculators who changed the business. Robert Sutton, a surgeon in Suffolk in eastern England, began offering what he advertised as a 'new method' of inoculating in the late 1750s and 1760s. He and his sons, especially Daniel Sutton, made only a tiny, shallow scratch instead of a deep cut, used material from people with mild cases, kept preparation short, and did not bleed their patients. They also encouraged patients to get fresh air during recovery. Their results were excellent, and they became famous and rich.","The Suttons kept their method secret, sharing it only within the family and with partners who paid to use it, a little like a modern franchise. By 1770 the Sutton family and their partners are said to have inoculated more than 300,000 people. Daniel Sutton finally explained the method in a book called 'The Inoculator' in 1796. Their simpler approach was safer and cheaper, and it helped inoculation spread to many more people, including whole villages that were sometimes inoculated together during an epidemic."]},{"heading":"Benjamin Franklin's Sorrow","paragraphs":["Some of the most powerful arguments for inoculation came not from doctors but from parents. One of them was Benjamin Franklin, the printer, writer and scientist from Philadelphia who would later help found the United States.","Franklin and his wife Deborah had a little son named Francis, nicknamed 'Franky', born on 20 October 1732. By every account he was a bright, much-loved child. In 1736 smallpox came to Philadelphia. Francis had been ill with a stomach complaint, and Franklin decided to wait until the boy was stronger before having him inoculated. But before that could happen, Francis caught smallpox naturally. He died on 21 November 1736, just after his fourth birthday.","Franklin's grief was made worse by rumours. Because he was known as a supporter of inoculation, some people in Philadelphia whispered that Francis had died from being inoculated. That would have been a gift to the opponents of the practice. So, heartbroken as he was, Franklin printed a notice in his own newspaper, the Pennsylvania Gazette, explaining that his son had not been inoculated but had caught the disease 'in the common way of infection'.","Many years later, in his famous autobiography, Franklin wrote that he had 'long regretted bitterly' that he had not given his son the protection of inoculation. He added that he was writing this for the sake of other parents, so that they would not face the same regret. The gravestone of little Francis in Philadelphia described him as 'the delight of all who knew him'.","Franklin turned his sorrow into action. He collected numbers on inoculation and death in Boston and Philadelphia, and he worked with doctors to make inoculation cheaper and easier. In 1759 he teamed up with the London physician William Heberden on a pamphlet that explained, in plain language, how parents could inoculate their own children at home without paying expensive doctors. Heberden paid to print copies to give away in America. The cost of inoculation, which could be several weeks of a working family's income, was one of the biggest barriers, and Franklin wanted to remove it.","Franklin's story is a reminder of how hard decisions about inoculation were in the 1700s. It was not a small, safe step. It meant deliberately giving a child a real disease, and sometimes that child died. Parents had to weigh a smaller risk now against a larger risk later, without knowing when or if smallpox would come."]},{"heading":"The Empress and the Quaker Doctor","paragraphs":["In the summer of 1768, an English doctor received an astonishing invitation. Catherine II, Empress of Russia, known to history as Catherine the Great, wanted him to come to St Petersburg and inoculate her.","The doctor was Thomas Dimsdale, born in 1712 in Essex, England, into a Quaker family. Quakers were a Christian group known for their plain dress and simple lives. Dimsdale had trained in London and worked for decades as a doctor in the town of Hertford. He was an expert in inoculation and used a gentle method similar to the Suttons'. In 1767 he published a book called 'The Present Method of Inoculating for the Small-Pox', which was popular enough to go through five editions in two years. News of the book reached Russia.","Catherine had good reasons to be worried. Smallpox was a terror in Russia, as everywhere in Europe. The young Russian emperor Peter II had died of it in 1730, at just 14 years old. Catherine had one son, Grand Duke Paul, born in 1754, the heir to the throne. If smallpox killed her or Paul, the empire could be thrown into chaos. Catherine was also proud of being a modern, scientific ruler who read the latest ideas from Western Europe, and she wanted to set an example for her people.","Dimsdale travelled to St Petersburg with his son Nathaniel, who was a medical student. He was nervous, and with good reason. If the empress died after he inoculated her, he could be blamed, and he had no idea how the Russian court might react. Before the operation, it was quietly arranged for relays of fresh horses to stand ready along the road out of Russia, so that the Dimsdales could escape quickly if things went wrong.","Dimsdale first tested his method on some young people in St Petersburg to find a healthy donor with a mild case. Then, in October 1768, he inoculated the 39-year-old empress, using material taken from a young boy, usually named as Alexander Markov, who had a mild case. According to the accounts, Catherine had herself inoculated quietly, without public fuss. She had a mild illness, recovered well, and was soon back at work. Her son Paul, aged 14, was inoculated shortly afterwards and also recovered.","Catherine celebrated the success loudly. Church services of thanksgiving were held, and the boy Alexander Markov was rewarded by being made a nobleman, with the extra surname 'Ospenny', from the Russian word for smallpox. Dimsdale was made a baron of the Russian Empire and, according to most accounts, given £10,000 (an enormous fortune at the time), £2,000 for his expenses and a yearly pension of £500. His son Nathaniel was also made a baron. More than 140 members of the Russian court followed the empress's example and were inoculated.","Catherine hoped her example would encourage ordinary Russians, and inoculation centres were opened in some Russian cities. Dimsdale later returned to Russia, in 1781, to inoculate Catherine's grandsons. Back in England, he became a banker and a Member of Parliament for Hertford. One of his patients there was Mai (sometimes called Omai), a young man from the Pacific island of Raiatea who had come to Britain on one of Captain Cook's ships."]},{"heading":"Washington's Secret Order","paragraphs":["George Washington knew smallpox personally. In 1751, when he was 19, he sailed to the island of Barbados in the Caribbean with his older half-brother Lawrence, who was ill with tuberculosis and hoped the warm climate would help. While there, George caught smallpox. He was sick for weeks but recovered, with a few faint scars on his face. From then on he was immune, which turned out to be very lucky for the future of his country.","Twenty-four years later, in 1775, the American Revolution began, and Washington became commander of the Continental Army, the army of the thirteen colonies fighting for independence from Britain. Smallpox quickly became one of his most dangerous enemies, deadlier in some months than British bullets.","Many British soldiers had grown up in crowded towns in Britain where smallpox was common, so a large share of them had already had it as children and were immune. Many American soldiers came from farms and small villages where smallpox was rare, so they had never had it. When thousands of these young men were packed together in camps, smallpox could spread fast.","The disaster came in the American attempt to capture Quebec in Canada in 1775 and 1776. During the long, freezing siege of Quebec City, smallpox swept through the American camp. By the end of March 1776, the American force had grown to nearly 3,000 men, but almost a quarter of them were too sick to fight, mostly because of smallpox. Some desperate soldiers tried to inoculate themselves secretly, scratching smallpox matter under their fingernails with pins or needles. That spread the disease even further. Weakened by sickness, the army was forced into a long, chaotic retreat.","Inoculation was risky for an army, because newly inoculated soldiers would be sick for weeks, and while they were sick they could spread smallpox to others. Earlier in the war, inoculating soldiers had actually been forbidden, because commanders feared it would spread the disease, and Washington had been cautious too. But by the winter of 1776 to 1777, he became convinced that smallpox might destroy the army if he did nothing.","In early 1777, while the army was in winter quarters at Morristown, New Jersey, Washington made his decision. In early February he wrote to Dr William Shippen Jr., one of the army's senior doctors, ordering that all soldiers who had not had smallpox should be inoculated. He explained that if the disease spread through the army in the natural way, with its usual violence, it could do more harm than the enemy.","The operation had to be done carefully and quietly. If the British learned that large parts of the American army were lying sick for weeks at a time, they might attack. So the inoculations were carried out in groups, in secret as far as possible, and new recruits were inoculated as they arrived, before they joined their units. Special houses and hospitals were used, including in Philadelphia, so that sick soldiers were kept away from healthy ones.","It worked. This is often described as the first mass inoculation of an entire army. Smallpox did not disappear from the war, but it never again crippled the Continental Army the way it had at Quebec. Washington's biographer Ron Chernow called the decision as important as any military measure Washington took during the war. Washington's own wife, Martha, had been inoculated in 1776, so the general knew from his family's experience that it could be done safely."]},{"heading":"The Price of the Gamble","paragraphs":["So why didn't everyone simply get inoculated? Because inoculation had real problems, and it is important to be honest about them.","First, there was the cost. A full inoculation, with weeks of preparation, the operation itself, and a stay in an inoculation house while recovering, could cost far more than a poor family earned in a month. Rich families could afford it; poor families often could not. Working people also could not easily stop working for several weeks, and servants or labourers might lose their jobs.","Second, there was the illness. Inoculation gave a person a real case of smallpox, usually mild but sometimes not. Most people had a fever and a few spots and recovered within two or three weeks. But roughly 1 or 2 in every 100 died. Even royal families were not safe. In 1783, Prince Octavius, a four-year-old son of King George III of Britain, died a few days after being inoculated. People at the time argued about whether the inoculation was to blame, but the loss hit his father very hard.","Third, and perhaps worst, inoculated people were contagious. They could pass the full, natural smallpox on to people around them who were not immune. If inoculated patients were not kept carefully apart, inoculation could actually start new outbreaks. In France, an epidemic in Paris in the 1760s was blamed on inoculation, and the authorities banned it within the city, so doctors moved their practices to the suburbs.","In some American towns, anger about this danger turned violent. People who lived near inoculation hospitals feared that the disease would escape into their streets. In the 1770s, crowds in places such as Marblehead, Massachusetts, protested against private inoculation hospitals, and in early 1774 one built on an island off Marblehead was burned down.","France offers a dramatic example of what refusing inoculation could cost. In 1774 King Louis XV died of smallpox. A few weeks later, the new king, Louis XVI, and his younger brothers were inoculated, a step that made inoculation much more popular in France. The French writer Voltaire had argued decades earlier that if the French had used inoculation like the English, thousands of lives would have been saved.","Every family in the 1700s that thought about inoculation was really doing a kind of risk calculation in their heads. They were comparing a small, immediate danger that they chose with a larger danger that might or might not come later. It was a gamble. And in the 1760s, one of the cleverest mathematicians in Europe decided to work out the odds properly."]},{"heading":"Doing the Maths: Daniel Bernoulli","paragraphs":["Daniel Bernoulli was born in 1700 in Groningen, in what is now the Netherlands, into a family of Swiss mathematicians from Basel so brilliant that historians have to number them to keep them apart. Today he is best known for 'Bernoulli's principle', which explains how the pressure in a moving fluid changes with its speed and helps explain how aeroplane wings work. But he had also trained as a doctor, and he was fascinated by probability, the mathematics of chance.","In 1760, Bernoulli presented a paper to the Royal Academy of Sciences in Paris that asked a simple but powerful question: if everyone were inoculated at birth, how much longer would people live on average? To answer it, he built what many historians consider the first mathematical model of the spread and effects of an infectious disease.","Bernoulli needed some numbers to start with. Using the death records available to him, he made estimates. Roughly speaking, he assumed that each year about 1 in 8 people who had never had smallpox would catch it, and that about 1 in 8 of those who caught it would die. He also took into account that many children would die of other causes before smallpox ever reached them. Then he used equations to follow an imaginary group of babies through life, year by year, counting how many caught smallpox, how many died from it, and how many survived.","He ran the calculation twice: once for a world with smallpox as usual, and once for a world where smallpox had been removed as a cause of death by inoculation, after subtracting the small risk of the operation itself. His answer was that average life expectancy at birth would rise from about 26 years and 7 months to about 29 years and 9 months, a gain of roughly three years.","You can picture his method with a thought experiment. Imagine 1,000 babies all born in the same year. Bernoulli's numbers let him estimate, year by year, how many of them would still be alive, how many had already survived smallpox and were now safe, and how many were still at risk. Each year, some of those at risk caught smallpox and some of those died. Over a lifetime, his calculation suggested that smallpox would kill around one in every thirteen of the babies, a huge toll. In the second, imaginary world, those smallpox deaths disappeared, apart from the few caused by inoculation, and more of the 1,000 survived into adulthood, raising the average.","Three years might not sound like much. But remember that this is an average over everyone, including the many who would never have died of smallpox. For the families who did lose someone, it meant a whole lifetime. Bernoulli concluded that, for society as a whole, inoculation was clearly worth it, and he argued that governments should support it. His paper was published in full in 1766.","Not everyone agreed with how he framed the question. The French mathematician Jean le Rond d'Alembert raised a thoughtful objection. Bernoulli's sums were about averages, he pointed out, but a real parent is not an average. Asking someone to accept a small risk of their child dying next month, in exchange for a longer life expectancy decades later, is not the same as simply comparing two numbers. People naturally care more about a danger right in front of them. D'Alembert did not oppose inoculation, but he argued that the decision involved feelings and values as well as mathematics.","That debate, between what is best for a whole population and how one person weighs a risk, is still discussed by scientists, doctors and philosophers today. Bernoulli's paper is often celebrated as the beginning of mathematical epidemiology, the science of using numbers and models to understand how diseases spread and how to control them."]},{"heading":"Rumours From the Cowshed","paragraphs":["By the 1790s, inoculation had come a very long way from the nutshell of an old woman in Constantinople and the memory of an enslaved man in Boston. It had protected an empress and her heir, a revolutionary army, princes and princesses, and hundreds of thousands of ordinary people. It had led to some of the first uses of statistics in medicine and to the first mathematical model of a disease.","And yet it was still a dangerous gamble. It still meant giving people a real case of smallpox. It still killed some of them. It still cost too much for many families. And it could still start new outbreaks. What the world really needed was a way to get the protection of smallpox without ever having smallpox at all.","The answer would come not from a palace or a university, but from the farms of the English countryside. In dairies in counties such as Gloucestershire and Dorset, people who milked cows sometimes caught a mild disease from sores on the cows' udders. It was called cowpox, and it gave the milkers a few blisters on their hands and perhaps a slight fever. And among the milkmaids and farmers, a curious piece of country wisdom was whispered: those who had caught cowpox from the cows never seemed to catch smallpox."]}],"deepDive":"Daniel Bernoulli's 1760 analysis, read to the Paris Academy of Sciences and published in 1766, is often called the first mathematical model in epidemiology. He set up equations for a group of people born at the same time and tracked two kinds of people at each age: those who had never had smallpox and those who had survived it. Using rough estimates drawn from the Breslau life tables and medical reports, he assumed that each year about 1 in 8 susceptible people caught smallpox and that about 1 in 8 of them died. Removing smallpox as a cause of death while allowing for the small risk of inoculation raised life expectancy at birth from about 26 years 7 months to about 29 years 9 months. Modern epidemiologists who have re-examined the paper, such as Klaus Dietz and Hans Heesterbeek, praise its logic while noting its simplifications. Bernoulli assumed a constant risk of infection at every age, so he ignored the way smallpox came in epidemic waves, and he did not model the fact that inoculated people could infect others. Jean le Rond d'Alembert's critique raised a deeper question that still matters in public health: how should an individual weigh a small immediate risk against a larger but delayed and uncertain one? Historians also debate how much the death rate from inoculation really fell during the century. Early figures of around 2 percent dropped substantially with the Sutton and Dimsdale methods, which used shallow scratches, mild donor material and no bleeding. These gentler methods probably produced milder infections because less material entered the body, although doctors at the time could not explain why. Washington's 1777 decision has been studied too. Because inoculation could spread smallpox, the army had to manage it with quarantine and timing, an early example of disease control planned as part of military strategy.","funFact":"When Catherine the Great was inoculated in 1768, the boy whose mild smallpox supplied the material, Alexander Markov, was made a nobleman and given the extra surname 'Ospenny', taken from the Russian word for smallpox.","check":{"q":"Which English doctor travelled to St Petersburg in 1768 to inoculate Catherine the Great?","a":"Thomas Dimsdale","wrong":["Charles Maitland","Zabdiel Boylston","William Heberden"]},"sources":[{"title":"Thomas Dimsdale","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Dimsdale"},{"title":"Variolation","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Variolation"},{"title":"History of smallpox","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_smallpox"},{"title":"Francis Folger Franklin","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Folger_Franklin"},{"title":"George Washington","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Washington"},{"title":"Invasion of Quebec (1775)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invasion_of_Quebec_(1775)"},{"title":"Mathematical modelling of infectious diseases","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_modelling_of_infectious_diseases"},{"title":"Edward Jenner and the history of smallpox and vaccination (Riedel, 2005)","publisher":"Proceedings (Baylor University Medical Center), via NIH PubMed Central","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1200696/"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/04-1.jpg","alt":"Baron Thomas Dimsdale, the English doctor who inoculated Catherine the Great; engraving by Ridley, 1802.","file":"Thomas Dimsdale (1712-1800).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Dimsdale_(1712-1800).jpg","author":"William Ridley, 1764-1838","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/04-2.jpg","alt":"Catherine the Great of Russia with sceptre and orb, painted by Fyodor Rokotov in 1763.","file":"Profile portrait of Catherine II by Fedor Rokotov (1763, Tretyakov gallery).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Profile_portrait_of_Catherine_II_by_Fedor_Rokotov_(1763,_Tretyakov_gallery).jpg","author":"Fyodor Rokotov","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/04-3.jpg","alt":"The Swiss mathematician Daniel Bernoulli as a young man, painted around 1720-1725.","file":"Porträt des Daniel Bernoulli (cropped).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portr%C3%A4t_des_Daniel_Bernoulli_(cropped).jpg","author":"UnknownEdited by Bammesk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":3762},{"n":5,"title":"The Milkmaid's Secret: Edward Jenner","when":"1774 – 1798","summary":"In the English countryside, people said that milkmaids who caught a mild illness from cows called cowpox never got smallpox. A farmer named Benjamin Jesty tried it on his family, and later a doctor named Edward Jenner tested the idea carefully on a boy called James Phipps. Jenner wrote it all down in a little book, and the word 'vaccine' was born from the Latin word for cow!","sections":[{"heading":"'I Shall Never Have Smallpox'","paragraphs":["Imagine walking down a muddy lane in Gloucestershire, in the west of England, in the 1770s. Hedges line the fields, church bells ring across the valley, and in every farmyard someone is milking cows by hand, twice a day, every day. Milking was skilled work, and it was very often done by young women and girls called milkmaids or dairymaids. They sat on low stools, pressed their cheeks against the warm flank of the cow, and squeezed the udder with their bare hands.","Sometimes the cows had sores on their udders and teats. This was a disease called cowpox. It made the cow uncomfortable, and it could spread to the hands of the person milking her. A milkmaid who caught cowpox might get a few painful blisters on her fingers or wrists, swollen glands under her arm and a mild fever for a few days. Then she got better. Cowpox was a nuisance, not a killer.","Here is the strange part. Country people had noticed, for a long time, that milkmaids who had once caught cowpox did not seem to catch smallpox, even when it swept through the village. In an age when smallpox killed many of the people it infected and scarred many survivors, that was a remarkable thing to notice. The belief was common enough that one dairymaid is reported to have told a doctor, quite calmly, 'I shall never have smallpox for I have had cowpox.'","Today we know why this folk wisdom was partly right. Cowpox and smallpox are caused by two different viruses, but they are close cousins. Both belong to a family of viruses scientists call orthopoxviruses. They look similar enough to the immune system, the body's defence network, that fighting off one of them can leave the body ready to recognise the other. Nobody in the 1700s knew anything about viruses or immune systems. They only knew what they saw on farms: the cowpox girls stayed safe.","Modern scientists have found that cows are not really the natural home of cowpox at all. The virus mostly lives in wild rodents, especially voles, and only now and then jumps into cattle, cats or people. That helps explain something that puzzled people at the time: cowpox came and went. Some years a farm would have it, and some years it would vanish completely. As we will see, that made it very hard to study.","It is worth remembering what people already had to protect themselves. As you read in earlier parts, variolation, giving a healthy person material from a real smallpox sore, had been used for centuries in Asia and Africa and had spread through Britain since the 1720s. It worked, but it was dangerous: about one or two in every hundred variolated people died, and while they were sick they could pass real smallpox to others. If a gentle cow disease could do the same job, it would be a gift. A few bold people decided to find out."]},{"heading":"The Farmer with a Darning Needle","paragraphs":["The first person we know of who deliberately used cowpox to protect his family was not a famous doctor. He was a farmer named Benjamin Jesty, born in the village of Yetminster in Dorset, in the south-west of England, and baptised there in August 1736. His father was a butcher. In 1770 Benjamin married Elizabeth Notley, and together they would have seven children.","Jesty had caught cowpox himself when he was younger, and so had two of the servants who worked on his farm. He had noticed that these servants could nurse people sick with smallpox without catching it. In 1774 smallpox broke out in the Yetminster area. Jesty was frightened for his wife and his two eldest sons, Robert and Benjamin. He knew of a farm at nearby Chetnole where the cows had cowpox.","So he made a decision that shocked his neighbours. He took Elizabeth and the two boys to the cows at Chetnole, and using a darning needle, a large sewing needle for mending socks, he scratched material from the cows' sores into their arms. The boys had only mild reactions. Elizabeth's arm became badly swollen and inflamed, and for a while the family worried, but she recovered.","Many local people were horrified. Putting a disease from an animal into a human body seemed unnatural and even wicked. According to later accounts, Jesty was called inhuman and was jeered at and pelted when he went to local markets. But the experiment seemed to work. In later years, when his sons were exposed to smallpox, they did not catch it.","Jesty did not write a book or send a report to any scientific society. He was a farmer, not a scientist, and his test involved only his own family. For more than twenty years, almost nobody outside Dorset knew about it. Around 1797 he moved his family to Downshay Manor Farm at Worth Matravers, near the Dorset coast.","His story was not completely forgotten. In 1805, after vaccination had become famous, a London group called the Original Vaccine Pock Institution invited Jesty to the city. Twelve medical men questioned him about what he had done. To show the protection had lasted, his eldest son Robert, then 28, agreed to be inoculated with smallpox material again, and once more he did not become ill. The Institution gave Jesty a written testimonial and a pair of gold-mounted lancets, small surgical knives, as a reward. An artist, Michael William Sharp, painted his portrait, which is now owned by the Wellcome Trust and has been shown at the Dorset Museum.","Jesty was not alone, either. In 1791 a teacher named Peter Plett, in what is now northern Germany, also tried giving people cowpox to protect them. Folk ideas about cowpox seem to have been known in several parts of Europe. Jesty died in April 1816, and his gravestone at Worth Matravers still says that he was the first known person to introduce cowpox by inoculation. So why do most people today remember a different name? To answer that, we need to meet a country doctor from Gloucestershire."]},{"heading":"A Curious Country Doctor","paragraphs":["Edward Jenner was born on 17 May 1749 in Berkeley, a small market town in Gloucestershire near the River Severn. He was the eighth of nine children. His father, the Reverend Stephen Jenner, was the vicar of Berkeley, and Edward grew up surrounded by books, fields, birds and farm animals.","When Edward was a schoolboy, he was variolated against smallpox, along with many other children of families who could afford it. He went through the whole process: the preparation, the deliberate infection and the illness that followed. He survived, but the experience stayed with him. Some historians believe that remembering how unpleasant and risky variolation was helped make him interested in finding something safer.","At the age of 13 he was apprenticed for seven years to Daniel Ludlow, a surgeon in the nearby town of Chipping Sodbury. An apprentice learned by doing: mixing medicines, dressing wounds, helping with operations and watching the patients. It was during this time, according to the story Jenner and his friends told later, that he heard a dairymaid boast that she could never catch smallpox because she had already had cowpox. Some historians doubt the story happened exactly like that, but one way or another the idea lodged in his mind.","In 1770, aged 21, Jenner went to London to study surgery and anatomy at St George's Hospital under John Hunter, one of the most famous surgeons and experimenters of the age. Hunter collected thousands of specimens and was always testing ideas. He passed on to Jenner a famous piece of advice that came from the physician William Harvey: 'Don't think; try.' Hunter meant that you should not just argue about an idea, you should do an experiment and see. Jenner and Hunter stayed friends and wrote letters to each other for years.","By 1773 Jenner was back in Berkeley, working as a family doctor and surgeon. He visited patients on horseback, often riding long distances along country lanes. He also played music, wrote poems and loved natural history. One of his great projects was a study of the cuckoo. People believed the adult cuckoo threw the other birds' eggs out of the nest where it had laid its own egg. Jenner watched carefully and showed that it was actually the newly hatched cuckoo chick that pushed out the eggs and chicks, using a little hollow in its back to lift them. His paper was published in 1788, and the same year he was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society, Britain's leading scientific club.","Not everyone believed his cuckoo observation. For more than a hundred years, some of his critics pointed to it as proof that Jenner was careless. Then, in the early 1920s, photographs and a film of a cuckoo chick showed he had been right all along. That tells us something about Jenner: he was patient, he watched closely, and he was willing to say things other people did not believe.","In 1788 he married Catherine Kingscote, and they had three children: Edward, Catherine and Robert. Through all these years, the cowpox question kept coming back to him. He collected stories from farmers and dairy workers and noted cases of people who had had cowpox and then failed to catch smallpox when variolated. But stories are not proof. To really know, he would need to test it."]},{"heading":"Sarah, Blossom and James: 14 May 1796","paragraphs":["Cowpox was unpredictable. For years it did not appear on the farms near Berkeley in the right way for Jenner to use it. Then, in the spring of 1796, it did. A young dairymaid named Sarah Nelmes caught cowpox while milking a cow. The cow had a name: Blossom. Sarah had fresh cowpox blisters on her hand, full of clear fluid.","Jenner now needed someone who had never had smallpox or cowpox, so that he could see clearly whether cowpox protected them. He chose James Phipps, the eight-year-old son of his gardener. It is important to say plainly that we do not know how much James or his parents understood about what was going on, and James was far too young to agree for himself. In the 1790s, doctors did not have rules about consent like we have today, and many experiments were done on children, servants and poor people who had little power to say no.","On 14 May 1796, Jenner took a lancet, a small, sharp surgical blade, and scraped some fluid from the sores on Sarah's hand. Then he made two small, shallow cuts on James's arm, each only about as long as a fingernail, and placed the cowpox material into them. That was all. It took a few minutes.","Over the next days, Jenner watched the boy closely and wrote down what happened. A small sore formed where the material had gone in, much like the ones on Sarah's hand. On about the seventh day James complained of discomfort in his armpit, where the glands had swollen. On the ninth day he felt chilly, lost his appetite and had a slight headache. He spent one restless night. The next day he was well again. The sore on his arm slowly healed, leaving a small scar.","Think about how different that was from variolation. A variolated child might be ill for a couple of weeks, covered in smallpox spots, and could pass real smallpox to everyone around them. James had a sore arm and one bad night. But Jenner still had not proved anything. A mild cowpox infection was only useful if it really protected against smallpox. And the only way people in 1796 knew to check for protection was a frightening one.","Blossom, by the way, became one of the most famous cows in history. Her horns now hang in Jenner's old house in Berkeley, which is a museum called Dr Jenner's House, and her hide is kept in the library of St George's, University of London, the medical school linked to the hospital where Jenner trained."]},{"heading":"The Test That Would Never Be Allowed Today","paragraphs":["On 1 July 1796, about seven weeks after the cowpox, Jenner did what doctors of his time did to check whether a person was already immune to smallpox. He variolated James. He took material from a real smallpox sore and put it into small cuts in the boy's arm, exactly as he would have done to any patient being inoculated in the normal way.","If James had not been protected, he would very likely have developed variolated smallpox, the same illness that Jenner himself had gone through as a boy, with its fever, spots and small risk of death. Instead, nothing much happened. A little redness appeared where the cuts were made, and then faded. James did not get sick. Months later, Jenner tested him again with smallpox material, and again there was no illness.","We need to be very clear about this: an experiment like this would never be allowed today. Deliberately exposing a child to a deadly disease to see what happens breaks the basic rules of modern medical ethics. Today, every test of a new medicine or vaccine must be checked in advance by an independent ethics committee. Volunteers, or for children their parents and the child too where possible, must be told about the risks and agree freely. The whole system of clinical trials you will meet later in this story was built partly because of past experiments in which people, often the poorest or least powerful, were put at risk without proper protection.","To be fair to Jenner, he was working inside the rules of his own time. Variolating someone was normal, everyday medicine in 1796, and thousands of parents chose it for their own children. Jenner was not inventing a new danger; he was using the standard test of the day. But we can admire his curiosity and still say that the way he tested his idea on a child would be wrong now.","James Phipps grew up, married and had a family, and he lived until 1853. He was variolated many more times during his life, twenty or more according to some accounts, and is said never to have caught smallpox. When Jenner later wrote up his research, James appeared as Case 17 in the list of patients."]},{"heading":"The Little Book That Named Vaccines","paragraphs":["One boy is not enough to prove anything. James might have been naturally resistant, or the smallpox material might have been weak. Jenner knew this. In 1797 he wrote a short report about his findings and sent it to the Royal Society. The Society did not publish it. The evidence, the reviewers felt, was too thin.","Jenner wanted more cases, but he faced a frustrating problem: cowpox had disappeared from the local farms. He had to wait. Only in the spring of 1798 did it come back. Now he worked quickly. He gave cowpox to more children, including his own baby son Robert, who was 11 months old. He also tried something important: taking material from the sore of one vaccinated child and putting it into the arm of the next child, to see whether cowpox could be passed on from person to person without a cow. It could. That discovery would turn out to be hugely important, as you will see in the next part.","In 1798 Jenner published, at his own expense, a short book with a very long title: 'An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae, a Disease Discovered in Some of the Western Counties of England, Particularly Gloucestershire, and Known by the Name of the Cow Pox.' It described 23 cases, including people who had caught cowpox naturally years before and then resisted variolation, and the children he had given it to on purpose. It included beautiful coloured pictures of cowpox sores, including the sores on Sarah Nelmes's hand.","'Variolae vaccinae' was Jenner's Latin name for cowpox. It means something like 'smallpox of the cow'. 'Variola' was the name for smallpox, and 'vacca' is Latin for cow. From this came the words 'vaccine' for the cowpox material and 'vaccination' for the process of giving it. Much later, the French scientist Louis Pasteur suggested using the word vaccine for all preparations like this, as a tribute to Jenner. So every time someone says the word vaccine today, they are, without realising it, talking about cows.","The Inquiry was not perfect. Jenner got some things wrong. He suggested that cowpox started in horses, coming from a horse disease called 'the grease' which was carried to cows on the hands of farm workers who looked after both animals. Most experts rejected that idea. He also believed at first that one vaccination would protect a person for their entire life; later experience showed that protection could fade over many years. And 23 cases was a small number by any standard. But the central idea was right, and it was written down clearly enough that other doctors could test it for themselves."]},{"heading":"Safer, Kinder, and Not Contagious","paragraphs":["Why did Jenner's idea matter so much, if variolation already existed? The answer is safety. Variolation used real smallpox virus. Even though the illness was usually milder than catching smallpox naturally, about one or two of every hundred variolated people died. Worse, a variolated person was infectious. They could start a real smallpox outbreak in their town, which is why they had to be kept away from other people for weeks.","Vaccination with cowpox was far safer. Serious problems were rare. The person usually had just a sore at the spot and maybe a day or two of feeling unwell. Most important of all, a vaccinated person could not spread smallpox to anyone, because there was no smallpox in them at all. They could go to school, go to work and see their families. That made it possible, for the first time, to imagine protecting whole cities and countries without risk of starting epidemics.","At first it was slow going. When Jenner went to London to find volunteers, he spent about three months without persuading anyone. Then other doctors took up the work, including the surgeon Henry Cline and the physicians George Pearson and William Woodville. They tested vaccination on many more people. Not everything went smoothly: some early London vaccine got mixed with smallpox material in a hospital that treated both, causing confusion. But the results kept coming in, and they were good.","Jenner spent much of the rest of his life promoting vaccination, answering letters from all over the world and sending vaccine to doctors who asked for it, usually for free. In the garden of his house in Berkeley he had a small, rustic summer house that he called the Temple of Vaccinia. There, according to local tradition, he vaccinated poor local people free of charge. It has been called the world's first vaccination clinic, and you can still visit it.","The British Parliament rewarded him with £10,000 in 1802 and another £20,000 in 1807, after the Royal College of Physicians confirmed that vaccination worked. Those were huge sums at the time. Even Napoleon, whose France was at war with Britain, had his soldiers vaccinated and gave Jenner a medal. When Jenner asked him to release some English prisoners, Napoleon is reported to have said that he could not refuse anything to one of the greatest benefactors of mankind. Jenner died in Berkeley on 26 January 1823, aged 73, and is buried in the church of St Mary there, close to the house where he did his work."]},{"heading":"Who Gets the Credit?","paragraphs":["So who invented vaccination? The honest answer is that it was not one person. Many people, probably over many years and in several countries, noticed that cowpox seemed to protect against smallpox. That idea belonged to milkmaids and farmers before it belonged to any doctor. Benjamin Jesty used cowpox on purpose about 22 years before Jenner's experiment with James Phipps, and Peter Plett tried it in 1791. And long before any of them, healers in China, India, Africa and the Ottoman Empire had worked out that a deliberate, controlled infection could protect against smallpox. Jenner was not the first person to protect anyone against smallpox, and he did not invent the idea of using cowpox.","What Jenner did was different, and still very important. He tested the idea in a careful, organised way, writing down what happened to each person. He showed that cowpox could be passed from person to person, which meant the vaccine did not depend on finding a sick cow. He published his results so that anyone could read them, check them and argue about them. And then he spent the rest of his life spreading the method, sending vaccine around the world and arguing its case. The historian Donald Hopkins has pointed out that Jenner's key contribution was not the first use of cowpox, but showing that the people he vaccinated really were immune to smallpox.","Science works a lot like this. An idea often floats around for years before someone tests it carefully and shares it widely. Recognising Jesty, Plett and the unnamed milkmaids does not take anything away from Jenner. It just makes the story truer, and it reminds us that discoveries are usually built by many hands.","By 1800, Jenner's little book had been translated into other languages and vaccination was spreading across Europe and beyond. But there was a serious problem. The vaccine was alive. It had to be taken fresh from a sore and put into the next person within days, or it stopped working. There were no refrigerators, no aeroplanes and no way to keep a living virus safe on a voyage lasting months. So how could anyone possibly send a living vaccine across oceans to the other side of the world?"]}],"deepDive":"Jenner's 1798 Inquiry is a fascinating document to read with modern eyes. Of its 23 cases, most were people who had caught cowpox naturally, sometimes decades earlier, and who then failed to develop smallpox when variolated or exposed. Only a handful were deliberate vaccinations, and the crucial evidence for protection rested on challenge by variolation, a method that is impossible to repeat today. By modern standards the study had no control group, tiny numbers and no blinding. What made it persuasive was the combination of a clear claim, a biological mechanism that others could reproduce, and Jenner's demonstration that cowpox could be passed arm to arm, freeing vaccination from the unpredictable appearance of cowpox on farms. Within a few years, thousands of vaccinations by other doctors provided the large-scale evidence that Jenner's own numbers could not. Why does cowpox protect against smallpox at all? Cowpox virus and variola virus are both orthopoxviruses, and they share many proteins with very similar shapes. Antibodies and immune cells trained against one can recognise the other, a phenomenon called cross-protection. That same family resemblance is why vaccines based on the related virus vaccinia were later used to eradicate smallpox. Historians still debate how to divide credit. Jesty's 1774 inoculation is well documented, and Jesty's gravestone calls him the first known person to introduce cowpox by inoculation. Others, such as Peter Plett in 1791, did similar things. Jenner's defenders argue that a discovery only becomes part of science when it is tested systematically and published, which Jenner did and the others did not. Critics point out that Jenner received £30,000 from Parliament while Jesty received nothing, and that Jenner made mistakes, such as his theory that cowpox came from the horse disease called grease and his early belief that one vaccination gave lifelong protection. Interestingly, the horse idea is not as silly as it once seemed: as the next part explains, genetic studies suggest the vaccine virus used later is closely related to horsepox. A fair conclusion is that vaccination grew from folk knowledge, the bravery and stubbornness of individuals like Jesty, and Jenner's careful work and tireless promotion, which turned a rural belief into a method used around the world.","funFact":"The horns of Blossom, the cow whose cowpox started Jenner's famous 1796 experiment, are still kept at Jenner's house museum in Berkeley, and her hide is kept at St George's medical school in London.","check":{"q":"Where did the cowpox material that Edward Jenner gave to James Phipps in 1796 come from?","a":"From cowpox sores on the hand of milkmaid Sarah Nelmes, who caught it from a cow named Blossom","wrong":["From a smallpox sore on a sick soldier","From a horse with a disease called the grease","From Benjamin Jesty's cows at Chetnole"]},"sources":[{"title":"Edward Jenner and the history of smallpox and vaccination (Riedel, 2005)","publisher":"Baylor University Medical Center Proceedings, via NIH PubMed Central","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1200696/"},{"title":"Edward Jenner","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Jenner"},{"title":"Benjamin Jesty","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjamin_Jesty"},{"title":"Cowpox","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cowpox"},{"title":"History of Smallpox","publisher":"US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/smallpox/about/history.html"},{"title":"Dr Jenner's House, Museum and Garden","publisher":"Jenner Trust","url":"https://jennermuseum.com/"},{"title":"James Phipps","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Phipps"},{"title":"Vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaccine"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/05-1.jpg","alt":"Edward Jenner at his desk, painted by James Northcote, with a book about cowpox showing a cow.","file":"Edward Jenner by James Northcote.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Jenner_by_James_Northcote.jpg","author":"James Northcote","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/05-2.jpg","alt":"Dorset farmer Benjamin Jesty, painted by Michael William Sharp in 1805.","file":"Benjamin Jesty. Oil painting by M.W. Sharp, 1805. Wellcome L0050588.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benjamin_Jesty._Oil_painting_by_M.W._Sharp,_1805._Wellcome_L0050588.jpg","author":"Michael William Sharp (1776/77—1840)","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/05-3.jpg","alt":"Title page of Edward Jenner's 1798 book 'An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae' (the cow pox).","file":"An inquiry into the causes and effects of the variolae vaccinae.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_inquiry_into_the_causes_and_effects_of_the_variolae_vaccinae.JPG","author":"Jenner, Edward, 1749-1823.","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3975},{"n":6,"title":"Around the World, Arm to Arm","when":"1798 – 1900","summary":"After Jenner's book, vaccination spread around the world amazingly fast. To carry the living vaccine across the ocean, a Spanish doctor and a nurse named Isabel Zendal sailed with 22 orphan boys, passing the cowpox from one boy's arm to the next like a relay race. Not everyone liked vaccines, though, and cartoonists even joked that people would sprout cows!","sections":[{"heading":"News Travels Faster Than Ships","paragraphs":["In 1798, Edward Jenner's little book about cowpox was printed in England. Within a few years, it had been translated into many European languages, and doctors from Spain to Russia were reading it and trying vaccination for themselves. For a world with no telephones, no internet and no trains, that was astonishingly quick.","Why did it spread so fast? Partly because smallpox was everywhere and everyone feared it, so a safer way to beat it was huge news. Partly because Jenner and his supporters were generous: Jenner sent samples of vaccine to almost anyone who asked, usually for free, and did not try to keep the method secret or make money from selling it. And partly because the method was simple. You did not need a laboratory, only a lancet, a steady hand and some fresh cowpox material.","Vaccine crossed the Atlantic quickly too. In 1798 a doctor named John Clinch began vaccinating in Trinity, Newfoundland, which was then a British colony. In New York in 1799, the physician Valentine Seaman vaccinated his own children. In Boston, Benjamin Waterhouse, a professor at Harvard's medical school, obtained vaccine from England and in 1800 vaccinated four of his own children. He is often credited with the first vaccinations in the United States, though as you can see, Seaman had already started in New York, and several doctors were working at almost the same time.","Waterhouse wanted powerful supporters, so he wrote to Thomas Jefferson, who was soon to become President of the United States. Jefferson, who loved science, answered in December 1800 and quickly became one of vaccination's most enthusiastic champions. He had members of his household and many people at his home, Monticello, in Virginia, vaccinated, and he helped Waterhouse spread the vaccine to doctors in the South.","On 14 May 1806, exactly ten years after James Phipps was vaccinated, Jefferson wrote a famous letter to Jenner. He thanked him on behalf of humanity, and predicted that one day, thanks to Jenner, people in the future would only know from history books that the terrible disease of smallpox had ever existed. It was a bold prediction. It would take more than 170 years to come true, but it did.","Even enemies agreed on vaccination. Britain and France were at war for most of the years between 1793 and 1815, yet Napoleon had his soldiers vaccinated, and he awarded Jenner a medal. Across the continent, governments began to set up vaccination programmes. Doctors, priests and local officials encouraged parents to bring their children. Vaccination had become one of the first truly international medical ideas."]},{"heading":"A Living Relay: Arm to Arm","paragraphs":["There was a catch. The vaccine was a living virus, though nobody yet knew what a virus really was. It had to be fresh to work. Today vaccines are made in factories and kept cold in fridges, but in 1800 the only reliable place to keep cowpox alive was inside a living body, either a cow's or a person's.","Remember from the last part that Jenner discovered cowpox could be passed from person to person. That made a clever system possible, called arm-to-arm vaccination. It worked like a relay race. A child was vaccinated. About a week later, a small blister had formed on their arm, filled with clear fluid that people called lymph. A doctor would gently prick the blister with a lancet and use that fresh fluid to vaccinate the next child. A week after that, the second child's blister could be used for a third child, and so on. As long as the chain was not broken, one cow's cowpox could be kept going for years and passed to thousands of people.","Doctors also tried to send vaccine by post or by ship. They dried the lymph on threads, on the tips of quills or between little pieces of glass, then sealed them up. Sometimes this worked over short journeys in cool weather. But on long voyages, especially through hot climates, the dried vaccine often died before it arrived, and when a doctor at the other end tried to use it, nothing happened. Over and over, attempts to send vaccine to faraway places simply failed.","Arm-to-arm vaccination had its own dangers. If a child in the chain carried another infection in their blood, the lancet could carry it along with the cowpox. Over the 1800s, doctors realised that occasionally diseases such as syphilis, a serious infection that was hard to detect, and skin infections such as erysipelas had been passed on this way. One modern estimate suggests syphilis was spread in roughly 750 out of every 100 million arm-to-arm vaccinations. That is a very small fraction, but with millions of vaccinations a year, it was a real problem and a real cause of fear. It became one of the strongest reasons people looked for a better way to make vaccine.","Still, for most of the 1800s, arm-to-arm vaccination was how the world was protected. Parents in many towns were asked to bring their child back eight days after vaccination so that other children could be vaccinated from their arm. Some families were proud to help. Others hated the idea. Either way, a great river of cowpox flowed from arm to arm through the century, linking millions of people who never met."]},{"heading":"Twenty-Two Boys and a Ship Called María Pita","paragraphs":["Now imagine the problem facing the King of Spain. In the early 1800s, Spain ruled a huge empire stretching across much of the Americas, from what is now the southern United States down through Mexico, Central America and much of South America, and across the Pacific to the Philippines. Smallpox killed huge numbers of people there, especially Indigenous communities. King Charles IV had seen smallpox up close: one of his daughters had been sick with it. He wanted the new vaccine taken to every part of his empire. But how could a living vaccine survive a voyage across the Atlantic Ocean, which took many weeks?","The answer came from a doctor named Francisco Javier de Balmis. He was born in Alicante, Spain, in 1753, the son of a barber-surgeon, and he trained as a military surgeon. He had already served in the Americas and knew Mexico well. He had become one of the King's physicians and had translated a French book about vaccination into Spanish. Balmis came up with a plan that sounds startling today: he would bring the vaccine across the ocean alive, inside a chain of children.","On 30 November 1803, a small ship called the María Pita sailed out of the port of A Coruña, in Galicia in north-west Spain. On board were about 37 people, including doctors, nurses and helpers, and 22 orphan boys, aged roughly 3 to 10, from local orphanages. The plan was to vaccinate two boys at a time. About a week or so later, when the blisters on their arms were ready, material from those blisters would be used to vaccinate the next two boys, and so on, all the way across the sea. Two at a time was a safety measure: if one boy's vaccination did not take, there was a backup.","The ship also carried medical instruments, glass containers for the vaccine, and copies of the vaccination book Balmis had translated, ready to hand out so that local doctors could carry on the work after the expedition left. The official name of the voyage was the Royal Philanthropic Vaccine Expedition. Some historians call it the first international public health expedition in history.","The boys could not be left to look after themselves on a crowded sailing ship, and they needed someone to comfort them, keep them clean and keep the different groups apart so the chain would not get mixed up. That job went to a woman named Isabel Zendal. She was the rectoress, or head, of the orphanage in A Coruña where many of the boys had lived, and she brought her own son, Benito, about nine years old, who was one of the children carrying the vaccine. We will come back to her later in this part."]},{"heading":"From the Caribbean to China","paragraphs":["The María Pita's first stop was Tenerife, in the Canary Islands, where the team spent about a month vaccinating people before sailing on in early January 1804. The Atlantic crossing was the hardest test of the chain. Every week or so, two more boys were vaccinated from the arms of the last two. If the timing slipped, or if both new vaccinations failed, the vaccine would die in the middle of the ocean. It did not. On 9 February 1804, the ship reached Puerto Rico with the vaccine still alive.","There was a small surprise waiting. Vaccine had already reached Puerto Rico from the nearby Danish island of Saint Thomas, so the islanders had begun vaccinating before Balmis arrived. The expedition sailed on to Venezuela, reaching Puerto Cabello on 20 March 1804. In Caracas, Balmis helped set up a vaccination board, a local committee whose job was to keep the vaccine chain going and organise vaccination after the expedition left. It is described as the first such board on the American continent.","At the port of La Guaira, the expedition split in two. Balmis's deputy, a young surgeon named José Salvany, took one group south into what is now Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Chile and Bolivia. His journey through mountains, rivers and jungles lasted about seven years. He became ill and exhausted, and he died in Cochabamba, in today's Bolivia, in 1810, still working. Balmis took the other group north. They reached Havana, in Cuba, on 26 May 1804, and then went on to Mexico, which was then called New Spain. Cuba also sent three enslaved girls to Campeche, in Mexico, to carry the vaccine there in their arms, a reminder that the people whose bodies carried this vaccine often had no choice.","In Mexico, Balmis persuaded the Viceroy, José de Iturrigaray, to have his own son vaccinated, which encouraged many families to follow. Vaccine was carried to many parts of New Spain. Then Balmis set out on an even longer journey. The Pacific Ocean is far wider than the Atlantic, so he needed a new chain of carriers. Twenty-six Mexican children were gathered for the job. On 8 February 1805, they sailed from Acapulco on a galleon, a big trading ship, called the Magallanes. They arrived in Manila, in the Philippines, on 15 April 1805, with the vaccine alive again. There, the Governor-General, Rafael María de Aguilar, set an example by having his five children vaccinated.","Balmis still was not finished. In September 1805 he sailed from Manila to Macau, on the coast of China, and from October he vaccinated people in several Chinese cities as far as the Canton region. On his way home to Spain in 1806 he stopped at the island of Saint Helena in the South Atlantic, which belonged to Britain. Spain and Britain were at war, but he persuaded the British authorities there to let him vaccinate the islanders. He reached Lisbon, in Portugal, on 14 August 1806, having travelled around the whole world.","Nobody knows exactly how many people were vaccinated by the expedition and the local doctors and boards it trained. Estimates run to hundreds of thousands, and one Spanish account suggests more than half a million. When Jenner heard about the voyage, he wrote that he did not imagine the annals of history furnish an example of philanthropy so noble, so extensive as this.","Isabel Zendal did not go back to Spain. She stayed in Puebla, Mexico, with her son Benito, and the Galician boys never returned home to Galicia. One boy is known to have died during the voyage. For a long time Isabel was almost forgotten. Her name was even spelled in about 300 different ways in old documents. Today she is honoured: in 1950 the World Health Organization recognised her as the first nurse in history to take part in an international health mission, a statue of her stands in A Coruña, and an emergency hospital built in Madrid during the COVID-19 pandemic was named after her."]},{"heading":"Calves, Glycerin and a Cleaner Vaccine","paragraphs":["The arm-to-arm system was clever, but it had those worrying problems: the chain could break, and occasionally it carried other diseases. In the 1800s doctors began to try a different idea. Instead of keeping the vaccine going in children, why not keep it going in cows, where it had come from in the first place?","From about the 1840s, doctors in parts of Europe, particularly Italy, began to grow vaccine on the skin of young cattle. Here is how it worked. A healthy calf was chosen, and an area of skin on its belly or side was shaved and cleaned. The vaccinator made many small scratches in the skin and rubbed vaccine into them. After several days, the scratches formed rows of blisters, full of vaccine virus. The material from these blisters, called calf lymph, was carefully scraped off and collected. A single calf could provide enough vaccine for a very large number of people. The calf was usually checked afterwards to make sure it had been healthy.","Calf lymph slowly spread, and by the 1880s it had become the leading kind of smallpox vaccine. It had a big advantage: no human blood was involved, so diseases like syphilis could not be passed from one child to another through it. Vaccine farms, sometimes called vaccine stations or institutes, appeared in many countries, with stables of calves kept specially for the purpose.","There was still the problem of bacteria. Material scraped from a calf's skin was not clean, and in 1900 a survey found thousands of bacteria per gram in some vaccine, and far more in some commercial products. An English government scientist named Sydney Arthur Monckton Copeman found an answer. He showed that mixing the vaccine with glycerin, a thick, sweet, colourless liquid, at about half and half, killed off most of the unwanted bacteria while leaving the vaccine still able to work. From 1899, the British government's vaccine establishment gave out only glycerinated calf lymph to public vaccinators.","Glycerinated vaccine still had a weakness. At warm temperatures it lost its strength quickly, which made it hard to use in tropical countries without ice. That problem would not really be solved until the middle of the 20th century, when scientists learned to freeze-dry vaccine into a stable powder, a story you will meet when the world sets out to wipe out smallpox completely."]},{"heading":"Sprouting Cows: Fear, Laws and Protests","paragraphs":["Not everybody welcomed vaccination. Some people felt it was wrong to put material from an animal into a human body. Some had religious objections; even in the 1720s a preacher had called variolation a 'diabolical operation', and similar arguments were now aimed at vaccination. Others simply did not trust doctors, or had heard frightening rumours. One doctor, William Rowley, published pictures claiming to show children whose faces had become cow-like after vaccination.","In 1802 the brilliant British cartoonist James Gillray turned these fears into a famous joke. His print 'The Cow-Pock, or the Wonderful Effects of the New Inoculation!' shows a crowded room in which a doctor, usually taken to be Jenner, is vaccinating a frightened woman, while all around patients are sprouting little cows from their arms, noses, cheeks and ears. Gillray's prints were displayed in the window of his publisher Hannah Humphrey's shop in London, where crowds gathered to look. Historians argue about whether Gillray was mocking vaccination or mocking the people who were scared of it. Either way, the picture shows how strange and unsettling the new idea seemed to many people.","As governments became convinced vaccination worked, they began to make laws about it. In Britain, the Vaccination Act of 1840 offered free vaccination to the poor and made variolation illegal, because variolated people could spread smallpox. Then, in 1853, a new Act made vaccination compulsory for babies in England and Wales: parents had to have their child vaccinated within three months of birth, or four months for orphans, and could be fined £1 if they did not. Later laws in 1867 and 1871 made the rules stricter, created vaccination officers to enforce them and allowed repeated fines.","Many people were angry. Some objected to the government telling parents what to do with their children's bodies. Some were worried about the real risks of arm-to-arm vaccination. Working-class families, who were most often fined, felt that the law fell hardest on them. In 1854 a writer named John Gibbs published one of the first pamphlets against compulsory vaccination, and in 1867 the Anti-Compulsory Vaccination League was formed. Similar groups later appeared in the United States.","The city of Leicester, in the English Midlands, became the centre of the protest. By 1885, more than 3,000 prosecutions of parents were reported to be waiting to be heard there. In 1885 a huge demonstration filled the streets of Leicester. Estimates of how many people took part vary widely: some sources say more than 20,000, while others claim as many as 100,000. The protesters wanted the compulsory laws changed.","The government set up a Royal Commission, a group of experts, to study the whole question; it met from 1889 to 1896. Its advice led to the Vaccination Act of 1898. This kept vaccination compulsory but added a 'conscience clause': parents who sincerely believed vaccination would harm their child could apply for an exemption certificate. People who used it were called conscientious objectors, one of the earliest uses of that phrase. The same law switched government vaccine from human arm-to-arm lymph to calf lymph. In 1907 the rules were relaxed further, so that parents only had to sign a simple declaration. In the United States, a famous 1905 Supreme Court case, Jacobson v. Massachusetts, decided that states could require smallpox vaccination during an outbreak. Debates about how to balance personal choice and protecting the community have continued ever since, and you will meet them again later in this story."]},{"heading":"The Mystery of Vaccinia","paragraphs":["Here is a twist that would have amazed Jenner. You might expect that the smallpox vaccine used in the 20th century, the one that eventually wiped smallpox off the planet, was simply cowpox. It was not. The modern smallpox vaccine contains a virus called vaccinia, named from the same Latin word for cow. Vaccinia is a close relative of cowpox, but it is not the same virus.","Scientists first showed the difference in 1939, when the British researcher Allan Watt Downie found that vaccinia and cowpox reacted differently in laboratory tests. They were related but distinct. So where did vaccinia come from? Nobody knows for certain. During the 1800s, vaccine was passed through so many arms, calves and laboratories, and records were so patchy, that somewhere along the way the virus being used changed. The CDC, the US public health agency, simply says that at some point in the 1800s the virus used for vaccination switched from cowpox to vaccinia.","Modern genetic studies have given an intriguing clue. When scientists read the full genetic code of vaccinia, they found it is most closely related not to the cowpox found in Britain, but to horsepox, a similar disease of horses. Horsepox seems to have vanished from the wild; the only known sample was collected in Mongolia in 1976. Then researchers studied old vaccine samples kept from the past. A smallpox vaccine made in 1902 by the American company Mulford turned out to be about 99.7 percent similar to horsepox.","Remember that Jenner had suggested cowpox started in horses, carried to cows on the hands of farm workers? Most experts dismissed that idea. It now seems that horses may have been part of the story after all, at least for some of the vaccine strains used in the 1800s and later. Some vaccine makers are known to have used material from horses as well as cows. The most common view today is that vaccinia, cowpox, horsepox and smallpox all share a common ancestor, and that vaccinia's exact origin may never be fully solved.","What mattered most for people in the 1800s was that vaccination worked. Smallpox deaths fell sharply in places where most people were vaccinated, and countries such as Sweden saw what happened when vaccination dropped: in Stockholm, where fewer than half of people were vaccinated in the early 1870s, a major smallpox epidemic broke out in 1873. Yet for all this success, there was something deeply strange about the whole business. Nobody knew why vaccination worked. Nobody knew what cowpox actually was, or what caused smallpox, or any other disease. Many doctors still believed illness came from bad air. The answer would come from microscopes, laboratories and a new idea about invisible living enemies."]}],"deepDive":"The Balmis Expedition was, in effect, a living cold chain. A cowpox vaccination produces a blister whose fluid is most useful for passing on at around the end of the first week, so a chain of carriers needs a new person roughly every week to ten days. With 22 boys, vaccinated two at a time, the María Pita could in theory sustain the chain for around ten or eleven links, enough to cover a crossing that took a little over two months from A Coruña to Puerto Rico, including the stay in Tenerife. The double pairing gave redundancy: if one vaccination failed to take, the other still carried the virus. The same logic required 26 new child carriers for the much longer Pacific crossing from Acapulco to Manila. Modern historians praise the scale and ambition of the expedition, which set up local vaccination boards that kept vaccinating long after the ships left, but they also stress its ethical problems: orphans and enslaved girls had no real choice about being used as living containers, and few records tell us what they experienced. Arm-to-arm vaccination raised other issues. Its occasional spread of syphilis, estimated at about 750 cases per 100 million vaccinations, became a major argument of nineteenth-century critics. The shift to calf lymph from the 1840s onward, becoming dominant by the 1880s, and Copeman's discovery that 50 percent glycerin suppressed contaminating bacteria, are early examples of improving vaccine safety by changing manufacturing rather than abandoning a useful tool. The compulsion debate also has lasting importance. Britain's 1853 Act made infant vaccination compulsory, the Leicester protest of 1885 showed the strength of opposition, and the 1898 conscience clause created an early legal model for exemption. In the United States, Jacobson v. Massachusetts (1905) upheld a state's power to require vaccination, stating that liberty does not mean freedom from restraint at all times and in all circumstances. Finally, genomic research has turned vaccinia into a historical puzzle. Sequencing shows modern vaccinia strains cluster with horsepox, and a 1902 Mulford vaccine is about 99.7 percent identical to horsepox, suggesting that horse-derived material entered vaccine stocks at some point. Because vaccine strains were passaged through humans, cows, horses and laboratories for over a century with poor record-keeping, the precise route may never be known.","funFact":"To carry living smallpox vaccine across the Atlantic in 1803, the Balmis Expedition used 22 orphan boys as a relay, vaccinating two at a time so that the cowpox stayed alive on their arms all the way to the Americas.","check":{"q":"How did the Balmis Expedition keep the vaccine alive during its long ocean voyage?","a":"By vaccinating orphan boys two at a time, passing the cowpox from arm to arm along the way","wrong":["By keeping the vaccine in a refrigerator on the ship","By bringing a herd of cows with cowpox on board","By freeze-drying the vaccine into a powder"]},"sources":[{"title":"Balmis Expedition","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balmis_Expedition"},{"title":"Real Expedición Filantrópica de la Vacuna","publisher":"Wikipedia (Spanish)","url":"https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real_Expedición_Filantrópica_de_la_Vacuna"},{"title":"Isabel Zendal","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabel_Zendal"},{"title":"Smallpox vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smallpox_vaccine"},{"title":"Vaccination Act","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaccination_Act"},{"title":"Vaccine hesitancy","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaccine_hesitancy"},{"title":"Vaccinia","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaccinia"},{"title":"History of Smallpox","publisher":"US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/smallpox/about/history.html"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/06-1.jpg","alt":"James Gillray's 1802 cartoon 'The Cow-Pock', joking that vaccinated people would sprout little cows.","file":"The cow-pock - or - the wonderful effects of the new inoculation LCCN94509853.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_cow-pock_-_or_-_the_wonderful_effects_of_the_new_inoculation_LCCN94509853.jpg","author":"British Cartoon Prints Collection","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/06-2.jpg","alt":"A painted-tile portrait of doctor Francisco Javier de Balmis in Alicante, Spain, his home town.","file":"Francisco Javier de Balmis y Berenguer, Diputación de Alicante, España.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_Javier_de_Balmis_y_Berenguer,_Diputaci%C3%B3n_de_Alicante,_Espa%C3%B1a.jpg","author":"Foundling","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/06-3.jpg","alt":"Stone monument to nurse Isabel Zendal and the children of the vaccine expedition, in A Coruña, Spain.","file":"Monumento a Isabel Zendal.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monumento_a_Isabel_Zendal.jpg","author":"Jglamela","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":3806},{"n":7,"title":"Invisible Enemies: Germ Theory","when":"1840s – 1890s","summary":"For a long time, people thought diseases came from smelly 'bad air'. Then a doctor showed that washing hands saved mothers, Louis Pasteur proved germs only come from other germs, and Robert Koch caught the exact germs behind anthrax and tuberculosis. Scientists even found something smaller than germs: viruses!","sections":[{"heading":"A World That Blamed Bad Air","paragraphs":["By the 1840s, doctors had been vaccinating people against smallpox for almost fifty years. Millions of arms carried the little round scar of Jenner's cowpox. Yet if you had walked into a hospital in London, Paris or Vienna and asked a doctor, 'Why does vaccination work? And what actually causes smallpox, or cholera, or fever?', you would have got a shrug, a long Latin word, or an answer that sounds very strange today.","The most popular answer was miasma. The word comes from Greek and means something like pollution or defilement. According to miasma theory, diseases rose up out of rotting things: piles of rubbish, swamps, open sewers, graveyards and crowded, filthy slums. The rot was supposed to poison the air, and when people breathed that foul-smelling air, they fell ill. The idea was ancient, and it was still the leading explanation among doctors and scientists for most of the nineteenth century.","You can still hear the miasma idea hiding inside an everyday word. Malaria, the disease spread by mosquitoes, gets its name from medieval Italian 'mala aria', which simply means bad air. People living near warm marshes noticed that fevers were common there. They blamed the swampy smell, not the whining insects.","Miasma theory was wrong, but it was not completely useless. Because people believed stinking air caused disease, reformers fought hard to clean cities up. In Britain, the reformer Edwin Chadwick famously argued that 'all smell is disease'. In the hot summer of 1858, the River Thames in London became so thick with sewage that the stench was nicknamed the Great Stink. Members of Parliament, working right beside the river, could hardly bear it. They quickly backed the engineer Joseph Bazalgette's plan to build huge new sewers that carried waste away from the city. The sewers were meant to remove bad smells, but they also kept sewage out of drinking water, and that is what really saved lives.","A few people had suspected something different. Back in 1546, the Italian doctor Girolamo Fracastoro wrote about invisible 'seeds' of disease that could pass from person to person, through objects, or through the air. In the 1670s, the Dutch cloth merchant and lens-maker Antonie van Leeuwenhoek peered through his homemade microscopes and saw tiny wriggling creatures in pond water and even in the gunk scraped from his own teeth. He called them animalcules, meaning little animals. But nobody could prove that these tiny living things had anything to do with illness.","In 1854 a London doctor named John Snow carefully mapped the deaths in a cholera outbreak in the Soho district and traced them to a single water pump on Broad Street. His map was powerful evidence that cholera travelled in water, not air. Even so, many experts stuck with miasma. Old ideas are sticky. To knock them loose, scientists needed experiments so clear that almost nobody could argue with them. The first crack came from a maternity hospital in Vienna."]},{"heading":"The Doctor Who Said 'Wash Your Hands'","paragraphs":["In July 1846 a young Hungarian doctor named Ignaz Semmelweis started work as an assistant in the First Obstetrical Clinic of the Vienna General Hospital, one of the biggest hospitals in Europe. Obstetrics is the part of medicine that looks after mothers having babies. The hospital had two maternity clinics, side by side, and Semmelweis soon noticed something terrible about them.","Many new mothers caught a disease called childbed fever, also known as puerperal fever, a few days after giving birth. In the First Clinic, it killed on average about one mother in every ten. In the Second Clinic, the death rate was under four in a hundred. The difference was so well known in Vienna that some pregnant women begged not to be sent to the First Clinic.","Semmelweis became a detective. He listed every difference he could find between the two clinics and ruled them out one by one. There was one big difference left. The First Clinic was where medical students and doctors trained. The Second Clinic was run by midwives. And the doctors and students often came to the maternity ward straight from the autopsy room, where they had been studying the bodies of people who had died.","In 1847 a tragedy gave Semmelweis his key clue. His friend and colleague Jakob Kolletschka, a professor of forensic medicine, was accidentally cut by a student's scalpel during an autopsy. Kolletschka became very ill and died. When Semmelweis read the report of what had happened inside his friend's body, he was struck by how similar it was to what happened inside the mothers who died of childbed fever. Perhaps, he thought, something from the dead bodies was being carried on the doctors' hands into the wards. He called it 'cadaverous particles'. He did not know what those particles were. He only knew how to test the idea.","In May 1847 Semmelweis ordered everyone in the First Clinic to wash their hands in a solution of chlorinated lime before examining patients. He chose chlorine because it removed the smell of the autopsy room from people's hands better than ordinary soap did. The results were dramatic. In April 1847 the monthly death rate in the First Clinic had been about 18 in every hundred mothers. By June it was about 2 in a hundred, and in July just over 1 in a hundred. Over the following months the First Clinic's death rate fell to about the same level as the midwives' clinic.","You might expect that the whole medical world would cheer. Instead, many senior doctors ignored Semmelweis or were offended. Some disliked the idea that gentlemen doctors' hands could carry death. Others pointed out, fairly, that he could not explain how his 'particles' worked, and for years he published very little about his results himself. Political turmoil in 1848 and a quarrel with his boss also hurt him. In 1849 he lost his post in Vienna and eventually moved back to Hungary.","In the city of Pest (now part of Budapest), Semmelweis ran a maternity ward from 1851 and used his methods again. Between 1851 and 1855 only eight of 933 mothers there died of childbed fever. In 1861 he finally published a book laying out his evidence, but most of the medical establishment still would not accept it, and his angry open letters to critics made things worse. His health and behaviour declined, and in 1865, at just 47, he died in an asylum from an infected wound. Only after Pasteur, Koch and others explained what germs were did the world understand that Semmelweis had been right all along. Today he is often called the 'saviour of mothers'."]},{"heading":"Pasteur: Flasks, Wine and Silkworms","paragraphs":["Louis Pasteur was born in 1822 in Dole, a small town in eastern France, the son of a tanner who prepared animal skins for leather. He was not a medical doctor. He trained as a chemist, and his first big discovery was about crystals. He showed that some molecules come in mirror-image forms, like a left hand and a right hand.","In 1854 Pasteur became dean of the new Faculty of Sciences in Lille, an industrial city in northern France full of breweries and factories making alcohol from sugar beet. Local businessmen came to him with a problem: some of their vats went sour and spoiled. Pasteur put drops from good vats and bad vats under his microscope. In the good vats he saw round yeast cells. In the spoiled ones he saw different, rod-shaped microbes. He came to a bold conclusion: fermentation, the process that turns sugar into alcohol, is done by living yeast cells, and other living microbes can make wine or beer go wrong. The famous German chemist Justus von Liebig disagreed, but Pasteur's experiments steadily won the argument.","That led Pasteur straight into one of the oldest debates in science: spontaneous generation. This was the belief that living things could appear by themselves from non-living matter. For centuries people thought maggots grew out of rotting meat and mice out of piles of old grain. In 1668 the Italian doctor Francesco Redi showed that maggots only appeared on meat if flies could reach it. But microbes were so small that the debate about them went on. In 1745 the English naturalist John Needham boiled broths, sealed them and still found them cloudy with life later. In 1768 the Italian priest-scientist Lazzaro Spallanzani boiled broths longer in sealed containers and found nothing grew, but supporters of spontaneous generation said he had simply damaged the 'vital force' in the air.","In 1859 the French naturalist Félix Pouchet published a big book defending spontaneous generation. That same year the French Academy of Sciences announced a prize for anyone who could throw new light on the question with well-conducted experiments. Pasteur decided to settle it.","His most famous experiment used a glass flask with a long neck that he heated and bent into an S-shape, like the neck of a swan. He poured in a nutritious meat broth and boiled it to kill any microbes inside. Then he left the flask open to the air. Air could drift in and out through the curving neck, so nobody could say the broth was cut off from air or any 'vital force'. But dust and microbes floating in the air settled in the low bend of the neck, the way mud settles in the bend of a river. The broth stayed clear for as long as he left it.","Then came the clever second step. When Pasteur tipped the flask so that the broth washed into the dusty bend of the neck and then back down, the broth soon turned cloudy with growing microbes. Same broth, same air, same flask. The only thing that changed was whether microbes from outside could reach it. Life, at least microbial life, came from other life.","Pasteur won the Academy's Alhumbert Prize in 1862. On 7 April 1864 he gave a dramatic lecture at the Sorbonne in Paris, showing his flasks to a fashionable audience. He declared that the doctrine of spontaneous generation would 'never' recover from 'the mortal blow of this simple experiment'. It was not quite the last word, because later the Irish physicist John Tyndall showed that some bacteria form tough spores that can survive ordinary boiling, which explained some confusing results. But the main point stood: microbes do not appear from nowhere.","If microbes spoil wine and broth, Pasteur reasoned, then killing them should stop the spoiling. He found that gently heating wine to a moderate temperature, well below boiling, killed most of the troublesome microbes without ruining the taste. He patented the method in 1865. Today we call it pasteurisation, and it is why the milk in your fridge carries his name in many languages.","In 1865 the French government asked Pasteur to help with a disaster in the south of France. A disease called pébrine was killing silkworms, and the country's silk industry was collapsing. Pasteur had never studied silkworms before. Over several years he worked out a way to check the moths under a microscope and keep only the eggs from healthy ones, which helped bring pébrine under control. A second silkworm disease, flacherie, was harder to stop. The work taught Pasteur something important: tiny living things could cause disease in animals, not just spoil food. In 1868, in the middle of this work, he suffered a stroke that left one side of his body partly paralysed, but he kept working for the rest of his life.","Across the Channel, a surgeon in Glasgow named Joseph Lister read Pasteur's papers on fermentation. In the 1860s, many patients survived an operation only to die later when their wounds became infected. Lister wondered whether microbes from the air and from dirty instruments were getting into wounds. He began cleaning wounds and instruments with carbolic acid, a chemical that kills microbes, and in 1867 reported far fewer deadly infections. This was antiseptic surgery, and it spread around the world. Lister generously credited Pasteur for the idea that started it.","Step by step, a new picture was forming. It is called the germ theory of disease: the idea that many illnesses are caused by microscopic living things, or germs, that invade the body. In the new picture, Semmelweis's 'cadaverous particles' were germs on doctors' hands, Snow's cholera was a germ in water, and spoiled wine and infected wounds were both the work of microbes. But a theory is not the same as proof. To prove that a particular germ caused a particular disease, someone needed better tools. That someone was a country doctor in a small town in what was then eastern Germany."]},{"heading":"Robert Koch: A Country Doctor and His Microscope","paragraphs":["Robert Koch was born in 1843 in Clausthal, a mining town in the Harz Mountains of Germany. After studying medicine and serving as an army surgeon in the Franco-Prussian War, he became a district doctor in Wollstein, a small town that is now Wolsztyn in Poland. He spent his days treating farmers, families and their injuries. But his wife, Emmy, gave him a microscope, and in a corner of his home he set up a little private laboratory.","Around Wollstein, a disease called anthrax was killing sheep and cattle, and it sometimes infected the farmers and wool workers who handled animals. Scientists had already seen rod-shaped bacteria in the blood of animals that died of anthrax, but nobody had proved that the rods caused the disease. Koch set out to do it. He took tiny amounts of infected material and put them into mice, and found that the mice developed anthrax. He grew the bacteria outside the body, in drops of fluid from the eyes of cattle that he watched under the microscope, and he followed them as they multiplied.","Koch's greatest discovery about anthrax was its life cycle. When conditions got harsh, the rod-shaped bacteria formed tiny, tough spores, like seeds in a hard shell. The spores could survive drying out and lie in the soil for years. When they got into a new animal, they turned back into active bacteria. This explained a mystery farmers had known about for generations: some fields seemed cursed, because animals that grazed there kept getting anthrax, year after year. The spores were waiting in the ground.","In 1876 Koch took his results to the botanist Ferdinand Cohn, a professor in the city of Breslau (now Wrocław in Poland). Cohn helped publish Koch's paper that year. For the first time, a specific bacterium had been convincingly linked to a specific disease.","Koch then invented or improved many of the tools that microbiologists still use. He dried thin smears of bacteria onto glass slides and stained them with coloured dyes so they stood out under the microscope. He was also one of the first to photograph bacteria through the microscope. He needed a way to grow pure colonies of one type of bacterium, separate from all the others. He tried slices of potato and then gelatin, but gelatin melts at body temperature. In 1881, Walther Hesse, a member of his team, suggested agar, a jelly made from seaweed. The idea came from Hesse's wife, Fanny Hesse, who used agar to make jellies in her kitchen. Agar stays solid when warm, and it is still used in labs everywhere. A few years later Koch's assistant Julius Richard Petri designed the shallow round glass dish with a lid that we still call a Petri dish.","With these tools, Koch went after one of the biggest killers of his time: tuberculosis, often called TB or consumption, a disease that usually attacks the lungs. In 1882 Koch himself estimated that it caused about one in every seven deaths. The TB germ was hard to see because it is small and grows very slowly. Koch found a special staining method that made the tuberculosis bacteria stand out in blue against a brown background. On 24 March 1882 he presented his evidence to the Physiological Society in Berlin. The young scientist Paul Ehrlich, who was in the audience, later called it his single greatest scientific experience. Today the World Health Organization marks 24 March every year as World Tuberculosis Day.","In 1883 and 1884 Koch travelled to Egypt and India, where he found the comma-shaped bacterium that causes cholera and linked it to contaminated water. Not everything went well: a substance called tuberculin that he hoped would cure TB did not work as a cure. But in 1905 he received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for his work on tuberculosis."]},{"heading":"Koch's Postulates: Rules for Catching a Culprit","paragraphs":["Like detectives building a case for court, Koch and his assistant Friedrich Loeffler wrote down a chain of evidence for germs. They worked them out in 1884, building on ideas from Koch's old teacher Jakob Henle, and Koch refined and published them in 1890. They are known as Koch's postulates. Put simply, they say:","One: the suspected germ should be found in plenty in people or animals with the disease, and not in healthy ones. Two: scientists must be able to take the germ from a sick body and grow it by itself, in a pure culture. Three: when that pure germ is put into a healthy animal, it should cause the same disease. Four: scientists must be able to take the germ back out of that newly sick animal and show it is the same one they started with.","These rules were a huge step forward. They gave scientists a clear standard, so that claims about germs could be tested instead of just argued about. In the twenty or so years after Koch's anthrax work, researchers in Germany, France, Japan and elsewhere identified the bacteria behind many diseases, including diphtheria, typhoid, tetanus and plague. Historians sometimes call this period the golden age of bacteriology.","But Koch himself found that nature did not always follow his rules. Some people carry cholera or typhoid bacteria without becoming ill, so the first postulate does not always hold. Not everyone exposed to a germ gets sick, because some people are immune, which complicates the third rule. And some germs simply refuse to grow in a dish on their own. Koch could never make animals catch cholera in the way people do, so he could not fully complete his postulates for it, even though he had found the right germ. Today scientists use updated methods, including genetic tests, but Koch's postulates are still taught as the starting point for thinking clearly about cause and effect in disease."]},{"heading":"Two Giants, Two Countries, One Big Rivalry","paragraphs":["Pasteur and Koch were the two greatest microbe hunters of their age, and they did not like each other much. Part of the reason was their different styles. Pasteur was a chemist who loved bold experiments and grand public demonstrations. Koch was a physician who cared most about careful methods, pure cultures and photographs. Each thought the other was sloppy in some way.","Part of the reason was history and national pride. In 1870 and 1871, France and the German states fought the Franco-Prussian War. France lost badly, and the German Empire was created. Pasteur was so upset that he sent back an honorary degree he had received from the University of Bonn in Germany. Koch had served as a doctor in the German army during the same war. When the two men argued about science a few years later, many people in France and Germany cheered for their own country's champion.","The two men were polite when they met at a medical congress in London in 1881, but soon quarrelled in print about anthrax, and in 1882 they clashed in public at a congress in Geneva. The rivalry was not always friendly, but it pushed both sides to work faster and check each other's results."]},{"heading":"Smaller Than a Bacterium: The Discovery of Viruses","paragraphs":["By the 1890s scientists had a powerful toolkit for bacteria. A bacterium is a single living cell, usually about one to a few thousandths of a millimetre long. That is tiny, but a good light microscope can show it, especially after staining. Bacteria can grow by themselves in a dish of agar if given the right food and warmth.","Yet for some diseases, nobody could find any bacteria at all. Smallpox, rabies and measles were among them. Something was causing these illnesses, but it seemed to be invisible. The clue that cracked the mystery came not from a human disease, but from a sick plant.","Tobacco mosaic disease makes the leaves of tobacco plants mottled with light and dark patches, like a mosaic. In 1886 the German scientist Adolf Mayer showed that juice from sick leaves could pass the disease to healthy plants. In 1892 the Russian scientist Dmitri Ivanovsky pushed the infected juice through a Chamberland filter. This was a tube of unglazed porcelain invented in 1884 by Charles Chamberland, one of Pasteur's assistants, to clean drinking water. Its pores were so small that bacteria could not get through. Yet the filtered juice could still make healthy plants sick.","In 1898 the Dutch microbiologist Martinus Beijerinck, working in Delft, repeated and extended the experiments. He showed that the infectious agent in the filtered juice could multiply inside living plants, so it was not just a poison that would get weaker as it spread. But it would not grow on its own in a dish the way bacteria did. Beijerinck called it a 'contagium vivum fluidum', Latin for a contagious living fluid, and he used the word virus for it, from a Latin word meaning poison or slimy liquid. In the same year, Friedrich Loeffler, Koch's old colleague, and Paul Frosch showed that foot-and-mouth disease of cattle was also caused by a filterable agent. It was the first animal virus to be found.","Beijerinck was wrong about one thing: viruses are not a fluid. They are particles, far smaller than bacteria. Most are between about 20 and 300 millionths of a millimetre across, too small to be seen with any ordinary light microscope, because light waves are too large to show such fine detail. In 1939, German scientists using the new electron microscope, which uses beams of electrons instead of light, finally photographed tobacco mosaic virus. Tobacco mosaic virus turned out to be a stiff rod about 300 millionths of a millimetre long and 18 millionths wide.","Here is the key difference. A bacterium is a living cell that can feed and divide by itself. A virus is a tiny package of genetic instructions wrapped in a protein coat. It cannot do anything on its own. It can only copy itself by getting inside a living cell and hijacking that cell's machinery. That is why viruses would not grow on agar, and why smallpox and rabies stayed so mysterious for so long. Jenner's cowpox and the smallpox it protected against were viruses, though nobody in Jenner's day had any way to know."]},{"heading":"A Question in Pasteur's Mind","paragraphs":["By the late 1870s the fog of miasma was clearing. Scientists now knew that specific germs cause specific diseases, that germs come from other germs, and that they can be spread by hands, water, wounds and air.","But knowing your enemy is not the same as being able to defend against it. Doctors still had no medicines that could kill bacteria inside the body; antibiotics were more than half a century away. The only weapon that had ever given lasting protection against a deadly disease was vaccination against smallpox, and nobody could yet explain how it worked.","Louis Pasteur, now in his mid-fifties and still partly paralysed from his stroke, had been thinking about Jenner. Cowpox was a mild cousin of smallpox, and catching it protected people against the deadly disease. Now that scientists could grow germs in flasks, Pasteur wondered: could a scientist take a dangerous germ and deliberately weaken it in the laboratory, so that it caused only a mild illness, or none at all, but still taught the body to resist the real thing? If that worked, perhaps there could be a 'cowpox' for every disease."]}],"deepDive":"Historians today warn against telling the germ theory story as a simple fight between clever heroes and foolish believers in bad air. In the mid-1800s, miasma theory was a reasonable explanation for what doctors actually saw: disease really was worse in crowded, dirty, smelly places, and cleaning those places up really did reduce illness. Many physicians also held mixed views, accepting that some diseases were 'contagious' while blaming others on local conditions. What changed minds was not one experiment but a pile-up of evidence from different directions: Snow's maps and statistics, Semmelweis's before-and-after numbers, Pasteur's flasks, Lister's surgical results and, above all, Koch's ability to show a specific microbe, grow it in pure culture, photograph it and reproduce the disease in animals. Semmelweis's data are a classic case study in medical statistics: monthly death rates in the First Clinic fell from about 18% in April 1847 to around 1–2% by mid-summer once chlorinated-lime handwashing began. Yet he lacked a mechanism, published little at first, and antagonised colleagues, which is part of why his findings were rejected; scientists sometimes call the reflex rejection of evidence that contradicts established beliefs the 'Semmelweis reflex'. Pasteur's reputation has also been re-examined. When the historian Gerald Geison studied Pasteur's private laboratory notebooks for a book published in 1995, he argued that Pasteur sometimes gave public accounts that were tidier than what actually happened in his lab, a debate that continues into the next part of this story. Koch's postulates, meanwhile, were enormously useful but never perfect: asymptomatic carriers, organisms that cannot be cultured, and diseases with several causes all break them. Viruses were the biggest exception, since they cannot be grown without living cells. Modern researchers use 'molecular' versions of the postulates, looking for a germ's genes rather than growing the whole organism, and epidemiologists use broader tests of causation such as the Bradford Hill criteria. The discovery of viruses also raised a deep question that is still debated: are viruses alive? They carry genes and evolve, but they cannot grow or copy themselves without hijacking a cell.","funFact":"The agar jelly used in microbiology labs all over the world was suggested to Robert Koch's team because Fanny Hesse already used it in her kitchen to set jellies.","check":{"q":"What did Ignaz Semmelweis ask doctors to do in 1847 to stop new mothers dying of childbed fever?","a":"Wash their hands in a chlorinated lime solution","wrong":["Open all the windows to let out bad air","Boil all the patients' drinking water","Vaccinate the mothers with cowpox"]},"sources":[{"title":"Ignaz Semmelweis","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ignaz_Semmelweis"},{"title":"Miasma theory","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miasma_theory"},{"title":"Spontaneous generation","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spontaneous_generation"},{"title":"Louis Pasteur","publisher":"Science History Institute","url":"https://www.sciencehistory.org/education/scientific-biographies/louis-pasteur/"},{"title":"Robert Koch","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Koch"},{"title":"Koch's postulates","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koch%27s_postulates"},{"title":"World Tuberculosis Day","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/campaigns/world-tb-day"},{"title":"Tobacco mosaic virus","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tobacco_mosaic_virus"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/07-1.jpg","alt":"A swan-necked flask used by Louis Pasteur in his experiments against spontaneous generation.","file":"Swan-necked flask used by Pasteur. Wellcome M0012521.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swan-necked_flask_used_by_Pasteur._Wellcome_M0012521.jpg","author":"Wellcome Collection","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/07-2.jpg","alt":"Portrait photograph of German doctor Robert Koch, who found the germs behind anthrax and tuberculosis.","file":"Robert Koch NLM.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Koch_NLM.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/07-3.jpg","alt":"Rod-shaped tobacco mosaic virus particles seen through an electron microscope. The scale bar is 0.2 micrometres.","file":"TMV virus under magnification.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TMV_virus_under_magnification.jpg","author":"No machine-readable author provided. Chb assumed (based on copyright claims).","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4315},{"n":8,"title":"Pasteur's Weakened Germs","when":"1879 – 1888","summary":"Louis Pasteur's team learned how to weaken germs in the lab so they could protect animals instead of killing them. They proved it in a famous test with sheep, then took on rabies, a disease caused by a germ they could not even see. A brave nine-year-old named Joseph Meister became the first person to receive their rabies treatment.","sections":[{"heading":"Chickens, Cholera and a Forgotten Flask","paragraphs":["Imagine a Paris laboratory in 1879. Glass flasks of cloudy broth line the shelves. In wire cages, chickens cluck and scratch. Louis Pasteur, now in his mid-fifties, walks with a slight limp from the stroke he had eleven years earlier, and he relies on his young assistants, especially Émile Roux, a trained doctor, and Charles Chamberland, a physicist and chemist, to do much of the delicate hands-on work.","The chickens were there because of a disease called chicken cholera, or fowl cholera. Despite its name, it has nothing to do with human cholera. It is caused by a bacterium now called Pasteurella multocida, named in Pasteur's honour. It can sweep through a farmyard and kill birds within hours. In 1879 Pasteur's team obtained the germ and grew it in a broth made from chicken meat. A few drops of fresh culture injected into a healthy hen usually killed it within a day or two.","What happened next has become one of the most famous stories in science, but it should be told carefully, because historians are not sure every detail is true. According to the story, the team left some chicken cholera cultures sitting on the shelf over the summer holidays of 1879. When they came back weeks later, they injected some chickens with the old culture. The chickens became only mildly ill and recovered. Then, the story goes, the same chickens were injected with a fresh, deadly culture, and they did not get sick at all. Chickens that had never had the old culture died as expected.","Whether or not it happened quite like that, the team's published work shows what they learned. A culture of the germ that had been left for a long time, exposed to oxygen in the air, became weaker. It could still trigger the chickens' defences, but it no longer killed them. And once a chicken had survived the weakened germ, it was protected against the strong one. Scientists call this attenuation, from a Latin word meaning to make thin or weak.","Pasteur liked to say that 'in the fields of observation, chance favours only the prepared mind'. He had first said it in a lecture in Lille in 1854. Whether or not chance played a part with the chickens, his team was prepared: they had been thinking for years about Jenner and cowpox, and they knew how to grow germs, how to count deaths and survivals, and how to repeat an experiment to check it.","Pasteur announced his chicken cholera vaccine in 1880. In practice it was not a great success on farms. It was hard to keep the weakened germ at exactly the right strength: sometimes it was too weak to protect, sometimes still strong enough to kill. But as a proof of an idea, it was enormous. For the first time, scientists had taken a germ grown in a laboratory, deliberately weakened it, and used it to protect animals against the disease it caused. Jenner had needed to find cowpox in nature. Pasteur's team had shown that, at least sometimes, you could make your own."]},{"heading":"A New Word to Honour Jenner","paragraphs":["Until this time, the word vaccine had a very narrow meaning. It came from the Latin word 'vacca', meaning cow, and from 'variolae vaccinae', the name Jenner used for cowpox. Vaccination meant one thing only: putting cowpox (or the vaccine virus descended from it) into a person to protect them against smallpox.","Pasteur believed his weakened germs were doing the same job as Jenner's cowpox, just for other diseases. So he decided the old word should grow. In 1881, speaking at an international medical congress in London, he proposed that the words vaccine and vaccination should be used for all such preparations, in honour of Edward Jenner. The idea stuck. Today a vaccine for measles, polio or COVID-19 has nothing to do with cows, but the name still carries a little memory of the milkmaid Sarah Nelmes, the cow called Blossom and the boy James Phipps.","This was more than a nice gesture. By choosing Jenner's word, Pasteur was making a scientific claim: that cowpox vaccination and his weakened germs were examples of one general principle. Give the body a harmless or weakened version of a germ, and it can learn to resist the dangerous version. Nobody yet understood how the body did that learning. But the principle could be tested on one disease after another, and the next test would be one of the most dramatic public experiments in the history of science."]},{"heading":"Anthrax and the Cursed Fields","paragraphs":["In the 1870s and 1880s, anthrax was a serious problem for French farmers. Whole flocks of sheep could die of it, along with cattle, and it sometimes infected the shepherds, butchers and wool workers who handled sick animals. As Robert Koch had shown in 1876, anthrax bacteria form tough spores that can survive in soil for years. Farmers spoke of 'cursed fields' where animals kept falling sick, summer after summer. A vaccine that could protect livestock would be worth a fortune to French farming.","Pasteur's team was not the only group working on one. Jean-Joseph-Henri Toussaint, a young professor at the veterinary school in Toulouse in southern France, had been studying both chicken cholera and anthrax. In 1880 he reported that he could protect animals against anthrax by treating infected blood to weaken or kill the germs, using heat and then carbolic acid, the same germ-killing chemical Joseph Lister used in surgery. His results were exciting but hard for others to repeat reliably, and the Paris scientific establishment, including Pasteur, was sceptical.","Pasteur's own team tried to weaken anthrax germs in a way similar to their chicken cholera method, by growing them in the presence of oxygen at a slightly raised temperature. Behind the scenes, Roux and Chamberland were also experimenting with chemical methods. Anthrax was tricky because of its spores: a weakened culture could form spores that might later turn back into strong germs. The team needed to be sure their vaccine was both safe and powerful enough to protect.","In early 1881, Pasteur announced to the French Academy of Sciences that his team had a working anthrax vaccine. Not everyone believed him. Some vets and doctors thought germ theory was exaggerated, and some were tired of Pasteur's confident pronouncements. A veterinarian from Melun, south-east of Paris, named Hippolyte Rossignol, helped organise a public challenge through a local agricultural society. If Pasteur's vaccine really worked, let him prove it in front of everyone, on a real farm, with real animals. Pasteur accepted. It was a huge gamble. If the trial failed, his reputation, and perhaps the whole idea of laboratory vaccines, could be badly damaged."]},{"heading":"The Showdown at Pouilly-le-Fort","paragraphs":["The trial took place at a farm called Pouilly-le-Fort, near the town of Melun, south-east of Paris, in May and June 1881. The animals were mostly sheep: 50 of them, together with a few cows and at least one goat. The plan was simple and fair, and that is what made it so powerful. Half the animals would be vaccinated. The other half would not. Then all of them would be given a dose of strong, deadly anthrax germs. If the vaccine worked, the vaccinated animals should live and the unvaccinated ones should die.","Scientists today would call the unvaccinated animals a control group. A control group is like a comparison team: it shows what would have happened without the treatment. Without one, if the vaccinated sheep survived, people could argue that the anthrax dose was simply too weak, or that these sheep were unusually tough. With a control group, the answer would be plain for everyone to see.","On 5 May 1881, in front of a crowd of farmers, vets, doctors and officials, Roux and Chamberland gave the first vaccine injection to 25 sheep and some of the other animals. On 17 May the same animals received a second, stronger dose. Then, on 31 May, every animal, vaccinated or not, was injected with a fresh, deadly anthrax culture. Pasteur had to wait two days to learn whether he had triumphed or failed in public.","On 2 June 1881 the crowd returned, larger than ever, with journalists among them. The result was striking. The vaccinated sheep were alive and well. Nearly all the unvaccinated sheep were already dead, and the remaining ones were dying. The vaccinated cattle were healthy, while the unvaccinated cattle had become ill. The news spread through France and then the world. Newspapers celebrated Pasteur, and farmers began asking for the vaccine. Chamberland was put in charge of producing it in large quantities, and over the following years very large numbers of French sheep and cattle were vaccinated.","There is an important twist, though, and it shows why honest record-keeping matters in science. In public, Pasteur suggested that the Pouilly-le-Fort vaccine had been weakened by his oxygen method. More than a century later, the historian Gerald Geison studied Pasteur's private laboratory notebooks, which the Pasteur family had kept closed for many years. For a 1995 book, Geison reported that the vaccine actually used in the trial had been prepared with a chemical, potassium dichromate, a method developed by Chamberland and Roux and closer in spirit to Toussaint's chemical approach. Pasteur had kept this private.","Why would he do that? Historians still argue. Pasteur may have wanted to protect his priority over Toussaint, or to keep a commercial advantage, or he may simply have believed his oxygen method would soon replace the chemical one. Some scientists, such as the Nobel Prize winner Max Perutz, defended Pasteur and argued that Geison was too harsh. What almost everyone agrees on is that the trial was real, the vaccinated animals really did survive, and Pasteur did not give fair credit to Toussaint, who fell ill in 1881 and died in 1890, aged only 43. Today we remember that the anthrax vaccine was a team effort and a contest between rivals, not the work of one man alone."]},{"heading":"Rabies: Chasing a Germ Nobody Could See","paragraphs":["Even while the anthrax work was going on, Pasteur's team had taken on a much more frightening disease: rabies. Its name comes from the Latin word for madness. Rabies is caused by a virus, and it spreads mainly through the bite of an infected animal. In Pasteur's time, as today, dogs were the main source of human infections; worldwide, dogs still cause up to 99% of human cases. An older name for the disease was hydrophobia, meaning fear of water, because in its late stages people find it very hard to swallow.","Rabies has two features that matter enormously for this story. First, once symptoms appear, it is almost always fatal, both in Pasteur's time and today. Second, there is usually a long delay between the bite and the first symptoms, often one to three months, as the virus slowly travels along nerves towards the brain. That delay creates a window of time. Pasteur wondered whether a vaccine given after the bite could train the body's defences fast enough to win the race.","There was a huge problem. Pasteur's team could not see the rabies germ. It was a virus, far too small for their microscopes, and it would not grow in flasks of broth like chicken cholera or anthrax. As you saw in Part 7, nobody even knew yet what a virus was. So the team had to work with something they could not see, could not grow in a dish and could not measure directly.","They solved this with a clever workaround. They knew that rabies attacked the brain and spinal cord, the body's central nervous system. Roux found that infected material placed directly onto the brain of an animal reliably passed the disease on, much more reliably than a bite. By passing the infection from rabbit to rabbit many times, the team produced a strain that behaved in a steady, predictable way in rabbits. The infected rabbits' spinal cords became, in effect, living containers for the invisible germ.","To weaken it, the team took spinal cords from rabbits that had died of rabies and hung them up to dry in sterile glass flasks, along with a chemical that soaked up moisture. The longer a piece of spinal cord dried, the weaker its virus became. A cord dried for about two weeks was almost harmless; a cord dried for only a day or two was still very strong. By grinding up pieces of cord in a liquid, they could make a series of injections, starting with the weakest and moving step by step to the strongest. The idea was to build up the body's protection gradually, the way you might train for a race by running a little farther each day.","Pasteur reported that this method protected dogs. He claimed success in about fifty dogs, although later historians who studied his notebooks found the evidence from dog experiments was thinner than he suggested, especially for treatment given after a bite. Roux in particular felt more animal testing was needed before anyone tried it on a human being. Then, in July 1885, a child arrived at the laboratory door."]},{"heading":"Joseph Meister's Ten Days","paragraphs":["Joseph Meister was nine years old, born on 21 February 1876, and he lived in Alsace, a region on the border of France and Germany that had become part of the German Empire after the war of 1870 to 1871. On 4 July 1885 he was attacked by a dog that was believed to be rabid. He was bitten fourteen times. A local doctor had heard about Pasteur's rabies experiments in Paris and urged Joseph's mother to take him there. They made the long journey by train and arrived at Pasteur's laboratory on 6 July 1885.","Pasteur faced a terrible choice. If he did nothing, and the dog really had rabies, Joseph might well develop the disease and die. If he tried his treatment, it had never been tested on a human, and it might fail or even harm the boy. Pasteur was also not a medical doctor and was not licensed to treat patients. He consulted two respected physicians, Alfred Vulpian and Jacques-Joseph Grancher. They examined Joseph's wounds and agreed that the risk of rabies was high and that the treatment should be tried.","On the evening of 6 July 1885, Grancher, a doctor, gave Joseph the first injection, under the skin. It was made from rabbit spinal cord that had been dried for about two weeks, so its virus was very weak. Over the next ten days Joseph received twelve more injections, thirteen in all, each made from a cord that had dried for a shorter time and so was a little stronger. The last injections used material that was strong enough to give rabies to a rabbit. Pasteur, according to accounts from his family, slept badly through those days, worried about the boy.","Joseph never developed rabies. He went home to Alsace healthy. Because nobody could be completely sure that the dog had been rabid, or that Joseph would have caught rabies without treatment, a single case could not prove that the treatment worked. But it gave Pasteur and his colleagues the confidence to try again. Like Jenner's test on James Phipps, giving an untested treatment to a child would never be allowed in this way today. Modern medicine requires careful animal studies, ethical review and step-by-step clinical trials first.","Joseph Meister kept a lifelong connection with Pasteur's work. As an adult he returned to Paris and became a caretaker at the Pasteur Institute, where he worked for the rest of his life. He died in June 1940, during the sad days when German forces had just occupied Paris in the Second World War."]},{"heading":"A Brave Shepherd and a Flood of Patients","paragraphs":["The second person to receive the treatment was Jean-Baptiste Jupille, a teenage shepherd of about 15 from the village of Villers-Farlay in the Jura region of eastern France, not far from where Pasteur had grown up. In October 1885 a dog that appeared to be rabid ran towards a group of younger children. Jupille stepped in to protect them, and, according to the accounts of the time, wrestled the dog to the ground and bound its jaws with his whip, but he was badly bitten on his hands. He was taken to Paris, treated in October 1885, and survived. Today a statue in the grounds of the Pasteur Institute shows a boy struggling with a dog in memory of his bravery.","Pasteur presented the cases of Meister and Jupille to the French Academy of Sciences in October 1885, and the news flew around the world. Within months, people who had been bitten by animals that might be rabid were arriving in Paris from across Europe and beyond. In 1886 alone, many hundreds of people, and by some counts more than two thousand, were treated in Paris. A group of Russian peasants from the Smolensk region who had been attacked by a rabid wolf made the long journey to Paris for treatment, and most of them survived. The first use of the treatment in the United States followed in 1886.","The treatment did not save everyone. Some patients arrived too late, after the virus had already reached the nervous system. One of the saddest cases was a ten-year-old girl, Louise Pelletier, who was brought to Pasteur more than a month after being bitten on the head. Pasteur knew the chances were poor but agreed to try, and she died. Her case showed that time mattered: the treatment worked best when it started soon after a bite, before symptoms began. Critics also argued about whether the treatment itself could sometimes be dangerous, and these debates pushed scientists to keep improving rabies vaccines.","And they did. Over the next century, the dried-spinal-cord method was replaced by safer vaccines. From the 1960s and later, rabies vaccines grown in cell cultures instead of nerve tissue made treatment much safer and simpler. Even so, rabies remains a serious problem in parts of the world where many dogs are not vaccinated: it still causes tens of thousands of human deaths a year (estimates range from about 44,000 to 59,000), mostly in Asia and Africa, and around four in ten of those who die are children under 15. Vaccinating dogs is one of the most powerful ways communities fight it today."]},{"heading":"A House for Science: The Pasteur Institute","paragraphs":["The rabies treatment made Pasteur one of the most famous people in the world. It also created a practical problem: his small laboratory could not cope with thousands of patients. In 1886 the French Academy of Sciences backed the idea of a special institute for treating rabies and studying infectious diseases. A public appeal for money was launched, and donations arrived from many countries, from emperors and wealthy families to ordinary people giving a few coins. On 4 June 1887 the institute was officially recognised as a charitable foundation.","The Pasteur Institute was inaugurated in Paris on 14 November 1888. It was organised into departments led by some of the best scientists of the time: Émile Duclaux, Charles Chamberland, Jacques-Joseph Grancher, Émile Roux, and the Russian zoologist Élie Metchnikoff, who studied the cells that swallow germs and whom you will meet again later in this story. Roux started the first course in microbiology there, training scientists from many countries.","The institute grew into a worldwide network. In 1891 Albert Calmette, one of Pasteur's younger colleagues, founded the first Pasteur Institute outside France, in Saigon (now Ho Chi Minh City, in Vietnam), to make rabies and smallpox vaccines. By the early 1900s there were dozens of rabies treatment centres around the world. Today the Pasteur Network links institutes in dozens of countries, and ten scientists linked to the Paris institute have won Nobel Prizes.","Louis Pasteur died on 28 September 1895, aged 72. He was buried in a beautiful chapel inside the institute, decorated with mosaics showing his discoveries. His apartment there became a museum. He was a brilliant and stubborn scientist who sometimes kept secrets and did not always share credit fairly, and who also changed medicine forever. His greatest legacy was not just a few vaccines, but an idea and a method: find the germ, grow it, weaken it, test it fairly, and protect people and animals before disease strikes.","Meanwhile, scientists at the new institute were already following a different clue. In 1888, Émile Roux and his colleague Alexandre Yersin found that the bacterium that causes diphtheria, a deadly childhood throat disease, harms the body by giving off a poison. Even broth that had been filtered so that no bacteria remained could still make animals sick. Some germs, it turned out, were dangerous mainly because of the toxins they released. And if the toxin was the real enemy, perhaps scientists could find a way to fight the poison itself."]}],"deepDive":"Pasteur's vaccine work rested on one powerful idea, attenuation: a germ can be weakened so it no longer causes serious disease but still triggers protection. His team tried several routes. For chicken cholera they relied on ageing cultures exposed to oxygen; for anthrax the trial vaccine was, according to Pasteur's private notebooks, prepared with potassium dichromate; for rabies they dried infected rabbit spinal cords for different lengths of time. Modern scientists would say the drying both weakened and partly destroyed the virus, so early injections contained little or no active virus and later ones contained more. Because nobody could see or count the rabies virus, doses were set by the age of the dried cord, an ingenious but imprecise method. The historian Gerald Geison's 1995 book 'The Private Science of Louis Pasteur', based on the notebooks, sparked a lasting debate. Geison argued that Pasteur's public accounts of Pouilly-le-Fort and of his rabies dog experiments were tidier and more confident than his lab records justified, and that Pasteur gave Joseph Meister an untested treatment after much less animal evidence than he implied. Defenders, including the Nobel laureate Max Perutz, replied that Pasteur's results were real and that scientists have always simplified their stories in public. Both sides agree on the basic facts: the Pouilly-le-Fort vaccinated sheep survived while nearly all the controls died, and Meister did not develop rabies. A single case cannot prove a treatment works, since not every bite from a rabid animal causes rabies, which is why statistics from thousands of treated patients in 1886 and after mattered so much. The anthrax story also raises questions about credit: Henry Toussaint published a chemically treated anthrax vaccine first, and Roux and Chamberland did much of the hands-on work. Rabies post-exposure treatment still follows Pasteur's basic logic of racing the virus during its long incubation, but today it uses much safer cell-culture vaccines, often with rabies antibodies given as well. With tens of thousands of human deaths a year, rabies remains a reminder that having a vaccine is not enough: it must also reach the people, and the dogs, who need it.","funFact":"Joseph Meister, the first person to receive Pasteur's rabies treatment, later worked as a caretaker at the Pasteur Institute for the rest of his life.","check":{"q":"In the 1881 anthrax trial at Pouilly-le-Fort, what happened to the sheep that had NOT been vaccinated?","a":"Nearly all of them died","wrong":["They all survived without getting ill","They caught cowpox instead","They were given rabies treatment and recovered"]},"sources":[{"title":"Louis Pasteur","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Pasteur"},{"title":"Rabies fact sheet","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/rabies"},{"title":"Anthrax vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthrax_vaccine"},{"title":"Jean Joseph Henri Toussaint","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_Joseph_Henri_Toussaint"},{"title":"Rabies vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabies_vaccine"},{"title":"Rabies","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabies"},{"title":"History of the Institut Pasteur","publisher":"Institut Pasteur","url":"https://www.pasteur.fr/en/institut-pasteur/history"},{"title":"Émile Roux","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89mile_Roux"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/08-1.jpg","alt":"Albert Edelfelt's 1885 painting of Louis Pasteur in his laboratory, studying a glass flask.","file":"Albert Edelfelt - Louis Pasteur - 1885.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albert_Edelfelt_-_Louis_Pasteur_-_1885.jpg","author":"Albert Edelfelt","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/08-2.jpg","alt":"In 1913, grown-up Jean-Baptiste Jupille, now an Institut Pasteur guard, stands beside the statue of his boyhood fight with a rabid dog.","file":"(15-11-13) Jupille et sa statue à l'Institut Pasteur (deuxième vacciné antirabique) - btv1b6927778z.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(15-11-13)_Jupille_et_sa_statue_%C3%A0_l%27Institut_Pasteur_(deuxi%C3%A8me_vaccin%C3%A9_antirabique)_-_btv1b6927778z.jpg","author":"Agence Rol. Agence photographique (commanditaire)","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/08-3.jpg","alt":"The original Institut Pasteur building in Paris, now the Pasteur Museum, with a bust of Pasteur in front.","file":"Musée Pasteur.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mus%C3%A9e_Pasteur.JPG","author":"Ab2804","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":3793},{"n":9,"title":"Antitoxins, Toxoids and a New Wave of Vaccines","when":"1890 – 1940s","summary":"Some germs hurt us by making poisons. Scientists found that the blood of protected animals could stop those poisons, and brave sled dogs raced across frozen Alaska to deliver it. Soon there were new vaccines against diphtheria, tetanus, whooping cough, typhoid, yellow fever and more.","sections":[{"heading":"The Disease That Built a Wall in the Throat","paragraphs":["Imagine a doctor in 1890 being called to a farmhouse at night. A five-year-old has a sore throat, a fever and a strange, harsh, barking breath. When the doctor shines a lamp into the child's mouth, there is a greyish-white layer stuck across the back of the throat, like a skin that should not be there. Parents in the 1800s knew that sign and feared it. It meant diphtheria.","Diphtheria is caused by a bacterium with a long name, Corynebacterium diphtheriae. It spreads from person to person through coughs, sneezes and close contact. The bacteria settle in the nose and throat, but the real damage does not come from the bacteria themselves. It comes from a poison, called a toxin, that they release. The toxin kills nearby cells, and the dead cells, along with fibres from the blood, form that tough grey layer doctors call a membrane. In bad cases the membrane spreads over the tonsils and the voice box and can block the airway completely, which is why the illness was so often deadly for small children with narrow throats.","The toxin can also travel through the blood to other parts of the body. There it can inflame the heart muscle, damage nerves (sometimes causing temporary paralysis) and harm the kidneys. Even with modern hospital care, about 5 to 10 people in every 100 who get serious diphtheria die. Before there were any treatments, the rate was much higher, especially among young children.","The disease was not new. The Greek physician Hippocrates described something like it about 2,400 years ago. In 1826 the French doctor Pierre Bretonneau gave it its modern name, from a Greek word meaning leather or hide, because of that leathery membrane. Then, in the age of germ theory you met in Part 7, scientists hunted down the cause. Edwin Klebs spotted the bacterium in throat membranes in the early 1880s, and in 1884 Friedrich Loeffler, who worked with Robert Koch, grew it in the laboratory.","The most important clue came in 1888 at Pasteur's institute in Paris. Émile Roux and Alexandre Yersin grew diphtheria bacteria in a broth, then strained the broth through a filter fine enough to remove every single bacterium. When they injected the clear, germ-free liquid into animals, the animals still became sick and died. Something the bacteria had made, and left behind in the liquid, was doing the harm. That something was the toxin.","This discovery changed the question. If a disease is caused by a poison, maybe you do not have to kill the germs at all. Maybe you only need to block the poison."]},{"heading":"Blood That Fights Back: Behring and Kitasato","paragraphs":["In 1890 two young scientists were working in Robert Koch's institute in Berlin. One was Emil von Behring, a German army doctor born in 1854 in a small village in what was then Prussia. The other was Kitasato Shibasaburō, a Japanese physician who had travelled to Berlin in 1885 to learn from Koch. Kitasato had already done something many experts thought impossible: in 1889 he became the first person to grow the tetanus bacterium as a pure culture, with no other microbes mixed in. Tetanus, sometimes called lockjaw, is another disease where a bacterial toxin does the damage, in this case by making muscles clench uncontrollably.","Behring and Kitasato tried an experiment. They gave animals small, carefully controlled amounts of tetanus or diphtheria toxin, or of weakened bacteria, so the animals got sick but survived. Then they took some blood from these survivors and separated out the serum, the clear, yellowish liquid left behind when blood clots. When they injected this serum into other animals, those animals were protected against the toxin. In some cases, serum even helped animals that were already sick.","Something in the survivors' blood was neutralising, or cancelling out, the poison. They called it antitoxin, meaning against the toxin. Today we know antitoxins are a kind of antibody, special proteins that lock onto a particular target. You will meet antibodies properly in the next part.","To make enough antitoxin for people, scientists needed big animals with lots of blood. Horses were ideal. A horse could be given gradually increasing doses of toxin over weeks, building up a powerful supply of antitoxin in its blood, and then small amounts of its blood could be collected again and again without harming it. Soon stables of horses at research institutes and companies were working as living antitoxin factories.","Getting the strength right was tricky, because one batch of serum could be far stronger than another. Paul Ehrlich, another scientist in Koch's circle, worked out how to measure and standardise it, so that a dose meant the same thing everywhere.","Successful treatment of children with diphtheria antitoxin began in 1894, and the results were dramatic. In hospitals that started using it, the share of diphtheria patients who died fell sharply. Newspapers called Behring the saviour of children. In 1901 he received the very first Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, for his work on serum therapy and especially its use against diphtheria. Kitasato, who had been part of the key work and had been nominated, did not share the prize, something many people see as unfair. Kitasato went home to Japan, founded an institute for infectious diseases, helped investigate an outbreak of plague in Hong Kong in 1894, and later founded the Kitasato Institute. Since 2024 his portrait has appeared on Japan's 1,000 yen banknote.","Here is an important point that is easy to mix up. Antitoxin is not a vaccine. A vaccine trains your own immune system to make its own protection, which can last a long time. Antitoxin is borrowed protection: ready-made antibodies from another animal, given as a treatment to someone who is already sick or at high risk. It works quickly, but it does not teach the body anything, and it fades away within weeks. Scientists call this kind of borrowed protection passive immunity, and the self-made kind active immunity.","Borrowed protection also brought a lesson about safety. In 1901, in St. Louis in the United States, diphtheria antitoxin was made from a horse that had caught tetanus, and the contaminated serum caused the deaths of thirteen children. The same year, contaminated smallpox vaccine was linked to the deaths of nine children in Camden, New Jersey. These tragedies led the US Congress to pass the Biologics Control Act in 1902. For the first time, makers of vaccines and antitoxins had to be licensed, had to label their products properly, and could be inspected without warning. It was an early step toward the careful safety rules that vaccines must meet today."]},{"heading":"The Great Race of Mercy","paragraphs":["Nome is a town on the western coast of Alaska, just below the Arctic Circle. In January 1925 it had about 1,400 people, and in winter it was almost completely cut off. Ships could not reach it because the sea froze, and there were no roads or railway into town. The only doctor, Curtis Welch, worked at a small hospital there. Late in 1924 and early in 1925 he began seeing children with sore throats. Soon he recognised the grey membrane of diphtheria. He had a terrible problem: the town's supply of antitoxin had expired, and the new order had not arrived before the port froze.","Welch sent urgent telegraph messages asking for help. Health officials found about 300,000 units of antitoxin in a hospital in Anchorage. It was packed carefully, wrapped against the cold and sent north by train to the town of Nenana, where the railway ended. That still left about 1,085 kilometres (674 miles) of frozen wilderness between Nenana and Nome. Aeroplanes of the time were open-cockpit and unreliable in deep cold, so officials decided on the method Alaskans already trusted for carrying mail: dog sleds.","The relay began on 27 January 1925. About 20 mushers, the people who drive dog sleds, and roughly 150 dogs took part. Each team carried the package for its stretch and then handed it to the next. Temperatures dropped to around minus 50 degrees Celsius (minus 60 Fahrenheit) in places, and on the open ice the wind made it feel even colder. At each handover, the cylinder of antitoxin was warmed by a stove for a while so it would not freeze solid and spoil.","The hardest and longest part of the run belonged to Leonhard Seppala, a Norwegian-born musher famous across Alaska, and his lead dog Togo, who was about 12 years old. Seppala's team covered roughly 420 kilometres (about 260 miles) in total, far more than anyone else, including a dangerous crossing of the frozen Norton Sound, where the ice can crack and drift out to sea. The final leg into Nome went to Gunnar Kaasen, with a lead dog named Balto. Kaasen drove through a blizzard so fierce he said he could hardly see the dogs in front of him, and the team arrived in Nome at about 5:30 in the morning on 2 February 1925.","The whole relay took about five and a half days, roughly 127 and a half hours. The usual mail route took much longer. Dr Welch used the antitoxin, more supplies followed, and the outbreak was brought under control. The official count of diphtheria deaths in Nome was small, between five and seven, though Welch believed there were many more cases in villages around the town that were never properly recorded.","The story made headlines around the world. Balto became a celebrity, toured the United States, and by December 1925 a bronze statue of him stood in New York's Central Park, where children still climb on it today. Seppala always felt that Togo was the true hero because his team had done the longest and most dangerous stretch, and many historians agree. Togo was later honoured too, including in a 2019 film.","Remember what the dogs were carrying. It was not a vaccine. It was antitoxin, a treatment for children who were already sick or had been exposed. The serum run showed both how powerful antitoxin was and its weakness: someone always had to rush it to an outbreak after the fact. The dream was something better, a way to protect children before the disease ever arrived."]},{"heading":"Taming the Poison: Gaston Ramon's Toxoids","paragraphs":["If a toxin causes diphtheria, could the body be taught to recognise the toxin before it ever met the real thing? The danger was obvious. You cannot simply inject a child with a deadly poison.","The answer came from a French veterinarian named Gaston Ramon. Born in 1886 in the village of Bellechaume, he trained at the veterinary school at Alfort near Paris and went to work at the Pasteur Institute, where one of his jobs was helping produce antitoxin from horses. He even married into the Pasteur family of scientists: his wife, Marthe Momont, was a grandniece of Émile Roux.","In the early 1920s Ramon found that if he treated diphtheria toxin with a small amount of formaldehyde (a chemical often used to preserve specimens) and kept it gently warm for several weeks, the poison lost its power to harm. Yet its shape stayed similar enough that the immune system still recognised it. He called the result an anatoxine. In English it is called a toxoid, meaning toxin-like. A toxoid is a bit like a wanted poster of a criminal: it shows the immune system exactly what to look for, but the poster itself cannot hurt anyone.","When a child was given toxoid, their immune system made its own antitoxin, its own antibodies against the diphtheria toxin, and kept the ability to make more. If real diphtheria bacteria later started releasing toxin in that child's throat, the body was ready. That is active immunity, the self-made kind, and it lasts much longer than borrowed serum. Ramon also invented a practical test to measure how strong each batch of toxin or toxoid was, which made factory production far more reliable.","Ramon and his colleague P. Descombey used the same trick on tetanus toxin, and a tetanus toxoid followed in the mid-1920s. In Britain, Alexander Glenny and his team discovered in 1926 that adding a little aluminium salt to diphtheria toxoid made it work better, causing a stronger immune response. Ingredients that boost the immune response in this way are called adjuvants, and aluminium salts are still used as adjuvants in some vaccines today.","Toxoid vaccines did not take over overnight. Diphtheria toxoid was developed in the early 1920s but was not widely used in the United States until the early 1930s, and mass programmes for all children came later still. But the effect, once they did, was enormous. In the United States, diphtheria cases fell from the 100,000 to 200,000 a year of the 1920s to about 19,000 in 1945, and then fell much further after vaccinating all children became routine in the late 1940s. A more effective tetanus toxoid, introduced in 1938, was given to soldiers during the Second World War, and tetanus became rare among troops whose wounds were often caked with soil, where tetanus bacteria live.","There was a lesson in all this that would matter for the rest of vaccine history. To make a vaccine, you do not always need the whole germ. Sometimes one key piece, the right molecule, shown to the immune system in a safe form, is enough."]},{"heading":"Killed Germs on the March: Typhoid, Cholera, Plague and TB","paragraphs":["While some scientists tamed toxins, others went after diseases where the whole germ seemed to matter. A popular approach was to grow huge numbers of bacteria and then kill them, often with heat or chemicals, before injecting them. Dead bacteria cannot multiply, but their surfaces still carry the shapes the immune system learns to recognise.","Typhoid fever, caused by Salmonella Typhi bacteria in contaminated water and food, was a terrible killer of soldiers. In army camps, more men often died of disease than of battle wounds. In 1896, the British doctor Almroth Wright developed a typhoid vaccine made of killed bacteria, and in Germany Richard Pfeiffer and Wilhelm Kolle worked on similar vaccines at the same time, so the credit is shared. He watched many British soldiers die of typhoid during the Boer War in South Africa (1899 to 1902) and argued fiercely for vaccinating troops. During the First World War, typhoid vaccination of soldiers became widespread, and he set up a research laboratory at a military hospital in Boulogne, France. One of the young scientists who worked in his department at St Mary's Hospital in London was Alexander Fleming, the future discoverer of penicillin.","The person who made cholera vaccination work on a large scale was Waldemar Haffkine. Born in Odessa (then in the Russian Empire) in 1860, he studied under Élie Metchnikoff, whom you will meet in the next part, and joined the Pasteur Institute in Paris in 1889. In 1892 he developed a cholera vaccine and, to show it was safe, tested it on himself first. Then he went to India, where cholera killed huge numbers of people. From 1893 he and his helpers vaccinated tens of thousands of people around Calcutta (now Kolkata) and elsewhere, often travelling to villages and tea plantations himself. Studies of vaccinated and unvaccinated groups suggested the vaccine gave real protection.","In 1896 bubonic plague, a bacterial disease spread mainly by fleas on rats, broke out in Bombay (now Mumbai). The Indian government asked Haffkine to help. He worked long hours in a makeshift laboratory, and by early 1897 he had a plague vaccine made from killed plague bacteria. Again, he tested it on himself before giving it to others. Joseph Lister, the famous surgeon, called him a saviour of humanity.","Haffkine's story also contains a tragedy. In 1902, in the village of Mulkowal in Punjab, 19 people who had received plague vaccine from one bottle died of tetanus. An official inquiry blamed Haffkine's laboratory, and he was removed from his post. Later investigations, including one by the Lister Institute in London, concluded the contamination had most likely happened after the vaccine left his laboratory, and he was cleared. In 1925 the laboratory he founded in Bombay was renamed the Haffkine Institute, and it still exists today.","Tuberculosis, or TB, the disease Koch had tracked down in 1882, needed a different approach. In 1908, at the Pasteur Institute in Lille, France, Albert Calmette and Camille Guérin started with a bacterium that causes TB in cattle and grew it again and again on a special food made from potato, glycerin and ox bile. Every few weeks they moved a little of the culture to fresh food. They kept this going right through the First World War, even when the city was occupied and supplies were short. After 239 transfers over 13 years, the bacterium had become so weakened that it no longer caused disease in animals, but it still trained the immune system. They called it Bacille Calmette-Guérin, or BCG.","BCG was first given to a human baby in Paris in 1921. Around 1930, in the German city of Lübeck, a terrible mistake happened: babies were given BCG that had accidentally been mixed with a dangerous, fully active TB strain stored in the same laboratory, and 72 of them died. The cause was contamination, not the BCG itself, and the disaster led to stricter controls. BCG is still used today in many countries, mostly for babies, because it protects young children especially well against the most severe forms of TB, although its protection against TB of the lungs in adults varies a lot from place to place."]},{"heading":"Two Scientists and a Whooping Cough","paragraphs":["Whooping cough, also called pertussis, is caused by a bacterium called Bordetella pertussis. It spreads very easily through the air. It starts like a cold, then turns into fits of coughing so violent and long that a child can barely breathe between coughs. When they finally gasp in air, it can make a high-pitched whooping sound, which gives the disease its name. Very young babies may not whoop at all, but they are the most at risk. In the United States, whooping cough killed thousands of people a year at its worst, almost all of them children, and more than 200,000 cases a year were reported before vaccines came into wide use.","There had been pertussis vaccines before the 1930s, but they were made in different ways and nobody had proved convincingly that they worked. Two women in Grand Rapids, Michigan, set out to change that. Pearl Kendrick, born in 1890 in Illinois, ran the state health department's laboratory in Grand Rapids. Grace Eldering, born in 1900 in Montana, joined her there in 1932. They worked alongside a laboratory scientist named Loney Clinton Gordon, an African American chemist and bacteriologist who later helped them find a better strain of the bacteria to use.","Kendrick and Eldering began by making the diagnosis more reliable. They used a cough plate, a dish of jelly-like growth food that a sick child coughed onto, so the bacteria could be grown and identified. They collected samples from coughing children around Grand Rapids, and they saw first-hand how the disease exhausted families. Their day jobs were full, so much of the vaccine work happened after hours.","Their vaccine used whole pertussis bacteria that had been killed. What made their work stand out was the testing. Between 1934 and 1937 they ran a trial involving more than 5,800 children. They compared vaccinated children with similar children who were not vaccinated, and they kept careful records of who caught whooping cough. Doing it properly meant thinking about fairness: the two groups had to be alike in age and living conditions so the comparison was honest. The results showed that the vaccinated children got whooping cough much less often, and when they did, it tended to be milder.","Money was always short. Eleanor Roosevelt, wife of the US president, took an interest and helped them get funding through the Works Progress Administration, a government programme of the Great Depression. By the late 1930s Michigan was producing the vaccine in large amounts and sending it out across the state and beyond, and whooping cough deaths fell. Kendrick and Eldering later helped develop a combined injection against diphtheria, tetanus and pertussis together, known as DTP, which became available in 1948 and meant children needed fewer shots."]},{"heading":"Eggs, Mosquitoes and the First Flu Shots","paragraphs":["Bacteria can be grown in broth or on jelly, but viruses are much harder. As Part 7 explained, a virus can only copy itself inside living cells. To make a vaccine against a virus, you need a supply of living cells to grow it in. For a long time that meant using whole animals, which was slow, expensive and messy.","In 1931 at Vanderbilt University in Nashville, Tennessee, the pathologist Ernest Goodpasture, working with Alice Miles Woodruff, found a neat solution: the fertilised chicken egg. By cutting a tiny window in the shell, they could place virus onto the thin, blood-rich membranes surrounding the developing chick inside. The virus grew in those living cells. Eggs were cheap, clean, sealed in their own sterile container, and available by the thousand. Within a few years scientists were growing many different viruses this way.","One of the first big successes involved yellow fever, a virus spread by mosquitoes that can damage the liver and turn the skin yellow, which gives the disease its name. It had killed tens of thousands of people in tropical America and Africa and had helped defeat the first, French attempt to build the Panama Canal. Max Theiler, a scientist born in Pretoria, South Africa, in 1899, worked on yellow fever at Harvard and then at the Rockefeller Foundation in New York. The research was dangerous: Theiler himself caught yellow fever in the lab, survived, and was immune afterwards.","Theiler's team took a strain of yellow fever virus called Asibi, named after a man in Ghana from whom it had been collected in 1927, and grew it again and again in tissue from chicken embryos, more than a hundred times in a row. Like Calmette and Guérin with BCG, they were hoping the virus would slowly change and become weaker. By 1937 they had a weakened strain, called 17D, that did not cause serious illness but trained the immune system strongly. Between 1940 and 1947 the Rockefeller Foundation made more than 28 million doses. In 1951 Theiler received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, and he is often described as the first African-born Nobel laureate in that field. The 17D vaccine is still used today, made in eggs.","Influenza, the flu, was next. In 1918 and 1919 a flu pandemic had killed tens of millions of people around the world, and the flu virus itself was first isolated from humans only in 1933. Using egg-grown virus, researchers including Thomas Francis Jr. and his young colleague Jonas Salk developed inactivated flu vaccines made from killed virus. The US military, remembering how flu had swept through army camps in 1918, began using flu vaccines on soldiers during the Second World War, and the vaccine became more widely available to the public from 1945."]},{"heading":"What Was Going On Inside?","paragraphs":["Step back and look at the toolbox scientists had built by the 1940s. There was borrowed antitoxin from horses for emergencies. There were toxoids, poisons made harmless, against diphtheria and tetanus. There were killed whole-germ vaccines against typhoid, cholera, plague and whooping cough. There were live but weakened germs, BCG and yellow fever 17D. Fertilised eggs were turning into vaccine factories.","Why does a body that has met a harmless toxoid recognise the real toxin years later? Why do some vaccines need several doses while others seem to work after one? Where does antitoxin come from, and which cells make it? Why does protection against some diseases last a lifetime, while protection against others fades?","Those questions had been argued about since the 1880s by two rival camps of scientists, one convinced that cells were the key, the other convinced it was all about substances floating in the blood. As it turned out, both camps were partly right. In the next part we zoom in, past the skin, past the blood, all the way down to single cells, to find out what is going on inside the body when a vaccine works."]}],"deepDive":"Historians of medicine often treat 1890 to 1945 as the period when immunisation stopped being a single trick (smallpox vaccination) and became a family of methods. Two ideas drove this. The first was the distinction between passive and active immunity. Behring and Kitasato's antitoxin was passive: antibodies made in a horse and transferred to a patient. It acted within hours but faded within weeks, and because it was a foreign animal protein it could trigger reactions called serum sickness. Toxoids produced active immunity, in which the recipient's own immune system makes antibodies and keeps the capacity to make more. The second idea was that a vaccine does not have to be the whole germ. Ramon's formaldehyde toxoids showed that a single molecule, chemically altered so it is harmless but still recognisable, could protect. That logic leads directly to today's subunit and protein vaccines. The era also forced scientists to think about evidence. Early vaccine claims often rested on comparisons that were not fair: people who chose to be vaccinated might be richer, healthier or better fed than those who did not. Kendrick and Eldering's Grand Rapids study (1934 to 1937) is admired because it tried to compare similar groups of children and recorded results systematically, an early step toward the controlled trials described in Part 12. Haffkine's Indian studies also compared vaccinated and unvaccinated groups, though historians debate how rigorous they were by modern standards. Safety problems shaped regulation. The 1901 St. Louis and Camden deaths led to the US Biologics Control Act of 1902; the Mulkowal tetanus deaths of 1902 and the Lübeck BCG disaster around 1930 showed that contamination during manufacture or handling could be catastrophic, pushing laboratories toward sterile technique, batch testing and record-keeping. Credit is another debate. Behring won the 1901 Nobel Prize, but Kitasato's role in the antitoxin work was central and Ehrlich's standardisation made the serum usable; typhoid vaccine credit is shared between Wright in Britain and Pfeiffer and Kolle in Germany; Ramon was nominated 155 times without winning. Many historians now stress that these vaccines came from teams and institutions, not lone geniuses. Finally, many of these vaccines were imperfect. Early cholera and typhoid vaccines gave partial, short-lived protection and frequent side effects, and BCG's effect on adult lung TB still varies widely between regions. They were improved, replaced or combined over the following decades.","funFact":"The tough grey layer that diphtheria builds in the throat gave the disease its name, from a Greek word meaning leather or hide.","check":{"q":"What were the sled dog teams of the 1925 Great Race of Mercy carrying to Nome, Alaska?","a":"Diphtheria antitoxin, a treatment","wrong":["A new diphtheria vaccine","Smallpox vaccine for the whole town","Penicillin to kill the bacteria"]},"sources":[{"title":"1925 serum run to Nome","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1925_serum_run_to_Nome"},{"title":"Diphtheria (Pink Book, Chapter 7)","publisher":"US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/pinkbook/hcp/table-of-contents/chapter-7-diphtheria.html"},{"title":"Pertussis (Pink Book, Chapter 16)","publisher":"US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/pinkbook/hcp/table-of-contents/chapter-16-pertussis.html"},{"title":"Emil von Behring","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emil_von_Behring"},{"title":"Kitasato Shibasaburō","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kitasato_Shibasabur%C5%8D"},{"title":"Waldemar Haffkine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waldemar_Haffkine"},{"title":"Max Theiler","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Max_Theiler"},{"title":"Grace Eldering","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grace_Eldering"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/09-1.jpg","alt":"Musher Gunnar Kaasen with Balto, the lead dog of the last team in the 1925 serum run to Nome.","file":"Gunnar Kaasen with Balto.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gunnar_Kaasen_with_Balto.jpg","author":"Brown Brothers","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/09-2.jpg","alt":"Emil von Behring, discoverer of diphtheria antitoxin, in his Marburg study on his 60th birthday in 1914.","file":"Emil von Behring im März 1914. Fotografie von Waldemar Titzenthaler.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emil_von_Behring_im_M%C3%A4rz_1914._Fotografie_von_Waldemar_Titzenthaler.jpg","author":"Waldemar Franz Hermann Titzenthaler","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/09-3.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Pearl Kendrick, who developed the whooping cough vaccine with Grace Eldering.","file":"Pearl Kendrick. Photograph. Wellcome V0027621.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pearl_Kendrick._Photograph._Wellcome_V0027621.jpg","author":"Wellcome Collection","license":"CC BY 4.0"}],"words":4404},{"n":10,"title":"Your Body's Defence Force, Part 1","when":"1880s – today (science)","summary":"Your body is like a castle with walls, guards and alarm bells. Skin, tears and stomach acid keep most germs out, and hungry cells called phagocytes gobble up the ones that sneak in. A scientist discovered these germ-eating cells by poking thorns into see-through baby starfish!","sections":[{"heading":"The Castle Walls","paragraphs":["Right now, as you read this sentence, you are under attack. Bacteria, viruses and fungal spores float in the air you breathe, sit on the book or screen you are touching, and ride along on the food you eat. Most of them are harmless, and many are even helpful. But some would happily move in and multiply if they could. The reason you are not sick all the time is your immune system, the body's defence network of cells, tissues and proteins that fights germs.","In the last few parts you watched scientists invent vaccines mostly by trial and error. In this part and the next, we look at the science they were missing: what is actually happening inside the body. A helpful way to picture it is a medieval castle. There are outer walls, there are guards who attack any intruder on sight, there are alarm bells, and, as you will see in Part 11, there are highly trained special forces who remember every enemy they have ever met.","The first line of defence is simply keeping germs out. Your biggest wall is your skin. The outer layer is made of flat, tough, dead cells stacked like roof tiles, packed with a protein called keratin, the same material as your fingernails. Most germs cannot get through unbroken skin. The skin is also fairly dry and slightly acidic, and its oils contain substances that slow the growth of many bacteria. Old skin cells are constantly flaking off, carrying germs away with them. That is why a cut or scrape matters: it is a hole in the castle wall.","Inside your nose, throat and lungs, the walls work differently. These surfaces are lined with mucus, a sticky liquid that traps dust and microbes like flypaper. In your airways, millions of tiny hair-like structures called cilia beat back and forth in waves, pushing the mucus upwards like a slow conveyor belt so it can be swallowed or coughed out. Sneezing and coughing are part of the same cleaning system, blasting unwanted particles back out of the body.","Your eyes are protected by tears, which wash them constantly each time you blink. Tears, saliva and the mucus in your nose contain an enzyme called lysozyme that can break down the walls of some bacteria. It was discovered in 1922 by Alexander Fleming, the same scientist who later found penicillin, reportedly after a drop of his own nasal mucus fell onto a dish of bacteria and dissolved some of them.","If germs are swallowed, they meet one of the harshest places in the body: the stomach. Stomach acid is strong enough to kill most microbes that arrive with food. Further along, the gut is home to trillions of friendly bacteria. Simply by living there and using up space and food, they make it harder for harmful bacteria to settle in. Even going to the toilet helps: the flow of urine flushes germs out of the urinary tract.","These barriers are not clever. They do not know one germ from another. But they stop the vast majority of invaders before any fighting begins. The question is what happens to the germs that do get through, through a splinter wound, a scraped knee or a breath of air carrying a virus. For a long time nobody knew. The answer began with a man staring through a microscope at a baby starfish."]},{"heading":"A Thorn in Messina","paragraphs":["Élie Metchnikoff, whose Russian name was Ilya Mechnikov, was born in 1845 in the village of Ivanovka, in what is now Ukraine. He was a zoologist, a scientist who studies animals, and he was especially interested in how simple sea creatures develop and digest their food. He was brilliant, restless and emotional, and he had already lived through hard times, including the death of his first wife from tuberculosis.","In 1882 he moved with his family to Messina, a port city on the island of Sicily in southern Italy, and set up a small private laboratory at home to study sea creatures. Among them were the larvae, or babies, of starfish. Starfish larvae are tiny and almost completely transparent, so under a microscope you can see right through them and watch individual cells moving about inside.","Metchnikoff noticed that some cells inside the larvae wandered freely, crawling about rather than staying put. He had seen cells like this swallowing bits of food in other simple animals, and one day, according to his own later account, a bold idea struck him: perhaps these wandering cells did not only digest food. Perhaps they also defended the animal against intruders.","To test it, he needed an intruder. He took a few small thorns from a plant in the garden next to his home and pushed them into some of the starfish larvae. Many retellings say they were rose thorns, but according to the usual accounts of his own story they were citrus thorns, from a little tangerine tree the family had decorated as a Christmas tree. He was so excited that he could hardly sleep that night. The next morning he looked again, and the thorns were surrounded by a crowd of the wandering cells, which had gathered around the invader as if trying to engulf it.","Metchnikoff realised that this was the same thing that happens when you get a splinter. The area around it becomes red and swollen, and pus forms. He suggested that pus is made largely of the body's own defender cells, which rush to the wound to attack the germs. He went on to watch cells swallow and digest bacteria in other animals too.","A professor named Carl Claus suggested a name for these cells: phagocytes, from Greek words meaning eating cells. The process of swallowing a germ is called phagocytosis. Metchnikoff argued that phagocytes are the body's front-line defence against infection.","Not everyone believed him. Many leading scientists thought immunity came from substances dissolved in the blood, such as the antitoxins Behring and Kitasato would describe in 1890. To them, phagocytes were just garbage collectors, cleaning up after the real battle. Metchnikoff spent years doing experiment after experiment to prove his case. In 1888 Louis Pasteur invited him to work at the new Pasteur Institute in Paris, where he stayed for the rest of his life. Waldemar Haffkine, the cholera vaccine pioneer from Part 9, had been one of his students back in Odessa. Today Metchnikoff is often called the father of innate immunity."]},{"heading":"The Eaters: How Phagocytes Work","paragraphs":["Phagocytes are a kind of white blood cell. Your blood looks red because it is crowded with red blood cells that carry oxygen, but mixed in among them are white blood cells, the body's mobile defenders. A healthy adult has roughly 4,000 to 11,000 white blood cells in every microlitre of blood, a drop smaller than a pinhead. Most of them are made in the bone marrow, the soft tissue inside your bones, and your body makes them in enormous numbers. Counting all types, a healthy adult makes roughly 100 billion new white blood cells every single day, many of them short-lived phagocytes that are constantly replaced.","The most common white blood cells are neutrophils, which make up about half to two thirds of the white cells in your blood. They are the castle's quick-reaction guards. When tissue is damaged or invaded, neutrophils can begin arriving within minutes. Each one is about 10 micrometres across, roughly a seventh of the width of a human hair. They do not live long, usually only a day or a few days, which is one reason the body has to keep making so many.","How does a neutrophil find a germ? It follows a trail of chemicals. Bacteria release certain molecules, and damaged cells and other immune cells release signals too. Phagocytes can sense which direction these chemicals are coming from and crawl towards the strongest smell, a bit like a shark following a trace of blood in the water. This is called chemotaxis. To leave the bloodstream, neutrophils grab onto the walls of tiny blood vessels near the trouble, roll along them, and squeeze out between the cells of the vessel wall into the tissue.","When a phagocyte reaches a bacterium, it grabs it with receptors on its surface, molecules that work a little like hands. Then the cell stretches its outer edge around the bacterium until the germ is completely enclosed in a bubble inside the cell. That bubble merges with other bubbles full of digestive chemicals, and the germ is broken apart. A neutrophil takes about nine minutes on average to swallow a bacterium.","Phagocytes also use chemical weapons. When a neutrophil swallows something, it suddenly uses much more oxygen and turns some of it into highly reactive molecules that damage microbes. One enzyme in neutrophils even makes hypochlorous acid, a chemical closely related to the active ingredient in household bleach. In some situations neutrophils can also throw out sticky, web-like nets made of their own DNA, which trap and kill bacteria outside the cell. When an infected wound fills with pus, much of that pus is made of neutrophils that have done their job and died.","Neutrophils have bigger, slower partners. Monocytes travel in the blood and, when they move into tissues, grow into macrophages, a word meaning big eaters. Macrophages are about twice as wide as neutrophils, live much longer, and stay on patrol in places like the lungs, liver and skin. They eat germs, old and worn-out cells and debris. Another type, called dendritic cells, after their long finger-like branches, swallow germs too, but their most important job is to carry pieces of those germs to the adaptive immune system, the special forces you will meet in the next part. They are the messengers between the two halves of your defence force."]},{"heading":"Alarm Bells: Inflammation and Fever","paragraphs":["Think about what happens when you get a splinter in your finger. Within hours the skin around it may turn red, feel warm, swell up and hurt. That is inflammation, and although it is uncomfortable, it is a sign that your defence force has sounded the alarm.","The alarm starts with guard cells already sitting in the tissue, such as macrophages and mast cells. They carry special sensors, called pattern recognition receptors, that can detect molecules found on many kinds of germs but not on our own cells, such as pieces of bacterial cell walls. These sensors do not identify an exact germ. They simply recognise that something germ-like is there. One important family of these sensors, called Toll-like receptors, was first discovered in fruit flies, and work on how they help start immune responses was honoured with the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 2011.","Once the alarm is raised, the guard cells release chemical messages. Mast cells, for example, release histamine, which makes nearby blood vessels wider and leakier. More blood flows to the area, which is why it looks red and feels warm. Fluid leaks out of the vessels into the tissue, which causes swelling. The leakiness also lets white blood cells and helpful proteins from the blood pour into the battle zone. The pain is partly caused by chemicals that sensitise nerves, and it encourages you to protect the injured spot while it heals.","Some of the helpful proteins that arrive belong to a group called the complement system. These are dozens of different proteins that float in the blood in an inactive state. When they meet a germ, they set off a chain reaction, each protein switching on the next like a row of falling dominoes. The end result can coat the germ so phagocytes grab it more easily, call in more white blood cells, or even punch holes in the outer surface of some bacteria so they burst.","There are other innate defenders too. Natural killer cells, a type of white blood cell, patrol the body looking for our own cells that have become infected by viruses or have turned cancerous. Infected cells often look a bit wrong on the outside, and natural killer cells can recognise that and order those cells to self-destruct, sacrificing them before the virus inside can spread further. Infected cells can also release warning molecules called interferons, which tell neighbouring cells to raise their defences against viruses.","Sometimes the alarm spreads to the whole body, and you get a fever. Your body temperature is controlled by a small region at the base of the brain called the hypothalamus, which works like a thermostat. During an infection, immune cells release messenger molecules that travel to the brain and turn the thermostat up. The body then acts as though it is too cold. Blood vessels in the skin narrow to keep heat in, and muscles start shivering to make heat, which is why people with a rising fever often feel chilly even though their temperature is going up.","Scientists think fever evolved because it can help. Many germs grow best at normal body temperature, and a higher temperature can slow some of them down. Warmth also seems to make some immune cells work faster. Evidence for this comes even from animals that cannot make their own heat: some grasshoppers move to warmer places when infected by a fungus, raising their body temperature by a few degrees, and honeybee colonies can warm their hive in response to a fungal infection of their young. Fever still makes people feel unwell, and how to manage it is something for doctors and trusted adults, not a science book."]},{"heading":"Paul Ehrlich and the Side Chains","paragraphs":["While Metchnikoff was championing cells, many other scientists, especially in Germany, believed the most important defence was something dissolved in the blood. The discovery of antitoxins in 1890 seemed to prove their point. Historians sometimes call this the argument between the cellular school (cells do the fighting) and the humoral school, from an old word for body fluids (substances in the blood do the fighting). The leading thinker on the blood side was Paul Ehrlich.","Ehrlich was born in 1854 in Strehlen, in the Prussian province of Silesia (today Strzelin in Poland). As a student he became fascinated by dyes. The German chemical industry was producing bright new artificial dyes, and Ehrlich discovered that different dyes stuck to different parts of cells. Using them, he could tell apart types of white blood cells that looked identical without stain. While still a student, he discovered mast cells, the histamine-releasing alarm cells you met above.","As you saw in Part 9, Ehrlich helped make diphtheria antitoxin practical by working out how to measure its strength. Thinking about how antitoxin could find and grab a toxin, he came up with a bold idea, published in detail in 1897, called the side-chain theory. He imagined that cells carry many different molecular side chains on their surface, each shaped to grab a particular substance, rather like keys sticking out of a lock. When a toxin attached to its matching side chain, the cell would respond by making many extra copies of that side chain and releasing the surplus into the blood. Those free-floating side chains would be the antitoxins, ready to catch more toxin before it reached the cells.","Not all of the details turned out to be right, but the central idea was remarkably close to the truth. Antibodies really do have specific shapes that fit specific targets, and they really are made by cells and released into the blood. Ehrlich also helped popularise the German word Antikörper, which became antibody in English, in his writings in the early 1890s.","Ehrlich dreamed of what he called a magic bullet, a chemical that would hit only a germ and leave the body unharmed, just as an antibody hits only its own target. In 1909, he and his Japanese colleague Sahachiro Hata found a compound, number 606 in their long list of tested chemicals, that could kill the bacterium that causes syphilis. Sold as Salvarsan from 1910, it was one of the first modern drugs designed to target a germ.","So who won the argument, the cells or the blood? In 1908 the Nobel committee gave its answer: both. The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine was shared by Metchnikoff and Ehrlich, in recognition of their work on immunity. Around the same time, the British scientist Almroth Wright, the typhoid vaccine pioneer from Part 9, showed that substances in the blood, which he called opsonins, coat bacteria and make them much easier for phagocytes to swallow. The two kinds of defence were not rivals at all. They were teammates."]},{"heading":"Name Tags and Y-Shaped Keys","paragraphs":["To understand vaccines, two words matter more than almost any others: antigen and antibody.","An antigen is any molecule that the immune system can recognise. Very often it is a protein or sugar on the outside of a germ, such as a spike on the surface of a virus or a molecule in a bacterium's outer coat. Toxins, like the diphtheria toxin from Part 9, can be antigens too. You can think of antigens as name tags. Each kind of germ wears its own particular set of name tags, and the immune system learns to read them. The word antigen was originally short for antibody generator, because antigens are what cause antibodies to be made. A germ's name tag is often large, and an antibody actually grips just one small patch of it, called an epitope.","An antibody is a protein made by the immune system that locks onto one specific antigen. Antibodies are shaped like the letter Y. Each one is built from four protein chains: two longer ones called heavy chains and two shorter ones called light chains, joined together by chemical bonds. An antibody is incredibly small, only about 10 nanometres across, which is roughly ten thousand times thinner than a human hair.","The two arms of the Y are where the magic happens. The tip of each arm has a shape that fits one particular antigen, a bit like a key that fits only one lock, or a glove that fits only one hand. Because both arms are the same, one antibody can grab two copies of its antigen at once. The stem of the Y does a different job: it acts like a handle that other parts of the immune system, such as phagocytes and complement proteins, can recognise and grab.","Once antibodies lock on, they protect the body in several ways. They can neutralise a germ or toxin by covering the parts it needs to attack cells, just as Behring's antitoxin did. They can clump germs together, since each antibody has two arms, making them easier to clean up. They can coat a germ like a label saying eat me, so phagocytes swallow it faster, which is exactly what Almroth Wright's opsonins were. And they can switch on the complement system to punch holes in bacteria.","Humans make five main classes of antibody, each with its own speciality. IgG is the most common in the blood and gives most long-term protection; it is also the only class that can cross from a mother to her unborn baby, giving newborns borrowed protection for their first months. IgA guards the wet surfaces of the body and is found in tears, saliva and breast milk. IgM is usually the first type made when the body meets a new germ. IgE is involved in allergies, and IgD helps certain immune cells sense their targets. The letters Ig stand for immunoglobulin, the scientific name for antibodies.","Perhaps the most astonishing fact is how many different antibodies the body can make. Estimates suggest a person can produce on the order of billions of different antibody shapes, enough to match almost any antigen, including germs that have never existed before. Working out the detailed structure of antibodies earned Rodney Porter and Gerald Edelman the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1972. But which cells make all those antibodies, and how does the body pick the right one? That belongs to the special forces of the next part."]},{"heading":"Why the Fast Team Is Not Always Enough","paragraphs":["Everything in this part, the barriers, phagocytes, inflammation, complement, natural killer cells and fever, belongs to what scientists call innate immunity. Innate means something you are born with. It is fast: it starts working within minutes to hours of an infection. It is also general: it recognises broad patterns that many germs share, so it can respond to almost anything. Some form of innate immunity is found in nearly all living things, from insects to plants, which use their own chemical alarm signals to fight off disease.","Most of the time, innate immunity wins. The vast majority of germs that get past your barriers are cleaned up by phagocytes and complement before you ever notice. But innate immunity has some important weaknesses.","First, it does not learn. The tenth time a neutrophil meets a particular type of bacterium, it responds in roughly the same way as the first time. It has no memory of past battles, so it cannot get better at fighting a particular enemy.","Second, some germs have evolved tricks to dodge it. Some bacteria grow thick, slippery outer coats called capsules that phagocytes find hard to grip. Some can survive inside phagocytes after being swallowed; the bacterium that causes tuberculosis, for example, can live for a long time inside macrophages. Viruses hide inside our own cells, where phagocytes cannot see them, and some viruses can switch off the warning signals that infected cells send out.","Third, some germs are simply too fast or too dangerous. A germ that multiplies extremely quickly, or that releases a powerful toxin, can cause serious harm before the general defences get it under control. The diphtheria toxin from Part 9 is a good example: phagocytes cannot stop a poison that is already spreading through the blood. What stops it is an antibody shaped to fit that one toxin exactly.","And that is the key. The antitoxins, the protection that survivors of smallpox had, and the immunity that vaccines produce are not mainly the work of the fast, general guards. They are specific: protection against one particular germ or toxin. They improve with experience. And they can last for years, sometimes a lifetime. That points to a second, slower, smarter part of the immune system, one with the ability to remember.","In the next part we meet these special forces: the B cells that make antibodies, the T cells that coordinate the defence and destroy infected cells, and the memory cells that explain why surviving a disease, or receiving a vaccine, can protect you for years. It is the part of the story that finally explains what Lady Mary, Onesimus, Jenner and Pasteur had all been relying on without knowing it."]}],"deepDive":"The cellular-versus-humoral debate of the 1880s to 1900s is a classic case of a scientific argument in which both sides were partly right. Metchnikoff's phagocytes are central to innate immunity, while the antitoxins of Behring, Kitasato and Ehrlich turned out to be antibodies, products of the adaptive system. The two systems are tightly linked: antibodies and complement act as opsonins that make phagocytosis far more efficient, and dendritic cells and macrophages present antigens that switch on adaptive responses. For decades innate immunity was treated as a crude first line of defence. That changed in the 1990s. In 1989 the immunologist Charles Janeway proposed that innate cells must carry receptors for conserved microbial patterns, and that these receptors also help switch on adaptive immunity. In 1996 Jules Hoffmann's group showed that fruit flies lacking a gene called Toll could not fight fungal infections, and in 1998 Bruce Beutler identified a related receptor in mice (TLR4) that detects lipopolysaccharide, a component of the outer membrane of many bacteria. Beutler and Hoffmann shared the 2011 Nobel Prize with Ralph Steinman, who discovered dendritic cells in 1973. Humans have about ten Toll-like receptors, each sensing a different class of microbial molecule, such as bacterial cell-wall pieces, flagellin, or viral genetic material. This matters for vaccines. Many vaccine adjuvants work, at least in part, by stimulating innate sensors, and modern mRNA vaccines use chemically modified building blocks partly to avoid triggering excessive innate inflammation, as Part 18 explains. Numbers in immunology are often given only roughly because they vary between people and over time. Neutrophils alone are produced at about 100 billion per day in a healthy adult, and a typical microlitre of blood contains about 4,000 to 11,000 white cells. Estimates of the number of distinct antibodies a person can make range from billions upwards, depending on how they are counted. There is also active research on whether innate immunity has a kind of short-term memory, sometimes called trained immunity, in which an earlier challenge (BCG vaccination is a much-studied example) changes how innate cells respond to later, unrelated infections. This is an area of ongoing study rather than settled fact.","funFact":"Élie Metchnikoff discovered the body's germ-eating cells in 1882 by pushing tiny thorns into transparent starfish larvae and watching wandering cells swarm around them.","check":{"q":"What shape is an antibody?","a":"Like the letter Y","wrong":["A perfect sphere","A long spiral like a spring","A flat ring like a doughnut"]},"sources":[{"title":"Élie Metchnikoff","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89lie_Metchnikoff"},{"title":"Phagocyte","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phagocyte"},{"title":"Paul Ehrlich","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Ehrlich"},{"title":"Antibody","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antibody"},{"title":"Innate immune system","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Innate_immune_system"},{"title":"Immune system explained","publisher":"Better Health Channel (Victoria State Government, Australia)","url":"https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/conditionsandtreatments/immune-system"},{"title":"Neutrophil","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neutrophil"},{"title":"Fever","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fever"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/10-1.jpg","alt":"Élie Metchnikoff, discoverer of phagocytes, at his microscope in his laboratory.","file":"Dr Metchnikoff in his Laboratory.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dr_Metchnikoff_in_his_Laboratory.jpg","author":"Maurice Branger (1874–1950) of Branger studio, 5 rue Cambon, Paris","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/10-2.jpg","alt":"Electron microscope image, coloured: a neutrophil white blood cell (yellow) engulfing rod-shaped anthrax bacteria (orange).","file":"Neutrophil with anthrax copy.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neutrophil_with_anthrax_copy.jpg","author":"Volker Brinkmann","license":"CC BY 2.5"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/10-3.jpg","alt":"Ribbon model of a Y-shaped antibody: two light chains (green, pink) and two heavy chains (blue, yellow).","file":"Antibody IgG1 structure.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antibody_IgG1_structure.png","author":"Tokenzero","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":4125},{"n":11,"title":"Your Body's Defence Force, Part 2: Memory","when":"1950s – today (science)","summary":"Deep inside you lives a secret army of special cells that can remember every germ they have ever beaten. The next time that germ shows up, they spring into action super fast. Vaccines work by giving this army a safe practice run, like showing them a wanted poster.","sections":[{"heading":"The Special Forces Arrive","paragraphs":["In the last part we met the body's first responders: the skin and mucus that block the door, and the hungry phagocytes that rush to swallow any intruder they can catch. These defenders are fast and brave, but they have one big weakness. They fight every germ in roughly the same way, a bit like guards who chase anyone wearing a mask without checking who is underneath. Against a truly dangerous germ, that is sometimes not enough.","So the body has a second team. Scientists call it the adaptive immune system, because it adapts, or changes, to fit each new enemy it meets. It is slower to get going than the first responders, but it is far more precise, and it has a superpower the first team does not: it can remember. Once it has beaten a particular germ, it can keep a record of that germ for years, sometimes for a whole lifetime.","The soldiers of this special force are two kinds of white blood cell called lymphocytes (say it LIM-fo-sites): B cells and T cells. Under a microscope, a resting B cell and a resting T cell look almost identical, small round cells with a big nucleus taking up most of the space. Yet they have very different jobs. Your body contains roughly 2 trillion lymphocytes, which is about 250 for every person alive on Earth, all packed inside one human body.","Both kinds begin life in the bone marrow, the soft, spongy tissue in the middle of your big bones. B cells grow up there. Young T cells, though, travel to a small organ called the thymus, which sits in your chest just behind the breastbone, and finish their training there. That is where the T in T cell comes from. The B was originally named after an organ in birds called the bursa of Fabricius, where these cells were first studied, but it is handy to remember B for bone marrow.","Here is a surprise: only about 2 in every 100 lymphocytes are floating in your blood at any moment. Most of them are on patrol through your tissues and through a separate network of thin tubes called the lymphatic system. Along these tubes sit hundreds of small, bean-shaped lymph nodes, plus the spleen, which works like a giant filter. Lymph nodes are the meeting halls of the immune system. If you have ever had swollen glands in your neck during a sore throat, you have felt your lymph nodes busily at work, packed with cells that have arrived for an emergency meeting.","Who calls the meeting? Some of the first-response cells, especially ones called dendritic cells, act as messengers. When they find an invader in your skin or lungs, they grab bits of it, then travel through the lymphatic tubes to the nearest lymph node. There they hold up those bits, the antigens or 'name tags' you met in part 10, for the lymphocytes to inspect. It is like a scout running back to camp with a torn flag from the enemy, shouting: does anyone recognise this?"]},{"heading":"B Cells: The Antibody Factories","paragraphs":["Every B cell carries thousands of copies of one particular antibody stuck on its surface like antennae. These surface antibodies act as the cell's receptors, its way of feeling the world. Each B cell's receptors fit only one shape of antigen, the way one key fits only one lock. A B cell whose receptors fit a measles protein will ignore a flu virus completely, and the other way round.","That raises an obvious puzzle. There are countless different germs in the world, and they keep changing. How could the body possibly have the right key ready for each one? The answer is one of the cleverest tricks in biology. Instead of storing one complete gene for every possible antibody, which would be impossible, young B cells build their antibody genes by shuffling and joining together small gene pieces, a bit like making a huge number of different words from a small set of letter tiles. Extra random changes are added at the joins. Thanks to this shuffling, a human can make more than a trillion different antibodies, even though the whole human genome contains fewer than 25,000 genes. The Japanese scientist Susumu Tonegawa won the 1987 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for showing how this gene shuffling happens.","So at any moment, your body already holds a gigantic library of B cells, each with a different key, most of them waiting for a lock they may never meet. When one of them finally does meet its matching antigen, and usually after getting a 'go' signal from a helper T cell (more about those soon), it wakes up.","An activated B cell can turn into a plasma cell, which is basically an antibody factory. A plasma cell stops wearing its antibodies as antennae and instead pours them out into the blood and body fluids, thousands of molecules every second. Many plasma cells only live a few days, working flat out until they wear themselves out, but some settle in the bone marrow and keep making antibodies for a very long time.","What do all those antibodies actually do? Remember they are Y-shaped proteins. The two tips of the Y grab the antigen. If an antibody grabs the part of a virus that the virus uses to unlock our cells, the virus is jammed and cannot get in. This is called neutralising the virus. Antibodies can also grab bacterial toxins, the poisons you read about in part 9, and stop them working. And because the stem of the Y sticks out, antibodies act like bright flags waving above a germ, making it much easier for phagocytes to find and swallow it. Some antibodies also switch on a set of blood proteins called complement, which can punch holes in bacteria."]},{"heading":"T Cells: Commanders and Killers","paragraphs":["Antibodies are brilliant at catching germs floating outside our cells. But viruses have a sneaky habit: they slip inside our cells and use them as factories to make more viruses. Once a virus is inside, antibodies in the blood cannot reach it. This is where T cells come in.","T cells have receptors too, and each T cell, like each B cell, has its own unique receptor shape. But T cells cannot see a whole germ floating past. Instead they inspect little pieces of protein that our own cells display on their surface in special holders called MHC molecules. You can think of MHC molecules as tiny shop windows. Almost every cell in your body regularly takes small samples of the proteins being made inside it, chops them into fragments, and puts them on display in these windows. Most of the time the display just says, in effect, all normal here. But if a virus has taken over the cell, fragments of virus protein appear in the window too.","There are two main kinds of T cell. Helper T cells are the commanders. They are often called CD4 cells, after a marker protein on their surface. When a helper T cell recognises an antigen shown to it by a dendritic cell in a lymph node, it begins sending out chemical messages called cytokines. These messages switch on the right B cells, tell phagocytes to fight harder, and help killer T cells to multiply. Helper T cells cannot kill anything themselves, but without them much of the adaptive immune system would barely get going. That is why a virus such as HIV, which attacks helper T cells, can weaken the whole immune system so badly.","Killer T cells, also called cytotoxic T cells or CD8 cells, are the specialists who deal with infected cells. A killer T cell slides along other cells, checking their windows. When it finds one displaying the exact virus fragment its receptor fits, it locks on and releases proteins with names like perforin and granzymes. Perforin makes holes in the infected cell's outer membrane, and granzymes go in and tell the cell to shut itself down in an orderly way. It sounds harsh, but it is a sacrifice for the good of the whole body: destroying one infected cell destroys the virus factory inside it before it can make thousands more viruses.","There is one more important safety rule. While young T cells are training in the thymus, any that react strongly to the body's own normal proteins are mostly removed. The same kind of check happens for young B cells in the bone marrow. This teaches the immune system to leave 'self' alone and attack only 'non-self'. When this training goes wrong, the immune system can attack the body's own tissues, which is what happens in autoimmune diseases such as type 1 diabetes."]},{"heading":"The Beetle Collector and the Big Idea","paragraphs":["By the 1950s, scientists knew that the body made antibodies that fitted germs remarkably well. But how? One popular idea was that an antibody was a kind of soft protein that folded itself around a germ like clay pressed around a key, taking its shape. It was a neat idea, but it could not explain everything, especially why the body seemed to remember germs for so long.","In 1955 the Danish scientist Niels Jerne suggested something different: maybe the body already contains a huge variety of antibodies before any germ arrives, and the germ simply picks out the ones that fit. In 1957 the American scientist David Talmage added an important twist: perhaps the selection happens to cells, not just to free-floating antibodies. If each antibody-making cell produced just one type of antibody, the germ could select the right cells, and those cells would multiply.","That same year, the Australian scientist Frank Macfarlane Burnet, known to friends as Mac, pulled these ideas together and gave the theory its name: the clonal selection theory. Burnet was born in 1899 in Traralgon, in the state of Victoria. As a boy he was a passionate beetle collector, and he kept collecting beetles as an adult. Perhaps all those hours sorting tiny differences between insects helped train his eye for patterns in nature. He spent most of his career at the Walter and Eliza Hall Institute in Melbourne, which he directed from 1944 to 1965. Earlier, he had become an expert at growing viruses in hens' eggs, work that helped lay foundations for how flu vaccines are still made today.","Burnet's theory said, in simple words: each lymphocyte carries just one kind of receptor. When a germ arrives, only the lymphocytes whose receptors fit it are switched on. Those chosen cells then copy themselves over and over, making a big family of identical cells, called a clone, all with the same receptor. Some of the clone fight the infection right away, and others stay behind for the long term. And lymphocytes that react against the body's own molecules are removed early in life.","Imagine a vast library holding millions of different keys, one key per hook. A burglar arrives with a strange new lock. The guards try keys until one fits. Then, instead of using just that one key, they rush it to a key-cutting machine and make thousands of copies, handing them out to an army. That is clonal selection. The 'selection' is the choosing of the right cell, and the 'clonal' part is the copying.","The first direct evidence came quickly. In 1958 Gustav Nossal, a young scientist working with Burnet in Melbourne, and the American Joshua Lederberg showed that a single B cell makes only one type of antibody, exactly as the theory predicted. Over the following decades, experiment after experiment supported the idea, and clonal selection became a cornerstone of modern immunology.","In 1960 Burnet shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with the British scientist Peter Medawar. Their prize was for discovering acquired immunological tolerance: the way the body learns, early in life, which things count as 'self' and should not be attacked. Burnet had proposed the idea, and Medawar's team proved it with experiments on young mice. That same year, Burnet became the very first Australian of the Year."]},{"heading":"First Time Slow, Second Time Fast","paragraphs":["Now we can solve the oldest mystery in this whole story, the one people noticed thousands of years ago: why do smallpox survivors almost never catch smallpox again?","Think about what happens the very first time a new germ gets into your body. Out of all those millions of different lymphocytes, only a tiny handful happen to have receptors that fit this particular germ. Those few cells must first be found, often by a dendritic cell carrying antigens to a lymph node. Then they need permission signals from helper T cells. Then they must divide again and again to build up a big enough army, and B cells must turn into plasma cells and start making antibodies. All of that takes time. Scientists call this the primary response. It usually takes about four to seven days before it becomes strong, and typically one to two weeks to build up fully. Meanwhile the germ is multiplying too, which is why you can feel quite ill during a first infection.","But when the battle is won, something important happens. Most of the extra fighting cells die off, because the body does not need a giant army hanging around forever. A smaller group stays behind. These are memory cells, both memory B cells and memory T cells. They are long-lived, they are already the right shape for this germ, and there are many more of them than there were of the original matching cells.","If the same germ ever comes back, the memory cells spot it quickly, often before it has had the chance to spread far. They multiply faster and need less help to get started. Memory B cells also tend to produce antibodies that grip more tightly, because during the first infection the B cells went through a kind of improvement contest. Small random changes were made to their antibody genes, and the cells whose antibodies fitted best were chosen to survive. This is called the secondary response, and it is faster, bigger and stronger than the first one. Often it is so fast that the germ is defeated before the person even notices any illness at all.","If you could draw a graph of antibody levels, the first infection would show a slow, low hill that rises after a week or so. The second meeting with the same germ would show a steep, tall mountain that rises within days. That difference between the low hill and the tall mountain is the whole secret of immune memory.","How long can memory last? It depends on the germ and on the person. Memory cells can survive for decades. One remarkable example was reported in 2008: scientists tested the blood of elderly Americans who had lived through the great influenza pandemic of 1918, about 90 years earlier, and found their immune systems could still make antibodies that recognised that 1918 virus. For some germs, though, memory fades more quickly, or the germ itself changes, which is why the story does not end here."]},{"heading":"A Safe Rehearsal: How a Vaccine Works","paragraphs":["Now put the pieces together. If a second meeting with a germ is so much safer than the first, what if your first meeting could happen without the danger? That is exactly what a vaccine does.","A vaccine shows your immune system something that looks like the germ, or part of the germ, but cannot cause the disease. It might be a weakened germ, a killed germ, a harmless piece of the germ's outer coat, a disarmed toxin, or a set of instructions that lets your own cells briefly make one harmless germ protein. You will meet all these kinds in the next part. Whatever form it takes, it carries the germ's antigens, its name tags.","Your immune system treats these antigens much as it would treat the real thing. Dendritic cells pick them up and carry them to the lymph nodes. The matching B cells and T cells are found, switched on and copied. Antibodies are made. And, most importantly, memory cells are left behind. It is like a fire drill: no real fire, nobody in danger, but everyone learns where the exits are, so if a real fire ever starts, they know exactly what to do.","Another way to picture it is a wanted poster. Imagine a town that pins up posters showing the face of a notorious thief. The thief is not in town yet, and the poster itself cannot steal anything. But if the thief ever walks down the main street, people will recognise him at once and call the sheriff. Jenner's cowpox was a kind of wanted poster for smallpox, because the two viruses look alike enough to the immune system that memory cells trained on one recognise the other.","Because a vaccine has to trigger the same slow primary response, protection does not appear instantly. It usually builds up over one to two weeks after a dose, and sometimes longer. It is also important to be honest: no vaccine protects every single person perfectly. Some people's immune systems respond less strongly, and some germs are harder to remember than others. Vaccines greatly lower the chances of getting ill, and especially of getting very ill, but they are not a magic shield. Sometimes the immune system's work causes a sore arm, tiredness or a mild fever for a day or two, which are signs of the training under way. Serious side effects are rare, and part 17 looks at how scientists track them."]},{"heading":"Reminders and Changing Faces","paragraphs":["If memory is so good, why do some vaccines need two, three or even more doses? There are several reasons, and they make sense once you know how the immune system works.","First, for some vaccines, a single meeting with the antigens is not enough to build a strong, long-lasting memory. A second or third dose, given weeks or months later, works like a revision lesson before a test: the memory cells made by the first dose respond, multiply and get better at their job, leaving a bigger and longer-lasting memory behind. That is why the World Health Organization notes that some vaccines are given weeks or months apart, to help long-lived antibodies and memory cells develop. Babies' immune systems are also still young, so they often need several doses in their first months of life.","Second, for some diseases, memory slowly fades over the years. Memory cells survive but divide only slowly, so over time their numbers can drop. A later dose called a booster tops them back up. Tetanus is a well-known example. In the United States, adults are advised to have a tetanus booster every 10 years, while the United Kingdom uses a different schedule with boosters early in life and none routinely after that. Other vaccines seem to leave very long-lasting memory: studies of the hepatitis B vaccine have found that immune memory can last at least 30 years, even after antibody levels in the blood fall too low to measure.","Third, and trickiest of all, some germs change their faces. Influenza, the flu, is the champion of disguise. Its genes copy themselves with lots of small mistakes, and over time the proteins on its surface slowly change shape, a process called antigenic drift. After a year or two, the flu virus going around may look different enough that memory cells trained on an older version do not recognise it as well. It is as if the thief on the wanted poster had grown a beard and dyed his hair.","To keep up, flu vaccines are updated every year. Around the world, 152 national influenza centres in more than 129 countries test flu viruses all year round, as part of the WHO's Global Influenza Surveillance and Response System. They send samples to WHO collaborating centres in Atlanta, London, Melbourne, Tokyo and Beijing. Twice a year, experts meet to decide which flu strains the next vaccine should target: in February for the northern half of the world, whose flu season is in its winter, and in September for the southern half. It is a scientific forecast, a bit like predicting next season's weather, and like weather forecasts it is not always perfect. That is one reason flu vaccines are usually less effective than, say, the measles vaccine, with protection against flu illness often estimated at somewhere between about 30 and 60 percent, and sometimes lower, varying a lot from year to year.","The measles virus, by contrast, hardly changes its outer coat at all, so the memory made by measles vaccines stays useful. Two doses of measles vaccine are about 97 percent effective at preventing the disease."]},{"heading":"Safety in Numbers: Herd Immunity","paragraphs":["So far we have looked inside one body. But germs that spread from person to person also live in a bigger world: a town, a school, a whole country. Here memory has one more amazing effect.","Scientists describe how fast a disease spreads using a number called R0, said 'R nought'. It means how many other people, on average, one sick person would infect in a group where nobody is immune. Seasonal flu has an R0 of a little over one. Smallpox is estimated at around 3.5 to 6. Polio is about 5 to 7. Measles is one of the most contagious diseases known, with an R0 of about 12 to 18. The WHO notes that one person with measles can infect up to 18 others, and that the measles virus can stay active in the air or on surfaces for up to two hours after a sick person has left a room.","Now imagine a germ trying to spread through a school. Each time it reaches someone who is immune, its path is blocked, like a fire reaching a patch of ground that has already been cleared. If enough people are immune, each sick person passes the germ on to fewer than one other person on average, and the outbreak fizzles out. This is called herd immunity, or community immunity. The phrase was used as long ago as 1894 by the American veterinary scientist Daniel Elmer Salmon to describe resistance in herds of animals, and by the 1920s doctors were applying it to human diseases.","There is a neat piece of maths behind it. If one case would normally cause R0 new cases, then the fraction of people who need to be immune to stop the spread is roughly 1 minus 1 divided by R0. For a germ with an R0 of 2, that is 1 minus a half, so about half the population. For measles, with an R0 of up to 18, it is about 1 minus one-eighteenth, which is around 94 percent. Because no vaccine works perfectly in everyone, public health experts often say that about 95 percent of people need to be immune to stop measles spreading. The Oxford Vaccine Group puts it simply: 19 out of every 20 people.","Herd immunity matters most for the people who cannot be protected directly. Newborn babies are too young for some vaccines. Some children and adults have immune systems weakened by illness or by strong medicines, such as chemotherapy for cancer, and cannot safely receive certain vaccines, or do not respond well to them. When the people around them are immune, the germ has a hard time reaching them. In a real sense, immune memory inside millions of separate bodies adds up to a shield around the whole community.","There are limits, though. Herd immunity only works for diseases that spread from person to person. Tetanus, for example, comes from bacteria living in soil, not from other people, so it gives no community protection at all. And an average can hide gaps. If immunity is high across a country but low in one town or school, a contagious disease such as measles can still break out there, as happened around Swansea, in Wales, in 2012 and 2013.","So the recipe for a vaccine is surprisingly simple to say: show the immune system a safe wanted poster, and let memory do the rest. But that raises a fascinating question. If vaccines just need to show the body a wanted poster, how many different ways are there to make one?"]}],"deepDive":"The clonal selection theory solved a problem that had puzzled immunologists for decades: how a finite body can respond specifically to an almost unlimited variety of foreign molecules, including artificial chemicals that never existed in nature. Earlier 'instructive' theories, such as the template idea associated with Linus Pauling in 1940, proposed that antigens physically moulded antibodies into a complementary shape. Instructive models struggled to explain immune memory, the faster secondary response, and tolerance to self. Burnet's 1957 paper, building on Niels Jerne's 1955 natural-selection theory and David Talmage's cellular version, argued instead that diversity exists before antigen arrives and that antigen merely selects. The theory made testable predictions: one cell, one specificity; activation by receptor binding; inherited specificity in daughter cells; and deletion of self-reactive clones early in development. Nossal and Lederberg's 1958 single-cell experiment supported the first prediction, and later work in molecular biology explained where the diversity comes from. Susumu Tonegawa showed in the 1970s that antibody genes are assembled by recombining gene segments (V(D)J recombination), producing more than a trillion possible antibodies from fewer than 25,000 genes; somatic hypermutation and selection in germinal centres then improve antibody fit after activation, a process called affinity maturation. Tolerance turned out to be more complex than simple deletion, with regulatory T cells and other mechanisms involved, but the core logic survives. At the population level, the herd immunity threshold of 1 minus 1/R0 is a simplification: it assumes random mixing, uniform susceptibility, and vaccine-induced immunity that blocks transmission as well as illness. Real populations cluster in households, schools and communities, so pockets of low coverage can sustain outbreaks even when national averages look high. R0 also varies with crowding and behaviour, which is why published R0 ranges, like 12 to 18 for measles, are wide. For diseases where vaccines protect against severe illness better than against infection, or where immunity wanes or the pathogen drifts, as with influenza, a stable herd immunity threshold may never be reached. These subtleties explain why public health experts talk about coverage targets such as 95 percent for measles rather than a single exact number.","funFact":"In 2008, scientists found that people who had survived the 1918 flu pandemic still had immune cells able to make antibodies against that virus about 90 years later.","check":{"q":"Why is the immune response faster the second time the body meets the same germ?","a":"Because memory B and T cells left over from the first meeting recognise it and multiply quickly","wrong":["Because the germ gets weaker every time it enters a body","Because phagocytes grow bigger after every infection","Because the skin becomes thicker after the first infection"]},"sources":[{"title":"Adaptive immune system","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adaptive_immune_system"},{"title":"CDC Seasonal Flu Vaccine Effectiveness Studies","publisher":"CDC","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/flu-vaccines-work/php/effectiveness-studies/index.html"},{"title":"Frank Macfarlane Burnet","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Macfarlane_Burnet"},{"title":"How do vaccines work?","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/feature-stories/detail/how-do-vaccines-work"},{"title":"Selecting viruses for the seasonal influenza vaccine","publisher":"CDC","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/flu/vaccine-process/vaccine-selection.html"},{"title":"Herd immunity","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herd_immunity"},{"title":"Herd immunity","publisher":"Vaccine Knowledge Project, University of Oxford","url":"https://vaccineknowledge.ox.ac.uk/herd-immunity"},{"title":"Measles fact sheet","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/measles"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/11-1.jpg","alt":"Australian scientist Frank Macfarlane Burnet in Stockholm in 1960, the year he won the Nobel Prize.","file":"Frank Macfarlane Burnet 1960.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frank_Macfarlane_Burnet_1960.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/11-2.jpg","alt":"The Y shape of an antibody: two arms (1) with antigen-grabbing tips (5), a stem (2), heavy chains (3) and light chains (4).","file":"Immunoglobulin basic unit.svg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Immunoglobulin_basic_unit.svg","author":"Y_tambe","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/11-3.jpg","alt":"Microscope image of killer T cells (green and red) closing in on a cancer cell (blue, centre).","file":"Killer T cells surround a cancer cell.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Killer_T_cells_surround_a_cancer_cell.png","author":"The National Institutes of Health","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4367},{"n":12,"title":"Inside a Vaccine: Types, Making and Testing","when":"1900s – today (science)","summary":"There are lots of ways to make a vaccine: weakened germs, killed germs, harmless germ pieces and even instructions for your cells. Vaccines are grown in eggs, in cells and even in yeast, then tested very carefully on volunteers. Finally they travel all the way to clinics in a chain of fridges that must never get too warm.","sections":[{"heading":"Many Ways to Make a Wanted Poster","paragraphs":["At the end of the last part we learned that a vaccine is really a wanted poster: something that shows the immune system what a germ looks like, without the danger of the real disease. But there are many ways to make a poster. You could use a photograph, a pencil sketch, a description of one striking feature, or even a set of instructions for drawing the face yourself. Vaccine scientists have found ways to do all of these, and each kind of vaccine has its own strengths.","The oldest kind is the live attenuated vaccine. Attenuated means weakened. These vaccines contain a living germ that has been tamed so it can still multiply a little inside the body but causes little or no illness. Jenner's cowpox was a sort of natural cousin of this idea, and Pasteur deliberately weakened germs in his laboratory. Today the measles, mumps and rubella vaccines are live attenuated vaccines, and so is Max Theiler's yellow fever vaccine, 17D, which you met in part 9. Theiler weakened the yellow fever virus by growing it again and again in tissue from mouse and chicken embryos, passing it from one culture to the next many times over, until a version appeared that no longer caused serious illness.","Because a live attenuated germ copies itself for a while, the immune system sees it in much the way it would see a real infection, and the memory it builds is often strong and long-lasting. One or two doses may be enough. The trade-off is that live vaccines are usually not given to people whose immune systems are seriously weakened, since even a tamed germ could cause trouble in a body that cannot control it. Many live vaccines are also fussy about temperature, as we will see later.","The next kind is the inactivated vaccine. Inactivated means killed. The germ is grown in large amounts and then treated with heat or chemicals, such as formaldehyde, so that it can no longer multiply at all. The killed germ still wears its name tags, so the immune system can learn from it. Jonas Salk's polio vaccine, which you will meet in part 14, works this way, as do the hepatitis A vaccine, rabies vaccines and many flu shots. Inactivated vaccines cannot cause the disease, but because the germ does not multiply, they often need several doses or boosters to build strong memory.","A third kind, the toxoid, is for bacteria whose main weapon is a poison. In part 9 you read how Gaston Ramon learned to treat toxins with formaldehyde and heat so they lost their poison but kept their shape. The tetanus and diphtheria vaccines are toxoids. They do not teach the body to attack the bacteria themselves, but to neutralise the toxin, which is the part that makes people ill."]},{"heading":"Just the Pieces: Subunits, Sugars and New Ideas","paragraphs":["Scientists then asked a clever question: does the immune system really need the whole germ? Maybe just one well-chosen piece would be enough, like a wanted poster that shows only the thief's unmistakable tattoo. This idea led to subunit vaccines, which contain only selected parts of a germ, usually proteins.","The hepatitis B vaccine is a famous example. Hepatitis B is a virus that attacks the liver. The first hepatitis B vaccine, approved in the United States in 1981, was made by collecting the virus's surface protein from the blood plasma of people who already carried the virus, and purifying it very carefully. It worked, but people worried about anything made from human blood. So scientists turned to genetic engineering. They took the gene for the virus's surface protein and placed it inside baker's yeast, the same kind of yeast used to make bread rise. The yeast cells then manufactured the viral protein, and nothing else from the virus. On 23 July 1986 the US Food and Drug Administration approved this yeast-made vaccine, developed with Maurice Hilleman's team at Merck. It was the first human vaccine made using recombinant DNA, which means DNA cut and joined in the laboratory. Part 16 tells more of this story.","Some subunit vaccines use a neat trick: virus-like particles. The HPV vaccine, which you will meet in part 19, contains copies of a virus's outer shell protein that clump together into tiny hollow balls that look like the virus from the outside but have no genes inside. To the immune system they look just like the real thing, but they are as empty as a chocolate egg with no toy.","Then there are germs that hide behind a coat of sugar. Some dangerous bacteria, such as Haemophilus influenzae type b, called Hib for short, wrap themselves in a slippery sugar capsule. A vaccine made only of that sugar was licensed in the United States in 1985, but it worked poorly in children under about 18 months, exactly the age group most at risk of Hib meningitis, a serious infection of the lining around the brain. The reason was that babies' immune systems cannot easily get T cell help against plain sugars, so they built weak, short memory. The solution was the conjugate vaccine. Conjugate means joined. Scientists chemically attached the sugar to a protein, such as tetanus toxoid, which the immune system responds to strongly. The protein brings in helper T cells, and the babies' B cells learn to make good antibodies and memory cells against the sugar. The first Hib conjugate vaccine was licensed in the United States in 1987. Afterwards, serious Hib disease in American children fell by more than 97 percent.","Finally there are two newer approaches, which you will explore properly in part 18. Viral vector vaccines use a harmless virus, often a weakened type of adenovirus, as a delivery van that carries a gene for one germ protein into our cells. Messenger RNA, or mRNA, vaccines skip the van and send the instructions themselves, wrapped in tiny bubbles of fat. In both cases, our own cells briefly make a harmless germ protein, which the immune system then learns to recognise. Instead of handing over a wanted poster, these vaccines hand the body a drawing kit and instructions."]},{"heading":"What Else Is in the Vial?","paragraphs":["A vaccine dose is tiny, often about half a millilitre, which is less than a tenth of a teaspoon. The most important ingredient is the antigen, the part that does the teaching. But a few other ingredients are usually there too, each with a particular job. Knowing what they are takes away a lot of the mystery.","Adjuvants are helpers that make the immune system respond more strongly to the antigen. The word comes from the Latin for 'to help'. Scientists stumbled on adjuvants in the early 1900s when they noticed that some batches of vaccine worked better than others, and that certain impurities seemed to boost the response. In 1926 the British scientist Alexander Glenny and his colleagues reported that diphtheria toxoid mixed with a form of aluminium called alum gave a stronger immune response. Aluminium salts, such as aluminium hydroxide and aluminium phosphate, are still the most commonly used adjuvants in human vaccines today. They seem to work partly by keeping the antigen at the injection site for longer and by alerting local immune cells, like a flashing light that says: pay attention here! The amount of aluminium is very small; the WHO notes that babies take in far more aluminium from food than from vaccines. Newer adjuvants include oil-in-water emulsions and special mixtures used in some shingles and malaria vaccines. Adjuvants are especially useful in subunit vaccines, because a lone protein piece can be too quiet to wake the immune system on its own.","Stabilisers keep the vaccine working while it travels and waits on a shelf. Many antigens are delicate proteins that can fall apart or stick to the walls of the vial. Common stabilisers include sugars such as sucrose and lactose, amino acids such as glycine, gelatin, and proteins such as albumin. Surfactants, which are a bit like a very gentle soap, help keep the mixture evenly blended so it does not clump or settle.","Preservatives stop bacteria and fungi from growing in multi-dose vials, which hold several doses and are opened again and again with fresh needles. Without a preservative, a single slip could let germs into the vial and harm the next people to receive a dose. Examples include 2-phenoxyethanol and thiomersal, also called thimerosal. Single-dose vials and syringes often contain no preservative at all, because they are used only once.","There may also be tiny traces of materials used during manufacturing, called residuals. These can include small amounts of egg protein, yeast or antibiotics, measured in parts per million or even parts per billion. Some vaccines are dried into a powder and come with a diluent, usually sterile water, that is mixed in just before the vaccine is given. Every ingredient is listed on the official information sheet for each vaccine, and regulators check them all."]},{"heading":"Vaccine Farms: Eggs, Cells and Yeast","paragraphs":["Before you can make millions of doses of a vaccine, you need to grow the antigen in huge amounts. Viruses are especially awkward, because, as you learned in part 7, they can only multiply inside living cells. So vaccine makers need a supply of living cells for the virus to grow in. Over the past century, they have used three main kinds of 'farm'.","The first is the humble chicken egg. In part 9 you met Ernest Goodpasture, whose team showed in 1931 that viruses could be grown inside fertilised hens' eggs, and Frank Macfarlane Burnet, from part 11, also became an expert in egg methods. Flu vaccines are still made this way in enormous numbers. Picture a factory where trays of fertilised eggs roll past machines. Each egg gets a tiny injection of flu virus. The eggs are then kept warm for several days while the virus multiplies in the fluid inside. Afterwards the virus-filled fluid is collected, and for most flu shots the virus is killed, purified and split into pieces. Up to three eggs can be needed to make one dose of some flu vaccines, and roughly 600 million eggs are used for flu vaccines around the world each year. This method has been in use for more than 70 years. It is reliable, but it is slow, and it needs a steady supply of special eggs months in advance.","The second kind of farm is cell culture: living animal or human cells grown in sterile flasks or giant steel tanks called bioreactors, bathed in a nutrient soup. This became possible thanks to John Enders, Thomas Weller and Frederick Robbins, who showed in 1949 that poliovirus could grow in cultures of non-nerve tissue, a breakthrough you will read about in part 13. Polio, measles, rubella, chickenpox and rabies vaccines have all been made using cell cultures. In 2012 the US approved the first flu vaccine made in cell culture instead of eggs. Cell culture can be scaled up faster, and it avoids the problem that flu viruses sometimes change slightly as they adapt to growing in eggs.","The third kind of farm uses genetic engineering to make only the antigen, with no virus involved at all. As you saw with hepatitis B, the gene for one germ protein can be placed into yeast cells, which then churn out that protein as they grow in big fermentation tanks, a bit like brewing. Other recombinant vaccines use insect cells or bacteria as the factory. A recombinant flu vaccine approved in the United States in 2013 is made by putting the gene for the flu virus's main surface protein into insect cells using a harmless insect virus as a carrier. No eggs or flu viruses are needed at any stage.","Growing the antigen is only the beginning. It must then be purified to remove almost everything else, carefully measured, mixed with the other ingredients, and filled into sterile vials or syringes in super-clean rooms where workers wear full-body suits. Every batch, called a lot, is tested for purity, strength and safety. In many countries, including the United States, government regulators can review the test results and check samples of a lot before it is released. Making and checking a batch of some vaccines can take many months from start to finish, and a large share of that time goes into testing rather than making."]},{"heading":"Testing, Testing: From Lab Bench to Phase 3","paragraphs":["Before a new vaccine can be used, scientists must answer two big questions with real evidence. Is it safe? And does it work? Answering them carefully is a long journey with several stages, and many candidate vaccines fail along the way.","The journey starts in the laboratory with what is called preclinical research. Scientists test whether the vaccine candidate triggers an immune response in cells and in animals such as mice, and whether it causes any obvious harm. If the results look promising, the vaccine makers ask regulators for permission to begin testing in people. Tests in people are called clinical trials, and they happen in phases, with more volunteers each time.","In Phase 1, a small group of healthy adult volunteers, often about 20 to 100 people, receives the vaccine. The main goals are to check safety, to find out what side effects occur, and to look at what dose seems right. Volunteers are watched closely, and the trial can be paused at once if anything worrying happens.","In Phase 2, the vaccine is given to several hundred people, often including people more like those who would eventually receive it, such as different age groups. Scientists continue to check safety, compare doses and schedules, and measure the immune response, for example by counting antibodies in blood samples.","Phase 3 is the big test. It often involves thousands to tens of thousands of volunteers, sometimes in many countries. The volunteers are divided randomly into groups, a bit like flipping a coin for each person. One group receives the vaccine. The other group receives a placebo, a dummy injection such as salt water, or sometimes an existing vaccine against a different disease. Random assignment matters because it makes the groups as similar as possible, so any difference between them is likely to be caused by the vaccine and not by something else, such as age or where people live.","Many Phase 3 trials are also double-blind. This means that neither the volunteers nor the doctors and nurses checking on them know who got the real vaccine and who got the placebo. Why go to such trouble? Because people's expectations can sneak into results without anyone meaning them to. A volunteer who knows they got the real vaccine might report symptoms differently, and a doctor who knows might examine them differently. Blinding protects the trial from these hidden biases. A separate, independent safety board can look at the unblinded data along the way to make sure nobody is being put at risk.","At the end, scientists compare how many people in each group caught the disease. Here is a simple example. Suppose 100 people in the placebo group fall ill, but only 10 in the same-sized vaccine group do. The vaccine group had 90 percent fewer cases, so the vaccine's efficacy would be about 90 percent. Phase 3 trials are also large enough to spot side effects that are fairly uncommon, perhaps affecting one person in a thousand.","All of this takes time and money. Traditionally, developing a vaccine from the first idea to approval took 10 years or more, often between 10 and 15 years, and many candidates fail somewhere along the way. Part 18 explains how, in an emergency, some of these steps were overlapped and run in parallel, without skipping any phase."]},{"heading":"Approval and Watching for Years Afterwards","paragraphs":["When the trials are finished, the vaccine maker sends all the results to regulators, the government agencies whose job is to decide whether a medicine is safe and effective enough to be used. Examples include the Food and Drug Administration in the United States, the European Medicines Agency in Europe, and the Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency in the United Kingdom. The WHO also runs a process called prequalification, which checks vaccines that United Nations agencies buy for lower-income countries. Regulators' experts go through the data in great detail, often thousands of pages, and they inspect the factories too. Separately, expert committees advise on who should be offered a vaccine and at what ages.","Approval is not the end of the story. Even a trial with 30,000 people cannot reveal a side effect that happens to only one person in a million. Such very rare effects only show up once a vaccine is given to millions. So vaccines keep being watched after approval, a stage often called Phase 4 or post-marketing surveillance.","Many countries run systems that collect reports of health problems after vaccination. In the United States, the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System, VAERS, accepts reports from anyone, including doctors, patients and parents. In the United Kingdom, the Yellow Card scheme does a similar job for all medicines. A report does not prove that a vaccine caused a problem, because people fall ill for all sorts of reasons and some events would have happened anyway. Instead, these reports act like smoke alarms. When a pattern appears, scientists investigate using other systems, such as the US Vaccine Safety Datalink, which links medical records from millions of people so researchers can compare vaccinated and unvaccinated groups.","This watching really works. Over the years, monitoring has spotted rare problems with particular vaccines, which led to changes in advice and in a few cases to vaccines being withdrawn. Part 17 tells some of those stories and explains how scientists separate real warning signs from coincidences."]},{"heading":"The Cold Chain: Keeping Vaccines Cool","paragraphs":["Imagine you have made a perfect vaccine. Now you have to get it from a factory in one country to a small clinic in a village hundreds of kilometres from the nearest city, perhaps across deserts, mountains or rivers. And all the way, it must stay at just the right temperature. This journey is called the cold chain, and every link matters: factory, airport, national store, regional store, local clinic, and finally the cool box carried by a health worker on foot, by bicycle, by motorbike or by boat.","Most vaccines must be kept between 2 and 8 degrees Celsius, about the temperature inside a normal kitchen fridge. If they get too warm, the delicate antigens can slowly break down, and the vaccine loses its power, even though it may look exactly the same. Some vaccines, such as certain chickenpox vaccines, are stored frozen, at around minus 15 degrees or colder. A few need extreme cold: the Pfizer-BioNTech COVID-19 vaccine was first shipped at around minus 70 degrees, colder than an Antarctic winter night, which needed special ultra-cold freezers that many countries did not have.","Too cold can be a problem as well. Some vaccines, especially those containing aluminium adjuvants, are damaged if they freeze, so health workers must be careful that vials do not sit right against ice packs. Getting the temperature right in hot countries with unreliable electricity is a real engineering challenge. Engineers have developed fridges powered by solar panels, cold boxes that stay cool for days, and portable carriers padded with insulation.","One of the smartest inventions in the whole cold chain is tiny: the vaccine vial monitor, or VVM. It is a small sticker printed on the label or cap of a vaccine vial, showing a square inside a circle. The square is made of a special heat-sensitive material that slowly darkens as it is exposed to heat over time, and faster if it is hotter. As long as the square is lighter than the circle around it, the vaccine is fine to use. If the square becomes as dark as the circle, or darker, the vaccine has had too much heat and should be thrown away.","The VVM was developed by the organisation PATH working with the WHO and industry partners, and it was first used on vaccines in 1996. By 2017, more than 6.6 billion VVMs had been used. Before VVMs, a health worker could not easily tell whether a vial had been damaged on the way, so some good vaccine was wasted out of caution, and some damaged vaccine may have been given by mistake. With a glance at a tiny square, a nurse in a remote village can now know that the vaccine in their hand still works.","So a vaccine is much more than a few drops of liquid. It is a carefully chosen antigen, helped by adjuvants and stabilisers, grown in eggs, cells or yeast, tested on thousands of volunteers, checked by regulators, watched for years, and carried through a chain of fridges to the arm of one person. Now let us travel back to the 1950s, when all this science was put to its most famous test, at a time when summer meant fear of a disease that could paralyse children."]}],"deepDive":"Vaccine design is a series of trade-offs between how strongly a vaccine stimulates immunity, how safe it is, and how practical it is to make and deliver. Live attenuated vaccines mimic natural infection, generating antibodies, T cell responses and often mucosal immunity, so single doses can give long protection. But attenuation is empirical: historically it was achieved by repeatedly passaging viruses through unnatural hosts or cell cultures until mutations reduced virulence, without knowing exactly which mutations mattered. That is why live vaccines must be checked for genetic stability and are generally avoided in people with seriously weakened immune systems. Inactivated, subunit and toxoid vaccines cannot replicate and are inherently safer in that sense, but they often need adjuvants and multiple doses. Conjugate vaccines solve a specific immunological problem: polysaccharide antigens activate B cells without T cell help, giving weak, short-lived responses in infants. Linking the sugar to a carrier protein lets B cells present carrier peptides to helper T cells, producing class-switched, high-affinity antibodies and memory. Clinical trial design also involves debate. Efficacy, measured in controlled trials, is calculated as 1 minus the ratio of attack rates in vaccinated and placebo groups; effectiveness, measured afterwards in real-world use, can differ because populations, circulating strains and conditions vary. Placebo-controlled trials raise ethical questions when an effective vaccine already exists; in such cases trials often compare against the existing vaccine instead. Endpoints matter too: a vaccine may prevent severe disease better than mild infection, and trials sized to detect one outcome may be underpowered for another. Rare adverse events at rates of one in 100,000 or less cannot be detected reliably by trials of 30,000 people, which is why post-licensure systems combine passive reporting, such as VAERS and Yellow Card, with active surveillance using linked health records. Finally, logistics shape global impact. Heat-stable formulations, freeze-dried products, multi-dose vials and devices like vaccine vial monitors may sound unglamorous, but they often decide whether a vaccine reaches the children who need it most.","funFact":"Roughly 600 million chicken eggs are used every year to help make flu vaccines around the world.","check":{"q":"What does it mean when the square on a vaccine vial monitor becomes as dark as the circle around it?","a":"The vaccine has been exposed to too much heat and should not be used","wrong":["The vaccine is fully mixed and ready to use","The vaccine has been frozen and must be warmed up","The vaccine has passed its Phase 3 trial"]},"sources":[{"title":"How are vaccines developed?","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/feature-stories/detail/how-are-vaccines-developed"},{"title":"Influenza vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Influenza_vaccine"},{"title":"Immunologic adjuvant","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immunologic_adjuvant"},{"title":"Hepatitis B vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hepatitis_B_vaccine"},{"title":"Hib vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hib_vaccine"},{"title":"How influenza (flu) vaccines are made","publisher":"CDC","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/flu/vaccine-process/index.html"},{"title":"Vaccine Development, Testing, and Regulation","publisher":"History of Vaccines (College of Physicians of Philadelphia)","url":"https://www.historyofvaccines.org/content/articles/vaccine-development-testing-and-regulation"},{"title":"Vaccine vial monitor","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaccine_vial_monitor"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/12-1.jpg","alt":"Vaccine vials in Indonesia; the square-in-a-circle on each label is a vial monitor that darkens if the vaccine gets too warm.","file":"BioFarma vaccine vials Bandung.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BioFarma_vaccine_vials_Bandung.jpg","author":"Ümit Kartoğlu for VOA","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/12-2.jpg","alt":"A lab worker injects flu virus into chicken eggs, where it grows before being harvested to make vaccine.","file":"Flu Vaccine Technologies (8392730215).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flu_Vaccine_Technologies_(8392730215).jpg","author":"The U.S. Food and Drug Administration","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/12-3.jpg","alt":"Inside a vaccine cold box in Mozambique: frozen ice packs keep polio and other vaccines cold on the journey.","file":"VillageReach vaccine cold chain.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VillageReach_vaccine_cold_chain.jpg","author":"GiveWell","license":"CC BY 3.0"}],"words":3804},{"n":13,"title":"The Summer Plague: Polio","when":"1890s – 1952","summary":"For many years, summer was the scariest time of year for parents, because a sneaky virus called polio could paralyse children. Swimming pools closed, families fled the cities, and some patients needed a giant metal machine to help them breathe. Then three scientists in Boston learned how to grow the virus in a dish, and the race for a vaccine began.","sections":[{"heading":"A Virus That Usually Hides","paragraphs":["Imagine it is July in an American town in the early 1950s. The air is hot and sticky, school is out, and you would love nothing more than a trip to the public swimming pool. But the pool has a sign on the gate: closed until further notice. The cinema is half empty. Your parents have told you not to drink from water fountains and not to play with children from the next street. Nobody says the word out loud very often, but everybody knows what they are afraid of. Polio.","Polio is short for poliomyelitis, a disease caused by a tiny virus called the poliovirus. The long name comes from Greek words: polios means grey, myelos means marrow, and the ending -itis means inflammation, or swelling and irritation. Put together, it means inflammation of the grey matter of the spinal cord, which is exactly the part of the body the virus can damage in its worst cases. In older books you will often see another name for it: infantile paralysis, because so many of its victims were small children.","The poliovirus does not usually travel through the air like a cold. Instead it lives and multiplies in the gut, the long tube of the intestines. An infected person sheds huge numbers of virus particles in their poo, sometimes for as long as six weeks. If even a tiny, invisible trace gets onto unwashed hands, toys, food or drinking water, the next person can swallow it. Scientists call this the faecal-oral route, which is a polite way of saying from poo to mouth. That is why clean water, good toilets and handwashing matter so much in the story of polio.","Here is the strange and important thing about polio: most people who catch it never know. Around seven in every ten infections cause no symptoms at all. About one in four causes a mild illness that looks like flu, with a sore throat, a slight fever or an upset tummy, and goes away within a week or two. A small number of people develop a stiff, painful neck and headache when the virus irritates the coverings of the brain and spinal cord, but they usually recover completely.","Only in a very small fraction of infections, fewer than one in a hundred and often far fewer, does the virus do its worst. In those cases it travels from the gut into the bloodstream and then along nerves into the spinal cord. There it attacks motor neurons, the long nerve cells that carry messages from the brain to the muscles telling them to move. When those nerve cells are destroyed, the muscles they controlled stop receiving any messages. They go floppy and weak, and over time they can waste away. This is called paralysis. Today the World Health Organization estimates that about one infection in every 200 leads to paralysis that does not go away, usually in the legs.","The most dangerous form is called bulbar polio, because it damages the brainstem, the part of the brain once called the bulb, which controls breathing and swallowing. If the muscles that fill the lungs with air are paralysed, a person can no longer breathe on their own. Before modern hospital machines, that often meant death. Even today, WHO estimates that between 5 and 10 in every 100 people who are paralysed by polio die because their breathing muscles stop working.","There are actually three slightly different types of poliovirus, called type 1, type 2 and type 3. Catching one type does not protect you against the other two, a fact that would make building a vaccine much harder. And because so many infected people show no symptoms at all, polio was a hidden enemy. For every child in hospital with a paralysed leg, there could be a hundred or more people walking around feeling perfectly well, quietly spreading the virus without knowing it."]},{"heading":"An Old Disease with a New Pattern","paragraphs":["Polio is not a new disease. A carved stone slab from ancient Egypt, made more than 3,000 years ago, shows a priest leaning on a staff, with one leg thin and shortened and his foot pointed downwards. Many doctors think he may have had polio, although nobody can be completely sure from a picture. Through the centuries, doctors occasionally described children who suddenly became lame after a fever. But for most of history, polio seems to have been an uncommon disease that appeared here and there, one child at a time.","That began to change in the 1800s. In 1840 a German doctor named Jakob Heine wrote a careful description of the disease and showed that the problem lay in the spinal cord. In 1890 a Swedish doctor, Karl Oskar Medin, studied a cluster of cases in Stockholm and realised that polio could strike many people at once, in an epidemic. Another Swede, Ivar Wickman, made a brilliant observation in the early 1900s: during an outbreak, children with only mild illness, or no obvious illness at all, were helping to spread it. Polio was more contagious than anyone had guessed.","Then came the first big outbreak in the United States. In the summer of 1894, in the Otter Creek valley around Rutland, Vermont, 132 people fell ill and 18 died. More and bigger summer epidemics followed in Europe and North America. In 1908 and 1909, an Austrian scientist named Karl Landsteiner, working in Vienna with Erwin Popper, took spinal cord tissue from a child who had died of polio, ground it up, passed it through a filter and injected it into monkeys. The monkeys became paralysed. Whatever caused polio was something that could pass through filters that stopped bacteria. It was a virus, even though nobody could yet see one.","So why did a disease that had been quietly present for thousands of years suddenly start exploding into epidemics just as cities were getting cleaner and healthier? It sounds backwards, but the leading explanation is that cleanliness itself changed the pattern of polio.","Think about life in a crowded town in, say, 1850. There was no clean piped water in many homes, few proper toilets and sewers, and plenty of dirt. Almost every baby would meet the poliovirus very early in life, often while still in nappies. At that age, babies still carry some protective antibodies that passed to them from their mothers before birth and through breast milk. Those borrowed antibodies helped keep the infection mild. The baby would then build its own lifelong immunity, almost always without anyone noticing.","By the late 1800s and early 1900s, wealthy countries were building sewers, filtering drinking water and teaching handwashing. These changes saved enormous numbers of lives from diseases such as cholera and typhoid. But they also meant that many children now met the poliovirus later, at age three, five, ten or even as teenagers or adults. By then the protection from their mothers had faded away, and an older child or adult is more likely to suffer paralysis than a baby. Most historians and scientists accept this explanation, although they still debate the details, such as exactly how much each factor mattered.","One clue supports the idea: polio epidemics often hit comfortable, clean neighbourhoods hard, sometimes harder than poor and crowded ones. That surprised people at the time, who were used to thinking of epidemics as diseases of dirt and poverty. Polio was different. It was, in a strange way, a disease of modern life."]},{"heading":"The Terrible Summer of 1916","paragraphs":["In June 1916, doctors in Brooklyn, New York, noticed that more and more young children were becoming paralysed. On 17 June the city's health department officially announced an epidemic. Over the following weeks, cases climbed steeply, peaking in early August. It became the worst polio epidemic the world had yet seen.","By the end of the year, New York City alone had recorded about 9,000 cases and more than 2,000 deaths. Across the United States there were about 27,000 cases and more than 6,000 deaths. The youngest children were hit hardest: in New York, children aged one to three made up a large share of cases, and most of the children who died were under five.","Nobody knew how polio spread, so health officials tried almost everything they could think of. Homes with a sick child had a printed warning card, called a placard, nailed beside the door, and families were kept inside under quarantine. Newspapers printed the names and addresses of patients each day. Cinemas and public gatherings were restricted, children under 16 were kept away from many public places, and parents were warned to keep their children away from swimming pools, beaches, fountains and amusement parks.","Fear also led people to wrong guesses. Some blamed flies, so cities cracked down on horse manure where flies bred. Some blamed sewers, or car exhaust. Many people believed pets spread polio, and tens of thousands of cats were killed in New York that summer, all for nothing, because cats do not carry human poliovirus. Some people unfairly blamed immigrant families, especially Italian Americans, and Black Americans, even though the disease struck every kind of neighbourhood. Staten Island, the part of the city with the fewest immigrants, actually had the highest proportion of cases.","Families who could afford it fled. The New York Times estimated that by early July around 50,000 children had been sent out of the city to the countryside and mountains. Some nearby towns posted police at train stations and ferry docks to turn back travellers from New York. In the city, some frightened mothers sealed the cracks around their windows with rags in the summer heat, hoping to keep the disease out.","The 1916 epidemic taught Americans to fear summer. Polio would return again and again over the next four decades, usually starting in late spring and reaching its peak in July, August and September. It is not fully clear why polio loved summer so much, although warm weather may help the virus survive longer outside the body, and children spend more time swimming and playing together. Whatever the reason, the phrase summer plague stuck. Every year, parents watched the newspapers for reports of the first cases, and every year some children who had gone happily to camp in June came home in braces, on crutches or in wheelchairs."]},{"heading":"A Future President Falls Ill","paragraphs":["Polio did not only strike children. In August 1921, a tall, energetic 39-year-old politician named Franklin Delano Roosevelt was on holiday with his family at their summer house on Campobello Island, just across the border from Maine in Canada. Roosevelt had already been the Assistant Secretary of the Navy and had run, unsuccessfully, for Vice President of the United States the year before. He was famous for his sailing, swimming and boundless energy.","On 10 August, after a tiring day outdoors, he went to bed feeling chilled and achy. Over the next few days things became much worse. He had a fever, and weakness crept upwards through his body until he could not stand. His legs became paralysed. On 25 August a well-known specialist, Dr Robert Lovett, examined him and declared that it was clearly poliomyelitis. Most of his other symptoms faded over the following months, but Roosevelt never walked unaided again. He was paralysed from the waist down for the rest of his life.","Was it really polio? In 2003 a team of researchers led by Armond Goldman looked again at the descriptions of Roosevelt's illness. They pointed out that some details, such as his age, the way the paralysis climbed upwards and spread evenly on both sides, his facial weakness and the numbness and painful sensitivity of his skin, fit better with a different illness called Guillain-Barré syndrome, in which the body's own immune system attacks the nerves. Other doctors have argued back that polio is still the best explanation. Nobody can test Roosevelt now, so the question may never be settled. What is certain is that Roosevelt himself, his doctors and the whole country believed he had polio, and that belief shaped history.","Roosevelt refused to let paralysis end his career. He worked for years to strengthen his body, learning to stand with heavy metal braces on his legs and to walk short distances while gripping a cane and someone's arm. In 1924 he visited Warm Springs, Georgia, where a natural spring fed a pool of warm water, and found that swimming in it helped him exercise. In 1926 he bought the resort and turned it into a treatment centre for polio patients, where people could exercise, swim and support one another.","In 1928 he was elected Governor of New York, and in 1932 he was elected President of the United States. He went on to win four presidential elections, more than anyone else in American history, and led the country through the Great Depression and most of the Second World War. Yet many Americans never fully realised how disabled he was. Photographers mostly agreed not to take pictures of him being lifted or using his wheelchair, his speeches were arranged so he could grip a sturdy lectern, and he usually appeared in public standing, holding the arm of an aide or one of his sons. Roosevelt hid his disability partly because in the 1920s and 1930s many people wrongly believed that a disabled person could not be a strong leader."]},{"heading":"Marching with Dimes","paragraphs":["Roosevelt could not hide from polio itself, though, and he decided to fight it. On 3 January 1938 he founded the National Foundation for Infantile Paralysis, a charity that would pay for patient care and, crucially, for scientific research. His old law partner and friend Basil O'Connor ran it, and he kept leading it for more than thirty years, building a network of over 3,000 local chapters across the country.","The foundation's genius was asking for tiny amounts of money from enormous numbers of people. The popular entertainer Eddie Cantor suggested on the radio that every American could send a dime, a ten-cent coin, to the President. He called the campaign the March of Dimes, a joke on a famous cinema newsreel of the time called The March of Time. The idea caught on wildly. Envelopes poured into the White House, some with a single coin taped to a card. By the final count, about 2,680,000 dimes arrived, worth 268,000 dollars, and the newspapers joked about a silver tide swamping the White House mail room. The charity later took March of Dimes as its official name.","Every January, around Roosevelt's birthday on the 30th, the campaign returned. Cinemas passed collection cans along the rows. Children collected coins at school. In 1950, mothers in Phoenix, Arizona, came up with the Mothers' March on Polio: on a chosen evening, porch lights were switched on as a signal, and volunteer mothers walked from door to door collecting donations. The idea spread across the country. Posters showed brave poster children in leg braces; the first, Donald Anderson of Oregon, had caught polio in 1943.","The money did two big jobs. First, it paid for care: hospital beds, braces, wheelchairs, physiotherapy and iron lungs, so that families did not have to face ruinous bills alone. Second, and most important for our story, it paid for research. Many of the scientists you will meet in this part and the next were funded by ordinary people's dimes. After Roosevelt died in April 1945, the US government honoured him by putting his face on the dime, first issued on 30 January 1946, his birthday. It is still on the dime today."]},{"heading":"The Machine That Breathed","paragraphs":["For the children with bulbar polio, whose breathing muscles stopped working, the danger was immediate. Without help they would slowly suffocate. In 1928 two researchers at the Harvard School of Public Health, the engineer Philip Drinker and the physiologist Louis Agassiz Shaw Jr., built a machine to breathe for them. It became known as the iron lung.","The iron lung was a large metal tank, roughly the size and shape of a small submarine, with the patient lying inside on a bed and only their head sticking out through a tight rubber collar at one end. Here is how it worked. Normally, when you breathe in, muscles pull your chest outwards. That makes a little more space inside your lungs, the air pressure inside them drops slightly, and outside air rushes in to fill the gap. The iron lung did the same job from the outside. A pump sucked some air out of the sealed tank, lowering the pressure around the patient's body. The chest expanded and air flowed in through the nose and mouth. Then the pressure in the tank rose back up, the chest sank down again, and air flowed out. Doctors call this negative-pressure ventilation.","Drinker and Shaw's first prototype used an electric motor and air pumps taken from two vacuum cleaners. On 12 October 1928, at Boston Children's Hospital, it was tried on an eight-year-old girl who was close to death because polio had stopped her breathing. Within moments of the pump starting, she was breathing again. The story of that first rescue helped make the iron lung famous.","The early machines were expensive. In 1930 an adult Drinker respirator cost about 2,000 dollars, a huge sum at the time, more than many families earned in a year. In 1931 an inventor named John Haven Emerson introduced an improved, cheaper version with a bed that slid in and out and windows in the side so nurses could reach in to adjust the patient. In Britain, a cheaper wooden version designed by Edward Both was produced in large numbers in the late 1930s with funding from the businessman Lord Nuffield.","During the great epidemics of the 1940s and 1950s, hospitals set up whole wards filled with rows of iron lungs, their pumps wheezing in rhythm day and night. Nurses fed patients, read to them and held up mirrors so they could see the room behind them. Many patients needed the machine for only a week or two until their breathing muscles recovered enough to work on their own. But some were left permanently paralysed and lived in iron lungs for years. The American Paul Alexander caught polio in 1952 at the age of six and used an iron lung for the rest of his life; despite this, he learned to breathe on his own for periods by gulping air, earned a law degree, worked as a lawyer and wrote a book. He died in 2024 at age 78, after 72 years in the machine.","In 1952 a terrible epidemic in Copenhagen, Denmark, overwhelmed the city's few respirators. The doctors, guided by the anaesthetist Bjørn Ibsen, tried a different approach: they placed a tube into the patient's windpipe and pushed air gently into the lungs with a squeezable bag. Because there were no machines to do it, hundreds of medical students took turns squeezing bags by hand, around the clock, for weeks. Death rates among patients with breathing paralysis fell dramatically. This positive-pressure method soon replaced the iron lung in most hospitals, and it was the beginning of the modern intensive care unit."]},{"heading":"Growing a Virus in a Dish","paragraphs":["Iron lungs saved lives, but they did not stop anyone from catching polio. To do that, scientists needed a vaccine. And to make a vaccine, they first needed to grow huge amounts of poliovirus. That was a big problem.","Remember that a virus cannot copy itself on its own. It has to get inside living cells and hijack them. For decades the only reliable way to grow poliovirus was inside the brains and spinal cords of living monkeys. That was slow, enormously expensive and used vast numbers of animals. Worse, vaccine made from nerve tissue carried a risk of causing dangerous allergic reactions in the brain. Many scientists believed poliovirus could only grow in nerve cells, so they saw no way around the problem.","At Boston Children's Hospital, a calm, careful virologist named John Enders was working with two young doctors, Thomas Weller and Frederick Robbins, on growing other viruses in tubes of living tissue kept alive in a nourishing liquid. Newly available antibiotics, such as penicillin and streptomycin, helped them keep their tissue cultures free of the bacteria that had so often ruined such experiments in the past. In 1948 they had some poliovirus sitting in the lab freezer, and they decided to try putting it into tubes of human tissue that was not nerve tissue at all.","To almost everyone's surprise, the virus grew. They could even see the effect under a microscope: the cells in infected tubes became damaged and died, while cells in uninfected tubes stayed healthy. That gave scientists a way to tell whether live virus was present in a sample, and whether a person's blood contained antibodies that could block it, all without using a single monkey for the test. Enders, Weller and Robbins published their discovery in the journal Science in January 1949.","It is hard to overstate how important this was. Now poliovirus could be grown in large quantities in flasks of cells, such as cells from monkey kidneys, instead of in the nervous systems of living animals. The method, called tissue culture or cell culture, became one of the basic tools of modern virology, and it is still used to make many vaccines today. In 1954 the three men shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, and Enders is sometimes called the father of modern vaccines.","The new tool arrived just in time, because polio was getting worse, not better. The year 1952 brought the worst polio epidemic in American history: 57,628 reported cases, 3,145 deaths and more than 21,000 people left with some degree of paralysis. Parents who had grown up during the 1916 epidemic now watched their own children face an even bigger one. The pressure on scientists to find a vaccine had never been greater.","Two researchers in particular were determined to be first. In Pittsburgh, a young scientist named Jonas Salk believed the safest approach was to kill the virus completely and inject it, so the immune system could learn its shape without any risk of infection. In Cincinnati, an older, sharp-tongued researcher named Albert Sabin was sure that only a live but weakened virus, swallowed by mouth, would give strong, long-lasting protection. Both men were brilliant, both were stubborn, and they disagreed with each other loudly. The race between their two very different ideas was about to begin."]}],"deepDive":"Why did polio become epidemic just as public health improved? The most widely accepted answer is often called the hygiene or delayed-exposure explanation. In places with poor sanitation, nearly every child is infected with poliovirus in the first year or two of life, while still partly protected by maternal antibodies passed across the placenta and in breast milk. Paralysis is rarer in infants than in older children and adults, so infection in infancy produces widespread immunity with relatively little visible disease. When sanitation improves, the average age of first infection rises, maternal protection is gone, and the proportion of infections that lead to paralysis climbs. Supporting evidence includes the observation that early 20th-century epidemics in the US and Scandinavia often hit better-off families hard, and that the age of polio patients rose over the first half of the century, with more cases among older children, teenagers and adults by the 1940s and 1950s. Historians caution that other factors may also have mattered, including changes in virus strains, better reporting and diagnosis, and population growth in cities, so the hygiene explanation is the leading account rather than the whole story. The numbers themselves need care too. Reported case counts from the early 1900s mostly reflect paralytic cases that doctors noticed, while the true number of infections was hundreds of times higher because most were silent. Figures for the 1916 epidemic differ slightly between sources: about 27,000 US cases and somewhere between 6,000 and 7,000 deaths, with over 2,000 deaths in New York City. Roosevelt's diagnosis is another live debate. A 2003 analysis in the Journal of Medical Biography used his symptoms and Bayesian reasoning to argue that Guillain-Barré syndrome was more likely; critics replied that the evidence is incomplete and polio remains plausible. Finally, the iron lung story shows how technology and epidemics shape each other: the 1952 Copenhagen epidemic, where death rates among patients with breathing paralysis fell sharply after the switch to tube-and-bag positive-pressure ventilation, is widely seen as the birth of intensive care medicine.","funFact":"When Drinker and Shaw built their first iron lung prototype in 1928, they powered it with air pumps borrowed from two vacuum cleaners.","check":{"q":"What breakthrough did John Enders, Thomas Weller and Frederick Robbins announce in 1949 that made a polio vaccine possible?","a":"They grew poliovirus in tubes of human tissue that was not nerve tissue","wrong":["They invented the iron lung to help paralysed patients breathe","They proved that cats and flies spread polio","They photographed the poliovirus with an ordinary light microscope"]},"sources":[{"title":"Poliomyelitis (fact sheet)","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/poliomyelitis"},{"title":"Polio","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polio"},{"title":"History of polio","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_polio"},{"title":"1916 New York City polio epidemic","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1916_New_York_City_polio_epidemic"},{"title":"Franklin D. Roosevelt's paralytic illness","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franklin_D._Roosevelt%27s_paralytic_illness"},{"title":"March of Dimes","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/March_of_Dimes"},{"title":"Iron lung","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_lung"},{"title":"John Franklin Enders","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Franklin_Enders"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/13-1.jpg","alt":"An iron lung breathing machine, used by polio patients who could not breathe alone, at the Thackray Museum of Medicine.","file":"Iron lung on display at Thackray Museum of Medicine.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iron_lung_on_display_at_Thackray_Museum_of_Medicine.jpg","author":"Museumjack","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/13-2.jpg","alt":"An ancient Egyptian stone carving (18th Dynasty) of a man with a thin, shortened leg, often thought to show polio.","file":"Polio Egyptian Stele.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polio_Egyptian_Stele.jpg","author":"Deutsches Grünes Kreuz","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/13-3.jpg","alt":"A 1930s poster for a President's Birthday Ball, a dance held to raise money to fight infantile paralysis (polio).","file":"Presidents-Birthday-Ball-1939.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Presidents-Birthday-Ball-1939.jpg","author":"Works Progress Administration, Federal Art Project; designer unknown","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4057},{"n":14,"title":"Salk, Sabin and the Polio Pioneers","when":"1952 – today","summary":"Jonas Salk made a polio vaccine from killed virus, and almost two million children helped test it in the biggest medical trial the world had ever seen. Then Albert Sabin made a different vaccine you could swallow on a sugar cube. Together their vaccines made polio cases crash, and today the disease is close to being gone for good.","sections":[{"heading":"Jonas Salk's Killed-Virus Plan","paragraphs":["Jonas Salk was born in New York City on 28 October 1914, the son of Jewish immigrant parents who had little money but big hopes for their children. He was the first in his family to go to university, studying chemistry at the City College of New York and then medicine at New York University. Instead of treating patients one at a time, he decided he wanted to do research that could help millions of people at once.","In 1941 he went to the University of Michigan to work with the virologist Thomas Francis Jr., one of America's leading experts on influenza. Together they worked on a flu vaccine for the US Army during the Second World War. That vaccine used influenza virus that had been killed with chemicals, and it worked. The experience convinced Salk of something important: you did not need a live virus to teach the immune system. A killed, or inactivated, virus could still show the body the virus's shape, its antigens, so the body could make antibodies against it.","In 1947 Salk set up his own laboratory at the University of Pittsburgh. The next year, the National Foundation for Infantile Paralysis, the March of Dimes charity you met in the last part, asked him to join a huge, dull but essential project: sorting all the known samples of poliovirus into types. The work confirmed that there were exactly three types of poliovirus. That mattered enormously. Any vaccine would have to protect against all three, or children would still catch the types it missed.","Salk then set to work on his vaccine. His team grew large amounts of each of the three types of poliovirus in flasks of monkey kidney cells, using the tissue culture method that John Enders, Thomas Weller and Frederick Robbins had pioneered. They then treated the virus with formaldehyde, a chemical that damages the virus so it can no longer infect cells, while leaving its outer shape mostly intact. The trick was getting the dose and timing exactly right: too little formaldehyde and some virus might survive; too much and the virus might be so damaged that it no longer trained the immune system well. Salk worked out careful rules for how long to treat the virus, and tested every batch.","In 1952 he began testing his vaccine in people. On 2 July 1952 he vaccinated 43 children at the D.T. Watson Home for Crippled Children near Pittsburgh, many of whom already had antibodies because they had survived polio. A few weeks later he vaccinated children and adults at the Polk State School, a home for people with intellectual disabilities. Today we would ask hard questions about testing a new vaccine at such institutions, where people could not easily give their own free consent; in the 1950s, rules about medical research were far weaker than they are now. In 1953 Salk vaccinated himself, his wife and his three sons, to show how much he trusted his work. Blood tests showed that vaccinated people were making antibodies against poliovirus, and nobody became ill. It was promising, but promising is not proof. To know whether the vaccine actually prevented polio, it would need to be tested on a gigantic scale."]},{"heading":"Henrietta's Cells","paragraphs":["Testing a vaccine on hundreds of thousands of children meant doing an enormous number of laboratory tests: checking batches for live virus, and checking blood samples for antibodies. Those tests needed living cells, and monkey cells were expensive and limited. A surprising answer came from a hospital in Baltimore, and from a young woman who never knew about it.","Henrietta Lacks was an African American mother of five, born in 1920 in Virginia. In early 1951 she went to Johns Hopkins Hospital in Baltimore, one of the few hospitals in the area that treated Black patients at the time, because she was ill. Doctors found she had cervical cancer. During her treatment, a surgeon took small samples of tissue from her tumour. Nobody asked her permission or told her what the samples would be used for. Sadly, the cancer spread quickly, and Henrietta Lacks died on 4 October 1951. She was 31 years old.","Her tissue samples went to the lab of a researcher named George Gey, who had been trying for years to keep human cells alive and dividing in the lab. Every earlier attempt had failed: human cells would divide a few times and then die. But Henrietta's cancer cells were different. They divided again and again, doubling roughly every day, and never stopped. Gey's lab named them HeLa cells, from the first two letters of her first and last names. They became the first human cells that could be grown in huge amounts indefinitely, which is why scientists call them an immortal cell line.","HeLa cells turned out to be easily infected by poliovirus, and when they were infected they died in a way scientists could see under a microscope. That made them perfect for polio testing. To supply the vaccine trial, a special laboratory was set up at the Tuskegee Institute, a historically Black university in Alabama, where a team of Black scientists and technicians grew HeLa cells in very large quantities and shipped them to testing labs around the country. HeLa cells went on to be used in research on cancer, viruses, genetics and many other areas, in labs all over the world.","Henrietta Lacks's family was not told about her cells until 1975, more than twenty years after her death, when they found out almost by chance. They were hurt and confused to learn that a part of her was being used all over the world while they had not even been asked. Her story became widely known through the 2010 book The Immortal Life of Henrietta Lacks by Rebecca Skloot. In 2013 the US National Institutes of Health reached an agreement giving her family a say in how the genetic information from HeLa cells is shared. In 2021 the World Health Organization gave a special award to her son Lawrence Lacks to honour her contribution to medicine.","Henrietta Lacks did not volunteer and did not donate her cells; they were taken without her knowledge. Today, rules in many countries require that people be told and asked before their tissue is used for research. Her story is now an important part of how scientists think about fairness, consent and respect, and it is a reminder that the polio vaccine, like so many advances, depended on people whose names were left out of the first history books."]},{"heading":"The Polio Pioneers","paragraphs":["In 1954 the National Foundation organised the biggest test of a medical product the world had ever seen. To make sure the results would be trusted, it asked Salk's old mentor Thomas Francis Jr. to run the trial independently from the University of Michigan in Ann Arbor. Salk himself would not be in charge of judging his own vaccine. That separation was a key part of good science: the person who invented something should not be the only one deciding whether it works.","The trial began in late April 1954 in McLean, Virginia, and spread across 44 US states, as well as parts of Canada and Finland. In the end about 1.8 million children took part, most of them in their first, second or third year of primary school. They became known as the Polio Pioneers, and many received a little button or card to prove it. Parents had to sign forms agreeing to let their children take part, and hundreds of thousands of parents did, which shows how frightened they were of polio and how much they hoped for a vaccine.","Francis used two different designs at once. In some areas, children in the second grade were offered the vaccine, while children in the first and third grades were simply watched to see how many caught polio. These were called observed controls. In other areas, Francis used a stronger design, called a placebo-controlled, double-blind trial, in which some children received a placebo, a harmless injection that looked exactly the same but contained no vaccine. Across the whole trial, about 440,000 children received the real vaccine, about 210,000 received a placebo, and around 1.2 million more were observed controls.","Double-blind meant that neither the children and their parents nor the doctors and nurses giving the injections knew who was getting the real vaccine and who was getting the placebo. Each bottle had only a code number. Why go to such trouble? Because people's hopes and fears can influence what they see. If a doctor knew a child had been vaccinated, they might, without meaning to, be less likely to diagnose a mild case of polio as polio. Blinding protects the results from those hidden biases, so that any difference between the two groups is very likely to come from the vaccine itself.","The trial was a gigantic team effort. More than 100 staff at the University of Michigan worked on it, along with tens of thousands of doctors, nurses, school principals and teachers, plus a huge network of community volunteers who kept records and organised schedules. Each child needed three injections. Blood samples were collected from some children to measure antibodies. Every reported case of polio among the participants was carefully investigated. The records filled mountains of paper, and the results were counted with the help of punched-card tabulating machines, early ancestors of computers. The cost was about 17.5 million dollars, paid largely from the dimes and dollars of ordinary people. Then the whole country had to wait almost a year for the answer."]},{"heading":"Safe, Effective and Potent","paragraphs":["On the morning of 12 April 1955, hundreds of people crowded into the Rackham Auditorium at the University of Michigan in Ann Arbor. There were scientists, officials from the National Foundation, and a large crowd of reporters, with television cameras and radio microphones ready. Across the country, people gathered around radios and televisions, and some cities even arranged for the news to be broadcast in public. The date had been chosen with care: it was exactly ten years since the death of Franklin Roosevelt, the man who had started the March of Dimes.","Thomas Francis Jr. stepped up to report the results. In plain words, the message that went round the world was that the Salk vaccine was safe, effective and potent. In the placebo-controlled part of the trial, the vaccine was about 60 to 70 percent effective against type 1 poliovirus, the most common cause of paralysis, and over 90 percent effective against types 2 and 3. It was about 94 percent effective against bulbar polio, the deadly kind that stopped people breathing. It was not perfect, as no vaccine is, but it was a huge step forward.","The reaction was astonishing. Church bells rang in some towns. Shops put signs in their windows saying thank you, Dr Salk. Teachers let classes out early. That same afternoon, the US government licensed the vaccine, and companies that had already been making it in advance began shipping it out. Jonas Salk, who was 40 years old, became one of the most famous people in the world almost overnight.","That evening, the television journalist Edward R. Murrow interviewed Salk and asked him who owned the patent on the vaccine. Salk replied that it belonged to the people, and then asked, could you patent the sun? A patent is a legal right that lets an inventor control who can make an invention and charge money for it. Historians point out that lawyers had already looked into whether the vaccine could be patented and doubted it could, because it used methods that were already known. Even so, Salk's answer became famous, and he never made money from the vaccine itself.","Salk was careful to praise the many people who had helped, but some of his colleagues felt he had not mentioned their names enough in his speech that day, and some fellow scientists were jealous of his sudden fame. He later founded the Salk Institute for Biological Studies in La Jolla, California, which opened in 1963 and is still a leading research centre. He died in 1995, at the age of 80."]},{"heading":"The Cutter Incident","paragraphs":["Within days of the celebration, something went badly wrong. Several companies were making the Salk vaccine, and making it on a huge scale for the first time. One of them, Cutter Laboratories in Berkeley, California, produced some batches in which the formaldehyde had not killed all of the virus. Some of the vaccine still contained live, dangerous poliovirus.","In late April 1955, doctors began reporting cases of paralytic polio in children who had just received the vaccine, often starting in the arm where they had been injected. On 27 April Cutter's vaccine was withdrawn, and vaccination across the country was briefly paused while every batch was checked. About 120,000 doses of the faulty vaccine had been given. Around 40,000 children developed mild, short-lived polio illness. About 200 people, many of them children and some of them family members or neighbours who caught the virus from vaccinated children, were left with some paralysis, and about ten died. Different studies count the cases slightly differently, but this was a tragedy, and the families affected never forgot it.","Investigators found that the safety rules had not been strict enough. Tiny clumps of virus could be protected from the formaldehyde, and the tests used to check each batch were not sensitive enough to find small amounts of surviving virus. The government laboratory that was supposed to oversee vaccine makers had trusted the companies' reports too much. Several senior officials lost their jobs or resigned, including the head of the US National Institutes of Health.","The Cutter incident changed vaccine safety forever. The government created much stricter rules for how vaccines are made, filtered, tested and inspected, and set up a stronger system for overseeing them. A court case, Gottsdanker v. Cutter Laboratories, found that a company could be held responsible for harm caused by its vaccine even if it had not been careless, which shaped laws about vaccines for decades. In a way, the strict safety testing described in part 12 of this story grew partly out of this painful lesson. With the new rules in place, vaccination started again, and the Salk vaccine went on to protect many millions of children.","The results were dramatic. In 1952 the United States had reported almost 58,000 polio cases. By 1957, after two years of Salk vaccine, there were about 5,600. Parents who had dreaded every summer began, slowly, to relax."]},{"heading":"Albert Sabin's Sugar Cubes","paragraphs":["Albert Sabin did not think the Salk vaccine was good enough. Sabin was born Abram Saperstejn in 1906 in Białystok, then part of the Russian Empire and now in Poland. His Jewish family emigrated to the United States in 1921 to escape poverty and persecution, and he grew up to study medicine and become an expert on viruses. From 1939 he worked at the Children's Hospital in Cincinnati, Ohio, where he spent about thirty years.","Sabin had made an important discovery by studying people who had died of polio. He showed that the virus multiplies first in the gut, and only later, in a few people, reaches the nervous system. So, he reasoned, the best vaccine would build protection right where the virus first takes hold: in the gut. An injected, killed vaccine like Salk's taught the blood to make antibodies, which stopped the virus reaching the nerves, but it did not stop the virus from multiplying in the intestines and being passed on to others nearly as well.","Sabin's idea was a live attenuated vaccine. Attenuated means weakened. He grew the three types of poliovirus over and over again in cells, at lower-than-normal temperatures and under other conditions, picking out strains that had changed so they could still multiply in the gut but had almost lost the ability to damage nerves. A person would swallow these weakened viruses. The viruses would multiply harmlessly in the gut for a while, and the immune system would learn to fight them, both in the blood and in the lining of the intestines.","It was not the only live polio vaccine. Another scientist, Hilary Koprowski, had already given a live, weakened oral vaccine to a child in 1950, and his vaccines were used in some countries. But Sabin's strains became the ones used worldwide.","By the mid-1950s, the US already had the Salk vaccine, and most American families had received it, so it was hard to run a big trial of Sabin's vaccine there. The answer came from an unexpected place: the Soviet Union, America's great rival in the Cold War. Soviet scientists Mikhail Chumakov and Marina Voroshilova worked with Sabin, who shared his virus strains and advice on how to make the vaccine. In 1959 about ten million children in the Soviet Union received the oral vaccine, and by 1961 tens of millions more people had been given it across the Soviet Union, Eastern Europe and other countries. The results showed it was safe and very effective. At a time when the two superpowers were building nuclear weapons aimed at each other, their scientists were quietly working together against a shared enemy.","The oral vaccine had another great advantage: it was easy to give. There was no needle. A few drops of liquid were placed in the mouth, or dripped onto a sugar cube to hide the bitter taste, and children simply ate the sugar cube. Huge numbers of people could be vaccinated in one day by volunteers. In 1960 more than 78,000 schoolchildren in Cincinnati received the oral vaccine. In the US it was licensed in stages, for types 1 and 2 in 1961 and type 3 in 1962, and a combined version came a little later. On special mass vaccination days, families lined up at schools and clinics to eat their sugar cubes. Like Salk, Sabin did not make a fortune from his vaccine. He died in 1993, at the age of 86."]},{"heading":"Two Vaccines, One Trade-off","paragraphs":["So which vaccine was better? The honest answer is that each has strengths and weaknesses, and doctors have been weighing them for over sixty years.","The oral vaccine is cheap, easy to give without needles or trained nurses, and builds strong protection in the gut. That gut protection is what stops vaccinated people from passing on wild poliovirus, the natural, dangerous virus, so it is very good at stopping outbreaks. Weakened vaccine virus can even spread from a vaccinated child to people around them, which can quietly immunise them too.","But the oral vaccine has a rare downside. Because it contains live virus, on very rare occasions the weakened virus changes back into a form that can cause paralysis. This happens in roughly one in every 2.7 million doses. And in communities where few children are vaccinated, the weakened virus can pass from person to person for a long time, slowly changing until it can cause outbreaks of its own. These are called vaccine-derived polio outbreaks, and they are rare but real. The injected Salk-type vaccine, by contrast, contains only killed virus. It can never cause polio, though it is more expensive, needs a needle and a trained health worker, and is less good at stopping the virus spreading through the gut.","For this reason, as polio disappeared from wealthy countries, many of them switched to the injected vaccine, often called IPV, for inactivated polio vaccine. An improved, more powerful version of Salk's vaccine was licensed in the US in 1987, and by around 2000 the US was using only injected vaccine. Many countries where polio was a bigger threat continued to use the oral vaccine, often called OPV, and many now use a mix of both. Scientists have also designed a new, more stable version of the oral vaccine for type 2, called nOPV2, which is much less likely to change back into a dangerous form; it was fully approved by WHO in December 2023.","Thanks to both vaccines, polio fell away with astonishing speed. US cases dropped from about 58,000 in 1952 to just 161 in 1961, well under 1,000 a year. The last cases caused by wild poliovirus spreading naturally in the United States happened in 1979. Other wealthy countries saw the same thing. A disease that had terrified parents every summer for half a century had become rare within a decade.","Around the world, the fight went on. In 1988, when wild poliovirus was still paralysing an estimated 350,000 children a year in more than 125 countries, the World Health Organization, UNICEF, Rotary International and other partners launched the Global Polio Eradication Initiative. Since then, wild polio cases have fallen by more than 99 percent. Wild poliovirus type 2 was declared eradicated in 2015, and type 3 in 2019. Only wild type 1 is left, and it still spreads in parts of just two countries, Pakistan and Afghanistan. Polio is not yet eradicated, but it is closer than ever before. You will hear how that final push is going in part 19.","Salk and Sabin argued with each other for the rest of their lives about whose vaccine was best. Yet in the end, the world needed both of them, along with Enders, Weller and Robbins, Henrietta Lacks, Thomas Francis Jr., the scientists at Tuskegee, Mikhail Chumakov, and nearly two million Polio Pioneers. Their success raised a thrilling question. If polio could be pushed out of one country, could a disease be wiped out everywhere, from every country on Earth, forever? Scientists already had a target in mind: the oldest enemy in this whole story, smallpox."]}],"deepDive":"The 1954 Francis Field Trial is a landmark in the history of medical statistics. Its organisers faced a hard problem: paralytic polio was rare even in epidemic years, striking only a few dozen children per 100,000, so a trial had to be enormous to show a clear difference. The National Foundation originally favoured an observed-control design, vaccinating second-graders and comparing them with unvaccinated first- and third-graders. Francis and many statisticians worried this would be biased: parents who volunteered their children might differ from those who did not, and doctors who knew a child's vaccination status might diagnose borderline cases differently. The compromise was to run both designs. In the randomised, double-blind placebo areas, roughly 200,000 children received vaccine and a similar number received placebo, and paralytic polio was markedly less common in the vaccine group. The observed-control areas gave broadly similar results, but statisticians still point to the placebo arm as the more trustworthy evidence, and the trial is often taught as a classic example of why randomisation and blinding matter. The Cutter incident is debated differently. Paul Offit's 2005 history argues that the US lawsuit that followed, Gottsdanker v. Cutter Laboratories, which held a manufacturer liable without proof of negligence, helped push companies out of vaccine making and eventually led to the US National Childhood Vaccine Injury Act of 1986, which created a no-fault compensation programme. Counts of Cutter-related cases also vary between sources, from about 200 to over 250 paralytic cases including family contacts, depending on how cases were defined. The Salk-versus-Sabin debate continues in modified form today. Oral vaccine is better at blocking intestinal infection and transmission, which made it the workhorse of global eradication, but each dose carries a tiny risk of vaccine-associated paralysis, and in poorly immunised populations vaccine-derived viruses can circulate. In 2016 every country using oral vaccine switched from the three-type version to a two-type version without type 2, because wild type 2 had been eradicated and the type 2 vaccine strain was causing most vaccine-derived outbreaks. The eventual goal is to stop all oral vaccine use once wild virus is gone, relying on inactivated vaccine to protect against any lingering risk.","funFact":"When Salk's polio vaccine results were announced on 12 April 1955, the date was chosen to fall exactly ten years after the death of Franklin Roosevelt, who had founded the March of Dimes to fight polio.","check":{"q":"What was the main difference between Jonas Salk's and Albert Sabin's polio vaccines?","a":"Salk's was an injection of killed (inactivated) virus; Sabin's was swallowed and used live, weakened virus","wrong":["Salk's used live virus on a sugar cube; Sabin's was a killed-virus injection","Salk's was made from cowpox; Sabin's was made from horsepox","Both were nasal sprays, but Sabin's was given only to adults"]},"sources":[{"title":"Jonas Salk","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jonas_Salk"},{"title":"Albert Sabin","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Sabin"},{"title":"Polio vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polio_vaccine"},{"title":"Thomas Francis Jr.","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Francis_Jr."},{"title":"Cutter Laboratories","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cutter_Laboratories"},{"title":"Henrietta Lacks","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henrietta_Lacks"},{"title":"Poliomyelitis (fact sheet)","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/poliomyelitis"},{"title":"HeLa","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HeLa"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/14-1.jpg","alt":"Dr Jonas Salk, creator of the first polio vaccine, at Copenhagen Airport.","file":"Jonas Salk candid.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jonas_Salk_candid.jpg","author":"SAS Scandinavian Airlines","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/14-2.jpg","alt":"Albert Sabin, who made the oral polio vaccine, at his microscope.","file":"Albert Bruce Sabin in EID Journal.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albert_Bruce_Sabin_in_EID_Journal.jpg","author":"Not stated","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/14-3.jpg","alt":"A health worker gives oral polio vaccine drops to a baby in a farming community in Nigeria.","file":"Polio Vaccination - Nigeria (16869996070).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polio_Vaccination_-_Nigeria_(16869996070).jpg","author":"CDC Global","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":3947},{"n":15,"title":"Wiping Smallpox Off the Earth","when":"1958 – 1980","summary":"In 1967, smallpox still made millions of people sick every year. A worldwide team of health workers used a tiny two-pronged needle and a clever plan: find every case, then protect everyone around it. By 1980 smallpox was gone from the whole planet, the first human disease ever wiped out.","sections":[{"heading":"A Bold Idea in Minneapolis","paragraphs":["In 1958, health ministers from around the world gathered in Minneapolis, in the United States, for the yearly meeting of the World Health Assembly, the group of countries that guides the World Health Organization (WHO). One of the speakers was Viktor Zhdanov, a virus scientist who was then Deputy Minister of Health of the Soviet Union. This was the middle of the Cold War, when the Soviet Union and the United States distrusted each other and competed in almost everything, from rockets to Olympic medals. Zhdanov stood up and suggested something that sounded almost impossible: the whole world should work together to get rid of smallpox completely.","His idea was not a wild dream. Smallpox had some weaknesses that made it a good target. It only infected humans, so there were no animals where the virus could hide and come back later. People who were infected nearly always got a rash you could see, so cases were hard to miss. And there was already a vaccine that worked very well. Zhdanov also pointed out that his own country had beaten smallpox inside its borders, and he promised that the Soviet Union would donate large amounts of vaccine.","The World Health Assembly agreed, and in 1959 the global smallpox eradication programme officially began. Eradication means removing a disease from the whole world forever, not just from one country. Some countries had already managed that local step. Smallpox had stopped spreading in North America by 1952 and in Europe by 1953, thanks to vaccination, careful reporting of cases and good health services.","But the first years of the world programme went slowly. WHO had very little money for it, few staff, and many governments had other urgent problems. The main plan was mass vaccination: try to vaccinate around 80 percent of the people in every country and hope the disease would run out of people to infect. In big, crowded countries with fast-growing populations, this was like trying to empty a bathtub with a teaspoon while the tap was still running. Vaccine quality was uneven too. Some batches arriving from different makers had lost their strength in the heat before they ever reached an arm.","By the mid-1960s, smallpox was still widespread across much of Africa and South Asia, and in Brazil. In 1966, the World Health Assembly decided to try again, with a bigger budget and a fresh plan. The United States and the Soviet Union, rivals in so many ways, ended up supporting the same campaign. It was one of the rare moments in the Cold War when both sides pulled in the same direction."]},{"heading":"1967: A Fresh Start with D.A. Henderson","paragraphs":["The new Intensified Smallpox Eradication Programme began on 1 January 1967. To lead it, WHO chose Donald Ainslie Henderson, an American doctor usually known as D.A. Henderson. He was 38 years old, tall, energetic and famously blunt. He had been running disease surveillance programmes at the US Communicable Disease Center (now called the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, or CDC) in Atlanta, and his team there had helped plan a big smallpox and measles programme for 18 countries in western and central Africa.","Henderson moved to WHO's headquarters in Geneva, Switzerland. What he faced was enormous. In 1967 smallpox was still endemic, meaning it was constantly spreading, in 31 countries. Experts estimated there were somewhere between 10 and 15 million cases a year, and about 2 million deaths. Only a small fraction of cases were ever officially reported, because many happened in remote villages far from any clinic. Part of the job was simply finding out where the disease really was.","Henderson set a target that many people thought was foolishly optimistic: smallpox should be gone in about ten years. He did not have a huge army or endless money. Instead, the programme relied on the national health services of each country, with their own vaccinators, nurses and officials, helped by a smaller group of international advisers. Over the years, staff and advisers from some 73 countries joined in. The CDC alone sent 26 young doctors and public health advisers in the early days, to help with training, transport and planning while local health workers did the vaccinating.","Henderson believed in two things above all. The first was good tools: vaccine that stayed strong in hot climates, and a simple way of giving it. The second was good information: every country needed a system for reporting each case quickly, so teams knew where to go, and every report needed checking. If a country claimed to have no smallpox, Henderson wanted to see evidence, not just hear promises.","Region by region, the map began to change. South America was cleared by 1971, with Brazil the last country there to stop the spread. Asia followed by 1975 and Africa by 1977. Each of those victories involved thousands of people, long journeys, setbacks and some very clever thinking, as you will see."]},{"heading":"Two Small Tools That Changed Everything","paragraphs":["The first tool was the vaccine itself. Smallpox vaccine is made from a living virus called vaccinia, a cousin of smallpox that trains the body without causing smallpox. In its old liquid form, the vaccine spoiled quickly in heat, which was a disaster in tropical countries without refrigerators. In the 1950s, British scientist Leslie Collier, working at the Lister Institute in London, improved a method for freeze-drying the vaccine. Freeze-drying means freezing a liquid and then removing the water as vapour, leaving a dry powder. Collier found that adding a protein mixture called peptone protected the virus during drying. His dried vaccine stayed effective for months even when kept at 37 degrees Celsius, about the temperature of a hot day in the tropics.","The dry powder could be carried in small glass vials for weeks across deserts and jungles. At the moment of use, the vaccinator mixed it with a little liquid. Once mixed, it only stayed good for a day or two in the heat, so teams planned carefully to use each vial quickly. During the intensified programme, WHO also checked the quality of vaccine from different factories, and donor countries, with the Soviet Union and the United States among the biggest, sent huge amounts of vaccine. Over time, more and more of it was made in laboratories inside the countries that needed it.","The second tool looked almost too simple to matter. In 1965, American microbiologist Benjamin Rubin, who worked at the drug company Wyeth, took the eye of a sewing-machine needle and ground it down to make a tiny fork with two prongs. This was the bifurcated needle, from a Latin word meaning 'two-forked'. When you dipped it into the vaccine, a single dose clung between the two prongs, held there by surface tension, the same force that lets a water droplet hang from a tap.","Using it was easy. The vaccinator held the needle upright against the upper arm and made about 15 quick jabs in a small circle about 5 millimetres across. The prongs only went into the top layer of skin, which is where the smallpox vaccine needs to be. A few days later, if the vaccine had 'taken', a small blister formed, then a scab, which fell off after two or three weeks and left a small round scar. Many grandparents still have this scar on their upper arm today.","The bifurcated needle had huge advantages. It used only about a quarter as much vaccine as older methods, so one vial could vaccinate up to about 100 people. The needles cost only about 5 US dollars per thousand, and they could be boiled or flamed and used again and again. Best of all, a person could learn to use one well in less than an hour, even with no medical training. Henderson's team adopted it for smallpox in 1967, and it was soon in use around the world. Rubin later estimated that in the final years of the campaign, about 200 million vaccinations a year were given with it. More than 7 million of these little forks were used in India alone."]},{"heading":"Find the Fire, Put It Out","paragraphs":["Good tools were not enough on their own. The most important change in the campaign was a new strategy, and it began with a young American doctor named William Foege, working as a medical missionary in eastern Nigeria in 1966.","When smallpox broke out in the area where he worked, Foege faced a frustrating problem: there was not nearly enough vaccine to vaccinate everybody. So instead of spreading the vaccine thinly over the whole population, he decided to aim it. Using radio messages between missionary stations and teams of local runners, he and his colleagues tracked down where the new cases were. Then they vaccinated the people around each case: family members, neighbours, and anyone who might have been in contact. They also looked for the next places the disease was likely to reach, such as villages connected by markets and family visits, and vaccinated there first.","To everyone's surprise, the outbreak stopped, even though only a fairly small share of the whole population had been vaccinated. Foege had shown that you did not need to vaccinate everyone to stop smallpox. You needed to vaccinate the right people, in the right places, quickly.","This approach became known as 'surveillance and containment'. Surveillance means watching carefully for cases and reporting them fast. Containment means building a wall of protection around each case. Later, people often called it 'ring vaccination', because you can imagine a ring of vaccinated people drawn around each sick person, and then a second ring around the contacts of those contacts. A simpler version had actually been tried in the English town of Leicester in the late 1800s, but Foege and the WHO teams turned it into a careful, organised system that could work across whole countries.","Think of smallpox like a fire jumping from house to house. Mass vaccination is like trying to soak every house in the country with water before the fire arrives. Ring vaccination is like having lookouts on hilltops who spot every new flame and then wetting all the houses around it, so the fire has nothing left to burn.","Ring vaccination worked especially well for smallpox for several reasons. People with smallpox were usually too sick to travel far, and they were most infectious once the rash appeared, so they did not usually spread it widely before anyone noticed. The rash was easy to recognise. Smallpox spread mainly through close, face-to-face contact, so the people at risk could be listed. And the vaccine could still help someone who had already been exposed, if it was given within a few days. That gave the teams a small but precious window of time.","By the late 1960s, surveillance and containment had become the heart of the whole programme. Mass vaccination did not stop completely, but the hunt for each individual case became the main job. Foege later wrote a book about these years called 'House on Fire', a title that fits his way of thinking perfectly."]},{"heading":"An Army of Searchers","paragraphs":["Finding every single case of a disease, in countries with hundreds of millions of people, many of them in villages without roads, telephones or electricity, was the hardest part of the job. It was done mostly by local health workers from each country, often travelling on foot, by bicycle, by boat or on the backs of animals.","The toughest fight came in South Asia. In 1974, India suffered a terrible smallpox epidemic, especially in the states of Bihar and Uttar Pradesh. Together those two states had about three quarters of all the world's smallpox cases that year. In May 1974 alone, India recorded more than 48,000 new cases. Altogether, the epidemic infected roughly 188,000 people and about 31,000 died. Some people began to wonder if eradication was impossible after all.","Instead of giving up, India and WHO organised one of the largest health searches in history. Teams went house to house, village by village, asking the same question again and again: have you seen anyone with a rash and fever? Extra containment teams, helicopters, vaccination checkpoints on roads and railways, and hundreds of vehicles were brought in. The Tata company, which ran a large steel works in Bihar where an outbreak had spread among workers, lent doctors, vehicles and communications. Sweden gave about 14 million US dollars, and the CDC sent trained staff.","One clever tool was the smallpox recognition card. It was a small card with a clear photograph of a child with the smallpox rash. Searchers showed it to villagers, shopkeepers, teachers and schoolchildren and asked: have you seen anyone who looks like this? A picture worked even where people could not read, and it helped people tell smallpox apart from milder rashes such as chickenpox. A photograph of a child from Bangladesh became one of the most widely shown pictures in the campaign.","Another tool was the reward. Anyone who reported a real case of smallpox could receive money. As cases became rarer, the rewards grew larger, which kept people looking. Posters announced the rewards in local languages in markets, bus stations and schools. Children, who wandered everywhere and noticed everything, often turned out to be excellent reporters.","Each case that was found led to a busy routine. A guard might be posted at the house so that visitors stayed away while the patient was sick. Everyone in the house and nearby houses was vaccinated, and contacts were checked again and again for the next couple of weeks. Workers drew maps marking each case and each exposed person. The searchers came back to the same areas weeks later to make sure nothing had been missed.","The effort paid off. India's last case of smallpox was recorded in May 1975. In neighbouring Bangladesh, which had been hit hard by war, floods and the movement of refugees, teams kept searching until the very last outbreaks were found."]},{"heading":"The Last Cases","paragraphs":["On 16 October 1975, on her third birthday, a little girl named Rahima Banu was reported to the smallpox team. She lived in the village of Kuralia, on Bhola Island in Bangladesh, where wide rivers meet the Bay of Bengal. The person who reported her was an eight-year-old girl called Bilkisunnessa, who received a reward of 250 taka. A telegram carried the news all the way to Henderson in Geneva.","Rahima Banu had variola major, the dangerous form of smallpox. A team moved in at once. A guard stayed at her house day and night, and everyone in the village and the surrounding area was vaccinated in a ring around her. She recovered and was declared cured on 24 November 1975. No one else caught smallpox from her. She turned out to be the last person in the world to catch variola major naturally. Scabs from her illness were sent to the CDC in Atlanta, where the virus sample is still known as 'Bangladesh 1975'.","Rahima Banu grew up, married and had four children. In interviews as an adult, she has said she is proud of her small part in history, but she has also spoken honestly about how hard life has been for her family, who have lived in poverty, and about people who unfairly treated her as if she might still be dangerous. Her story reminds us that the people behind famous health milestones are real people with lives of their own.","With Asia free, the last battle moved to the Horn of Africa, first Ethiopia and then Somalia, where the milder form, variola minor, was still spreading among nomadic families who moved with their camels and goats across dry country. In 1977 teams of Somali and international workers hunted through huge distances to find each case.","On 12 October 1977, two sick children were found in a nomad camp and taken by vehicle to an isolation camp near the port town of Merca. A 23-year-old hospital cook named Ali Maow Maalin, who sometimes helped the vaccination teams, rode along to show the driver the way. The trip lasted only a few minutes, but it was enough. Maalin had never been successfully vaccinated; he later said he had been afraid of the needle.","Ten days later he became ill. At first his illness was mistaken for malaria and then chickenpox. When a nurse reported his case on 30 October and smallpox was confirmed, an enormous response began. The team found 161 people who had been in contact with him and watched them all for six weeks. Merca's hospital stopped taking new patients, checkpoints were set up around the town, and in two weeks 54,777 people were vaccinated. Nobody else caught smallpox. Maalin recovered fully.","Ali Maow Maalin was the last person in the world known to catch smallpox naturally. Years later, he turned his experience into a mission. He became a volunteer and then a local coordinator in Somalia's campaign against polio, travelling from village to village and telling hesitant parents his own story so they would understand why protecting children mattered. He died of malaria in 2013 while working on that campaign, still helping to fight the next disease."]},{"heading":"One More Tragedy, Then Victory","paragraphs":["There was one more, very sad chapter. In 1978, smallpox virus was still kept in a few research laboratories around the world. One of them was at the University of Birmingham Medical School in England. Janet Parker, a 40-year-old medical photographer, worked in a darkroom on the floor above the smallpox laboratory. In August 1978 she fell ill, and doctors found she had smallpox.","Health officials acted quickly. Everyone who had been near her was traced, vaccinated and watched. Her mother caught a very mild case but recovered. Sadly, Janet Parker died on 11 September 1978. She was the last known person in the world to die of smallpox.","An official inquiry led by microbiologist Reginald Shooter concluded that the virus had most likely escaped from the laboratory below, possibly carried through the air along a service duct, although exactly how she was infected has never been proven. The inquiry found that the laboratory did not meet the required safety standards. As a result, rules for working with dangerous germs in Britain became much stricter, and countries around the world decided that smallpox virus should be kept in as few places as possible.","Meanwhile, the work of proving that smallpox was really gone continued. It is very hard to prove that something no longer exists anywhere. So teams spent two years after the last natural case searching across the countries that had most recently had smallpox, following up every rumour of a rash and testing samples. Rewards were offered for reporting any case at all. Every rumour turned out to be something else, such as chickenpox or measles.","In December 1979, an independent Global Commission of scientists from many countries, meeting at WHO headquarters in Geneva, signed a statement certifying that smallpox had been eradicated. Then, on 8 May 1980, the 33rd World Health Assembly officially declared that the world and all its peoples had won freedom from smallpox. It was almost two centuries since Edward Jenner had vaccinated James Phipps in 1796.","Smallpox was the first human disease ever eradicated, and it is still the only one. Because the disease was gone, the World Health Assembly recommended that countries could stop routine smallpox vaccination, and over the following years they did. Many children born after the 1970s have never needed a smallpox vaccine, which is why younger people usually do not have the little round scar their grandparents may have. The only other disease humans have ever eradicated is rinderpest, a deadly illness of cattle and buffalo. Its last confirmed case was in Kenya in 2001, and its eradication was confirmed in 2011."]},{"heading":"Two Locked Freezers and a Big Debate","paragraphs":["After the Birmingham tragedy, laboratories around the world destroyed their smallpox samples or sent them to safer places. In 1981 four countries still held the virus. By 1984 only two official stores were left, and that is still true today: one at the CDC in Atlanta, in the United States, and one at the VECTOR research centre in Koltsovo, in Russia. Both are high-security laboratories built for the most dangerous germs, and WHO inspects them regularly.","Ever since, people have argued about whether these last samples should be destroyed. Those who want them destroyed say that as long as the virus exists, there is a small risk it could escape or be misused, and that keeping it goes against the whole point of eradication. In 1996, the World Health Assembly agreed that the stocks should be destroyed in June 1999.","But others argued that scientists still needed the live virus to develop better tests, safer vaccines and medicines, just in case smallpox ever came back, for example from a secret or forgotten sample. In 2002, the World Health Assembly allowed the samples to be kept for a while longer, only for approved research. An expert committee meets every year to check what research is being done and whether it is still needed.","The worry about forgotten samples is not just imagination. In 2014, workers clearing out an old storeroom at a US government laboratory in Bethesda, Maryland, found six sealed glass vials labelled 'variola' from the 1950s. Tests showed that virus in at least two of them could still grow. They were destroyed under WHO observation in 2015. The discovery showed how carefully old laboratories need to be checked.","Today the debate continues, with sensible people on both sides. Meanwhile, the legacy of the eradication programme lives on in other ways. The surveillance and containment ideas it pioneered have been used against other diseases, including ring vaccination against Ebola in the 2010s. Many of the people trained in the smallpox campaign went on to lead health programmes around the world, and its success gave energy to new efforts, including the campaigns against polio and Guinea worm disease.","The smallpox story proved something people had not been sure of before: that countries which disagreed about almost everything could work together to defeat a disease everywhere, forever. And while the hunt for the last cases of smallpox was going on, scientists in other laboratories were busy creating a whole new set of vaccines to protect children from measles, mumps, rubella and many other diseases."]}],"deepDive":"Why could smallpox be eradicated when so many other diseases cannot? Epidemiologists usually point to a combination of biological and practical features. Variola virus had no animal reservoir, so once human-to-human transmission stopped, there was nowhere for it to survive in nature. There was no long-term carrier state: people either recovered with lasting immunity or died, and nobody silently carried the virus for years. Infection almost always produced an obvious, distinctive rash, so cases could be found by non-specialists using a picture card. Transmission mostly needed close, prolonged contact, and patients were most infectious after the rash appeared, which limited spread before detection. Finally, a single dose of vaccinia vaccine gave strong protection, and vaccination within a few days after exposure could still prevent or soften disease. Compare this with polio, where most infections cause no symptoms at all, so each visible case of paralysis hides many silent infections, or measles, which is far more contagious and spreads before the rash appears. Historians also debate how much credit belongs to strategy versus resources. The shift from mass vaccination toward surveillance and containment is often presented as the key insight, but in practice the two worked together: high background vaccination levels slowed spread so that containment teams could keep up, and in dense areas like Bihar both approaches ran side by side. Another debate concerns cost-effectiveness. The intensified programme cost a few hundred million US dollars over about a decade, including spending by the affected countries themselves, while the savings from no longer vaccinating, treating cases or checking travellers' vaccination certificates are generally estimated to have repaid that investment many times over every year. Historians such as those writing about the Cold War also stress the politics: a Soviet proposal, American leadership, and money from both superpowers made it a rare cooperative project. Finally, the decision to keep the last variola stocks remains unresolved, balancing the small risk of keeping live virus against the research value of having it to test new antiviral medicines and diagnostic tools.","funFact":"The bifurcated needle that helped wipe out smallpox was made by grinding down the eye of an ordinary sewing-machine needle, and the needles cost only about 5 US dollars per thousand.","check":{"q":"What was the strategy of finding each smallpox case and then vaccinating everyone around it called?","a":"Surveillance and containment (ring vaccination)","wrong":["Mass vaccination of every person in the country","Variolation with scab powder","Arm-to-arm vaccination"]},"sources":[{"title":"History of Smallpox","publisher":"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/smallpox/about/history.html"},{"title":"Smallpox (health topic)","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/health-topics/smallpox"},{"title":"D. A. Henderson","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D._A._Henderson"},{"title":"Smallpox eradication (Story of CDC)","publisher":"CDC David J. Sencer Museum","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/museum/online/story-of-cdc/smallpox/index.html"},{"title":"Bifurcated needle","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bifurcated_needle"},{"title":"Ali Maow Maalin","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Maow_Maalin"},{"title":"Rahima Banu","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rahima_Banu"},{"title":"1974 smallpox epidemic in India","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1974_smallpox_epidemic_in_India"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/15-1.jpg","alt":"A bifurcated (two-pronged) needle: its forked tip holds a tiny drop of smallpox vaccine.","file":"Smallpox vaccination needle.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smallpox_vaccination_needle.jpg","author":"James Gathany","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/15-2.jpg","alt":"1980: three former leaders of the smallpox eradication programme read the news: \"smallpox is dead!\"","file":"Directors of Global Smallpox Eradication Program.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Directors_of_Global_Smallpox_Eradication_Program.jpg","author":"Photo Credit: Content Providers(s): CDC","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/15-3.jpg","alt":"Dr D.A. Henderson, leader of the WHO smallpox eradication programme, with his Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2002.","file":"DAHenderson.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DAHenderson.jpg","author":"Wikifier at English Wikipedia","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4028},{"n":16,"title":"The Golden Age of Childhood Vaccines","when":"1954 – 2000s","summary":"After polio, scientists raced to protect children from measles, mumps, rubella, chickenpox and more. One scientist even made a vaccine from germs he swabbed from his own daughter's throat! Then new programmes worked to bring these vaccines to babies in every country in the world.","sections":[{"heading":"A Boston School and a Boy Named David","paragraphs":["Ask your grandparents about measles, and some of them may remember it well. Before the 1960s, almost every child caught it. Measles is caused by a virus that spreads through the air when an infected person breathes, coughs or sneezes, and it is one of the most contagious diseases known. The virus can stay floating in a room for up to two hours after the sick person has left. One person with measles can pass it on to as many as 18 others who are not immune.","For most children, measles meant a high fever, a cough, red eyes and a blotchy red rash that spread from the face down the body, followed by a week or so in bed. But measles could also cause pneumonia, a serious lung infection, and in about one in a thousand cases, swelling of the brain called encephalitis. In the United States alone, before the vaccine, about 3 to 4 million people caught measles each year, about 48,000 needed hospital care, and an estimated 400 to 500 died. Worldwide, before a vaccine was available, measles is estimated to have caused about 2.6 million deaths every year, most of them young children.","The road to a measles vaccine began in early 1954, during a measles outbreak at a boarding school near Boston, Massachusetts. A young doctor named Thomas Peebles, working in the laboratory of John Enders, went to the school with a simple plan. He asked sick students for blood samples and throat swabs, hoping to catch the virus and grow it in the lab. Enders had just helped solve the problem of growing poliovirus in tissue cultures, the work that would win him a share of the 1954 Nobel Prize, so his laboratory was one of the best places in the world to try.","Peebles succeeded with a sample from a 13-year-old boy named David Edmonston. The virus from David's blood grew in cultures of human and monkey kidney cells, which had never been done reliably with measles before. To this day, the measles virus used in many vaccines is called the 'Edmonston' strain, after him.","Then came the slow part: weakening the virus. Enders and his colleagues grew it again and again in different kinds of cells, including cells from chicken embryos. Each time the virus copied itself in these unfamiliar cells, it changed slightly and became less able to cause illness in humans, while still looking enough like measles to train the immune system. This process is called attenuation, and the weakened virus is called attenuated.","The first measles vaccines were licensed in the United States in 1963. The early version often caused a fever and rash, so it was sometimes given with a small injection of antibodies to soften the reaction. In 1968, Maurice Hilleman and his team at the drug company Merck developed a further weakened, gentler version, which became the standard US measles vaccine. Measles cases in the United States dropped dramatically, and in 2000 measles was declared eliminated there, meaning it no longer spread continuously within the country, although cases can still arrive from abroad.","Other teams around the world made their own weakened measles strains too. In 1960, the British doctor David Morley tested an early live measles vaccine in Nigeria, where measles was a major killer of young children, and a 1962 trial in New York City, run with WHO, compared different versions to find the gentlest one. Today, one dose of measles vaccine is about 93 percent effective at preventing measles and two doses about 97 percent, which is why many countries give two doses. No vaccine is perfect, but that is a very high level of protection. According to WHO, measles vaccination prevented an estimated 59 million deaths around the world between 2000 and 2024."]},{"heading":"The Man Who Made Forty Vaccines","paragraphs":["If you have had the routine childhood vaccines in many countries, there is a good chance at least one of them traces back to a man most people have never heard of. Maurice Hilleman was born on 30 August 1919 in Miles City, Montana, and grew up on a farm, where he looked after chickens. That turned out to be useful training: much of his later work involved growing viruses in fertilised chicken eggs and chick cells.","Hilleman was known as a tough, hard-working and very direct scientist. In 1957, while working at a US Army research institute, he read reports of a new kind of influenza spreading in Hong Kong. He suspected it could become a worldwide pandemic, and he was right. He obtained samples of the virus and pushed drug companies to start making vaccine immediately. About 40 million doses were prepared in the United States, and his quick action is believed to have saved many lives.","That same year he joined Merck, where he led virus research at its laboratories in West Point, Pennsylvania. Over his career he developed more than 40 vaccines, for animals as well as people. Of the 14 vaccines routinely recommended for American children in the early 2000s, he and his team developed eight, including vaccines for measles, mumps, rubella, hepatitis A, hepatitis B, chickenpox and the bacteria called Hib.","The most famous Hilleman story happened in 1963. His young daughter, Jeryl Lynn, then about five years old, woke up feeling ill with a sore, swollen face. Hilleman recognised the signs of mumps, a virus that makes the salivary glands under the jaw and in front of the ears swell up, giving children a 'hamster-cheek' look. Mumps was usually mild, but it could sometimes cause deafness, meningitis (swelling of the coverings of the brain and spinal cord) and other problems. According to the story Hilleman often told, he gently swabbed the back of her throat, then drove to his laboratory to store the sample before going back home.","He grew the virus from Jeryl Lynn's sample in chicken eggs and then in chick embryo cells, weakening it step by step. In 1967, the vaccine, called Mumpsvax, was licensed in the United States. It had taken about four years, which was a record for speed at the time. The virus strain is called the 'Jeryl Lynn' strain, and it is still used in mumps vaccines today. Few people can say that a vaccine given to hundreds of millions of children is named after them.","Hilleman's next big idea was to combine several vaccines into one injection. In 1971 the MMR vaccine, protecting against measles, mumps and rubella, was licensed in the United States. It was the first approved vaccine to combine several different live weakened viruses. Fewer injections meant fewer visits, fewer tears and more children protected. Hilleman died in 2005 at the age of 85. Some experts have estimated that his vaccines save millions of lives every year."]},{"heading":"Rubella: A Mild Illness with a Hidden Danger","paragraphs":["Rubella, sometimes called German measles, is usually a mild disease in children: a light rash, a slight fever, swollen glands behind the ears, and that is often it. For a long time doctors did not worry much about it. Then in 1941, an Australian eye doctor named Norman Gregg noticed something alarming. Many babies with cataracts, a clouding of the lens of the eye, had mothers who had caught rubella early in their pregnancy. He realised the virus could cross from a pregnant mother to her unborn baby and harm it as it was forming.","This condition became known as congenital rubella syndrome. 'Congenital' means 'present from birth'. Babies with it could be born deaf or blind, with heart problems or with difficulties in learning and development. Rubella infection early in pregnancy could also cause miscarriage, the loss of the pregnancy.","Between 1962 and 1965, a huge rubella epidemic swept across Europe and then the United States. In the US in 1964 and 1965, an estimated 12.5 million people caught rubella, and about 20,000 babies were born with congenital rubella syndrome. Many families and doctors were heartbroken, and the epidemic made a rubella vaccine an urgent priority.","Several teams went to work. At the US National Institutes of Health, Harry Meyer and Paul Parkman weakened the rubella virus by growing it 77 times in monkey kidney cells, creating a strain called HPV-77. The first rubella vaccine licence was issued in June 1969. At the Wistar Institute in Philadelphia, Stanley Plotkin developed another weakened strain, called RA 27/3, grown in a line of human cells called WI-38. It produced a stronger immune response, was approved in Britain in 1970, and in 1979 became the rubella part of the MMR vaccine in the United States. It is still used today.","Rubella vaccination had an unusual goal. Most vaccines are mainly about protecting the person who receives them. Rubella vaccination is also about protecting babies not yet born, by making sure the virus cannot circulate and reach pregnant women. Thanks to widespread vaccination, rubella no longer spreads continuously in the United States or in many other countries, although it still causes congenital rubella syndrome in parts of the world where vaccination is less common."]},{"heading":"A Father in Japan and the Chickenpox Vaccine","paragraphs":["Chickenpox is caused by the varicella-zoster virus. It brings a fever and an itchy rash of small blisters that crust over, and for most healthy children it is an uncomfortable week off school. But it can be much more serious for babies, adults, and people whose immune systems are weakened by illness or medical treatment, and it sometimes leads to skin infections, pneumonia or brain swelling. The virus also has a sneaky habit: after the illness is over, it hides quietly in nerve cells, and decades later it can wake up and cause shingles, a painful band of rash.","In the 1960s, a Japanese virus scientist named Michiaki Takahashi from Osaka University was working in the United States when his eldest son, Teruyuki, caught a bad case of chickenpox. Watching his son struggle with a high fever made a deep impression on him, and when he returned to Japan he decided to try to make a chickenpox vaccine.","Takahashi began the work in 1971. He took the virus from a young child with ordinary chickenpox, a strain that became known as the 'Oka' strain, and weakened it by growing it many times at low temperatures in human cells and then in guinea pig cells. Growing a virus at cooler temperatures than the human body favours versions of it that copy well in the cold but poorly at body temperature, which helps weaken it. He completed the vaccine in 1973 and reported his results in 1974.","The vaccine was tested first on children in hospitals who were at high risk of dangerous chickenpox. It proved safe and protective. In 1984 WHO judged the Oka vaccine the most suitable chickenpox vaccine, and widespread vaccination began in Japan in 1986 and in South Korea in 1988. In the United States, Merck licensed a version of Takahashi's Oka vaccine in 1995. It is about 70 to 90 percent effective at preventing chickenpox and more than 95 percent effective at preventing severe disease.","Because it is a live weakened virus, the vaccine virus can also hide in nerve cells, but vaccinated children are much less likely to get shingles later than children who caught wild chickenpox. Most chickenpox vaccines used around the world today come from the Oka strain Takahashi created. He died in 2013, aged 85."]},{"heading":"Hepatitis B: A Vaccine Made by Yeast that Prevents Cancer","paragraphs":["Hepatitis means inflammation of the liver. Hepatitis B is caused by a virus that spreads through blood and other body fluids, including from a mother to her baby at birth. Some people fight it off, but others, especially babies infected at birth, can carry the virus for the rest of their lives. Over many years, long-lasting infection can scar the liver and lead to liver cancer.","The discovery of the virus came from an unexpected direction. In the early 1960s, an American scientist named Baruch Blumberg was not studying hepatitis at all. He was collecting blood samples from people around the world to study inherited differences between populations. In a blood sample from an Aboriginal Australian man, his team found an unusual protein, which they called the 'Australia antigen'. Blumberg's famous paper describing it was published in 1965.","Later, Blumberg's colleague Alton Sutnick and others noticed that people with the Australia antigen often had liver disease. It turned out to be a protein from the surface of the hepatitis B virus. This discovery led to a blood test that could screen donated blood, making transfusions much safer. Blumberg shared the 1976 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. He later became director of the NASA Astrobiology Institute, studying the possibility of life beyond Earth.","Blumberg and his colleague Irving Millman realised that the surface protein could itself be used as a vaccine, because it would teach the immune system to recognise the virus without causing infection. The first hepatitis B vaccine, made by Merck, was approved in the United States in 1981. But it had a big drawback: the surface protein had to be collected and carefully purified from the blood of people who already carried the virus. Supply was limited and some people worried about using blood products.","The solution came from genetic engineering, a set of methods for moving genes from one living thing into another. Scientists including William Rutter, Pablo Valenzuela and their colleagues at the University of California, San Francisco, and Benjamin Hall's team at the University of Washington, took the gene for the hepatitis B surface protein and put it into ordinary baker's yeast. The yeast cells then followed the new instructions and made the virus surface protein as they grew, without ever making the whole virus. Working with Merck and a company called Chiron, this led to Recombivax HB, approved in the United States on 23 July 1986. It was the first human vaccine made with recombinant DNA technology.","Because hepatitis B can cause liver cancer, this vaccine became the first vaccine that helps prevent a human cancer. Taiwan, where hepatitis B was very common, began vaccinating all newborn babies in 1984, and in the following years, liver cancer in Taiwanese children fell markedly. Today WHO recommends the first dose within 24 hours of birth, and the vaccine protects more than 95 percent of healthy people who complete the series."]},{"heading":"Sugar Coats and Protein Handles: Conjugate Vaccines","paragraphs":["Haemophilus influenzae type b, or Hib for short, is a bacterium with a confusing name. In the 1890s, scientists wrongly thought it caused influenza, the flu. Influenza is actually caused by a virus, but the name stuck. Hib was dangerous mainly for children under five. In the early 1980s in the United States, it caused an estimated 20,000 serious cases a year, and it was a leading cause of bacterial meningitis in young children, as well as pneumonia and a dangerous swelling in the throat called epiglottitis.","Hib bacteria wear a slippery outer coat made of sugar molecules, called a polysaccharide capsule. This coat helps them hide from the immune system. A vaccine made from the coat alone was licensed in the US in 1985, but there was a frustrating problem: babies and toddlers under about 18 months, the very children most at risk, did not respond well to it. Their young immune systems were not yet good at learning to recognise sugar coats on their own.","Scientists found a clever trick. At the US National Institutes of Health, John Robbins and Rachel Schneerson, building on earlier ideas, chemically attached the sugar coat to a protein, such as a harmless toxoid, the inactivated form of a bacterial toxin. Porter Anderson and David Smith in Boston worked on related vaccines too. This joined-together molecule is called a conjugate, from a Latin word meaning 'joined together'.","Here is why it works. Babies' immune systems respond well to proteins, because helper T cells can recognise pieces of proteins and then help B cells make strong antibodies and long-lasting memory cells. By joining the sugar coat to a protein, the protein acts like a handle that brings in the T cells' help, so the B cells that recognise the sugar get the support they need. The baby's body ends up making antibodies against the Hib sugar coat.","The first conjugate Hib vaccine was licensed in the United States in 1987, and soon afterwards versions were approved for young babies. The results were dramatic. Serious Hib disease in American children fell from about 40 to 100 cases per 100,000 children to fewer than 1 per 100,000. Doctors noticed an extra bonus: vaccinated children carried Hib bacteria in their noses and throats less often, so even unvaccinated babies were protected because the germ spread less. The same conjugate idea was later used to make vaccines against pneumococcal and meningococcal bacteria, which also cause meningitis and pneumonia."]},{"heading":"Vaccines for Every Child: EPI and Gavi","paragraphs":["By the 1970s, rich countries had a growing list of vaccines, but most children in the world could not get them. In 1974, inspired by the success of the smallpox campaign, the World Health Assembly created the Expanded Programme on Immunization, or EPI. When EPI began, fewer than 5 percent of the world's babies received the basic routine vaccines.","EPI's first goal was to protect children everywhere against six diseases: diphtheria, whooping cough (pertussis), tetanus, polio, measles and tuberculosis, alongside smallpox while it still existed. That meant building systems to train vaccinators, keep records, order supplies and keep vaccines cold all the way from factory to village clinic using refrigerators, cool boxes and ice packs, the 'cold chain' you met earlier in this story. It also meant persuading governments to treat childhood vaccination as a basic health service, just like clean water.","Progress was slow at first, but in the 1980s, with strong support from UNICEF and many governments, a big push called Universal Childhood Immunization raised coverage a great deal. Today the programme is called the Essential Programme on Immunization, and WHO recommends vaccines against many more diseases for all children. Around 84 percent of the world's babies now receive three doses of the diphtheria, tetanus and pertussis vaccine, a common measure of how well routine vaccination is reaching children.","New vaccines such as hepatitis B and Hib were expensive, though, and at first poorer countries could not afford them. In 2000, Gavi, the Vaccine Alliance, was created to help. Its founding partners included WHO, UNICEF, the World Bank and the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation. Gavi pools money from donors and negotiates lower prices by buying vaccines in huge quantities for lower-income countries, which also pay part of the cost themselves. According to Gavi, more than 1.3 billion children have been immunised with its support.","How much difference has all this made? In 2024, a study led by WHO estimated that vaccination programmes had saved at least 154 million lives over the previous 50 years. That works out to about six lives every minute. About 101 million of those lives were babies. Measles vaccine alone accounted for about 94 million lives saved. Measles deaths fell from about 780,000 in the year 2000 to an estimated 95,000 in 2024, though that is still far too many, mostly young children in places where vaccines do not reach everyone."]},{"heading":"Victims of Their Own Success","paragraphs":["By the end of the twentieth century, an extraordinary thing had happened in many countries. Diseases that every parent once feared had become rare. Doctors trained in the 1990s in places like North America and Western Europe might never see a child with measles, Hib meningitis or diphtheria. Hospital wards once full of children with these diseases were used for other things.","Think about what that means for an ordinary family. In the 1950s, most parents knew a child who had been badly harmed by measles, mumps, polio or whooping cough. A generation or two later, many parents had never seen any of these diseases at all. They had only heard about them, the way you might hear about something from a history book.","This created a strange problem. When a disease is all around you, the benefits of protection feel obvious. When the disease has almost disappeared, people start to notice the vaccine more than the disease it prevents. A sore arm or a feverish night after an injection becomes the most visible thing, and the danger it guards against becomes invisible. Public health experts sometimes say that vaccines can become 'victims of their own success'.","At the same time, scientists kept building better and better systems to watch for any rare problems vaccines might cause, and to check claims carefully. As diseases disappeared, people forgot how frightening they had been, and new worries about vaccines began to grow. How scientists, doctors and families dealt with those worries, real and imagined, is the next part of our story."]}],"deepDive":"The vaccines of this era illustrate three different routes to a safe antigen. The first is classic attenuation by serial passage: measles (Edmonston), mumps (Jeryl Lynn), rubella (HPV-77 and RA 27/3) and varicella (Oka) were all weakened by growing them repeatedly in non-human or unusual cell environments, sometimes at reduced temperature, until mutations accumulated that impaired growth in human tissue while preserving the surface antigens. This was largely empirical: researchers selected strains by testing, without knowing exactly which mutations mattered. Modern sequencing has since mapped many of those changes. The second route is the subunit approach of hepatitis B, where a single viral protein suffices. The recombinant version was a landmark because the antigen was produced by yeast that never contained the virus, removing reliance on donor plasma and making large-scale, consistent production possible. The surface protein assembles into virus-like particles about 22 nanometres across, which the immune system finds especially easy to recognise. The third route, conjugation, solved an immunological problem. Pure polysaccharides are T-cell-independent antigens: they can stimulate some mature B cells directly but generate weak responses and little memory in infants under about two years. Linking them to a carrier protein recruits helper T cells, producing class-switched, high-affinity antibodies and immune memory. Conjugate technology later underpinned pneumococcal and meningococcal vaccines. Historians and health economists debate how to measure this era's impact. The 2024 WHO-led estimate of 154 million lives saved since 1974 relies on mathematical models for 14 diseases, combining coverage data with assumptions about what would have happened without vaccination, so the exact figure carries uncertainty, but every major analysis agrees the effect has been very large, with measles accounting for most of it. Another discussion concerns equity: new vaccines such as Hib and hepatitis B often reached low-income countries one or two decades after rich ones, a gap that Gavi was designed to narrow through pooled purchasing and tiered pricing. Ethical questions also arose: some early vaccine trials in this period took place in institutions for disabled children, practices that would not meet today's standards of consent.","funFact":"The mumps vaccine strain used around the world today is named the 'Jeryl Lynn' strain after Maurice Hilleman's daughter, whose throat he swabbed when she had mumps in 1963.","check":{"q":"What did scientists put the hepatitis B surface protein gene into to make the first recombinant vaccine in 1986?","a":"Yeast","wrong":["Chicken eggs","Calves' skin","Monkey kidney cells"]},"sources":[{"title":"History of Measles","publisher":"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/measles/about/history.html"},{"title":"Measles fact sheet","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/measles"},{"title":"Global immunization efforts have saved at least 154 million lives over the past 50 years","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-04-2024-global-immunization-efforts-have-saved-at-least-154-million-lives-over-the-past-50-years"},{"title":"Maurice Hilleman","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maurice_Hilleman"},{"title":"Rubella vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubella_vaccine"},{"title":"Hepatitis B vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hepatitis_B_vaccine"},{"title":"Hib vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hib_vaccine"},{"title":"About our Alliance","publisher":"Gavi, the Vaccine Alliance","url":"https://www.gavi.org/our-alliance/about"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/16-1.jpg","alt":"Maurice Hilleman around 1958, the scientist who later made the mumps and MMR vaccines.","file":"Hilleman-Walter-Reed.jpeg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hilleman-Walter-Reed.jpeg","author":"Walter Reed Army Medical Center","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/16-2.jpg","alt":"A single measles virus particle, seen through an electron microscope at the CDC.","file":"Measles virus.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Measles_virus.JPG","author":"Photo Credit: Cynthia S. Goldsmith Content Providers(s): CDC/ Courtesy of…","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/16-3.jpg","alt":"Baruch Blumberg in 1976, the year he won a Nobel Prize for discovering the hepatitis B 'Australia antigen'.","file":"Baruch Samuel Blumberg 1976.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baruch_Samuel_Blumberg_1976.jpg","author":"Eddie Adams","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3773},{"n":17,"title":"Side Effects, Myths and Trust","when":"1970s – today","summary":"Vaccines worked so well that people forgot how scary the old diseases were, and new worries grew. Scientists built \"alarm systems\" to spot rare problems, and they caught some real ones. They also tested a famous false claim about autism with hundreds of thousands of children and showed it was wrong.","sections":[{"heading":"The Problem with Winning, and a Sore Arm","paragraphs":["By the 1980s, something odd had happened in many richer countries. Parents who had grown up in the 1950s could remember polio wards and classmates in leg braces. Their children could not. Measles, which once filled hospital beds every winter, had become rare. Diphtheria, the old \"strangling angel\" of children, was something you read about in history books. Vaccines had worked so well that the diseases they prevented had nearly vanished from everyday life.","That success created a strange new puzzle. When a disease is all around you, the danger is easy to see and a sore arm seems like nothing. When the disease has disappeared, people stop weighing the vaccine against the illness, because they have never seen the illness. Instead, all they notice is the needle, the crying baby and any worry they hear about. Public-health experts sometimes call this the \"victim of its own success\" problem.","This part of the story is about side effects, scary rumours, real mistakes and the question of trust. It is a story about how scientists check claims, how safety systems spot rare problems, and why some fears turned out to be true while others were false. It is not a guide to what any reader should do. Questions about anyone's own health always belong with a doctor and trusted adults.","So let's begin with a simple question that turns out to be surprisingly interesting: what actually happens to most people after a vaccination?","The most common side effects of vaccines are small and short. The place where the needle went in can become sore, red or a little swollen. Some people feel tired, get a headache, ache a bit or run a mild fever for a day or two. Babies may be grumpy and sleepy. These effects usually fade within a few days.","Here is the surprising part: many of these effects are signs that the immune system is doing its job. Remember from earlier parts how the immune system raises the alarm when it meets something new. Immune cells rush to the spot, release chemical messengers and turn up the body's \"thermostat\". That is why a vaccine can cause a little of the same tiredness and warmth you feel at the start of an illness, without the illness itself.","Rarer reactions exist too. The one that clinics are most prepared for is a sudden, severe allergic reaction called anaphylaxis. For most vaccines it happens roughly once in every million doses, which is why people are often asked to wait a short while after a vaccination, and why clinics keep medicine ready to treat it quickly. Fainting is another reaction that sounds dramatic but is usually caused by nerves about the needle rather than the vaccine, especially in teenagers.","Scientists are careful to say that no vaccine is perfectly free of side effects, and no vaccine protects 100 per cent of people. What they try to measure, as accurately as possible, is how often each problem happens and how that compares with the harm the disease itself would cause. To measure something that might happen once in a million times, you need a very big net. That net is the vaccine safety monitoring system."]},{"heading":"Alarm Bells: How Safety Systems Work","paragraphs":["Even the biggest clinical trial, with tens of thousands of volunteers, cannot spot a problem that happens once in 100,000 or once in a million people. Only when millions of people receive a vaccine does a very rare effect have a chance to show up. That is why safety checking does not stop when a vaccine is approved. It carries on for as long as the vaccine is used. Scientists call this post-marketing surveillance, or \"Phase 4\".","Britain was one of the first countries to build such a system for all medicines. In 1964, still shaken by the thalidomide disaster, in which a medicine taken during pregnancy caused serious harm to thousands of babies around the world, the UK launched the Yellow Card scheme, developed by the doctor Bill Inman. Today it is run by the Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency (MHRA). Anyone, from a doctor or pharmacist to a patient or parent, can send in a report of a suspected side effect.","In the United States, the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System, or VAERS, began in 1990. It grew out of a law passed in 1986, the National Childhood Vaccine Injury Act, and is run jointly by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) and the Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Like the Yellow Card scheme, it is open to everyone. It is designed as an early-warning system: a smoke alarm rather than a fire investigator.","That difference really matters. A report to VAERS only says \"this happened after a vaccination\". It does not prove the vaccine caused it. Imagine that a million children are vaccinated on a Monday. By pure chance, some of them will catch a cold, fall off a bike or develop an illness they were going to get anyway in the following week. Some of those events will be reported. So when a pattern appears, scientists ask a sharper question: is this happening MORE often than we would expect if there were no vaccine at all? They compare the number of reports with the normal \"background rate\" of that illness.","To answer such questions properly, the CDC also runs the Vaccine Safety Datalink, set up in 1990 as well. It links the electronic health records of several large health-care organisations (13 member sites today), so researchers can compare vaccinated and unvaccinated people, or people in the weeks just after vaccination with the same people at other times. Many other countries, such as Denmark and Finland, have national health registers that allow similar checks on whole populations. Together these systems have caught real problems, as the next two stories show."]},{"heading":"When the Alarms Were Right: 1976 and 1999","paragraphs":["In February 1976, soldiers at Fort Dix, an army base in New Jersey, fell ill with a respiratory infection. Tests showed a type of swine flu virus (H1N1) that seemed related to the virus behind the terrible 1918 flu pandemic. One young soldier, Private David Lewis, died, and about a dozen others were taken to hospital. Health officials were frightened that a giant pandemic might be starting.","The US government launched a huge programme to vaccinate everyone. President Gerald Ford rolled up his sleeve for the cameras. Starting in the autumn of 1976, about 43 to 45 million Americans were vaccinated in just a few months. But the feared pandemic never came: the virus did not spread beyond Fort Dix. Meanwhile, reports began to arrive of Guillain-Barré syndrome, a rare condition in which the immune system attacks the nerves, causing weakness that most people recover from slowly. The CDC later estimated that the swine flu vaccine added roughly one extra case for every 100,000 people vaccinated. On 16 December 1976 the programme was stopped and never restarted. Some researchers have since questioned how strong the link was, but the episode taught health officials hard lessons about speed, communication and watching closely.","The second story concerns rotavirus, a germ that causes severe diarrhoea in babies and at that time killed hundreds of thousands of young children every year around the world. In 1998 the first rotavirus vaccine, called RotaShield, was licensed in the United States. Its trials had shown it worked well. But once it was in wide use, reports reached VAERS of a rare bowel problem called intussusception, in which part of the intestine slides into the next part like a folding telescope and becomes blocked. CDC investigators found the risk rose in the week after the first dose, by about one extra case for every 5,000 to 10,000 babies vaccinated.","Within about a year of licensing, in 1999, the recommendation was withdrawn and the maker took RotaShield off the market. The next rotavirus vaccines, RotaTeq (approved in the United States in 2006) and Rotarix, were tested in much larger trials designed specifically to look for this rare problem before approval. These two stories show something important: the safety net is not just for show. When it finds a real signal, vaccines are paused, restricted or withdrawn."]},{"heading":"Rare Signals in a Pandemic","paragraphs":["The same kind of watching happened again, on a gigantic scale, when COVID-19 vaccines were rolled out from December 2020 (their creation is the story of the next part). Because billions of doses were given within months, problems far too rare for any trial to detect could finally be seen.","In March 2021, doctors in Europe noticed a small number of unusual cases among people who had recently received the Oxford-AstraZeneca vaccine. A few people had developed blood clots in unusual places, such as veins draining the brain, together with a low number of platelets, the tiny blood cells that help blood clot. The condition was given a long name: vaccine-induced immune thrombotic thrombocytopenia, or VITT. It was later also linked to the Johnson & Johnson (Janssen) vaccine, which was built in a similar way. It was very rare, roughly one or two cases per 100,000 doses in the figures reported by Germany and the UK, but it could be serious.","Countries reacted in different ways. Denmark stopped using the AstraZeneca vaccine. Germany and the Netherlands kept it mainly for people over 60. The UK offered younger adults, first under 30 and later under 40, a different vaccine. Australia preferred another vaccine for adults under 50. Doctors also learned how to recognise and treat VITT quickly. Researchers pointed out that the same kind of brain clot was several times more common after COVID-19 infection itself than after the vaccine, which is the sort of comparison regulators weigh up.","A different rare effect was found with the mRNA vaccines made by Pfizer-BioNTech and Moderna: myocarditis, an inflammation of the heart muscle, and pericarditis, inflammation of the thin bag around the heart. It appeared mostly in teenage boys and young men, usually a few days after the second dose. Regulators estimated the overall risk at about one case per 100,000 people vaccinated, higher in young males. Most cases were mild and people recovered quickly with rest and treatment. Information leaflets were updated, and some countries adjusted the gap between doses or which vaccine they offered to young men. Once again, the system had seen a real signal and responded."]},{"heading":"The Paper That Fooled the World","paragraphs":["Not every scare comes from a real signal. The most famous false alarm in vaccine history began on 28 February 1998, when the respected British medical journal The Lancet published a paper led by a London doctor named Andrew Wakefield. It described just 12 children with bowel problems and developmental conditions, and suggested a possible link between the MMR vaccine (measles, mumps and rubella) and autism.","Twelve children is a tiny number, and the paper itself did not prove anything. But at a press conference Wakefield suggested that parents might choose single, separate vaccines instead of the combined MMR. Newspapers and television ran the story for years. Many parents, understandably wanting to protect their children, became frightened.","Then a journalist, Brian Deer, began investigating. Over several years he discovered that Wakefield had serious conflicts of interest that he had not declared, including payments linked to lawyers who were preparing to sue vaccine makers. Deer also found that details of the children's histories had been changed to fit the story the paper told. In 2004 most of Wakefield's co-authors withdrew their support for the paper's interpretation.","On 2 February 2010, The Lancet fully retracted the paper, which means it officially withdrew it as untrustworthy. Its editor, Richard Horton, said that claims in it were \"utterly false\". In May 2010 Britain's General Medical Council found Wakefield guilty of serious professional misconduct, and he was struck off the UK medical register, so he can no longer work as a doctor there. In January 2011 the British Medical Journal published Deer's findings with an editorial calling the original paper \"an elaborate fraud\". Wakefield has continued to deny wrongdoing, but the scientific verdict is clear: the paper was dishonest, and vaccines do not cause autism."]},{"heading":"How Scientists Check a Claim","paragraphs":["How can scientists be so confident? Not by trusting one expert, but by testing the idea again and again in huge groups of people. Here is the reasoning. If MMR caused autism, then children who had the vaccine should be diagnosed with autism more often than children who did not. That is something you can count.","First, a word about autism itself. Autism is a difference in how a person's brain develops, affecting how they communicate, sense the world and interact with others. Autistic people are as varied as anyone else, and many describe autism as simply part of who they are. Researchers have found that genes play a large part, and that the brain differences linked to autism begin developing before birth. Part of what made the vaccine scare so hurtful was that it treated autism as damage someone must be blamed for. Major scientific bodies, including the US Institute of Medicine, the World Health Organization, the FDA and the CDC, all reviewed the evidence and rejected the idea that vaccines cause it.","Denmark is especially good at this kind of counting, because every person has an ID number linked to health records. In a study published in 2019 in the journal Annals of Internal Medicine, Anders Hviid and colleagues followed 657,461 Danish children born between 1999 and 2010, adding up to more than 5 million \"person-years\" of watching. Children who received MMR were not more likely to be diagnosed with autism than children who did not. That stayed true even in groups thought to be at higher risk, such as children with an autistic brother or sister. A 2014 review combining studies of more than 1.25 million children found the same thing, and so have many other studies in different countries.","Why did the myth feel believable to so many people? Partly because of timing. The first signs of autism are often noticed by parents in a child's second year of life, which is also when the MMR vaccine is usually given. When two things happen around the same time, our brains easily link them, even when one did not cause the other. Scientists have a phrase for this: correlation is not causation. Large studies are a way of escaping that trap.","A related worry concerned thimerosal (also spelled thiomersal), a preservative containing a form of mercury that was used in some multi-dose vaccine vials to stop bacteria and fungi growing. In July 1999 American health authorities said they had found no evidence of harm from the tiny amounts in vaccines, apart from occasional skin reactions, but asked manufacturers to remove it from children's vaccines as a precaution. By 2001 it had been taken out of, or reduced to trace amounts in, routine US childhood vaccines. If thimerosal had been causing autism, diagnoses should have dropped afterwards. Instead, autism diagnoses kept rising, which researchers link mainly to wider definitions and better recognition of autism.","This is how science is supposed to work. A claim was made, it was taken seriously, and it was tested over and over by independent teams. The answer kept coming back the same. Scientists also stress that questioning is healthy: asking \"how do we know?\" is exactly what led to these studies in the first place."]},{"heading":"When Measles Came Back","paragraphs":["Fear has real effects. In the UK, the share of young children receiving MMR fell from about 92 per cent in 1996 to about 84 per cent by 2002, and in some parts of London it dropped to around 61 per cent. Remember from part 11 that measles is so contagious that around 95 per cent of people need to be immune to stop it spreading. Gaps in protection let it back in. Recorded measles cases in the UK rose from 56 in 1998 to hundreds a year by the mid-2000s, and in 2008 measles was declared endemic again, meaning it was circulating steadily within the country. In Ireland, an outbreak in 2000 caused about 1,500 cases and three children died.","The United States had declared measles eliminated in 2000, meaning it no longer spread continuously within the country. But travellers can bring it in, and where groups of unvaccinated people live close together, it spreads. In 2019 the US recorded 1,274 confirmed measles cases across 31 states, the largest number in decades. Preliminary CDC figures show 2,289 cases in 2025 and 4,852 by 29 October 2026, as outbreaks grew again.","Why do health officials worry so much about a disease that many people remember as a childhood rash? Measles spreads through the air when an infected person coughs or sneezes, and the virus can hang in a room for up to about two hours after they leave. Most children recover, but some develop pneumonia or swelling of the brain, and a small number die, especially babies and children who are poorly nourished. Measles can also weaken the immune system's memory of other germs for months or years afterwards, a finding scientists sometimes call \"immune amnesia\". This is why a rise of a few percentage points in unprotected children can turn into hundreds or thousands of cases.","In 2019 the World Health Organization listed \"vaccine hesitancy\", which it defined as reluctance or refusal to vaccinate despite vaccines being available, as one of ten threats to global health. WHO said vaccination prevents about 2 to 3 million deaths a year and that about 1.5 million more could be avoided if coverage improved. It also noted that the reasons for hesitancy are complicated: some people feel the diseases are no longer a danger (complacency), some find it hard to get to a clinic, and some lack confidence in vaccines or in the people offering them. Health workers in local communities, WHO found, are the voices people trust most."]},{"heading":"Trust, History and the Hard Question of Rules","paragraphs":["Trust is not just a feeling. It is built, or broken, by how people have been treated. One of the darkest examples in American medicine is the Tuskegee syphilis study, which was NOT a vaccine study but still shapes how many people feel about medical research. From 1932 to 1972, the US Public Health Service followed 600 poor Black men in Macon County, Alabama, 399 of whom had syphilis. The men were never told their diagnosis, and even after penicillin became the standard cure in the 1940s they were not given it. A Public Health Service worker, Peter Buxtun, raised the alarm, and a news story by reporter Jean Heller exposed the study in July 1972. It was stopped that year. In 1997 President Bill Clinton formally apologised to the survivors.","The scandal led to new rules. The National Research Act of 1974 and the Belmont Report of 1979 set out principles that now guide research on people: respect, informed consent, fair treatment, and review of every study by an independent ethics committee before it can start. Historians and researchers have found that the revelations deepened mistrust of medicine among many Black Americans, a mistrust with roots in earlier abuses too. Remembering such history helps explain why some people ask hard questions, and why honesty and fairness matter so much in public health.","Then there is the oldest argument of all: should vaccination ever be required by law? As part 6 described, England made infant smallpox vaccination compulsory in 1853, protests followed, and in 1898 parents gained the right to object on grounds of conscience. In 1904 a smallpox vaccination law in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, set off days of rioting known as the Vaccine Revolt. In 1905 the US Supreme Court heard the case of Henning Jacobson, a pastor in Cambridge, Massachusetts, who had refused smallpox vaccination during an outbreak and been fined 5 dollars. In Jacobson v. Massachusetts the court ruled 7 to 2 that a state could require vaccination to protect public health, while noting that courts could step in for a person who would be harmed by it.","Today, countries make very different choices. All US states require certain vaccinations for children to attend school, but most allow some exemptions, and the rules keep being debated. Australia in 2016 linked some family benefits to children's vaccination records, with exemptions for medical reasons. In 2021 the European Court of Human Rights decided that the Czech Republic's childhood vaccination requirement did not break human-rights law. Other countries rely only on recommendations and free clinics. People who value personal freedom and people who focus on protecting the community, especially those who cannot be vaccinated, have argued about where to draw the line for more than 170 years. This book will not tell you the answer; it is a question societies keep working out together.","By the end of 2019, the picture was mixed. Vaccines were saving millions of lives a year, safety systems were watching closely, and science had answered the biggest myths. Yet trust was wobbling in many places. Then, in the first days of 2020, doctors in Wuhan, China, began reporting cases of pneumonia caused by a new coronavirus. Within weeks it was spreading across the world, and humanity needed a vaccine faster than it had ever made one before."]}],"deepDive":"Vaccine safety science is mostly a problem of rare events and big numbers. A Phase 3 trial of 30,000 to 40,000 people can reliably detect side effects that occur in roughly 1 in 1,000 to 1 in 10,000 recipients, but not ones at 1 in 100,000 or 1 in a million. A rough rule of thumb (the \"rule of three\") says that if no cases of an event are seen among N people, you can be about 95 per cent confident its true rate is below 3 in N. So a 40,000-person trial with no cases still allows a risk of up to about 1 in 13,000. That is why passive reporting systems (VAERS, Yellow Card) and active systems (the Vaccine Safety Datalink, Nordic national registers) are essential after approval.\n\nPassive systems are sensitive but noisy. Anyone can report, so reports include coincidences, duplicates and, sometimes, entries filed for lawsuits. Analysts therefore look for \"disproportionality\": an event reported more often after one vaccine than after others. A signal is then tested with designs that control for chance and bias, such as the self-controlled case series, which compares the same individuals in a \"risk window\" just after vaccination with other times, cancelling out personal factors like genetics. This is how the RotaShield intussusception risk (about 1 in 5,000 to 10,000) and the mRNA myocarditis risk in young males were pinned down.\n\nThe MMR-autism question shows the other side of the coin: proving a negative as far as science can. No study can prove a risk is exactly zero, but the Danish cohort of 657,461 children produced a hazard ratio of 0.93 with a 95 per cent confidence interval of 0.85 to 1.02. In plain words, any hidden effect would have to be extremely small, and the best estimate is no effect at all. Combined with other cohorts totalling well over a million children, the evidence is about as strong as epidemiology can provide.\n\nResearchers who study hesitancy describe the \"3 Cs\": confidence (trust in vaccines and the system), complacency (feeling the disease is not a threat) and convenience (how easy it is to get vaccinated). Debates about mandates turn on ethics as much as data: how to balance personal liberty against harm to others, and whether incentives, exemptions or education work best. Different democracies have reached different answers, and the evidence on which policies raise coverage is still argued over.","funFact":"The famous 1905 US Supreme Court case about compulsory vaccination, Jacobson v. Massachusetts, began with a fine of just 5 dollars.","check":{"q":"Which medical journal published Andrew Wakefield's 1998 paper and then fully retracted it in 2010?","a":"The Lancet","wrong":["Nature","Scientific American","National Geographic"]},"sources":[{"title":"MMR vaccine and autism","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MMR_vaccine_and_autism"},{"title":"Measles, Mumps, Rubella Vaccination and Autism: A Nationwide Cohort Study (Hviid et al., 2019)","publisher":"Annals of Internal Medicine via PubMed (US National Library of Medicine)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30831578/"},{"title":"Ten threats to global health in 2019","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/spotlight/ten-threats-to-global-health-in-2019"},{"title":"Measles Cases and Outbreaks: Data and Research","publisher":"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/measles/data-research/index.html"},{"title":"Vaccine Safety Datalink (VSD)","publisher":"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/vaccine-safety-systems/vsd/index.html"},{"title":"1976 swine flu outbreak","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1976_swine_flu_outbreak"},{"title":"Vaccine-induced immune thrombotic thrombocytopenia","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vaccine-induced_immune_thrombotic_thrombocytopenia"},{"title":"Tuskegee Syphilis Study","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tuskegee_Syphilis_Study"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/17-1.jpg","alt":"President Gerald Ford gets his swine flu shot from the White House doctor on October 14, 1976.","file":"President Ford receives a swine flu inoculation - NARA - 7064718.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Ford_receives_a_swine_flu_inoculation_-_NARA_-_7064718.jpg","author":"David Hume Kennerly","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/17-2.jpg","alt":"A pack of Priorix MMR vaccine: a vial of freeze-dried vaccine, a syringe of liquid to mix it with, and needles.","file":"Priorix.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Priorix.jpg","author":"Dctrzl","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/17-3.jpg","alt":"A colorized electron microscope image of a measles virus particle (red and gold).","file":"Measlesvirus.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Measlesvirus.jpg","author":"NIAID","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3913},{"n":18,"title":"The mRNA Breakthrough","when":"1990s – 2021","summary":"mRNA is like a recipe card that a cell reads and then throws away. Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman spent years learning how to send these recipe cards into the body without causing trouble. When COVID-19 arrived in 2020, their work helped scientists build vaccines faster than ever, after testing them on tens of thousands of volunteers.","sections":[{"heading":"A Recipe Card That Melts Away","paragraphs":["Every cell in your body contains a library of instructions written in DNA. That library is kept safe inside a locked room in the middle of the cell, called the nucleus. But the cell's protein-building machines, called ribosomes, work outside the nucleus, in the busy jelly-like space called the cytoplasm. So how do the instructions get out?","The answer is messenger RNA, or mRNA. When a cell needs a particular protein, it copies the right section of DNA into a strand of mRNA, like copying a single recipe from a precious cookbook onto a scrap of paper. The mRNA travels out into the cytoplasm, where ribosomes read it three letters at a time and link together building blocks called amino acids, in the right order, to make the protein. Then, a bit like a recipe card made of ice, the mRNA breaks apart. Cells are full of enzymes that chop up old mRNA, so most of it lasts only minutes to hours.","Scientists realised decades ago that this could become a new kind of vaccine. Remember from earlier parts that a vaccine works by showing the immune system a \"wanted poster\": a harmless version or piece of a germ, so the body can build memory without getting the disease. Instead of growing a germ in eggs or vats and then killing or weakening it, what if you simply delivered the recipe for one harmless piece of the germ, and let the body's own cells build the poster?","For the coronavirus that causes COVID-19, called SARS-CoV-2, the chosen piece was the spike protein: the knobbly protein that sticks out of the virus's surface and helps it grab onto our cells. On its own, a spike protein cannot cause an infection. It is just a shape. After an mRNA vaccine is injected into the arm muscle, some cells near the injection site and in nearby lymph nodes read the mRNA, make spike proteins and display them. The immune system spots these unfamiliar shapes, makes antibodies and trains T cells, and keeps memory cells ready for the real virus.","It is important to be clear about what an mRNA vaccine does NOT do. The mRNA never enters the nucleus, where DNA is kept, and it does not change a person's DNA. It is broken down by the body within a few days, like any other mRNA. The spike proteins are cleared away too. What stays behind is the immune system's memory, which is the whole point.","The first hints came early. In 1989 researchers reported delivering designed mRNA, packed in tiny fat bubbles, into cells in the laboratory. In 1990 a team led by Jon Wolff in Wisconsin injected plain mRNA into the leg muscles of mice and found that the muscle cells made the protein it described. In 1993 a French team showed that mRNA for a virus protein, wrapped in fat bubbles, could train T cells in mice. These were exciting results, but the mRNA was unstable, hard to deliver and set off unwanted inflammation, so most companies lost interest.","It sounds simple. In fact, turning this idea into a working vaccine took more than 30 years, and it nearly did not happen at all."]},{"heading":"The Scientist Who Would Not Quit","paragraphs":["Katalin Karikó was born in 1955 in Szolnok, Hungary, and grew up in the small town of Kisújszállás, in a house with no running water, no refrigerator and no television. Her father was a butcher, and she later said that watching him work gave her an early fascination with how living things are put together. She studied biology and then biochemistry at the University of Szeged, earning her PhD in 1982, and began working on RNA at Hungary's Biological Research Centre.","In 1985 her lab ran out of funding. Karikó, her husband and their two-year-old daughter Susan left for the United States, where she had been offered a job at Temple University in Philadelphia. Money was strictly controlled in communist Hungary, so, according to her own account, the family sold their car and smuggled the savings out sewn inside the daughter's teddy bear: about 900 British pounds' worth.","In 1989 she moved to the University of Pennsylvania (often called Penn) to work on mRNA. Her dream was to use it as a medicine, for example to make cells produce proteins that could help heal damaged tissue. But mRNA was famously difficult. It was fragile, it broke down quickly, and when injected into animals it set off strong inflammation, the immune system's alarm response. Grant committees, which decide who gets money for research, turned her down again and again. In 1995 she was demoted, and she never got tenure, the permanent job security that university scientists hope for. She kept going anyway, often on low pay.","Then came a lucky meeting. In 1997 a new immunologist arrived at Penn: Drew Weissman, born in 1959 in Lexington, Massachusetts, who had trained at Boston University and in the laboratory of Anthony Fauci at the US National Institutes of Health. Weissman wanted to build vaccines, including an HIV vaccine. The two met, as both have told the story, at a shared photocopier where they were copying scientific articles. Karikó could make mRNA; Weissman knew the immune system. They decided to work together. Years later Weissman summed up their struggle simply: \"We had to fight the entire way.\""]},{"heading":"The Inflammation Puzzle and the Pseudouridine Trick","paragraphs":["Karikó and Weissman's first big problem was that the mRNA they made in the lab, called in vitro transcribed mRNA (made \"in glass\", outside a cell), caused inflammation. When they added it to dendritic cells, the immune system's sentries, the cells reacted as if they had found an invader. In a living body that reaction would cause side effects and also block the cell from making much protein.","Curiously, the body's own mRNA does not set off this alarm. Why not? The two scientists knew that natural RNA in our cells contains small chemical changes to some of its building blocks, called modified nucleosides. RNA is spelled with four main letters, A, C, G and U, and the cell sometimes swaps in slightly altered versions. One of these is pseudouridine, a twisted-around cousin of U (uridine).","They tried making mRNA with modified building blocks. In a paper published in August 2005 in the journal Immunity, Karikó, Weissman and their colleagues Michael Buckstein and Houping Ni reported that ordinary lab-made RNA set off alarm sensors called Toll-like receptors, but RNA made with modified nucleosides such as pseudouridine did not. Dendritic cells exposed to the modified RNA stayed calm. The scientists suggested a beautiful explanation: the immune system may use the absence of these modifications as a clue that RNA came from bacteria or damaged cells rather than healthy human ones.","Follow-up studies in 2008 and 2010 showed something even better: mRNA containing pseudouridine produced much more protein, because the cell's machinery was not being slowed down by the alarm. The modified mRNA was quieter and more productive at the same time.","At the time, few people noticed. The paper was not a sensation. But a handful of companies did take an interest. One was BioNTech, in Mainz, Germany, which Karikó joined as a vice-president in 2013. Another was the American company Moderna, founded in 2010, whose very name comes from \"modified RNA\". In 2023, Karikó and Weissman were awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for their discoveries about nucleoside base modifications. Karikó said during Nobel Week: \"I dreamt about doing research, not getting an award.\""]},{"heading":"Tiny Bubbles of Fat","paragraphs":["Even calm mRNA has a big problem: it is fragile. Our skin and blood are full of enzymes called ribonucleases that shred loose RNA within moments, a defence against viruses. And an mRNA molecule is large and carries a negative electric charge, so it cannot easily slip through the oily outer skin, or membrane, of a cell. You cannot just inject naked mRNA and expect much of it to reach the inside of cells.","The solution was a delivery package called a lipid nanoparticle, or LNP. Lipids are fats and oily molecules, the same family as the material in cell membranes. A lipid nanoparticle is a tiny ball of these fats, roughly 100 nanometres across. That is about a thousandth of the width of a human hair. The mRNA is tucked safely inside. When a cell swallows the particle, special lipids help it open up and release the mRNA into the cytoplasm, where ribosomes can read it.","A leading pioneer of this technology was Pieter Cullis, a Canadian biochemist and physicist at the University of British Columbia in Vancouver, who has worked on lipid delivery systems since the 1980s. Cullis and his colleagues co-founded a string of small companies that improved the particles step by step, one of which, Acuitas Therapeutics, developed the lipid nanoparticle used in the Pfizer-BioNTech COVID-19 vaccine. Many other teams around the world worked on LNPs too.","The first big success of these particles was not a vaccine at all. In August 2018 the US Food and Drug Administration approved Onpattro, a medicine for a rare inherited nerve disease. It used lipid nanoparticles to carry a different kind of RNA, called siRNA, into liver cells. It was the first approved drug of its kind, and it proved that lipid nanoparticles could be made safely and at scale. When the COVID-19 pandemic began, the delivery truck was ready.","There was a price to pay. mRNA in lipid nanoparticles is delicate, which is why the first COVID-19 mRNA vaccines needed very cold storage. Pfizer-BioNTech's vaccine was shipped at about minus 80 to minus 60 degrees Celsius, about as cold as the coldest parts of Antarctica in midwinter, in special boxes packed with dry ice. Moderna's could be kept in an ordinary freezer and in a fridge for up to 30 days. This made the cold chain, described in part 12, more important than ever."]},{"heading":"Freezing the Spike in Its Ready Pose","paragraphs":["There was one more piece of the puzzle, and it came from scientists who study the shapes of proteins. The spike protein on a coronavirus is a shape-shifter. Before it attacks a cell it sits in a \"prefusion\" shape, like a spring that is wound up. When it latches onto a cell, it snaps into a completely different \"postfusion\" shape that helps the virus's outer skin merge with the cell. The trouble is that a spike made on its own tends to flop into the postfusion shape, and antibodies trained on that shape are much less useful, because the dangerous version of the virus shows the prefusion shape.","Barney Graham, a doctor and virologist at the Vaccine Research Center of the US National Institutes of Health, had spent years on this problem with another virus, respiratory syncytial virus (RSV), which causes serious chest infections in babies and older people. Working with the structural biologist Jason McLellan and others, he helped show around 2013 that a protein locked in its prefusion shape made a far better vaccine target. That work later led to the first RSV vaccines, approved in 2023.","Graham, McLellan and colleagues then turned to coronaviruses, which had caused the SARS outbreak in 2002–2003 and MERS from 2012. They found that swapping just two of the spike protein's amino acids for one called proline, at a key hinge, acted like a doorstop, holding the spike in its prefusion shape. The design became known as \"2P\". Because they had worked this out for other coronaviruses before 2020, they were ready when a new one appeared.","In February 2020, McLellan's team at the University of Texas at Austin, working with the NIH Vaccine Research Center, used a powerful technique called cryo-electron microscopy, which takes images of frozen protein molecules, to reveal the first atomic-scale 3D structure of the new coronavirus's spike. It was published online in the journal Science on 19 February 2020. The stabilised 2P spike became the design used in the Moderna and Pfizer-BioNTech vaccines, and also in the Johnson & Johnson and Novavax vaccines."]},{"heading":"The Race of 2020","paragraphs":["On 5 January 2020, a team led by Professor Zhang Yongzhen in Shanghai finished reading the genetic sequence of the new virus from a patient sample. On 11 January, with Zhang's permission, his collaborator Edward Holmes, a virologist in Sydney, posted the sequence on a public website called virological.org so that scientists anywhere could use it. It was a string of about 30,000 letters of RNA, and it contained the recipe for the spike protein.","That was all mRNA vaccine makers needed to begin. They did not need a sample of the virus itself. Moderna and the NIH Vaccine Research Center designed their vaccine, called mRNA-1273, within days, combining the 2P spike recipe with Moderna's mRNA and lipid nanoparticle system. On 16 March 2020, the first volunteer in a Phase 1 trial received a dose at the Kaiser Permanente Washington Health Research Institute in Seattle. The trial began with 45 healthy adults.","Why could mRNA move so fast? Because making mRNA is more like printing than farming. Older vaccines often need a germ to be grown in eggs or in huge tanks of cells, which can take many months to set up for each new disease. To make mRNA, workers start with a loop of DNA carrying the recipe, then use an enzyme in a tank to copy it into mRNA, in a chemical reaction that takes hours, not weeks. The mRNA is purified and mixed with lipids in tiny channels where the fats wrap around it to form nanoparticles. To make a vaccine against a different germ, you mostly change the recipe, not the factory. Scientists call this a \"platform\" technology.","In Mainz, Germany, BioNTech's founders, the husband-and-wife doctors and scientists Uğur Şahin and Özlem Türeci, had been working on mRNA treatments for cancer since founding the company in 2008. Şahin read about the outbreak in January 2020 and quickly redirected the company toward a coronavirus vaccine, a project they nicknamed \"Lightspeed\". In March 2020 BioNTech teamed up with the giant American drug company Pfizer, which brought experience with huge trials and factories. They tested several candidate designs side by side and chose the one called BNT162b2.","Other teams took different routes. At the University of Oxford's Jenner Institute, named after Edward Jenner from part 5, Sarah Gilbert's team put the spike gene into a harmless, weakened chimpanzee adenovirus called ChAdOx1. This kind of vaccine, called a viral vector, uses a disabled virus as a delivery van for the instructions. Partnered with AstraZeneca, the Oxford-AstraZeneca vaccine could be kept in an ordinary fridge, and over 3 billion doses were supplied around the world. Johnson & Johnson and Russia's Gamaleya Institute (Sputnik V) also made viral-vector vaccines. Chinese companies Sinovac and Sinopharm made inactivated vaccines, using the old method of growing the virus and killing it. Novavax made a protein vaccine using spike proteins grown in moth cells. By the end of 2022, about 50 COVID-19 vaccines had been authorised in at least one country."]},{"heading":"Tens of Thousands of Volunteers","paragraphs":["Speed in the lab means nothing without proof from trials. In July 2020, Pfizer-BioNTech and Moderna both began Phase 3 trials. In the Pfizer-BioNTech trial, 43,548 people aged 16 and over were randomly divided into two groups: about half received the vaccine and half a placebo, an injection of salt water. Neither the volunteers nor the staff giving the injections knew who got which, which is called blinding. Volunteers came from the United States, Argentina, Brazil, Germany, South Africa and Turkey.","Then everyone went on with their lives during a pandemic, and the researchers waited for people to catch COVID-19. By November 2020 there were 170 cases of COVID-19 with symptoms that counted for the main result. Only 8 were in the vaccine group; 162 were in the placebo group. Because the two groups were about the same size, you can see the efficacy with simple arithmetic: 8 divided by 162 is about 0.05, so the vaccinated people had about 5 per cent of the risk, meaning the vaccine was about 95 per cent effective at preventing symptomatic illness in the trial. The results were published in the New England Journal of Medicine in December 2020.","Moderna's Phase 3 trial, with about 30,000 volunteers, gave similar results: 11 cases of COVID-19 in the vaccine group and 185 in the placebo group, an efficacy of about 94 per cent. Common side effects in both trials were the familiar ones from part 17: a sore arm, tiredness, headache, aches and sometimes a fever, usually gone within a day or two. As described in part 17, rarer effects such as myocarditis only became visible later, when millions of people had been vaccinated.","Before giving emergency permission, the US Food and Drug Administration asked the companies to follow at least half of their volunteers for a median of two months after the second dose, because historically most vaccine side effects have appeared within that time. Independent safety boards, groups of outside experts who can see the unblinded results, checked the data along the way, and regulators then examined the raw data themselves. In the UK, Europe, Canada and elsewhere, separate teams of regulators did their own reviews. Thousands of the volunteers were older adults or had conditions like diabetes or heart disease, since those were the people most at risk from COVID-19.","On 2 December 2020, the UK's Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency became the first regulator in the world to authorise the Pfizer-BioNTech vaccine, and the first to authorise any mRNA vaccine. Six days later, on 8 December 2020, at University Hospital Coventry, 90-year-old Margaret Keenan became the first person in the world to receive an authorised COVID-19 vaccine outside a trial. The second was an 81-year-old man whose name delighted reporters: William Shakespeare. The US FDA gave emergency authorisation to the Pfizer-BioNTech vaccine on 11 December and to Moderna's a week later, and in August 2021 Pfizer-BioNTech's became the first COVID-19 vaccine fully approved in the United States. The Oxford-AstraZeneca vaccine was authorised in the UK on 30 December 2020."]},{"heading":"Fast, But Not Skipped","paragraphs":["Part 12 explained that making a vaccine traditionally took 10 years or more. So how did COVID-19 vaccines arrive in under a year? It was not by skipping trial phases. Every authorised vaccine went through Phase 1, 2 and 3 trials. The speed came from several other things happening at once.","First, decades of earlier research: Karikó and Weissman's modified mRNA, Cullis's lipid nanoparticles, Graham and McLellan's stabilised spikes, and years of work on SARS and MERS vaccines meant the hardest science was already done. Second, phases overlapped: instead of finishing one phase, writing it up, waiting for money and then starting the next, teams planned all three in advance and began later phases as soon as early safety data allowed. Third, enormous funding from governments, such as the US programme called Operation Warp Speed, and from organisations like CEPI, removed money as a bottleneck. Fourth, companies made millions of doses \"at risk\" before knowing whether the vaccines worked, accepting that they might have to throw them away. And fifth, regulators reviewed data as it arrived, in \"rolling reviews\", instead of waiting for one giant pile of paperwork at the end. A pandemic also made trials faster in a sad way: because the virus was everywhere, enough cases piled up quickly to give clear answers.","What followed was the largest vaccination campaign in history. More than 13 billion doses of COVID-19 vaccines were given around the world. A modelling study led by Oliver Watson at Imperial College London, published in The Lancet Infectious Diseases in 2022, estimated that in the first year of vaccination, from 8 December 2020 to 8 December 2021, COVID-19 vaccines prevented about 14.4 million deaths in 185 countries and territories, and about 19.8 million if you count all the extra deaths the pandemic caused rather than only officially reported ones. Like all models, these are estimates, not exact counts.","Not everything went smoothly. Rich countries bought most of the early doses, and many poorer countries waited months longer, despite efforts like COVAX, a global scheme set up to share vaccines more fairly. The virus kept changing, producing new variants such as Delta and Omicron, and protection against infection faded over time, although protection against severe illness lasted better. Vaccines were updated, much as flu vaccines are. Rare side effects had to be studied and explained honestly. These lessons are still being argued over and learned.","Still, something remarkable had happened. An idea that grant committees had turned down for years had become one of the fastest-built vaccines in history. The toolbox of vaccine science now held mRNA alongside Pasteur's weakened germs, Ramon's toxoids, Salk's killed viruses and yeast-made proteins. Scientists began asking an exciting question: what else could these tools, old and new, defeat next? Malaria? Cancer? The next pandemic before it even starts?"]}],"deepDive":"Three separate lines of research had to converge for mRNA vaccines to work. The first was chemistry. In vitro transcribed mRNA is detected by innate immune sensors, notably Toll-like receptors 3, 7 and 8 and cytoplasmic sensors such as RIG-I. Karikó and Weissman's 2005 paper in Immunity showed that incorporating modified nucleosides, including pseudouridine, largely abolished this activation; later work showed modified mRNA is also translated more efficiently. The licensed COVID-19 vaccines use N1-methylpseudouridine, a further refinement. Purification to remove double-stranded RNA contaminants and careful design of the untranslated regions and 5' cap also improved protein output.\n\nThe second was delivery. Modern lipid nanoparticles contain four components: an ionisable lipid that is nearly neutral at blood pH but becomes positively charged in the acidic interior of an endosome, helping release the mRNA; a helper phospholipid; cholesterol; and a PEG-lipid that controls particle size and stability. The ionisable lipid was the crucial advance, refined over two decades by groups including Pieter Cullis's at UBC and companies such as Acuitas. Ownership of LNP intellectual property has since been the subject of significant patent litigation, a reminder that many hands built the platform.\n\nThe third was antigen design. The \"2P\" substitutions (two prolines at positions 986 and 987 of the SARS-CoV-2 spike) hold the protein in its prefusion conformation, the form that displays the most important neutralising-antibody targets. Structure-based design of this kind grew from the RSV prefusion F work of Graham, McLellan and Peter Kwong.\n\nOn efficacy numbers: the 95 per cent figure means a 95 per cent reduction in the risk of symptomatic, laboratory-confirmed COVID-19 in the trial period compared with placebo, not that 5 per cent of vaccinated people fell ill. Real-world effectiveness against infection later declined as immunity waned and variants such as Delta and Omicron appeared, while protection against severe disease held up better, especially after boosters.\n\nDebates continue on several fronts: global equity (by late 2021 most low-income countries had vaccinated only a small fraction of their populations), how to weigh rare adverse events such as myocarditis in young males when designing dosing schedules, and whether the \"100 days\" speed of future vaccines can be achieved without eroding public trust. The death-averted estimates come from models and carry uncertainty, but independent groups have consistently found very large benefits in the first year of rollout.","funFact":"The Oxford team behind the Oxford-AstraZeneca COVID-19 vaccine worked at the Jenner Institute, named after Edward Jenner, who tested his cowpox vaccine in 1796.","check":{"q":"What modified building block did Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman put into mRNA to stop it from setting off harmful inflammation?","a":"Pseudouridine (a modified nucleoside)","wrong":["Extra DNA from the cell nucleus","Weakened rabies virus","Formaldehyde"]},"sources":[{"title":"Suppression of RNA recognition by Toll-like receptors: the impact of nucleoside modification and the evolutionary origin of RNA (Karikó et al., 2005)","publisher":"Immunity via PubMed (US National Library of Medicine)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16111635/"},{"title":"Safety and Efficacy of the BNT162b2 mRNA Covid-19 Vaccine (Polack et al., 2020)","publisher":"New England Journal of Medicine via PubMed (US National Library of Medicine)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33301246/"},{"title":"Global impact of the first year of COVID-19 vaccination: a mathematical modelling study (Watson et al., 2022)","publisher":"The Lancet Infectious Diseases via PubMed (US National Library of Medicine)","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35753318/"},{"title":"Katalin Karikó","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katalin_Karik%C3%B3"},{"title":"mRNA vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MRNA_vaccine"},{"title":"Pieter Cullis","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pieter_Cullis"},{"title":"Jason McLellan","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jason_McLellan"},{"title":"Zhang Yongzhen","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhang_Yongzhen"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/18-1.jpg","alt":"Biochemist Katalin Karikó in 2024, wearing a necklace shaped like a molecule.","file":"Katalin Karikó by Michel 2024 02.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Katalin_Karik%C3%B3_by_Michel_2024_02.jpg","author":"Christopher Michel","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/18-2.jpg","alt":"A computer model of the coronavirus spike protein; its three matching chains are colored red, yellow and blue.","file":"SARS-CoV-2 spike protein trimer multicolor.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SARS-CoV-2_spike_protein_trimer_multicolor.png","author":"Imnulls","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/18-3.jpg","alt":"Diagram of a lipid nanoparticle: a tiny fatty bubble with strands of mRNA (red) packed inside.","file":"Vaccines-09-00065-g001.webp","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaccines-09-00065-g001.webp","author":"Michael D. Buschmann, Manuel J. Carrasco, Suman Alishetty, Mikell Paige,…","license":"CC BY 4.0"}],"words":3844},{"n":19,"title":"New Frontiers","when":"2006 – the future","summary":"Scientists are still inventing new vaccines today. They have made the first vaccines against a cancer-causing virus, against Ebola and against the malaria parasite, and they are working on flu, tuberculosis and the next pandemic.","sections":[{"heading":"A Toolbox That Keeps Growing","paragraphs":["By the end of 2020, the world had watched scientists design, test and roll out brand-new COVID-19 vaccines in less than a year. It would be easy to think the story of vaccines ends there, with a happy finish line. It does not. Some of the most exciting vaccine work of the last twenty years has nothing to do with COVID-19 at all, and much of it is still going on as you read this.","One important thing to remember as you read: some of what follows is finished and approved, and some of it is research. Research means scientists are testing ideas. Many promising ideas fail in trials; that is how science sorts good ideas from bad ones. When something is still being tested, this chapter will say so clearly. And as always, this is a story about science and history. Questions about anyone's own health belong with a doctor and trusted adults."]},{"heading":"An Empty Shell That Fights Cancer: HPV","paragraphs":["Most people think of cancer as something that just happens inside the body, with no germ involved. That is usually true. But scientists have discovered that a few viruses can, over many years, push the cells they infect toward becoming cancer. One of the most important is the human papillomavirus, or HPV. There are more than a hundred types of HPV. Most cause no trouble at all, and the body clears them on its own. But a handful of 'high-risk' types, especially types 16 and 18, can stay hidden in cells for years and slowly cause changes that may become cancer of the cervix (the lower part of the womb), as well as some cancers of the throat, mouth and other parts of the body.","Stopping HPV infections could prevent a whole group of cancers decades later. But HPV is very hard to grow in a laboratory, so the old recipe of weakening or killing the whole germ could not work. The solution came from Ian Frazer, an immunologist born in Glasgow, Scotland, in 1953, who moved to Australia around 1981 and later worked at the University of Queensland in Brisbane, and from Jian Zhou, a talented virologist from China who joined him. Starting in 1990, they tried a clever trick. Instead of using the whole virus, they took the gene for L1, the main protein in the virus's outer coat. They put that gene into cells in the lab so the cells would make L1 protein. Then came the surprise: the L1 proteins clicked together all by themselves into round, hollow balls that looked almost exactly like real HPV particles under an electron microscope.","In March 1991 Zhou's wife, Xiao-Yi Sun, who was also a researcher, assembled these particles in the lab following Zhou's design. Scientists call them virus-like particles, or VLPs. Think of a VLP as a perfect toy model of a virus: it has the same outside shape, so the immune system learns to recognise it, but it is completely empty inside. It contains no genetic material, so it cannot infect anyone or copy itself. To the immune system, though, it looks like the real thing, and it makes lots of antibodies against it.","It took many more years, and the work of large companies, to turn this idea into real vaccines and test them in tens of thousands of volunteers. In 2006 the US Food and Drug Administration approved the first HPV vaccine, called Gardasil, made by Merck. Canada and Australia approved it the same year, and Frazer, who was named Australian of the Year in 2006, gave the first official HPV vaccinations in Australia. A second vaccine, Cervarix, made by GlaxoSmithKline, targets types 16 and 18. A newer version, Gardasil 9, covers nine HPV types.","Sadly, Jian Zhou did not live to see this. He died in 1999, still in his early forties, years before the vaccine he helped invent was approved. Today many scientists make a point of naming him, and Xiao-Yi Sun, whenever the HPV story is told.","The HPV vaccine is special for another reason too. It was one of the first vaccines made mainly to prevent cancer. (You met the other big one in part 16: the hepatitis B vaccine, which helps prevent liver cancer.) Because HPV cancers usually take decades to develop, the full effect is still arriving. But studies in countries that started vaccinating early, such as Scotland, Sweden and England, have already reported large drops in cervix cancer and pre-cancer among young women who were vaccinated as girls. The World Health Organization (WHO) now has a plan to eliminate cervical cancer as a public health problem, and one of its main targets is that 90% of girls should be fully vaccinated against HPV by age 15. In 2022, after studies showed that a single dose gave strong protection, WHO said a one-dose schedule could be used for younger people, which makes it much cheaper and easier for poorer countries to reach everyone."]},{"heading":"Chasing Ebola Ring by Ring","paragraphs":["Ebola is a virus disease first identified in 1976, near the Ebola River in what is now the Democratic Republic of the Congo. It spreads through direct contact with the body fluids of someone who is sick, or who has died of the disease. It causes high fever and severe illness, and in many outbreaks around half of the people infected have died.","Then, in December 2013, an outbreak began in Guinea and spread into Sierra Leone and Liberia. By the time it ended in 2016, it had become by far the largest Ebola epidemic ever recorded, with about 28,600 cases and about 11,300 deaths. Health workers, many of them local nurses and doctors, risked their lives caring for patients. Suddenly the whole world wanted an Ebola vaccine, fast.","Luckily, one was already waiting on a shelf. Years earlier, scientists at Canada's National Microbiology Laboratory in Winnipeg, part of the Public Health Agency of Canada, had built an experimental vaccine called rVSV-ZEBOV. It is a viral vector vaccine, a type you met briefly in part 12. The scientists took a mild animal virus called vesicular stomatitis virus, or VSV, which rarely bothers people, and swapped one of its genes for the gene of the Ebola virus's surface protein, called the glycoprotein. The result is a harmless 'disguised' virus wearing an Ebola costume. When it is injected, it copies itself a little and shows the immune system the Ebola glycoprotein, so the body learns to make antibodies against the real thing. It had protected monkeys in experiments, but it had never been tested in a big outbreak.","How do you run a fair test during an emergency, when cases are scattered and nobody knows where the next one will pop up? The answer was an old idea from smallpox days: ring vaccination, the strategy William Foege used in Nigeria (part 15). The trial, nicknamed 'Ebola ça Suffit!' ('Ebola, that's enough!'), began in Guinea in 2015. It was led by WHO with Guinea's health ministry and partners including Médecins Sans Frontières and Norwegian public health experts. Each time a new Ebola case was found, teams listed that person's contacts, and the contacts of those contacts. That group was called a ring.","At first, the rings were randomly split into two groups. Some rings were vaccinated straight away. Others were vaccinated 21 days later. Everyone got the vaccine in the end, but the delay let scientists compare the two groups. The early results, announced at the end of July 2015, were striking: among people vaccinated immediately, no Ebola cases appeared from ten days after vaccination onwards, while cases did still appear in the delayed group. Because the vaccine seemed so effective, the delayed group was dropped and everyone in the rings was offered the vaccine at once. Final results were published in the medical journal The Lancet at the end of 2016.","Some scientists pointed out that the trial had no placebo group and was fairly small, so claims of '100% protection' were too strong. That kind of careful criticism is normal in good science. Later experience gave a clearer answer: in Ebola outbreaks in the Democratic Republic of the Congo between 2018 and 2020, more than 90,000 people were vaccinated during one long outbreak in the Kivu region alone, and early estimates suggested very high protection.","In November 2019 the vaccine, now made by Merck and named Ervebo, received approval in Europe, and WHO prequalified it (approved it for purchase by UN agencies) within days. The US approved it in December 2019. Ervebo has one awkward feature: it must be stored frozen at very low temperatures, far colder than a home freezer, which makes it tricky to carry into hot, remote areas. Today an emergency stockpile of Ebola vaccine is kept ready, so that when a new outbreak starts, rings of people can be vaccinated within days instead of years."]},{"heading":"Beating a Parasite: The First Malaria Vaccines","paragraphs":["Every vaccine you have met so far in this story protects against a virus or a bacterium. Malaria is different. It is caused by a parasite, a single-celled living thing called Plasmodium, which is far bigger and more complicated than any virus. Five kinds infect people, and the deadliest, Plasmodium falciparum, is most common in Africa. The parasite is spread by the bites of certain female Anopheles mosquitoes. It does not pass directly from one person to another.","Malaria is one of the oldest and biggest killers of humankind. WHO estimates that in 2024 there were about 282 million malaria cases and about 610,000 deaths. About 95% of those deaths were in Africa, and about three out of every four deaths in the African region were children under five years old. Bed nets, insect sprays and medicines have saved millions of lives, but scientists dreamed for more than a century of adding a vaccine to that toolkit.","Why was it so hard? Imagine trying to make a 'wanted poster' for a criminal who keeps changing disguises. After a mosquito bite, the parasite travels in a form called a sporozoite to the liver. There it hides and multiplies inside liver cells for about a week. Then it bursts out in a new form that invades red blood cells, and later some parasites turn into yet another form that mosquitoes can pick up. Each stage wears different proteins on its surface. The parasite also has thousands of genes, compared with about a dozen for a virus like measles, and natural infection does not give people strong, lasting protection the way measles or smallpox did.","The first breakthrough began in the late 1980s at a company laboratory in Belgium, then called SmithKline Beecham Biologicals and today part of GSK, working with the Walter Reed Army Institute of Research in the United States. Their vaccine, called RTS,S (brand name Mosquirix), aims at the very first stage, the sporozoite, to stop the parasite before it can settle in the liver. It joins part of the parasite's main surface protein, the circumsporozoite protein, to the hepatitis B surface antigen, the same protein used in the hepatitis B vaccine you met in part 16. Together they assemble into virus-like particles, a bit like the HPV vaccine. A strong adjuvant called AS01 helps the immune system respond. (The odd name RTS,S comes from the bits of protein it contains: R for a 'repeat' region, T for a T-cell target region, and S for the hepatitis B surface antigen.)","Making it work took about three decades of trials, with support from the charity PATH and the Gates Foundation. A large Phase 3 trial in seven African countries found that the vaccine roughly halved malaria cases in young children over the first year, though protection faded over time and a fourth dose helped. That is far from perfect, but in places where a child might catch malaria several times a year, even partial protection can prevent a huge number of illnesses.","In 2019, Malawi, Ghana and Kenya began a big pilot programme, giving the vaccine to young children through ordinary clinics. Researchers watched closely for safety and for real-world benefits. The results showed fewer hospital admissions for severe malaria, and the vaccine was given safely to well over a million children. In October 2021, WHO recommended RTS,S for wide use in children living where malaria is common. It was the first vaccine ever recommended against a human parasitic disease, a true milestone in medical history.","Just two years later a second vaccine arrived. R21/Matrix-M was developed by the Jenner Institute at the University of Oxford (named, of course, after Edward Jenner) with partners including the Kenya Medical Research Institute, and it is manufactured by the Serum Institute of India, one of the world's biggest vaccine makers. It uses a similar idea to RTS,S, with a different adjuvant called Matrix-M. In trials it reached about 75% efficacy against malaria in young children in places where malaria comes in seasonal waves. Ghana became the first country to approve it, in April 2023, and WHO recommended it in October 2023. Because the Serum Institute can make huge numbers of doses, it plans to produce well over a hundred million doses a year. African countries began adding these vaccines to regular childhood schedules in 2024, alongside bed nets, medicines and mosquito control rather than instead of them."]},{"heading":"Old Enemies, New Weapons: RSV and Tuberculosis","paragraphs":["Respiratory syncytial virus, or RSV, is a common virus that usually causes a cold. Almost every child catches it by the age of two. But in tiny babies and in older adults it can infect the lungs and cause serious breathing problems, and it sends many people to hospital each winter.","The search for an RSV vaccine has a sad history that taught scientists an important lesson. In the 1960s, researchers tested a vaccine made of RSV killed with a chemical called formalin. Instead of protecting children, it made some of them sicker when they later caught RSV naturally, and two children died. The immune system had been trained to make the wrong kind of response. That shock made scientists extremely careful, and for more than fifty years there was no RSV vaccine at all.","The key that finally unlocked the problem came from structural biology, the science of seeing the exact shapes of molecules. In 2013, Jason McLellan, Barney Graham and their colleagues at the US National Institutes of Health mapped the structure of RSV's fusion protein, called F, the protein the virus uses to break into cells. They found that F has two shapes: a 'before' shape (prefusion) and an 'after' shape (postfusion). The best protective antibodies attack the 'before' shape, but the protein naturally flips into its 'after' shape, a bit like a mousetrap snapping shut. The team found ways to lock it in the 'before' shape. You may recognise the names: the same scientists later used similar tricks to stabilise the coronavirus spike protein for COVID-19 vaccines (part 18).","This work paid off in 2023. In May 2023, the US approved Arexvy, made by GSK, for adults aged 60 and over, followed by Abrysvo, made by Pfizer. In August 2023 Abrysvo was also approved for use during pregnancy, so that a mother's antibodies pass to her baby and help protect the newborn during its first months. In 2024 an mRNA RSV vaccine, Moderna's mRESVIA, was approved too.","Tuberculosis, or TB, is an even older foe. You met its bacterium in part 7, when Robert Koch identified it in 1882, and the BCG vaccine in part 9, first given in 1921. Astonishingly, more than a hundred years later, BCG is still the only TB vaccine in common use. It helps protect babies and young children from the most severe forms of TB, but its protection for teenagers and adults, who spread most TB, varies enormously between studies, from very little to fairly good. Meanwhile TB still kills more than a million people a year, making it one of the deadliest infectious diseases on Earth.","Several new TB vaccines are now in large trials. One, called M72/AS01E, combines two TB proteins with the same kind of adjuvant used in the RTS,S malaria vaccine. In a trial in Kenya, South Africa and Zambia, adults who already carried TB bacteria without being ill were about half as likely to develop TB disease over three years if they had the vaccine. That was promising enough to launch a much larger Phase 3 trial in 2024 with many thousands of volunteers, which is expected to run until the end of the 2020s. Another candidate, MTBVAC, is a weakened form of the human TB bacterium itself and is also in Phase 3 trials. Nobody yet knows if these will succeed. If one does, it could be the first new TB vaccine in over a century."]},{"heading":"Ideas Still in the Lab","paragraphs":["Everything in this section is research, not a promise. First, the universal flu vaccine. In part 11 you learned that flu vaccines are updated every year because flu viruses keep changing. The flu virus is covered in a protein called hemagglutinin, which is shaped a little like a lollipop or a mushroom, with a round head on top of a stalk. Most of the antibodies our bodies make attack the head, but the head changes constantly, so last year's antibodies may not fit this year's virus. The stalk changes much less. Scientists at places such as the Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai in New York have designed vaccines that give people a series of 'mismatched' heads on the same stalk, hoping to steer the immune system toward the stalk instead. Early human trials have been encouraging, but at least one large trial of a 'universal' candidate found no protection at all. As of the mid-2020s, no universal flu vaccine has been approved.","Second, cancer vaccines. There are two very different ideas here. Preventive cancer vaccines, like the HPV and hepatitis B vaccines, stop cancer-causing viruses from infecting people. Therapeutic cancer vaccines are meant to help the immune system attack a cancer that is already there. That is much harder, because cancer cells come from the body's own cells, and the immune system is trained not to attack 'self'. Only a few therapeutic cancer vaccines have ever been approved, such as sipuleucel-T for advanced prostate cancer in 2010, and many others have failed in trials.","The newest approach is personalised. Cancer cells pile up mistakes in their DNA, and some of those mistakes create unusual proteins, called neoantigens, that healthy cells do not have. Researchers can read the genes of a patient's tumour, use computers to pick out the most promising neoantigens, and then build an mRNA vaccine carrying instructions for those exact targets, all within a matter of weeks. Each vaccine is made for just one person. Companies including Moderna with Merck, and BioNTech, the company you met in part 18, are testing this idea in trials for skin cancer (melanoma), pancreatic cancer and other cancers. Some mid-stage results have been encouraging, but large trials still need to show whether these vaccines really help, and for whom.","Third, vaccines without needles. Many people dislike injections, and needles need trained staff and safe disposal. A few vaccines can already be swallowed (like Sabin's polio drops) or sprayed into the nose (like one kind of flu vaccine). Researchers are also building microneedle patches: small sticky patches covered with hundreds of tiny points, each shorter than a millimetre, that carry the vaccine just into the top layers of the skin and then dissolve. Patches could be easier to transport, could perhaps be given without a fully trained nurse, and leave no sharp needles to throw away. Patches for measles and rubella have been tested in young children in early trials, but they are not yet in routine use.","Fourth, vaccines that do not need a fridge. Remember the cold chain from part 12: most vaccines must stay between 2°C and 8°C all the way from the factory to the clinic. In very hot places with unreliable electricity, that is one of the biggest obstacles to protecting children. Scientists are experimenting with dry powders, special sugars that 'freeze' proteins in place like insects in amber, and tougher vaccine designs. A vaccine that could sit happily in a backpack for weeks would be a gift to health workers everywhere."]},{"heading":"Getting Ready for Disease X","paragraphs":["What about the next pandemic? Nobody knows what germ will cause it, so WHO and scientists sometimes give it a placeholder name: Disease X. Preparing for Disease X means building tools in advance, so that the world can respond faster than it did in 2020.","One of the main groups doing this is CEPI, the Coalition for Epidemic Preparedness Innovations. It was launched in January 2017 at the World Economic Forum meeting in Davos, Switzerland, by the governments of Norway and India, the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation, the Wellcome Trust and the World Economic Forum, and it is based in Oslo, Norway. CEPI was partly inspired by the lesson of Ebola: vaccine research for dangerous but rare diseases had been neglected until it was almost too late. CEPI funds work on vaccines for diseases such as Lassa fever, Nipah virus, MERS and Rift Valley fever, which have caused deadly outbreaks but have few or no approved vaccines.","In 2022 CEPI and its partners set a bold goal called the 100 Days Mission: to be able to produce a safe, effective vaccine against a new pandemic threat within 100 days of identifying it. To see how ambitious that is, count the days for COVID-19. The coronavirus's genetic sequence was shared on 11 January 2020, and the first vaccine was authorised in the UK on 2 December 2020. That is 326 days, which was a world record, yet the 100 Days Mission aims to cut it by more than two-thirds. To get there, scientists are building 'libraries' of vaccine designs for whole families of viruses, so that much of the work is already done before a new virus appears.","There is also unfinished business from earlier in this story: polio. In 1988, when the Global Polio Eradication Initiative began, wild polio paralysed an estimated 350,000 people a year in more than 125 countries. Since then cases have fallen by more than 99%. Two of the three types of wild poliovirus have been eradicated: type 2 was certified gone in 2015 and type 3 in 2019. India was certified polio-free as part of its region in 2014, and Africa was declared free of wild polio in 2020. As of the mid-2020s, wild poliovirus is still passed on in only two countries, Pakistan and Afghanistan. Polio is not eradicated yet, and stopping the last chains of infection in those areas, where conflict and difficult terrain make vaccination hard, is one of the toughest jobs in global health.","There is one more complication, which you met in part 14. In places where few children are vaccinated, the weakened virus in Sabin's oral vaccine can very rarely spread from child to child for a long time and change back into a form that can cause paralysis. Scientists call these outbreaks 'vaccine-derived' polio. They are rare, and they can be stopped by vaccinating more children. To reduce the risk, scientists have designed a new, more genetically stable oral vaccine called nOPV2, first used in 2021 under an emergency listing and given full WHO prequalification in December 2023. The long-term plan is to stop using live oral polio vaccine once wild polio is gone, so that only the inactivated vaccine that Jonas Salk pioneered remains.","Look at how many ideas from earlier chapters appear in these new frontiers: Foege's ring vaccination stopping Ebola, the hepatitis B antigen inside a malaria vaccine, Jenner's name on an Oxford institute, Sabin's and Salk's vaccines still fighting polio, and mRNA reaching toward cancer. New inventions almost never appear from nowhere. They are built on everything that came before. Which makes this the perfect moment to stop, turn around, and look back along the whole road, from a pinch of scab powder blown up a nose to a strand of genetic code in a tiny bubble of fat."]}],"deepDive":"Many of the new frontiers share one theme: vaccines are increasingly designed rather than discovered. Jenner and Pasteur worked with whole organisms they could not see; the HPV, RSV and malaria vaccines were built around single, carefully chosen proteins. Structure-based design, used for RSV's prefusion F protein, uses X-ray crystallography and cryo-electron microscopy to see an antigen at near-atomic detail, then adds small mutations, such as extra chemical bonds, that lock it into the shape that provokes the best antibodies. The RSV story also shows why history matters. The formalin-inactivated RSV vaccine of the 1960s caused 'enhanced respiratory disease', most likely because it presented the post-fusion form of F and steered the immune system toward antibodies that bound poorly and inflammatory responses that did harm. Regulators still examine new respiratory vaccines for this risk. Malaria shows the limits of the protein approach. RTS,S and R21 target only the sporozoite stage; a single sporozoite that escapes and reaches the liver can still start an infection, which helps explain why efficacy is partial and fades without boosters. Researchers are testing blood-stage and 'transmission-blocking' vaccines that would stop mosquitoes picking up the parasite, and combinations of stages. Debates also surround how trials are designed. The Guinea Ebola trial used 'cluster randomisation' (whole rings, not individuals, were randomised) with delayed vaccination rather than a placebo, because withholding a possibly life-saving vaccine during an epidemic seemed unethical. Critics, including a 2017 report from the US National Academies, argued its small number of cases made the precise efficacy figure uncertain, even though the vaccine was clearly protective. Finally, the 100 Days Mission depends as much on manufacturing, regulation and fairness as on science. During COVID-19, high-income countries received most early doses; many experts argue that building factories and regulatory capacity in Africa, Asia and Latin America, such as the planned R21 production in Ghana, is essential if the next fast vaccine is to reach everyone quickly.","funFact":"The RTS,S malaria vaccine contains a piece of the hepatitis B virus's surface protein, so a vaccine invented to fight a liver-attacking virus ended up helping to carry the first-ever vaccine against a parasite.","check":{"q":"What was special about the RTS,S vaccine that WHO recommended in October 2021?","a":"It was the first vaccine recommended against a human parasite (malaria)","wrong":["It was the first vaccine made with mRNA","It was the first vaccine against a cancer-causing virus","It was the first vaccine that needed no refrigeration"]},"sources":[{"title":"HPV vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HPV_vaccine"},{"title":"Ian Frazer","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ian_Frazer"},{"title":"rVSV-ZEBOV vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RVSV-ZEBOV_vaccine"},{"title":"Ebola vaccine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebola_vaccine"},{"title":"Malaria fact sheet","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/malaria"},{"title":"RTS,S","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RTS,S"},{"title":"Poliomyelitis fact sheet","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/poliomyelitis"},{"title":"Coalition for Epidemic Preparedness Innovations","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coalition_for_Epidemic_Preparedness_Innovations"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/19-1.jpg","alt":"3D models of human papillomavirus (HPV) shells (red) over an electron microscope image of HPV particles.","file":"HumanPapillomavirus.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HumanPapillomavirus.jpg","author":"NIAID","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/19-2.jpg","alt":"An Anopheles gambiae mosquito, which spreads malaria, landing on skin to feed.","file":"Anopheles gambiae mosquito feeding (CDC PHIL 1354).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anopheles_gambiae_mosquito_feeding_(CDC_PHIL_1354).jpg","author":"CDC / James D. Gathany","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/19-3.jpg","alt":"Health workers give UN peacekeepers the Ebola vaccine in Pinga, Democratic Republic of the Congo, in 2019.","file":"EBOLA Vaccination - Pinga, Nord Kivu, 2019 (48812384386).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EBOLA_Vaccination_-_Pinga,_Nord_Kivu,_2019_(48812384386).jpg","author":"MONUSCO Photos","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"words":4313},{"n":20,"title":"From Scabs to Code: The Whole Story","when":"Ancient times – today","summary":"Vaccines were not invented by one genius in one day. Over hundreds of years, healers, farmers, doctors, nurses, volunteers and scientists all added a piece, and together they saved well over a hundred million lives.","sections":[{"heading":"One Clue, Many Centuries","paragraphs":["Imagine laying out every object from this story along one very long museum table. At one end sits a small dish of dried smallpox scabs, ground to powder, with a thin tube for blowing it into a nose. A little further along is a lancet, a tiny blade like the one Edward Jenner used in 1796. Then come glass flasks from Louis Pasteur's laboratory, a dried rabbit spinal cord in a jar, a vial of diphtheria toxoid, an iron lung, a sugar cube with a pink drop of polio vaccine, a two-pronged smallpox needle, and at the very end, a small glass vial of mRNA vaccine that had to be kept frozen. Thousands of years separate the two ends of that table. Yet they are connected by a single chain of ideas.","The chain begins with an observation that people made in many places, long before anyone knew what a germ was: if you survived smallpox, you almost never caught it again (part 1). The body, somehow, remembered. That simple clue is the root of everything else in this story. It is also a good reminder that science often starts with ordinary people noticing patterns in the world around them.","The next link was a brave, risky idea: if surviving smallpox protects you, why not catch a mild case on purpose? That was variolation, also called inoculation (part 2). Healers in China blew scab powder into the nose; written descriptions date from the mid-1500s, although stories of much earlier origins are legends. In India, Africa and the Ottoman Empire, people scratched smallpox material into the skin. Variolation killed roughly 1 or 2 people in every 100, and the people who had it could spread real smallpox, but that was far better than the roughly 30 in 100 who died of natural smallpox.","Knowledge of inoculation travelled to Europe and America through very different people (parts 3 and 4). Lady Mary Wortley Montagu, an English noblewoman scarred by smallpox, watched it in Constantinople and had her own children inoculated. Onesimus, an enslaved African man in Boston, explained to Cotton Mather how he had been inoculated as a child. Doctors such as Zabdiel Boylston kept careful records showing that inoculated people died far less often than those who caught smallpox naturally. Empresses, presidents and armies followed: Catherine the Great in 1768 and George Washington's Continental Army in 1777."]},{"heading":"From Cowpox to Weakened Germs","paragraphs":["The third link was a safer substitute. Country people had noticed that milkmaids who caught cowpox seemed to escape smallpox. Benjamin Jesty, a Dorset farmer, tried cowpox on his own family in 1774 (part 5). Edward Jenner did not invent the idea, but on 14 May 1796 he took material from the cowpox sores of milkmaid Sarah Nelmes and put it into the arm of eight-year-old James Phipps. He tested it, recorded it, published it in 1798 and spent the rest of his life promoting it. The new method did not spread smallpox to others, and it was far safer than variolation. Because it came from a cow, it was called vaccination, from the Latin word vacca. (Jenner's test on a child, and his deliberate exposure of James to smallpox, would never be allowed today.)","Then came the problem of distance: how do you keep a living vaccine alive on a long journey with no refrigerators? The answer was arm-to-arm vaccination (part 6). On the Balmis Expedition, which left Spain in 1803, a chain of orphan boys, cared for by the nurse Isabel Zendal, carried cowpox across the ocean, one child passing it to the next. Later, vaccine was grown on the skin of calves and stored in glycerin. Along the way, opposition grew too, from cartoons of people sprouting cow heads to mass protests against compulsory vaccination laws. Debates about trust and choice are almost as old as vaccines themselves.","Nobody yet knew why any of this worked. The next link was germ theory (part 7). Ignaz Semmelweis showed that doctors washing their hands saved mothers' lives. Louis Pasteur showed that microbes come from other microbes. Robert Koch proved that a specific bacterium causes anthrax and identified the germ that causes tuberculosis in 1882. Then Dmitri Ivanovsky and Martinus Beijerinck found disease agents so small they slipped through filters that trapped bacteria: viruses.","Once people knew germs caused disease, Pasteur and his team asked whether a weakened germ could protect people the way cowpox did (part 8). They weakened the germs of chicken cholera and anthrax, and they made a rabies treatment from dried rabbit spinal cords, even though they could not see the rabies virus. In July 1885, nine-year-old Joseph Meister, bitten by a rabid dog, received a course of injections, given by doctors because Pasteur was a chemist, not a physician, and he survived. In Jenner's honour, Pasteur used the word 'vaccine' for all such preparations, not just cowpox.","Next came toxins and toxoids (part 9). Emil von Behring and Kitasato Shibasaburō discovered antitoxins, antibodies that neutralise bacterial poisons. Antitoxin was a treatment, the precious cargo carried by dog-sled teams to Nome, Alaska, in 1925. Gaston Ramon then learned to make toxins harmless with formaldehyde and heat, creating toxoid vaccines against diphtheria and tetanus. In the same era came vaccines for typhoid, cholera, plague, tuberculosis (BCG), yellow fever and whooping cough, each one the work of a dedicated team."]},{"heading":"Opening the Body's Black Box","paragraphs":["For most of this history, the immune system was a mysterious black box. You put a vaccine in, protection came out, and nobody could see what happened in between. Slowly, the box was opened (parts 10 and 11). Élie Metchnikoff watched mobile cells swarm around a rose thorn stuck into a see-through starfish larva in 1882, and discovered phagocytes, the body's 'eating cells'. Paul Ehrlich imagined antibodies as molecules that fit their targets like keys in locks. Frank Macfarlane Burnet's clonal selection theory in 1957 explained how the one rare B cell or T cell that matches a germ gets chosen and copies itself thousands of times.","The most important discovery for vaccines was memory. After an infection, or a vaccine, some of those chosen cells become long-lived memory cells. The next time the same germ appears, they respond faster and more strongly, often stopping the germ before you even feel ill. That is the answer to the ancient puzzle of part 1. When smallpox survivors did not catch it again, it was their memory cells doing their job. A vaccine works by showing the immune system a harmless version or piece of a germ, like a wanted poster, so it can build that memory without the person having to suffer the disease.","Once scientists understood this, they could design vaccines on purpose, choosing which piece of a germ to show (part 12). Some vaccines use live weakened germs, some use killed germs, some use toxoids, and some use just a single protein. They also learned to test vaccines properly: first in the lab and in animals, then in clinical trials in three phases with more and more volunteers, with comparison groups and 'blinding' so that nobody's hopes could tip the results. Even after approval, safety monitoring continues. And they built the cold chain, the line of fridges and cool boxes that keeps vaccines at the right temperature from factory to faraway clinic."]},{"heading":"Polio, Eradication and the Genetic Age","paragraphs":["A key that unlocked the modern era was cell culture, growing viruses in flasks of living cells instead of in animals. In 1949 John Enders, Thomas Weller and Frederick Robbins grew poliovirus in non-nerve tissue, which made a polio vaccine possible (part 13). Jonas Salk's injected, inactivated vaccine was tested in 1954 on about 1.8 million 'Polio Pioneers' and announced as safe and effective in April 1955. Albert Sabin's live, weakened oral vaccine followed, tested on millions of people in the Soviet Union with the help of Mikhail Chumakov (part 14). In the United States, polio cases fell from about 58,000 in 1952 to under 1,000 by 1962.","The polio story also taught hard lessons. In the Cutter incident of 1955, some batches from one company contained live virus and caused paralysis in about 200 children, which led to much stricter rules for checking every batch. Ever since, careful manufacturing and inspection have been as important to vaccines as clever science.","Then came the most ambitious idea of all: not just protecting people, but wiping a disease off the planet (part 15). After Soviet scientist Viktor Zhdanov proposed it in 1958, WHO's Intensified Smallpox Eradication Programme began in 1967, led by D.A. Henderson. With freeze-dried vaccine that survived heat, Benjamin Rubin's simple bifurcated needle, and William Foege's strategy of finding each case and vaccinating everyone around it, tens of thousands of health workers tracked smallpox into its final hiding places. Ali Maow Maalin, a hospital cook in Merca, Somalia, had the last natural case in October 1977. In May 1980 the World Health Assembly declared smallpox eradicated, the first and so far only human disease ever wiped out.","Meanwhile, a golden age of childhood vaccines arrived (part 16): measles in 1963, mumps, rubella, and the combined MMR vaccine developed by Maurice Hilleman's team, chickenpox in Japan, and the hepatitis B vaccine. In 1986 the hepatitis B vaccine became the first vaccine made by genetic engineering, produced by yeast cells given a single viral gene. It was also the first vaccine that helps prevent a cancer. WHO's Expanded Programme on Immunization, founded in 1974, and later Gavi, the Vaccine Alliance, worked to carry these vaccines to children in every country.","As diseases faded, fear of them faded too, and worries about vaccines grew (part 17). Some concerns were real and were caught by monitoring systems, which led to vaccines being changed or withdrawn. Others, such as Andrew Wakefield's fraudulent 1998 paper claiming a link between MMR and autism, were proven false by huge studies, yet still caused measles to return in places where vaccination fell.","The newest link in the chain was mRNA (part 18). For decades Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman worked on a molecule most scientists had given up on. Their 2005 discovery that modified building blocks stop mRNA causing harmful inflammation, combined with lipid nanoparticles to carry it, and stabilised spike proteins designed by Barney Graham and Jason McLellan, allowed COVID-19 vaccines to be built and tested in record time in 2020, without skipping any trial phase. Over 13 billion doses of COVID-19 vaccines were given worldwide. And as part 19 showed, the chain is still growing, with vaccines against HPV, Ebola, malaria and RSV, and research into many more."]},{"heading":"The People History Almost Forgot","paragraphs":["When people tell the history of vaccines, a few famous names usually take the spotlight: Jenner, Pasteur, Salk. They earned their fame. But this story would not exist without many people who were never famous, and some who were not even asked.","Onesimus, an enslaved man from West Africa, shared knowledge from his homeland that helped save lives in Boston in 1721, yet he was not free, and we do not even know the name he was born with. Lady Mary Wortley Montagu spoke up for inoculation at a time when women were rarely listened to in medicine. Benjamin Jesty, the Dorset farmer, protected his family with cowpox 22 years before Jenner's experiment, and only late in life received some recognition for it. Countless women healers in the Ottoman Empire, Africa and Asia, whose names no one wrote down, carried out inoculations for generations.","Isabel Zendal, the nurse of the Balmis Expedition, cared for the orphan boys whose arms carried the vaccine across oceans. In 1950 WHO recognised her as the first nurse known to take part in an international health mission, and in 2020, during the COVID-19 pandemic, a new emergency hospital in Madrid was named after her. And the boys themselves, about 22 at the start of the voyage, were living links in the chain, too young to choose that role.","Pearl Kendrick and Grace Eldering, working at a state laboratory in Michigan in the 1930s, often after their normal working hours, built a whooping cough vaccine and ran some of the most careful field trials of their time. Henrietta Lacks, an African American woman, had cells taken from her tumour in 1951 without her knowledge or consent; those HeLa cells were used to test polio vaccines and in a huge range of other research. Her family's long struggle for recognition helped change the rules about consent in medical research.","Then there are the people almost nobody can name. The hundreds of thousands of local vaccinators and searchers in the smallpox campaign, who walked from village to village with recognition cards and bifurcated needles. Rahima Banu, the little girl in Bangladesh who had the last natural case of the deadliest form of smallpox in 1975. Ali Maow Maalin, who after his own illness spent years helping to vaccinate children against polio in Somalia. The parents who signed up their children as Polio Pioneers. The tens of thousands of volunteers in every Phase 3 trial, including the trials of COVID-19, malaria and Ebola vaccines. Each one added a link to the chain."]},{"heading":"How the World Changed","paragraphs":["What has all this effort achieved? Let us look at some numbers. In 2024, a WHO-led study published in The Lancet estimated that immunisation had saved at least 154 million lives over the previous 50 years, roughly six lives every minute for half a century. About 101 million of those lives were babies. The measles vaccine alone accounted for about 60% of the lives saved. The study also found that vaccination against 14 diseases accounted for about 40% of the worldwide drop in infant deaths, and more than half of the drop in Africa. On average, each life saved meant about 66 extra years of healthy life.","Smallpox, which killed an estimated 300 million people in the 20th century alone, is gone. It took about 180 years to get from James Phipps's arm in 1796 to the last natural case in 1977, but it happened. Children no longer have to be vaccinated against it, because there is nothing left to catch. The only remaining samples of the virus are kept under strict security in two laboratories, one in the United States and one in Russia.","Polio is close behind. Since 1988, cases of wild polio have fallen by more than 99%, from about 350,000 a year in more than 125 countries. Two of the three types of wild poliovirus have been eradicated, and wild polio now survives in only two countries, Pakistan and Afghanistan. It is not gone yet, and the last steps are the hardest, but most of the world's children now grow up without ever seeing anyone paralysed by polio.","Measles shows the change especially clearly. Before the measles vaccine became widely used, measles is estimated to have caused about 2.6 million deaths a year around the world, most of them young children. Measles is so contagious that about 95% of people need to be immune to stop it spreading, so reaching that level took decades of work by health services in every country. That single vaccine is the biggest reason the 154-million figure is so large.","Think about what that means for ordinary life. In the 1700s, parents often waited until their children had survived smallpox before counting them as safe. In the 1950s, many towns closed swimming pools each summer in fear of polio. Today, in much of the world, those fears have become history lessons, which is exactly why it can be hard to remember how frightening these diseases once were.","But the story is not finished. Millions of children each year still miss out on some or all of their routine vaccines, often because they live in poor, remote or war-torn places. Diseases like measles return wherever vaccination falls. And diseases such as tuberculosis, malaria and HIV still kill huge numbers of people. The work continues."]},{"heading":"Lessons From Three Hundred Years","paragraphs":["What can we learn from this long story? The first lesson is careful testing. Lady Mary, Boylston, Jenner and Pasteur were all, in their own ways, trying to collect evidence. Over time that turned into the rigorous modern system of clinical trials, comparison groups, independent review and safety monitoring. Disasters like the Cutter incident taught that every batch must be checked. The point of all this testing is not to slow things down for no reason, but to find out what really works and what is truly safe.","The second lesson is honest science. Scientists are human, and they can make mistakes or, very rarely, cheat, as Andrew Wakefield did. Science corrects itself when other researchers check claims, repeat experiments and look at very large groups of people. The same honesty means admitting when something goes wrong, and fixing it, as happened with the 1976 swine flu vaccine and RotaShield.","The third lesson is ethics. Some famous experiments in this story, like Jenner's test on James Phipps or the inoculation of prisoners at Newgate in 1721, would never be allowed today. Henrietta Lacks's cells were taken without her consent. Modern rules exist because people in the past were harmed or not asked. Today, volunteers must understand what a trial involves and freely agree to it.","The fourth lesson is fairness. Vaccines only work if they reach people. The Balmis Expedition, the smallpox eradication campaign, the Expanded Programme on Immunization and Gavi were all attempts to share vaccines across the world. During COVID-19, wealthy countries received most of the first doses, while many poorer countries waited months. Many experts argue that building vaccine factories and expertise in every region, as is now happening with malaria vaccine production in Africa, is one of the best ways to make the next response fairer.","The fifth lesson is trust. Vaccines are given to healthy people, often to babies, so trust matters enormously. People have always asked questions about them, from the 1700s to today, and asking questions is reasonable. Trust is built when scientists explain their evidence clearly, admit uncertainty, listen to people's worries, and are honest about past mistakes."]},{"heading":"Your Link in the Chain","paragraphs":["If there is one big idea to take from these 20 parts, it is this: science is a team effort that stretches across centuries and continents. No single person invented vaccines. A Chinese healer, a Turkish woman with a needle, an enslaved man in Boston, a Dorset farmer, an English country doctor, a French chemist, a Japanese bacteriologist, two Michigan scientists, an Australian lab, a Hungarian biochemist who refused to give up, and millions of volunteers and health workers each added a link. Many of them were told they were wrong before they were proved right.","That means the next link could be added by someone reading this right now. The challenges ahead are real: a vaccine for tuberculosis that protects adults, a flu vaccine that lasts, vaccines that never need a fridge, ways to finish off polio, and readiness for whatever Disease X turns out to be. Solving them will need biologists, chemists, engineers, computer scientists, nurses, data experts, writers who can explain science clearly, and people who are good at building trust in their communities. Curiosity is the starting point. Asking 'why?' and 'how do we know?' is exactly what every scientist in this story did.","One last important reminder. This story is about the science and history of vaccines. It is not medical advice. If you have questions about your own health, or about any vaccine for yourself or your family, the right people to ask are a doctor, a nurse and the trusted adults in your life.","Now picture one more scene. It is 14 May 1796, in the village of Berkeley in Gloucestershire, England. A boy named James Phipps holds out his arm, and a country doctor makes two small cuts. Nobody watching could have imagined where that moment would lead. Follow the chain forward: to orphan boys on a Spanish ship, to sheep in a field at Pouilly-le-Fort, to Joseph Meister in Paris, to Polio Pioneers lining up in school gyms, to a toddler in Bangladesh and a cook in Somalia, to Margaret Keenan rolling up her sleeve in Coventry in 2020. Today, every year, more than a hundred million babies receive vaccines. Billions of arms around the world, in cities, villages, deserts and mountains, are linked by an invisible thread to one small arm in an English country garden more than two centuries ago."]}],"deepDive":"Historians often warn against telling the vaccine story as a straight line of heroic discoveries, even though a 20-part recap inevitably makes it look that way. The real path was full of dead ends, rivalries and luck. Jenner's own explanation of why cowpox worked was mistaken (he thought cowpox and smallpox both came from a horse disease called 'grease'), and the vaccinia virus used in the 20th century turns out to be closer to horsepox than cowpox, with an uncertain origin. Pasteur kept parts of his methods private, and historians later found from his notebooks that the anthrax vaccine used at Pouilly-le-Fort was prepared with a chemical method similar to his rival Toussaint's. Credit is another live debate. Older textbooks credited Jenner with 'discovering vaccination', while recent historians emphasise Asian and African inoculation, women healers, enslaved knowledge-holders such as Onesimus, and the thousands of health workers in eradication campaigns. Measuring impact is also hard. The 154-million-lives estimate comes from mathematical models of 14 diseases across almost every country, comparing what happened with what would have happened without vaccines; it depends on assumptions about disease rates and treatment, so it is best read as an approximate lower bound rather than a precise count. Eradication is rarer than many people expect. Of all the human diseases targeted, only smallpox has been eradicated; a successful target usually needs no animal reservoir, an infection that is easy to recognise, and a vaccine giving long-lasting protection. Smallpox met all three, while polio's often-silent infections make it far harder to trace. Finally, equity remains the biggest unsolved problem. Global coverage of the third dose of the diphtheria-tetanus-pertussis vaccine (a standard measure) reached the mid-80s per cent by the 2010s but then stalled, and millions of so-called 'zero-dose' children receive no routine vaccines at all. Many specialists argue that the next great advance may come not from a new molecule but from delivering existing vaccines to every child.","funFact":"The hide of Blossom, the cow that gave milkmaid Sarah Nelmes the cowpox Jenner used in 1796, still hangs in the library of St George's medical school in London, and her horns are kept at Jenner's house museum in Berkeley.","check":{"q":"According to a 2024 WHO-led study, about how many lives has immunisation saved over the previous 50 years?","a":"At least 154 million","wrong":["About 1.5 million","About 15 million","About 1.5 billion"]},"sources":[{"title":"Global immunization efforts have saved at least 154 million lives over the past 50 years","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-04-2024-global-immunization-efforts-have-saved-at-least-154-million-lives-over-the-past-50-years"},{"title":"Smallpox","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/health-topics/smallpox"},{"title":"Poliomyelitis fact sheet","publisher":"World Health Organization","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/poliomyelitis"},{"title":"Edward Jenner","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Jenner"},{"title":"James Phipps","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Phipps"},{"title":"Onesimus (Bostonian)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onesimus_(Bostonian)"},{"title":"Isabel Zendal","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabel_Zendal"},{"title":"Polio eradication","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polio_eradication"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/20-1.jpg","alt":"Edward Jenner vaccinating a young child held by its mother, in an 1884 oil painting by Eugène Hillemacher.","file":"Edward Jenner vaccinating a boy. Oil painting by E.-E. Hille Wellcome L0029094 (cropped).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Jenner_vaccinating_a_boy._Oil_painting_by_E.-E._Hille_Wellcome_L0029094_(cropped).jpg","author":"Eugène Ernest Hillemacher","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/vaccines/20-2.jpg","alt":"Dr Jenner's House in Berkeley, Gloucestershire, England, where he lived and which is now a museum.","file":"Dr Edward Jenner's House, Berkeley, Gloucestershire - geograph.org.uk - 5019311.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dr_Edward_Jenner%27s_House,_Berkeley,_Gloucestershire_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5019311.jpg","author":"Alf Beard","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"words":3706}],"words":79336};
